window.REFS={"01-sepsis":{"slogan":"Treat right, treat fast · control the source · stop the fluid when it stops helping","legend":"NE = norepinephrine · CRT = capillary refill time · SSC = Surviving Sepsis Campaign","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – ESICM Emergency Response (sepsis modules) · Sepsis and Septic Shock Part IV (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise sepsis and shock","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Sepsis</b> = organ dysfunction from a dysregulated response to infection (Sepsis-3; use SOFA)</li> <li>Consider it in <b>every</b> critically ill patient until proven otherwise</li> <li><b>Hypotension:</b> MAP &lt; 65, SBP &lt; 90, or fall &gt; 40 mmHg from baseline</li> <li><b>Three windows:</b> skin (mottling, long CRT), kidney (low urine output), brain (altered mentation)</li> <li><b>Lactate</b>: dysoxia marker; follow its kinetics</li> <li>Multimodal: examination + lactate + CRT + haemodynamics</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"First hour: treat right, treat fast","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Antibiotics immediately, ideally &lt; 1 h</b> in shock or likely sepsis</li> <li><b>≥ 2 blood culture pairs</b> (≥ 10 mL per bottle) + site cultures first, <b>without delaying</b> antibiotics</li> <li>Fluids: SSC <b>≥ 30 mL/kg crystalloid within 3 h</b>, reassessing often (low-quality evidence, not mandatory)</li> <li><b>Noradrenaline within 1 h</b> if fluids fail to reach MAP target</li> <li>Initial <b>MAP target 65 mmHg</b></li> <li>Search for the source and plan source control at once</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Fluids: which and how much","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Crystalloids first</b>; balanced or saline (avoid hyperchloraemia)</li> <li><b>Albumin</b> if large crystalloid volumes needed</li> <li>Fluid challenge: continue only while haemodynamics improve</li> <li>Phases: resuscitation (first 4–6 h) → optimisation → stabilisation → <b>de-resuscitation</b> (from 24–48 h)</li> <li>Fluid overload raises mortality</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>No HES</b>; avoid gelatins</li></ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Is the patient fluid responsive?","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Test</td><td><b>Positive</b></td><td><b>Limits</b></td></tr> <tr><td>PPV / SVV</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 12%</td><td>Sedated, passive, regular rhythm, good compliance</td></tr> <tr><td>Passive leg raise</td><td class=\"tgt\">CO ↑ ≥ 10%</td><td>Needs CO measure; not with ↑ ICP</td></tr> <tr><td>End-exp. occlusion</td><td class=\"tgt\">CO ↑ ≥ 5%</td><td>Intubated, 15 s pause; less valid at VT 6 mL/kg</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"5","h":"Vasopressors and inotropes (ACE Table 6)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Agent</td><td><b>Initial</b></td><td><b>Maximum</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Noradrenaline (1st)</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.1 mcg/kg/min</td><td>3 mcg/kg/min <span class=\"flag\">per ACE – verify</span></td></tr> <tr><td>Adrenaline</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.05 mcg/kg/min</td><td>1 mcg/kg/min</td></tr> <tr><td>Phenylephrine</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.1 mcg/kg/min</td><td>1 mcg/kg/min</td></tr> <tr><td>Dopamine (α dose)</td><td class=\"tgt\">10 mcg/kg/min</td><td>20 mcg/kg/min</td></tr> <tr><td>Vasopressin</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.1 mU/kg/min</td><td>2 mU/kg/min</td></tr> <tr><td>Terlipressin</td><td class=\"tgt\">1.3 mcg/kg/h</td><td>20 mcg/kg/h</td></tr> <tr><td>Angiotensin II</td><td class=\"tgt\">20 ng/kg/min</td><td>80 ng/kg/min</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>Dobutamine</b> for low output with myocardial dysfunction: titrate to CI 3.0–3.5 L/min/m² (no evidence base; dose per CMH formulary). Low-dose dopamine does not protect the kidney.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Rising noradrenaline: escalate","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>NE dose</td><td><b>Action</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Mild &lt; 0.1</td><td>Titrate to MAP target</td></tr> <tr><td>Moderate 0.1–0.19</td><td>Check ScvO₂, echo, ionised Ca, pH; consider 2nd vasopressor</td></tr> <tr><td>Severe 0.2–0.29</td><td>Add 2nd vasopressor; <b>stress-dose steroid</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Resistant 0.3–0.9</td><td>Two vasopressors</td></tr> <tr><td>Refractory &gt; 1 (NE-equiv.)</td><td>Rescue options below</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Units mcg/kg/min. Rescue: hydrocortisone ± fludrocortisone, levosimendan, methylene blue, angiotensin II, blood purification, VA-ECMO (by mechanism); doses per CMH formulary unless listed.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Resuscitation endpoints","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>MAP 65</b>; higher only in selected patients</li> <li><b>Lactate</b> falling (prognostic; not only hypoxia)</li> <li><b>CRT</b>-guided resuscitation non-inferior to lactate; repeat often</li> <li><b>ScvO₂</b>: high/normal does not exclude hypoperfusion</li> <li>High lactate + low ScvO₂ → suspect hypoperfusion; add CO₂-derived variables</li> <li>Also HR, urine output, skin, mentation; echo / CO if not improving</li> <li>Plan <b>de-resuscitation</b> once stable</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Find and control the source","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>History, exam, labs, imaging, microbiology, molecular tests</li> <li>Biomarkers: PCT &gt; 0.5 ng/mL (better than CRP &gt; 10 mg/L); falling PCT helps stop antibiotics</li> <li><b>Source control early (≤ 6 h)</b>: drain, debride, remove infected devices; think multidisciplinary</li> <li>Re-target (escalate / de-escalate) by cultures, <b>within 72 h</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Source → control intervention","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Source</td><td><b>Intervention</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Abdomen / biliary</td><td>Surgical, percutaneous or endoscopic drainage of collections or duct obstruction; remove necrotic tissue</td></tr> <tr><td>Chest</td><td>Drain empyema or abscess</td></tr> <tr><td>Urinary</td><td>Drain hydronephrosis or abscess</td></tr> <tr><td>Skin / soft tissue</td><td>Drainage, lavage, debridement (necrotising fasciitis)</td></tr> <tr><td>Other</td><td>Bone/joint: remove prosthesis · heart: valve replacement · uterus: curettage · infected devices: remove</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"10","h":"Targeted tests (ACE)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Test</td><td><b>Positive</b></td></tr> <tr><td>CRP</td><td>&gt; 10 mg/L</td></tr> <tr><td>PCT</td><td>&gt; 0.5 ng/mL</td></tr> <tr><td>β-D-glucan</td><td>&gt; 80 pg/mL (PJP, Aspergillus, Candida)</td></tr> <tr><td>Galactomannan</td><td>&gt; 1 ng/mL (Aspergillus)</td></tr> <tr><td>Others</td><td>Legionella, influenza Ag, C. difficile toxin, Quantiferon</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">PCT falsely ↑ in CKD, trauma, surgery, malignancy.</div>"},{"n":"11","h":"Supportive care (SSC 2026)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Issue</td><td><b>Do</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Corticosteroid</td><td><b>Hydrocortisone 200 mg/day IV</b> in septic shock with ongoing vasopressor need (≈ NE ≥ 0.25 mcg/kg/min ≥ 4 h; no strict threshold); no ACTH test</td></tr> <tr><td>Glucose</td><td>Insulin if <b>≥ 180 mg/dL (10 mmol/L)</b> twice; target <b>144–180 mg/dL (8–10 mmol/L)</b>; check 1–2 h until stable, then 4 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Blood</td><td>RBC if <b>Hb &lt; 7 g/dL</b> (unless ischaemia / bleeding); keep 7–9; FFP only if bleeding or procedure</td></tr> <tr><td>VTE prophylaxis</td><td><b>Pharmacological, LMWH over UFH</b> (dose per CMH formulary; adjust in renal failure)</td></tr> <tr><td>Stress ulcer</td><td>PPI if GI bleeding risk (AKI, coagulopathy, shock, chronic liver disease)</td></tr> <tr><td>Sedation</td><td>Minimal, titrated to RASS / SAS; avoid benzodiazepines (delirium)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"12","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk cols\"> <li>Do not delay antibiotics to get cultures</li> <li>Do not give HES; avoid gelatins</li> <li>Do not give fluid that no longer improves haemodynamics</li> <li>Do not use low-dose dopamine for the kidney</li> <li>Do not give IVIG, vitamin C, blood purification or polymyxin B haemoperfusion routinely</li> <li>Do not use erythropoietin or antithrombin</li> <li>Do not target glucose 80–110 mg/dL</li> </ul>"}]},"01b-tropical-sepsis":{"slogan":"Ask where they have been · treat early · isolate until excluded","legend":"VHF = viral haemorrhagic fever · ACT = artemisinin combination therapy","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Sepsis from tropical diseases Part I · Part II · Part III (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Fever after travel: think tropical","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Take <b>travel / exposure history</b>: region, mosquito, tick, rodent, fresh water, animal blood, caves</li> <li>Incubation &lt; 3 weeks: malaria, dengue, VHF, typhoid, rickettsia, leptospirosis, plague</li> <li>&gt; 3 weeks: hepatitis, schistosomiasis, rabies, TB</li> <li><b>Malaria first</b> in any fever from an endemic area; altered consciousness = suspect severe malaria</li> <li><b>Leukopenia + low platelets + AST &gt; ALT</b> = typical VHF pattern; normal platelets and AST make VHF unlikely</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Confirm the diagnosis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Malaria:</b> thick/thin smear (reference), RDT as add-on, PCR; repeat smear daily</li> <li><b>Dengue:</b> RT-PCR early, then serology</li> <li><b>VHF:</b> only specialist / point-of-care labs; repeat test if negative within 72 h of onset</li> <li><b>TB:</b> Xpert MTB/RIF first (also rifampicin resistance); urine LAM if HIV with CD4 &lt; 100 or seriously ill</li> <li>Severe malaria: repeated glucose, FBC, chemistry, coagulation; 3× liver enzymes → malarial hepatitis</li> <li>Look for bacterial co-infection (bloodstream, pneumonia)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Severe falciparum malaria (adults): any one","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Feature</td><td><b>Threshold</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Impaired consciousness</td><td class=\"tgt\">GCS &lt; 11</td></tr> <tr><td>Seizures / prostration</td><td>&gt; 2 in 24 h · cannot sit or walk</td></tr> <tr><td>Acidosis</td><td class=\"tgt\">BD &gt; 8, HCO₃ &lt; 15 or lactate ≥ 5 mmol/L</td></tr> <tr><td>Hypoglycaemia</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 2.2 mmol/L (&lt; 40 mg/dL)</td></tr> <tr><td>Anaemia</td><td>Hb &lt; 7 g/dL or Hct &lt; 20%</td></tr> <tr><td>Renal</td><td>Creat &gt; 265 µmol/L (3 mg/dL) or urea &gt; 20</td></tr> <tr><td>Jaundice</td><td>Bili &gt; 50 µmol/L + parasites &gt; 100,000/µL</td></tr> <tr><td>Pulmonary oedema</td><td>SpO₂ &lt; 92% RA + RR &gt; 30</td></tr> <tr><td>Shock · bleeding</td><td>SBP &lt; 80 with poor perfusion · significant bleeding</td></tr> <tr><td>Hyperparasitaemia</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 10%</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Treat severe malaria","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Line</td><td><b>Drug and dose (ACE Table 4)</b></td></tr> <tr><td>First</td><td class=\"tgt\">Artesunate IV 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h, then q24 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Second</td><td>Artemether IM 3.2 mg/kg, then 1.6 mg/kg q24 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Third</td><td>Quinine IV 20 mg/kg over 4 h, then 10 mg/kg over 4 h q8 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Then</td><td>Oral ACT × 3 days once eating (≥ 24 h)</td></tr> <tr><td>SE Asia / China</td><td>Artesunate + quinine IV (expert opinion)</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li>No artesunate dose change in renal / hepatic failure or RRT</li> <li>Avoid artesunate–mefloquine in cerebral malaria</li> <li>Antibiotics: all children; adults if shock / suspected co-infection</li> <li>Slow parasite clearance → think resistance</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Malaria ICU care: careful with fluid","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Normotensive: crystalloid <b>2–4 mL/kg/h</b> (1 mL/kg/h if enteral fluids)</li> <li>Hypotension (?bacterial): careful boluses, <b>early noradrenaline</b>; avoid adrenaline (lactic acidosis)</li> <li>Fluid overload → pulmonary oedema / ARDS: protective MV (VT 6 mL/kg, Pplat &lt; 30), diuretics or RRT for negative balance</li> <li>Cerebral malaria: avoid hypercapnia; seizure prophylaxis levetiracetam or phenytoin; no mannitol</li> <li>AKI: RRT may be needed 2–3 weeks; frequent glucose checks; paracetamol may protect kidney</li> <li>Coma: secure airway before NG feeding (aspiration)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Severe dengue","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Warning: platelets <b>&lt; 100 G/L</b>, Hct ↑ <b>&gt; 20%</b>, albumin falls, narrow pulse pressure <b>≤ 20 mmHg</b></li> <li>Leak lasts <b>24–48 h</b>; start fluid at first signs of leak</li> <li>Adults: <b>500–1000 mL over 1 h</b> (children 10 mL/kg); no rapid &gt; 15 mL/kg bolus if not hypotensive</li> <li>Continue after stabilisation, then reduce as Hct falls, urine output and abdomen improve</li> <li>Bleeding: platelets + FFP; RBC at Hb 7 g/dL</li> <li>Recovery: diuretic may be needed (obese, elderly, poor cardiac reserve)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Viral haemorrhagic fever","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Ribavirin</b> early, often empirical: Lassa, Crimean-Congo, Hantavirus HFRS</li> <li>IV load <b>30–34 mg/kg</b> (max 2 g), then <b>15–17 mg/kg q6 h</b> (max 1 g); 7.5 mg/kg if AKI; dilute in 100–500 mL saline, slow</li> <li>Crystalloids; maintenance ≈ 25 mL/kg/day plus losses; no synthetic colloid</li> <li>Plasma + platelets for bleeding; paracetamol, metamizole, opioids for pain</li> <li>Switch failing NIV to <b>early elective intubation</b> (aerosol risk)</li> <li>Late phase: shock, haemorrhage, AKI, liver failure, ARDS, DIC, encephalopathy</li> <li>Thromboprophylaxis only in convalescence</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Tuberculosis in ICU","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Drug</td><td><b>Adult dose</b></td><td><b>Renal adj.</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Rifampicin</td><td class=\"tgt\">10 mg/kg/d (max 600)</td><td>No</td></tr> <tr><td>Isoniazid</td><td class=\"tgt\">5 mg/kg/d + pyridoxine 10–25 mg</td><td>No</td></tr> <tr><td>Ethambutol</td><td class=\"tgt\">15 mg/kg/d</td><td>Yes</td></tr> <tr><td>Pyrazinamide</td><td class=\"tgt\">25 mg/kg/d</td><td>Yes</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li>2 months 4 drugs, then 4 months RH; 12 months in meningitis</li> <li>Meningitis / pericarditis: <b>dexamethasone 0.4 mg/kg/d</b> or prednisolone 60 mg/d</li> <li>HIV: ART within 2 weeks (meningitis: after 8 weeks); rifampicin interactions</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Severe malaria: organ picture","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Cerebral malaria:</b> diffuse symmetrical encephalopathy; coma depth predicts outcome; focal signs rare</li> <li><b>Respiratory distress</b> 25% adults: anaemia, acidosis, non-cardiogenic oedema, pneumonia</li> <li><b>AKI</b> 25–30% adults (tubular necrosis), often with jaundice</li> <li>Low platelets almost universal: absence → rethink diagnosis</li> <li>Risk: non-immune travellers, pregnancy, HIV, children 6 m–5 y</li> <li>Case fatality 5–50%</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Dengue course","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Incubation 3–14 days; febrile phase looks like simple dengue</li> <li><b>Critical phase starts at defervescence</b>: leak, shock, bleeding</li> <li>BP e.g. 102/85: high diastolic, narrow pulse pressure</li> <li>Secondary infection (endemic areas) ↑ severe disease risk</li> <li>Warning signs → repeated close observation</li> <li>Untreated mortality &gt; 20%; depends on ICU support</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"TB: ICU pearls","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Early deaths (≤ 21 days): respiratory failure, septic shock, MOF</li> <li>Critical illness alters drug levels (absorption, Vd, renal clearance)</li> <li>Pericarditis: 10% large effusion / tamponade → drain</li> <li>Rifampicin lowers azoles, steroids, anticonvulsants, warfarin, many more</li> <li><b>IRIS</b> ≈ 2 weeks after ART: severe → prednisolone 1.5 mg/kg/d × 2 wk then 0.75 mg/kg/d × 2 wk; rarely stop ART</li> <li>MDR / XDR TB: very high mortality</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Isolate and protect staff","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>VHF:</b> strict isolation until excluded; buddy system for donning and <b>doffing</b>; single-use kit, minimal sharps and blood tests; no samples to routine labs; lift after 2 negative blood PCRs 48–72 h apart</li> <li><b>TB:</b> single room, negative pressure, ≥ 6 air changes/h; <b>N95/FFP2</b>, FFP3 for aerosol procedures</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not give liberal fluid in severe malaria</li> <li>Do not use quinine first if artesunate available</li> <li>Do not use exchange transfusion routinely</li> <li>Do not transfuse platelets prophylactically in dengue (≤ 20 G/L)</li> <li>Do not give NSAIDs / aspirin in dengue or VHF</li> <li>Do not give routine pharmacological thromboprophylaxis in acute dengue / VHF</li> <li>Do not use mannitol in cerebral malaria; avoid phenobarbital unless ventilation is available</li> <li>Do not send suspected VHF samples to routine labs</li> </ul>"}]},"01c-immunocompromised":{"slogan":"Know the immune defect · treat within the hour · look hard for the cause","legend":"ANC = absolute neutrophil count · HCT = haematopoietic cell transplant · SOT = solid organ transplant","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Immunocompromised patients Part I–IV (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise the defect → expect the bug","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Defect</td><td><b>Typical cause</b></td><td><b>Pathogens</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Innate / neutrophil</td><td>Chemotherapy, neutropenia, barrier loss</td><td>Severe bacterial and fungal</td></tr> <tr><td>Humoral</td><td>Low IgG, myeloma, CLL, asplenia, anti-CD20</td><td>Encapsulated: pneumococcus, H. influenzae, meningococcus</td></tr> <tr><td>Cell-mediated</td><td>HIV, HCT, SOT, lymphoma, steroids</td><td>PJP, CMV, herpes, Cryptococcus, Toxoplasma, Listeria, moulds, mycobacteria</td></tr> <tr><td>ICU-acquired</td><td>Sepsis, trauma, burns, long ventilation</td><td>Immunoparalysis, secondary infection</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>History:</b> diseases, drugs (steroids, biologics, CAR-T), splenectomy, travel, contacts. Severity: steroids ≤ 10 mg/day prednisone = low; &gt; 50 mg/day prolonged, intensive chemo, prolonged neutropenia = high.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Neutropenic fever = emergency","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Neutropenia ANC &lt; 1500; <b>severe &lt; 500</b> (or expected within 48 h); &lt; 200 highest risk</li> <li>Fever: <b>single ≥ 38.3 °C</b> or ≥ 38 °C over 1 h</li> <li>Blood cultures, then <b>antibiotics within 60 min</b></li> <li>Signs may be blunted: examine mouth, skin, lines, perineum</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">High risk if any</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>MASCC &lt; 21 or CISNE ≥ 3; inpatient; unstable; allogeneic HCT</li> <li>ANC ≤ 100 expected ≥ 7 days; liver / renal failure; mucositis 3–4</li> <li>Uncontrolled cancer; pneumonia; CAR-T</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Empirical treatment (doses per CMH formulary)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Antipseudomonal β-lactam monotherapy:</b> piperacillin-tazobactam, ceftazidime / cefepime, or carbapenem (if ESBL risk); choose by site, local resistance, prior cultures</li> <li><b>Add vancomycin</b> only if: shock, pneumonia, MRSA colonisation, Gram-positive blood culture, line infection, skin / soft tissue infection</li> <li><b>Antifungal</b> if fever persists 4–7 days and neutropenia &gt; 7 days (earlier if unstable): echinocandin, voriconazole or liposomal amphotericin B</li> <li>Treat SARS-CoV-2, influenza, CMV, HSV specifically</li> <li>Remove CVC for S. aureus, Pseudomonas, Candida / fungi, NTM bacteraemia</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Respiratory failure: find the cause","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CT chest</b> (more sensitive than CXR) → <b>BAL</b>; serology; biopsy rarely</li> <li>Infective: bacterial (commonest), fungal (Aspergillus, Mucor, PJP), viral, parasitic</li> <li>Non-infective: oedema, alveolar haemorrhage, drug or radiation pneumonitis, leukaemic infiltrate, engraftment, GVHD</li> <li>NIV / HFNC: conflicting evidence</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>Do not delay intubation</b> when non-invasive support fails</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"HIV in ICU: CD4 → risk","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>CD4/mm³</td><td><b>Opportunistic infection</b></td></tr> <tr><td>200–500</td><td>Pneumococcus, pulmonary TB, zoster, thrush</td></tr> <tr><td>&lt; 200</td><td><b>PJP</b>, miliary / extrapulmonary TB, toxoplasmosis, cryptococcosis</td></tr> <tr><td>&lt; 50</td><td>MAC, disseminated CMV</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li>Respiratory failure: 3rd-gen cephalosporin + atypical cover</li> <li><b>PJP:</b> TMP-SMX + steroid if PaO₂ &lt; 70 mmHg on air (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>CNS: TB meningitis, toxoplasma, cryptococcus → MRI + LP</li> <li>CMV: quantitative PCR → look for retinitis, colitis, pneumonitis; ganciclovir IV</li> <li>TB with CD4 &lt; 200: atypical (miliary, diffuse, extrapulmonary)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"ART in ICU","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Start ART <b>within 2 weeks</b> of opportunistic-infection treatment</li> <li>Delay in <b>cryptococcal</b> or <b>CNS TB</b> infection</li> <li>Immediate ART in severe acute HIV or HIV encephalitis</li> <li><b>IRIS</b>: worsening of treated / hidden infection weeks–months after ART</li> <li>Toxicity ≈ 5% of admissions: lactic acidosis (zidovudine), tubulopathy / AKI (tenofovir), TEN (nevirapine)</li> <li>Check drug interactions</li> <li>Outcome depends on age, comorbidity, organ failure, not CD4</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Solid organ transplant: timeline","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Time</td><td><b>Think</b></td></tr> <tr><td>&lt; 1 month</td><td>Surgical: wound, aspiration, line, UTI, collections; donor-derived; HSV</td></tr> <tr><td>1–6 months</td><td><b>Opportunistic:</b> PJP, CMV / herpes, Nocardia, TB, fungi, hepatitis B/C, respiratory viruses</td></tr> <tr><td>&gt; 6 months</td><td>Community pathogens; opportunistic if rejection / high immunosuppression</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>CMV</b> (D+/R− highest risk): p65 antigen or PCR (not serology); ganciclovir; valganciclovir prophylaxis.</div>"},{"n":"8","h":"HCT: timeline","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Phase</td><td><b>Think</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Pre-engraftment (to day 20–30)</td><td>As neutropenic fever: bacteria, mucositis, lines, fungi</td></tr> <tr><td>Early post (to day 100)</td><td>CMV (up to 80% reactivation), adenovirus, GVHD diarrhoea, C. difficile, liver injury</td></tr> <tr><td>Late (&gt; day 100)</td><td>Low risk unless chronic GVHD</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"9","h":"CT patterns → think","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Pattern</td><td><b>Think</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Ground glass</td><td>PJP</td></tr> <tr><td>Nodules, halo sign</td><td>Invasive aspergillosis</td></tr> <tr><td>Cavitating nodules</td><td>Septic emboli</td></tr> <tr><td>Segmental consolidation</td><td>Legionella</td></tr> <tr><td>Septal thickening</td><td>Viral pneumonia</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"10","h":"Biomarkers and rapid tests","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>PCT</b>: for de-escalation, not for starting; may be low in fungal infection</li> <li><b>β-D-glucan</b>: sensitive, false + with dialysis, IVIG, blood products</li> <li><b>Galactomannan</b>: unreliable on azole prophylaxis</li> <li>Very high ferritin → macrophage-activation-like syndrome</li> <li>Multiplex PCR / mNGS help only with stewardship</li> <li>Follow trends, not single values</li> <li>Endotypes (research): ferritin &gt; 4420 ng/mL = MALS; mHLA-DR &lt; 5000 = immunoparalysis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Neutropenic site problems","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Mucositis</b>: Candida, HSV secondary infection</li> <li><b>Neutropenic enterocolitis</b>: fever + abdominal pain in severe neutropenia; polymicrobial, Candida fungaemia</li> <li><b>CVC infection</b> common: remove for high-risk organisms</li> <li>Solid-organ, fungal infections: worse outcome than isolated bacteraemia</li> <li>Fungi rarely cause the first fever; suspect in persistent fever</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Prevent infection","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Hand hygiene</b> (alcohol rub) at all 5 moments; strict in neutropenia / HCT</li> <li>Contact precautions for MDRO; single rooms, good ventilation</li> <li>Device bundles; vaccinate before chemo or transplant</li> <li>Early enteral nutrition (24–48 h); safe food; multidisciplinary education</li> <li>Prophylaxis per local policy: <b>TMP-SMX</b> for PJP (SOT ≥ 6–12 months), valganciclovir (CMV), aciclovir / valaciclovir (HSV, VZV), fluconazole, fluoroquinolone in high-risk neutropenia (doses per CMH formulary)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not wait for cultures to give antibiotics</li> <li>Do not add vancomycin routinely</li> <li>Do not give G-CSF routinely in febrile neutropenia</li> <li>Do not rely on CXR alone: get CT</li> <li>Do not use serology for active CMV</li> <li>Do not delay intubation when NIV / HFNC fails</li> <li>Do not give routine probiotics or IVIG</li> <li>Do not confuse colonisation with infection</li> <li>Do not use immunomodulators (GM-CSF, IFN-γ, IL-7, anti-PD-L1) outside trials</li> <li>Do not start antibiotics on PCT alone</li> </ul>"}]},"02-ards":{"slogan":"Protect the baby lung · prone early · find the cause","legend":"ΔP = driving pressure · R/I = recruitment-to-inflation ratio · PBW = predicted body weight","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise ARDS","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Criterion</td><td><b>Berlin</b></td><td><b>Global 2024 / Kigali</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Timing</td><td colspan=\"2\">Within 7 days of a known insult</td></tr> <tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Bilateral opacities on CXR / CT, not effusion, collapse or nodules</td><td>Also <b>lung ultrasound</b> (trained operator)</td></tr> <tr><td>Oedema</td><td colspan=\"2\">Not fully explained by cardiac failure / fluid overload</td></tr> <tr><td>Oxygenation</td><td>P/F on PEEP ≥ 5</td><td>Includes HFNC ≥ 30 L/min or NIV; <b>SpO₂/FiO₂ ≤ 315</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Severity (P/F)</td><td class=\"tgt\" colspan=\"2\">Mild 201–300 · Moderate 101–200 · Severe ≤ 100 mmHg</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"2","h":"Find the cause, think mechanism","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Pneumonia</b> is the main cause; pulmonary or extrapulmonary; often several</li> <li>Identify and treat the cause: essential for therapy</li> <li><b>Shunt</b> drives hypoxaemia; <b>dead space</b> predicts mortality</li> <li><b>\"Baby lung\":</b> small, not stiff. Compliance reflects aerated lung size</li> <li>Only half have DAD; exudative → proliferative → fibrotic phases</li> <li>Obesity: low lung volume, airway closure, high chest-wall elastance</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Before intubation: non-invasive support","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>HFNC first-line</b> for most de novo hypoxaemic failure</li> <li><b>NIV / helmet</b> if higher pressure needed (high drive, high effort)</li> <li><b>ROX</b> = (SpO₂/FiO₂)/RR: <b>&gt; 4.88</b> at 2, 6, 12 h → lower intubation risk; HACOR for NIV failure</li> <li>Monitor RR, gas exchange, consciousness, haemodynamics and <b>effort</b></li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>Delayed intubation worsens outcome</b>: no score replaces judgement</li></ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Lung-protective ventilation","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Variable</td><td><b>Target</b></td></tr> <tr><td>VT</td><td class=\"tgt\">≈ 6 mL/kg PBW start; individualise to baby lung</td></tr> <tr><td>Plateau pressure</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 27–30 cmH₂O</td></tr> <tr><td>Driving pressure</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 15 cmH₂O</td></tr> <tr><td>pH / PaCO₂</td><td class=\"tgt\">Permissive hypercapnia, pH &gt; 7.20</td></tr> <tr><td>Oxygenation</td><td class=\"tgt\">PaO₂ 60–80 mmHg / SpO₂ 88–95%; avoid PaO₂ &gt; 120–150</td></tr> <tr><td>Mode</td><td>VC or PC: no outcome difference; VC eases mechanics</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"5","h":"Set PEEP by recruitability","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Assess recruitability <b>before</b> titrating PEEP</li> <li><b>R/I ratio</b> (PEEP 15 → 5): <b>≥ 0.5</b> recruitable → higher PEEP; <b>&lt; 0.57</b> reliably poor recruiter (non-focal ARDS) → lower PEEP</li> <li>Also: stress index, P<sub>L</sub> via oesophageal pressure, EIT, LUS, airway opening pressure</li> <li>Oxygenation, compliance or ΔP alone do not identify recruiters</li> <li>Higher PEEP in poor recruiters → dead space, overdistension, ↓ cardiac output: check CO</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Sedation and paralysis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Default: <b>light, protocolised</b> sedation</li> <li>Deep sedation / NMBA only in early severe ARDS for excessive effort, severe asynchrony, or proning</li> <li>NMBA: ESICM 2023 against routine use; ATS 2024 short course in early severe ARDS; usually <b>≤ 48 h</b> (agent and dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Very low compliance (&lt; 30–35 mL/cmH₂O) may benefit most</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"P/F < 150 → escalate stepwise","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Step</td><td><b>How (ACE)</b></td><td><b>Notes</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Prone position</td><td class=\"tgt\">Early, ≥ 16 consecutive h per session</td><td>NNT 6 (PROSEVA); stop when supine P/F &gt; 150 on PEEP &lt; 10, FiO₂ &lt; 0.60 for 4 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Recruitment manoeuvre</td><td>Rescue only, in the most hypoxaemic</td><td>Not ≥ 35 cmH₂O &gt; 1 min; ART trial harm; use the method you know</td></tr> <tr><td>Inhaled NO</td><td class=\"tgt\">2–10 ppm, short-term rescue</td><td>Refractory hypoxaemia or RV failure; no mortality benefit, possible AKI</td></tr> <tr><td>VV-ECMO</td><td class=\"tgt\">P/F &lt; 50 (FiO₂ ≥ 0.8) &gt; 3 h; &lt; 80 &gt; 6 h; pH &lt; 7.25 with PaCO₂ ≥ 60 &gt; 6 h</td><td>Within the first week; <b>call the ECMO centre early</b></td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"8","h":"Steroids and fluids","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ATS / SCCM 2024: suggest corticosteroids in ARDS; best <b>early, low-dose</b>; no preferred molecule</li> <li>Classic ARDS: <b>dexamethasone 20 mg IV daily × 5 d, then 10 mg × 5 d</b></li> <li>COVID-19 ARDS: <b>dexamethasone 6 mg daily × 10 d</b></li> <li>Severe CAP: <b>hydrocortisone 200 mg/day</b> (CAPE-COD)</li> <li>Not for influenza alone</li> <li><b>Avoid fluid overload</b>: conservative strategy after shock resuscitation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Monitor the heart and the lung","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Echo</b> in ARDS + shock: <b>acute cor pulmonale</b> ≈ 20% (severe 10%) = dilated RV + septal dyskinesia</li> <li>ACP: limit ΔP and PaCO₂, prone, iNO, dobutamine (dose per CMH formulary), diuretics</li> <li>~75% need vasopressors early</li> <li>Ventilatory ratio and capnography track dead space</li> <li><b>Pneumothorax</b> &lt; 10%; risk with Pplat &gt; 35 (not PEEP): ↑ Pplat (VC) or ↓ VT (PC); LUS helps</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Measure mechanics serially","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pplat, ΔP, compliance as trends, not single thresholds</li> <li><b>AOP</b> (low-flow inflation): airway closure if AOP &gt; PEEP</li> <li>Stress index: tidal recruitment vs overdistension</li> <li>Oesophageal pressure: lung vs chest wall (obese, high abdominal pressure)</li> <li>ΔP + RR capture most of mechanical power</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Check drive and effort","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Index</td><td><b>Meaning</b></td></tr> <tr><td>P0.1 &lt; 1</td><td>Low drive, over-assist</td></tr> <tr><td>P0.1 &gt; 3.5–4</td><td>Excess drive → check effort</td></tr> <tr><td>ΔPes &lt; −15</td><td>Vigorous effort (P-SILI risk)</td></tr> <tr><td>ΔPes &gt; −5</td><td>Weak effort</td></tr> <tr><td>Pocc</td><td>¾ Pocc ≈ Pmus; ⅔ Pocc ≈ ΔP<sub>L,dyn</sub></td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Units cmH₂O. High P0.1 ≠ high effort: measure both.</div>"},{"n":"12","h":"Why VILI happens","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Volutrauma / barotrauma</b>: overdistension</li> <li><b>Atelectrauma</b>: cyclic opening and closing</li> <li><b>Biotrauma</b>: inflammation from stress</li> <li>Small, uneven baby lung concentrates stress even at \"protective\" VT</li> <li>Spontaneous effort adds <b>P-SILI</b>, worst in severe ARDS</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Recovery and non-resolution","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Move to assisted (PSV) when drive and effort allow: protect lung <b>and</b> diaphragm</li> <li>Watch <b>double triggering</b> (breath stacking) and <b>reverse triggering</b></li> <li>VAP is hard to diagnose: quantitative cultures help</li> <li><b>Non-resolving ≥ 5–7 days</b> → BAL + CT for mimics; open biopsy rarely</li> <li>Resolved: P/F &gt; 300 (S/F &gt; 315) &gt; 24 h and normal support</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not delay intubation when non-invasive support fails</li> <li>Do not chase normal PaCO₂ or high PaO₂</li> <li>Do not use routine continuous NMBA or deep sedation</li> <li>Do not use aggressive recruitment with decremental PEEP (ART)</li> <li>Do not use iNO routinely</li> <li>Do not give steroids for influenza pneumonia alone</li> <li>Do not let fluid balance drift positive</li> </ul>"}]},"02b-copd-asthma":{"slogan":"Treat the bronchospasm · let the lung empty · watch for hyperinflation","legend":"DH = dynamic hyperinflation · PEEPi = intrinsic PEEP · PEF = peak expiratory flow","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – COPD and Asthma Part I–V (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise severity","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td></td><td><b>Severe asthma</b></td><td><b>Imminent arrest</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Signs</td><td>Can't finish sentences, accessory muscles, agitated, hunched</td><td><b>Silent chest</b>, weak effort, paradox, confusion/coma, cyanosis</td></tr> <tr><td>Numbers</td><td>RR &gt; 30, HR &gt; 120, SpO₂ &lt; 90%, PEF ≤ 50%</td><td>Bradycardia, hypotension; loss of pulsus paradoxus = fatigue</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li><b>Severe AECOPD:</b> VAS ≥ 5, RR ≥ 24, HR ≥ 95, SaO₂ &lt; 92%, CRP ≥ 10; PaO₂ ≤ 60, PaCO₂ &gt; 45, pH &lt; 7.35</li> <li><b>Rising PaCO₂ despite treatment</b> = exhaustion, predicts fatal asthma</li> <li>Key tests: <b>ABG, PEF</b> (asthma; not useful in COPD), <b>CXR</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Triage and mimics","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Asthma not better in <b>1–2 h</b> → ICU / ventilatory support available. <b>In doubt, admit to ICU</b></li> <li><b>Asthma death risk:</b> prior intubation, admission in last year, current/recent oral steroid, no ICS, &gt; 1 SABA canister/month, psychosocial problems, poor adherence, food allergy</li> <li><b>COPD failure:</b> RR &gt; 24, HR &gt; 95, accessory muscles; Venturi FiO₂ &gt; 35–40% needed; PaCO₂ &gt; 60 or mental change = ventilatory failure</li> <li>Mimics / aggravators: pneumonia, PE, heart failure (often RV), arrhythmia, pneumothorax; in hypercapnia think OSA / OHS</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Risk of respiratory failure: COPD vs asthma","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td></td><td><b>COPD</b></td><td><b>Asthma</b></td></tr> <tr><td>History</td><td>Baseline severity (spirometry), number of previous exacerbations, comorbidities</td><td>Previous ICU / ventilation, deterioration despite oral steroid, rising β-agonist use over days</td></tr> <tr><td>Clinical</td><td>Dyspnoea, RR, accessory muscles, mental change, haemodynamic instability; PEF not useful</td><td>Dyspnoea, RR, accessory muscles, <b>silent chest</b>, tachycardia, agitation → stupor; <b>no improvement in 1–2 h</b>; PEF useful</td></tr> <tr><td>Blood gases</td><td>Hypoxaemia, respiratory acidosis, no improvement with treatment</td><td>Normo- or hypercapnia, hypoxaemia; <b>PaCO₂ not falling</b>, hypoxaemia not corrected</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Immediate treatment: asthma","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>O₂</b> to SaO₂ <b>93–95%</b>; controlled, not 100%</li> <li><b>Salbutamol</b> pMDI + spacer = nebuliser: <b>4–10 puffs q20 min × 3</b>, then 4–10 puffs q3–4 h up to 6–10 puffs q1–2 h</li> <li><b>Ipratropium</b> neb <b>0.5 mg q4–6 h</b> if moderate–severe or poor response</li> <li><b>Steroid within 1 h</b> (works after ≥ 4 h): <b>prednisolone 50 mg</b> once daily or <b>hydrocortisone 200 mg/day</b> divided; 5–7 days, oral = IV</li> <li><b>MgSO₄ 2 g IV over 20 min</b> once: FEV₁ &lt; 25–30% or no response (not routine)</li> <li><b>IM adrenaline</b> only with anaphylaxis / angioedema (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>NIV: no recommendation; if tried, monitor closely</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Immediate treatment: AECOPD","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Controlled O₂</b> (Venturi) if SpO₂ &lt; 88% → target <b>88–92%</b>; O₂ can raise PaCO₂</li> <li><b>SABA ± ipratropium</b>: 1–2 puffs hourly × 2–3, then q2–4 h; no continuous neb; <b>air-driven neb if hypercapnic</b></li> <li><b>Prednisolone 40 mg</b> once daily × <b>5 days</b> within 1 h; oral = IV; no taper</li> <li><b>Antibiotics 5–7 days</b> if ventilated (NIV / IMV), or ≥ 2 of ↑ dyspnoea, fever, sputum volume, purulence (purulence one of them), or prior positive culture; choose by local resistance (Pseudomonas risk if ventilated / recurrent)</li> <li><b>NIV first-line</b> for hypercapnic failure (preferred over HFNC)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"NIV (BiPAP) for AECOPD","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Seated, reassured, well-fitting face mask; <b>BiPAP</b> initial mode</li> <li><b>EPAP</b> ↑ 1–2 cmH₂O steps (counters PEEPi, fewer missed triggers)</li> <li><b>IPAP</b> ↑ 2 cmH₂O steps → RR ≤ 25, VT 6–8 mL/kg; total pressure ≤ 20 cmH₂O</li> <li>Success: pH, PaCO₂, PaO₂ and RR better within <b>1–4 h</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">NIV failing → intubate</div> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>pH / RR worse or unchanged at <b>2–4 h</b>; intolerance; hypotension</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Intubate when","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Severe dyspnoea with paradox · <b>RR &gt; 35</b></li> <li>PaO₂ &lt; 40 mmHg or P/F &lt; 200</li> <li><b>pH &lt; 7.25</b> and/or PaCO₂ &gt; 60 mmHg, worsening</li> <li>Somnolence · NIV failure · cannot clear secretions · arrest</li> <li>Hypotension, shock, heart failure; sepsis, pneumonia, PE, barotrauma, large effusion</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Controlled ventilation: let the lung empty","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Setting</td><td><b>Start</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Tidal volume</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 6–8 mL/kg</td></tr> <tr><td>Respiratory rate</td><td class=\"tgt\">10–14 /min</td></tr> <tr><td>Inspiratory flow</td><td class=\"tgt\">60–100 L/min, no pause</td></tr> <tr><td>FiO₂</td><td class=\"tgt\">25–40% → PaO₂ &gt; 60 (SaO₂ &gt; 90%)</td></tr> <tr><td>External PEEP</td><td class=\"tgt\">Consider if flow limitation</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li><b>Controlled hypoventilation</b>: accept hypercapnia; correct pH &lt; 7.2 slowly; aim pH, not PaCO₂</li> <li>Sedate, rest muscles ≥ 24 h; switch to assisted modes soon</li> <li>High Ppeak is acceptable (not transmitted to alveoli)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Monitor hyperinflation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Expiratory flow not reaching zero = PEEPi</li> <li><b>End-expiratory hold</b> (&gt; 5 s if severe) → static PEEPi; trend it</li> <li>Asthma: <b>occult PEEPi</b> → suspect if Pplat − PEEPi is high</li> <li><b>EELV &gt; 20 mL/kg</b> predicts hypotension and barotrauma</li> <li>Track PEEPi and Pplat; BP, HR, urine output improve as DH falls</li> <li>Normal: Rmax &lt; 10, Rmin &lt; 8 cmH₂O/L/s; Cst &gt; 50 mL/cmH₂O</li> </ul> <div class=\"kbox\"><b>Ventilated + hypotensive?</b> Think hyperinflation: <b>briefly disconnect</b>; BP recovers fast. Then exclude pneumothorax.</div>"},{"n":"10","h":"NIV contraindications","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Needs immediate intubation; post respiratory or cardiac arrest</li> <li>↓ consciousness or agitation not controlled by sedation</li> <li>Vomiting / massive aspiration; profuse secretions</li> <li>Significant haemodynamic instability</li> <li>Severe ventricular or supraventricular arrhythmia</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Adverse effects: skin and nasal-bridge ulcers, gastric insufflation, leaks; major: pneumothorax, hypotension, aspiration.</div>"},{"n":"11","h":"Secretions & atelectasis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Causes: secretions, poor cough with ETT, opioids / anticholinergics, high FiO₂ (absorption), weak flat diaphragm, ↓ consciousness</li> <li>Wean as early as possible</li> <li>Endotracheal suction when secretions are abundant (safe standard procedure)</li> <li><b>Bronchoscopy only if indicated</b> (e.g. lobar / lung collapse)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Complications & outcome","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Barotrauma:</b> pneumothorax, surgical emphysema, pneumomediastinum</li> <li><b>VAP:</b> COPD 2.35× risk (≥ 48 h, fever, WBC change, purulent secretions)</li> <li><b>ICUAW 25–60%</b> if ventilated &gt; 7 days: steroids, NMBA, sepsis, immobility</li> <li>Asthma: good long-term outcome; COPD depends on pre-morbid status, comorbidity, nutrition</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Assisted modes and ventilated support","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Goals: unload effort and keep synchrony; <b>PEEPe ≈ 80% of PEEPi</b></li> <li>Avoid PaO₂ &gt; 70 mmHg (CO₂ retention)</li> <li>Inhaled doses on MV are higher: salbutamol pMDI 200–400 mcg / neb 2.5–5 mg; ipratropium 80–160 mcg / 0.25–0.5 mg</li> <li>Heliox (70–80% He) only if refractory</li> <li>Heated humidifier (less dead space); early enteral feed, avoid overfeeding</li> <li>Watch fluid balance; CXR may miss overload</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not give high-flow 100% O₂ in COPD</li> <li>Do not give IV aminophylline / theophylline routinely</li> <li>Do not use routine MgSO₄ or antibiotics in asthma</li> <li>Do not normalise PaCO₂ fast (alkalosis, more DH)</li> <li>Do not use routine neuromuscular blockers (weakness)</li> <li>Do not add inspiratory pause or long Ti</li> <li>Do not persist with failing NIV; do not push too hard</li> </ul>"}]},"02c-airway-management":{"slogan":"Plan for difficulty · oxygenate first · confirm with capnography","legend":"FONA = front-of-neck access · SGA = supraglottic airway","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Airway Management Part I: Airway Assessment and Interventions · Part II: Intubation and Care of the Intubated Patient · Part III: Extubation (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise airway obstruction","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Complete:</b> silent effort, <b>see-saw</b> chest/abdomen, accessory muscles, cyanosis</li> <li><b>Partial:</b> voice change, snoring, can't swallow, swelling, tachypnoea, agitation, tracheal tug, recession, exhaustion</li> <li><b>Noise → level:</b> stertor = pharynx · inspiratory stridor = larynx · expiratory = tracheobronchial · biphasic = glottic/subglottic</li> <li>Causes: ↓ consciousness, oedema (anaphylaxis, angioedema, burns), infection (epiglottitis, abscess, Ludwig's), haematoma, tumour, foreign body / blood / vomit, laryngospasm, cord palsy, stenosis</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Basics first: position, clear the airway, opening manoeuvres, adjuncts, oxygen.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Predict difficulty: MACOCHA (≥ 3 = difficult)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Factor</td><td><b>Points</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Mallampati III–IV</td><td class=\"tgt\">5</td></tr> <tr><td>Obstructive sleep apnoea</td><td class=\"tgt\">2</td></tr> <tr><td>Reduced cervical spine mobility</td><td class=\"tgt\">1</td></tr> <tr><td>Mouth opening &lt; 3 cm</td><td class=\"tgt\">1</td></tr> <tr><td>Coma</td><td class=\"tgt\">1</td></tr> <tr><td>Severe hypoxaemia (SpO₂ &lt; 80%)</td><td class=\"tgt\">1</td></tr> <tr><td>Non-anaesthesiologist operator</td><td class=\"tgt\">1</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">NPV 98%, PPV only 36%. Difficult intubation in ICU up to 23%; peri-intubation arrest 1.7–4.2%. Ultrasound marks cricothyroid membrane for FONA.</div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Prepare: oxygenate","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Pre-intubation checklist</b> (fewer complications); separate ICU airway trolley, minimal equipment</li> <li><b>Upright / head-up</b>; sniffing position (ear level with sternum)</li> <li>Preoxygenate <b>≥ 3 min</b> high-flow O₂ or 8 vital-capacity breaths; target ETO₂ 90%</li> <li>Shunt / low FRC: <b>NIV or BVM with PEEP valve</b>; HFNO helps</li> <li><b>Apnoeic O₂:</b> nasal cannula 15 L/min or HFNO 40–70 L/min</li> <li>Agitated: <b>delayed sequence</b> with small ketamine or dexmedetomidine increments (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Recruitment manoeuvre after intubation ↑ PaO₂</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Prepare: circulation and monitoring","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pre-intubation hypotension is the strongest predictor of arrest; collapse in 25–46% within 60 min</li> <li><b>Treat hypotension before induction:</b> fluid if responsive and tolerant; otherwise start <b>noradrenaline</b> before induction (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li><b>≥ 2 IV accesses</b>; intra-osseous in emergency</li> <li>Have dilute vasopressor boluses ready after intubation</li> <li>Monitor: SpO₂, <b>waveform capnography</b>, BP, HR, 3-lead ECG, ETO₂ if available</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Induce and paralyse (doses per CMH formulary unless stated)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Drug</td><td><b>Use when</b></td><td><b>Watch for</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Ketamine</td><td>Shock, bronchospasm; brain injury acceptable (CPP ↑)</td><td>Slower onset, ↑ myocardial O₂ use, hallucinations</td></tr> <tr><td>Etomidate</td><td>Haemodynamic instability, ↑ ICP</td><td>Adrenal suppression (caution in sepsis); limited ICU use</td></tr> <tr><td>Propofol</td><td>Stable patient, ↑ ICP</td><td>Hypotension, bradycardia</td></tr> <tr><td>Thiopentone</td><td>↑ ICP</td><td>Hypotension, laryngospasm, histamine; not in porphyria</td></tr> <tr><td>Fentanyl / alfentanil</td><td>Co-induction → lower induction dose</td><td>—</td></tr> <tr><td>Rocuronium</td><td class=\"tgt\">1.2 mg/kg = intubating conditions of suxamethonium</td><td>Reverse with <b>sugammadex 16 mg/kg</b> (CICV) within 3 min</td></tr> <tr><td>Suxamethonium</td><td>Rapid onset</td><td>K⁺ ↑ 0.5 (much more in burns / spinal injury), bradycardia, anaphylaxis, MH, ↑ O₂ use</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">NMBAs are recommended: better views, fewer attempts, fewer complications. Dexmedetomidine bolus → hypotension, bradycardia.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Laryngoscopy and failed intubation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Max 3 attempts</b>; after the 1st failure open the FONA set and optimise the view</li> <li><b>Bougie / stylet</b> if poor view or hyperangulated VL</li> <li>VL available for all; first choice if difficulty predicted (trained staff)</li> <li><b>Confirm with waveform capnography</b></li> <li>3 failures → <b>oxygenate</b> via SGA or face mask</li> <li><b>CICO → FONA</b> (cricothyroidotomy)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Care of the tube","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O</b> (&lt; 20 aspiration / VAP; &gt; 30 mucosal necrosis, stenosis)</li> <li>Secure fixation; fixed length at teeth; hands ≥ 20 cm from tube</li> <li>Manage agitation, pain, delirium; weaning protocols</li> <li>Suction for secretions, tube block, ↑ airway pressure, hypoxia (risk: hypoxia, trauma, bronchospasm, ↑ ICP)</li> <li>Unplanned extubation (0.3–14%): risk with agitation, delirium, poor sedation or fixation, restraint, poor supervision; 43–63% need reintubation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Capnography: read the trace","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Trace</td><td><b>Think</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Flat / falls to zero</td><td>Oesophageal tube, displacement, disconnection, occlusion, apnoea, analyser failure</td></tr> <tr><td>Rapidly falling</td><td>Loss of pulmonary blood flow (arrest, PE); small trace during CPR</td></tr> <tr><td>Steep phase 3 slope</td><td>Slow-emptying alveoli (long time constants, obstruction)</td></tr> <tr><td>Cleft in plateau</td><td>Patient effort, dyssynchrony</td></tr> <tr><td>Raised baseline</td><td>Rebreathing (inadequate fresh gas)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"9","h":"Ready to extubate?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Cause improving; <b>P/F ≥ 150 or SpO₂ ≥ 90%</b> on FiO₂ ≤ 40%, PEEP ≤ 5–8</li> <li><b>pH &gt; 7.25</b>; MAP ≥ 60–65 (low-dose vasopressor acceptable)</li> <li>Passed SBT; awake, obeys commands (GCS ≥ 8–9)</li> <li>Cough: <b>PEF &gt; 60 L/min</b> (≤ 60 → 5× reintubation); index-card test</li> <li>Avoid positive balance in the last 24 h; Hb ≥ 7–8; no fever</li> <li>Plan for difficult reintubation (staff, kit, location)</li> <li>Fully awake, minimal opioid, NMB fully reversed; 10–20% still fail after a passed SBT</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Stridor risk → cuff leak test","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Risk: intubated ≥ 36 h–6 days, age &gt; 80, ETT &gt; 8 mm (men) / &gt; 7 mm (women), GCS &lt; 8, suction &gt; 15/day, traumatic intubation, female</li> <li><b>Leak &lt; 110 mL or &lt; 12–24% of VT</b> = reduced</li> <li>Reduced / absent → delay, <b>steroid ≥ 6–8 h</b> (dose per CMH formulary), retest</li> <li>High-risk extubation → HFNC or NIV; reassess early, reintubate early if failing</li> <li>Tracheostomy: failed airway, prolonged ventilation, poor airway protection; percutaneous (PDT) usually preferred</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not induce a hypotensive patient before resuscitation</li> <li>Do not make more than 3 laryngoscopy attempts</li> <li>Do not accept tube placement without capnography</li> <li>Do not do a cuff leak test in unselected patients</li> <li>Do not extubate before reintubation difficulty is planned</li> <li>Do not do PDT with FiO₂ &gt; 70% or PEEP &gt; 10, coagulopathy, unstable C-spine, infection at site</li> </ul>"}]},"02d-resp-monitoring":{"slogan":"Look at the patient, then the gas, then the waveforms","legend":"PL = transpulmonary pressure · Pes = oesophageal pressure","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Respiratory Assessment and Monitoring PART I & PART II (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Bedside assessment","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">History clues</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Smoking, weight (OSA, obesity hypoventilation), occupation, travel (TB), systemic/rheumatic disease and drugs (e.g. methotrexate pneumonitis), family history (CF, α1-antitrypsin), thoracic surgery</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Work of breathing</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Phasic sternocleidomastoid contraction</b> = most direct sign of ↑ work of breathing; deep chest excursions, accessory muscles, active expiration</li> <li><b>Tachypnoea alone ≠ ↑ work of breathing</b></li> <li>\"Silent hypoxaemia\": severe hypoxaemia without dyspnoea; ≈ 40% of ventilated patients have dyspnoea</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Examination</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Asymmetric expansion → atelectasis / pneumothorax; percussion → effusion / pneumothorax</li> <li>Bronchial breathing = consolidation; end-inspiratory crackles = pneumonia; Velcro crackles = fibrosis</li> <li><b>Significant haemoptysis</b> = &gt; 200 mL in one episode</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Imaging: pick the right tool","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CXR:</b> targeted, not daily: after intubation, CVC, chest drain; supine AP film falsely enlarges heart and mediastinum</li> <li><b>Lung ultrasound:</b> quick, repeatable; effusion, <b>pneumothorax (no lung sliding)</b>, consolidation; guides drains; accepted for ARDS diagnosis (global definition)</li> <li><b>Diaphragm US:</b> excursion + thickening fraction → neuromuscular disease, phrenic injury, <b>weaning failure</b></li> <li><b>CT:</b> parenchyma, PE angiography, ILD, mediastinum, drainage/biopsy; in ARDS identifies cause and helps personalise PEEP; weigh transfer risk</li> <li><b>EIT:</b> bedside, radiation-free; regional aeration, PEEP titration (research tool)</li> <li><b>Bronchoscopy:</b> BAL (vital in immunocompromised), alveolar haemorrhage, foreign body, tracheomalacia, percutaneous tracheostomy. Scope ↑ peak pressure, ↓ VT, loss of volume with suction</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Oxygenation","html":"<div class=\"kbox\"><b>CaO₂ = 1.34 × Hb × SaO₂ + 0.003 × PaO₂</b> (normal 16–20 mL/dL) · <b>DO₂ = CO × CaO₂</b><br/>Anaemia reduces O₂ carriage far more than a low PaO₂.</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>SpO₂</b> (660/940 nm) tracks PaO₂ well, but less reliable with nail polish, bilirubin, hypothermia, low perfusion, and <b>dark skin</b> (≈ 3× more occult hypoxaemia)</li> <li><b>SvO₂ normal 70–80%</b>: low = inadequate supply; &gt; 80% = hyperdynamic / poor extraction (sepsis)</li> <li>Use SvO₂/ScvO₂ with other perfusion markers and as a <b>trend</b>, not a single target</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"CO₂ & dead space","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Capnography:</b> confirms tube placement, detects dislodgement; in CPR confirms tube, checks compression quality, detects ROSC</li> <li>EtCO₂ ≈ PaCO₂ − 0.7 kPa (5 mmHg) in health; <b>unpredictable gap</b> in critical illness</li> <li><b>\"Shark fin\"</b> (steep phase 3) = bronchospasm, but <b>more often a kinked or compressed tube</b></li> <li>Notches in plateau = spontaneous efforts / asynchrony</li> <li>↑ physiological dead space: shock, low pulmonary perfusion, PE/microthrombi; ↑ apparatus dead space with HME, connectors, long tubing (matters with low VT)</li> </ul> <div class=\"kbox\"><b>Ventilatory ratio</b> = (V̇E mL/min × PaCO₂ mmHg) / (PBW × 100 × 37.5). Higher = more dead space, worse outcome, longer weaning.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Read the ventilator: what changed?","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:20%\">Finding</th><th style=\"width:38%\">Meaning</th><th>Next step</th></tr> <tr><td>VC: ↑ Ppeak, Pplat unchanged</td><td><b>Resistance ↑</b> (e.g. secretions, bronchospasm, kinked tube)</td><td>Suction, check tube, bronchodilator</td></tr> <tr><td>VC: ↑ Ppeak and ↑ Pplat equally</td><td><b>Compliance ↓</b> (e.g. atelectasis, pneumothorax, high chest-wall elastance)</td><td>Examine, lung US / CXR, review VT and PEEP</td></tr> <tr><td>Stress index &gt; 1 (upward concavity)</td><td>Tidal <b>overdistension</b></td><td>Reduce PEEP / VT</td></tr> <tr><td>Stress index &lt; 1 (downward concavity)</td><td>Tidal recruitment</td><td>Consider PEEP adjustment</td></tr> <tr><td>Expiratory flow not back to zero</td><td>Air trapping / <b>intrinsic PEEP</b></td><td>End-expiratory pause; ↑ expiratory time</td></tr> <tr><td>PC: ↓ and flattened inspiratory flow</td><td>Resistance ↑ (low compliance: lower peak, shorter flow)</td><td>As above; check delivered VT</td></tr> <tr><td>Flow–volume \"sawtooth\"</td><td><b>Secretions</b></td><td>Suction</td></tr> <tr><td>Flow–volume loop not closing</td><td>Air leak</td><td>Check cuff and circuit</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Pause manoeuvres (VC, passive patient): end-inspiratory pause ≥ 3 s → Pplat, static compliance, Rmin = (Ppeak − Pint)/flow, ΔR (↑ in COPD exacerbation); end-expiratory pause → intrinsic PEEP.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Oesophageal pressure (Pes ≈ pleural)","html":"<div class=\"kbox\">PL end-insp = Pplat − Pes end-insp · PL end-exp = PEEP − Pes end-exp · ΔPL = difference</div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>ARDS target</th><th>Limit</th></tr> <tr><td>Tidal ΔPL (tidal stress)</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 10–12 cmH₂O</td></tr> <tr><td>End-inspiratory PL (total stress)</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 18–20 cmH₂O</td></tr> <tr><td>End-expiratory PL (collapse if negative)</td><td class=\"tgt\">0–2 cmH₂O</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Use when <b>chest wall elastance high</b>: morbid obesity, abdominal compartment syndrome, chest wall oedema/burns, deformity</li> <li>Needs correct balloon position and inflation</li></ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Patient effort on assisted modes","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Normal resting Pes / Pdi swing <b>7 ± 3 cmH₂O</b></li> <li>Target <b>Pes swing 5–15 cmH₂O</b>; &gt; 14–18 = excessive effort</li> <li><b>PTP 50–200 cmH₂O·s/min</b>: &lt; 50 over-assist, &gt; 200 under-assist</li> <li>WOB normal 0.3–0.7 J/L</li> <li><b>ΔPocc</b> (brief airway occlusion swing): simple non-invasive screen for high effort</li> <li>Too much or too little assist injures lung and diaphragm</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Symptoms & signs","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Chronic cough: COPD, asthma, reflux, ACE inhibitor</li> <li>Sputum colour/smell not diagnostic: send for microbiology (early-morning samples for TB)</li> <li>Peripheral cyanosis can be local (cold, DVT)</li> <li>Clubbing: lung cancer, abscess, empyema</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Capnogram phases","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>1</b>: dead-space gas, CO₂ ≈ 0 (rises with rebreathing)</li> <li><b>2</b>: rapid upstroke (mixed alveolar + dead space)</li> <li><b>3</b>: alveolar plateau, slight rise → <b>EtCO₂</b> at its end</li> <li><b>4</b>: rapid fall to zero (inspiration)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Lung vs chest wall","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>E<sub>L</sub> = ΔPL / VT · E<sub>CW</sub> = ΔPes / VT</li> <li>Pdi = Pgas − Pes (diaphragm pressure, needs gastric balloon)</li> <li>Pmus = chest wall recoil − Pes (all inspiratory muscles)</li> <li>High E<sub>CW</sub> → airway pressures overstate lung stress</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Recruitment","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>RM = transient ↑ PL (&gt; 40 cmH₂O holds, PEEP steps, sighs); hold gain with PEEP</li> <li>Monitor: CT, EIT (best PEEP between collapse and overdistension), N₂ washout EELV, driving pressure, P-V loops</li> <li><b>R/I ratio &lt; 0.5</b> = low recruitability</li> <li>Prone = gentle, gradual recruitment</li> <li>EVLW (transpulmonary thermodilution) + permeability index separate cardiogenic vs non-cardiogenic oedema</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Use <b>routine brief high-pressure RMs</b> in ARDS (ESICM: barotrauma, haemodynamic collapse); selected use only (e.g. obese after intubation)</li> <li>Order a daily routine CXR</li> <li>Trust SpO₂ blindly in low perfusion or dark skin: check ABG</li> <li>Assume EtCO₂ equals PaCO₂ in critical illness</li> <li>Treat a \"shark fin\" as bronchospasm before checking the tube</li> </ul>"}]},"02e-ards-imaging-ecls":{"slogan":"Image to decide · protect the lung · ECMO early at an expert centre","legend":"LUS = lung ultrasound · EIT = electrical impedance tomography","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Lung imaging in ARDS Part I & II · Acute respiratory failure ECLS Part I & II (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Choose the imaging tool","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Tool</td><td><b>Use it for</b></td><td><b>Limits</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Chest X-ray</td><td>Initial assessment, device position, suspected complication; <b>on demand, not daily routine</b></td><td>Confounded by settings, body habitus, atelectasis; <b>cannot assess recruitment or guide PEEP</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Lung ultrasound</td><td>Bedside daily aeration, ARDS vs cardiogenic oedema, effusion, pneumothorax, PEEP and prone response, fluids</td><td>Obesity, surgical emphysema, dressings; underestimates posterior/basal loss; <b>cannot see overdistension</b></td></tr> <tr><td>CT</td><td>Unclear diagnosis, occult complications (effusion, abscess, pneumothorax, barotrauma), recruitability, prognosis</td><td>Transport, radiation; quantitative analysis needs software</td></tr> <tr><td>EIT</td><td>Bedside regional ventilation, PEEP titration (collapse vs overdistension)</td><td>Relative changes only; static lesions are invisible; perfusion still research</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"2","h":"LUS: read aeration","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Pattern</td><td><b>Aeration</b></td></tr> <tr><td>A-lines + sliding</td><td>Normal</td></tr> <tr><td>Well-spaced B-lines</td><td>Moderate loss (interstitial)</td></tr> <tr><td>Coalescent B-lines &lt; 3 mm</td><td>Severe loss (partial alveolar filling)</td></tr> <tr><td>Consolidation ± dynamic bronchogram</td><td>Complete loss</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li>Scan <b>6 regions per lung</b> (anterior / lateral / posterior, upper and lower) → aeration score</li> <li><b>ARDS:</b> spared areas, uneven B-lines, pleural-line changes, dependent consolidation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"CT: compartments and recruitment","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Density (HU)</td><td><b>Compartment</b></td></tr> <tr><td>−1000 to −900</td><td>Hyperinflated</td></tr> <tr><td>−900 to −500</td><td>Normally aerated</td></tr> <tr><td>−500 to −100</td><td>Poorly aerated</td></tr> <tr><td>−100 to +100</td><td>Non-aerated</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li><b>\"Baby lung\"</b>: aerated lung can be 20–30% of normal: small, not stiff</li> <li>Recruitment = non-aerated tissue regained between 2 scans (e.g. PEEP 5 vs 15)</li> <li><b>Diffuse</b> loss recruits better than <b>focal</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Imaging-guided ventilation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>PEEP:</b> LUS / EIT reaeration plus ΔP and R/I ratio</li> <li><b>EIT:</b> decremental PEEP trial from 25 cmH₂O, best compromise of collapse vs overdistension (best pixel compliance)</li> <li><b>Prone:</b> LUS shows dorsal reaeration, anterior de-recruitment, plugging</li> <li><b>Not improving:</b> CT for occult complications</li> <li>R/I ratio estimates recruitability without imaging</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Refractory despite protective MV + prone → ECLS?","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">ELSO: consider if ≥ 1</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>P/F &lt; 80 mmHg</b> despite optimal care incl. prone trial</li> <li><b>pH &lt; 7.25</b> despite protective MV and RR 35</li> <li>Bridge to lung transplant / primary graft dysfunction</li> <li>Cardiogenic shock: SBP &lt; 90, UO &lt; 30 mL/h, lactate &gt; 2, SvO₂ &lt; 60%, altered mental state ≥ 6 h despite optimal therapy</li> <li>Selected refractory cardiac arrest (good neuro potential)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"EOLIA criteria (ESICM: strong rec. at experienced centres)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Criterion</td><td><b>Threshold despite optimised MV</b></td></tr> <tr><td>P/F</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 50 mmHg, FiO₂ &gt; 0.8, &gt; 3 h</td></tr> <tr><td>P/F</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 80 mmHg, FiO₂ &gt; 0.8, &gt; 6 h</td></tr> <tr><td>pH</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 7.25 for &gt; 6 h</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">EOLIA mortality 35% vs 46% (Bayesian and meta-analysis support benefit). ECCO2R for ARDS: <b>not recommended</b> (REST, ESICM 2023, ATS 2024).</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Contraindications","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">Absolute</div> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Comorbidity preventing meaningful recovery (metastatic cancer, catastrophic brain injury)</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Relative</div> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Cannot anticoagulate (CNS bleed, uncontrolled bleeding)</li> <li>Injurious MV <b>&gt; 7–10 days</b></li> <li>Irreversible cause, no transplant option · established MOF</li> <li>Poor functional status · anatomy unsuitable</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Pick the configuration","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Mode</td><td><b>When</b></td><td><b>Watch for</b></td></tr> <tr><td>V-V</td><td>Respiratory failure (fem–IJ, fem–fem, bicaval dual-lumen)</td><td>Recirculation</td></tr> <tr><td>V-Pa</td><td>Respiratory + RV dysfunction</td><td>RH catheterisation, fluoroscopy</td></tr> <tr><td>V-A</td><td>Cardiac support</td><td>↑ LV afterload, stroke, limb ischaemia (6–8 Fr distal perfusion), differential hypoxaemia</td></tr> <tr><td>V-AV</td><td>Combined heart + lung failure</td><td>Complex circuit</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"9","h":"Running ECMO","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Start with high blood flow; lower as lungs recover</li> <li><b>Hypercapnic start: low sweep</b>, slow PaCO₂ fall (cerebral vasoconstriction risk)</li> <li>Max circuit support first, then shift back to the ventilator; ideal \"rest\" settings uncertain</li> <li>Prone on ECMO: possible survival benefit (observational)</li> <li>pH goals as ARDSnet; O₂ target uncertain</li> <li>Hb: ELSO &gt; 10 g/dL; many centres 7 g/dL</li> <li>UFH usual: monitor ACT, aPTT or anti-Xa (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Altered drug PK (↑ Vd, circuit sequestration)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Complications → action","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Problem</td><td><b>Action (ACE)</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Air in circuit</td><td>Minor: vent oxygenator, find cause. Major: clamp, change circuit</td></tr> <tr><td>Bleeding</td><td>Lower anticoagulation target or pause; TXA / rFVIIa (dose per CMH formulary)</td></tr> <tr><td>Clot / thrombus</td><td>Check flow, anticoagulation, platelets, HIT</td></tr> <tr><td>HIT</td><td>Switch to argatroban or bivalirudin (dose per CMH formulary)</td></tr> <tr><td>Haemolysis</td><td>Lower flows; look for patient causes</td></tr> <tr><td>Limb ischaemia</td><td>Distal perfusion catheter, ↑ flow, ↓ vasopressor</td></tr> <tr><td>No flow after insertion</td><td>Kink, thrombus, cannula depth</td></tr> <tr><td>LV distension on V-A</td><td>Pulse pressure &lt; 5–10, oedema → unload (microaxial device, IABP, venting)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"11","h":"Wean and do not","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>V-V:</b> wean sweep to <b>zero</b> → native lung alone</li> <li><b>V-A:</b> step down blood flow with echo of native output</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not order daily routine CXR</li> <li>Do not use CXR to judge recruitment or PEEP</li> <li>Do not use routine ECCO2R in ARDS</li> <li>Do not use V-A ECMO routinely in infarct shock (ECLS-SHOCK)</li> <li>Do not correct hypercapnia too fast</li> <li>Do not place unnecessary central lines</li> </ul>"}]},"03a-mv-basics-modes":{"slogan":"First do no harm · protect the lung · match the patient","legend":"ΔP = driving pressure · PEEPi = intrinsic PEEP · PS = pressure support","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Mechanical Ventilation PART I–IV (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise: who needs ventilation?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Failure to maintain ventilation</b> (CO₂) or <b>oxygenation</b>, of any cause</li> <li>Distress: nasal flaring, mouth breathing, accessory and expiratory muscles, tracheal tug, intercostal recession, <b>paradoxical abdomen</b>, tachypnoea, tachycardia, sweating</li> <li>Shock: ventilation unloads the muscles (respiratory muscles take a large share of cardiac output)</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Aims</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Adequate CO₂ elimination · better oxygenation · unload respiratory muscles (also prevents self-induced lung injury)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Choose the interface: ETT or NIV mask","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Feature</td><td><b>ETT</b></td><td><b>NIV mask</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Protects from aspiration · suction · leak-proof</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr> <tr><td>Relieves fixed upper airway obstruction</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr> <tr><td>Comfort, cough, speech, no sedation</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr> <tr><td>Needs patient cooperation</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li><b>NIV first-line:</b> AECOPD (hypercapnic failure), cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, post-extubation</li> <li><b>Contraindications:</b> cannot protect airway, cannot clear secretions, shock (relative)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"NIV: start and stop criteria","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Start NIV (no contraindication)</td><td><b>Stop NIV → intubate</b></td></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">Moderate–severe dyspnoea; ↑ work of breathing, abdominal paradox</td><td>Haemodynamic instability · ↓ consciousness</td></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">RR <b>&gt; 24</b> (obstructive) / <b>&gt; 30</b> (restrictive)</td><td>Worsening pH, PaCO₂ or PaO₂ · RR &gt; 30</td></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">PaCO₂ <b>&gt; 45 mmHg</b>, pH <b>&lt; 7.35</b></td><td>Intense dyspnoea · ↑ work of breathing</td></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">P/F &lt; 300; hypoxaemia despite O₂ (caution)</td><td>Cannot clear secretions · agitation / intolerance</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Watch closely for the first <b>1–2 h</b>. Failure predictors: high VT / minute ventilation, no improvement after 1–2 h, severity, shock, ↓ consciousness, severe acidosis, non-COVID ARDS.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Gas exchange targets","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>PaCO₂ = V'CO₂ / alveolar ventilation</b>: raise VT or RR, lower dead space</li> <li><b>Normal PaCO₂ / pH:</b> healthy lungs (peri-operative) and <b>brain injury</b> (ICP)</li> <li><b>Permissive hypercapnia:</b> accepted compromise when protective VT is needed</li> <li><b>SpO₂:</b> above 90–92% is reasonable; avoid supra-normal PaO₂ (FiO₂ titrated to SpO₂ 94–98% in ACE Part IV)</li> <li>Oxygenation: <b>PEEP + FiO₂</b> · CO₂: <b>VT + RR</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Initial settings in controlled modes (VC / PC)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Setting</td><td><b>Start / target</b></td><td><b>ACE notes</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Tidal volume</td><td class=\"tgt\">6–8 mL/kg IBW</td><td>Compromise between PaCO₂ target and VILI; obstructive disease: limit VT to cut hyperinflation</td></tr> <tr><td>PEEP – normal lungs</td><td class=\"tgt\">5 cmH₂O</td><td>Hypoxaemic lung injury: higher PEEP, personalised</td></tr> <tr><td>PEEP – ARDS</td><td class=\"tgt\">PEEP-FiO₂ table as start</td><td>Then individualise: best compliance, lowest ΔP, end-expiratory P<sub>L</sub> 0–10 cmH₂O, imaging (CT/EIT)</td></tr> <tr><td>FiO₂</td><td class=\"tgt\">Lowest for SpO₂ target</td><td>Set after PEEP is chosen</td></tr> <tr><td>RR and I:E</td><td class=\"tgt\">I:E 1:1.5–1:2</td><td>Obstructive disease <b>1:3–1:6</b>: longer expiration; no inspiratory plateau</td></tr> <tr><td>Inspiratory plateau</td><td class=\"tgt\">Optional</td><td>May help ARDS; avoid in obstructive disease</td></tr> <tr><td>VC vs PC</td><td class=\"tgt\">VC: VT set, pressure varies</td><td>PC: pressure set, VT varies with compliance and resistance; all modern control modes are assist-control</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"6","h":"Lung-protection limits (VILI)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Index</td><td><b>Danger threshold</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Plateau pressure</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 30 cmH₂O</td></tr> <tr><td>Driving pressure (Pplat − PEEPtot)</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 14 cmH₂O (&lt; 12 if &gt; 65 y)</td></tr> <tr><td>End-inspiratory transpulmonary P</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 24 cmH₂O</td></tr> <tr><td>Stress index</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 1 = overdistension</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">ΔP is the strongest outcome predictor. Mechanical power and stress index are not yet validated in large trials.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Measure mechanics (passive patient, VC constant flow)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>End-inspiratory hold</b> (1–3 s) → Pplat; <b>end-expiratory hold</b> → total PEEP / PEEPi</li> <li>Crs = VT / (Pplat − PEEPtot) · R = (Ppeak − Pplat) / flow</li> <li><b>↑ Ppeak, Pplat same</b> → resistance (secretions, bronchospasm, tube)</li> <li><b>↑ Ppeak and Pplat</b> → elastance (stiff lung / chest wall)</li> <li>P1 − Pplat normal 1–2 cmH₂O (↑ in AECOPD)</li> <li>Airway closure (obese, asthma, ARDS on low PEEP): low-flow inflation ≈ 6 L/min</li> <li>Valid only with <b>no patient effort</b></li> <li>Set the <b>Ppeak alarm</b> in VC: it catches both causes</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"PEEP: benefit vs harm","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <div><div class=\"sub l\">Benefit</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>↑ FRC, ↓ shunt, better oxygenation</li> <li>Stops cyclic opening/closing (VILI)</li> <li>Counteracts PEEPi → ↓ work of breathing</li> <li>↓ LV transmural pressure</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub l\">Harm</div> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Overdistension → dead space, barotrauma</li> <li>↑ RV load, ↓ venous return, ↓ cardiac output</li> </ul></div> </div> <div class=\"note\"><b>Recruitable:</b> higher PEEP → ↑ oxygenation and compliance, ↓ ΔP, <b>no rise in PaCO₂</b>. ΔP rises with PEEP → overdistension. Decremental trial from 20–30 cmH₂O with VT ≈ 4 mL/kg.</div>"},{"n":"9","h":"Assisted modes: set and titrate","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>PS:</b> fixed pressure; VT depends on support, effort and compliance. Set trigger, PS level, cycling-off (% peak flow), PEEP, FiO₂</li> <li>Trigger: <b>flow 2–3 L/min</b> or <b>pressure 1–1.5 cmH₂O</b>, individualised</li> <li>Titrate PS by VT, RR, VE, effort and comfort; avoid high VT and distending pressures</li> <li><b>PAV+:</b> set IBW, ETT size, Pmax 40; start <b>70–80%</b>, ↑ 5% steps to 90% if no runaway</li> <li><b>NAVA:</b> trigger EAdi 0.5 µV, cycles off at 70% fall; level by preview, Brander, or −60% EAdi vs SBT</li> <li>RR is a poor marker of drive; over-assist → apnoeas, periodic breathing</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Asynchrony: recognise → fix","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Problem</td><td><b>Action (ACE)</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Trigger delay / ineffective efforts</td><td>↓ hyperinflation (↓ PS/VT, bronchodilator), ↓ sedation or PS, external PEEP vs PEEPi, more sensitive (flow) trigger</td></tr> <tr><td>Auto-triggering</td><td>Leaks, water, cardiac oscillation, hiccups + low threshold → fix cause, less sensitive trigger</td></tr> <tr><td>Premature cycling (double trigger)</td><td>Lower cycling-off % (longer Ti); may mimic ineffective efforts</td></tr> <tr><td>Delayed cycling</td><td>↓ PS level, ↑ cycling-off flow threshold</td></tr> <tr><td>Uncorrectable leaks</td><td>Longer rise time (lower inspiratory flow)</td></tr> <tr><td>Read the flow curve</td><td>Curved inspiratory flow = effort continuing; exponential decay = effort ended. Pes is the best detector</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"11","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not keep controlled ventilation longer than needed</li> <li>Do not use PEEP-FiO₂ tables as the final PEEP choice: start point only</li> <li>Do not add inspiratory plateau or short expiration in obstructive disease</li> <li>Do not change circuits routinely to prevent VAP</li> <li>Do not trust mechanics measured during patient effort</li> <li>Do not delay intubation when NIV is failing at 1–2 h</li> <li>Do not use NIV if airway or secretions cannot be managed</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Bi-level / APRV (inverse I:E), SIMV, MMV and \"safe-VT\" modes are combinations of the same trigger, control and cycling variables.</div>"}]},"03b-mv-weaning-complications":{"slogan":"Screen every day · SBT early · find the cause of failure","legend":"SBT = spontaneous breathing trial · RSBI = rapid shallow breathing index","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Mechanical Ventilation PART V & PART VI (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Daily screen: ready to wean?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Oxygenation:</b> PaO₂/FiO₂ <b>&gt; 200 mmHg</b> (27 kPa) on <b>PEEP ≤ 5</b></li> <li><b>Haemodynamics:</b> no continuous vasopressor infusion</li> <li><b>Consciousness:</b> awake or easily roused</li> <li><b>Cough:</b> coughs effectively on suction</li> <li><b>RSBI &lt; 100</b> (RR/VT after 2 min spontaneous breathing): sensitive screen, does not predict SBT success</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Delay to first SBT ↑ ventilation days and ICU/hospital stay. Failure predictors at first attempt: higher RR, lower P/F, higher PEEP. Extubation failure &gt; 20% in medical vs &lt; 10% in surgical patients.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Spontaneous breathing trial","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>T-piece</b> best mimics post-extubation load; low PS (≤ 7 cmH₂O) ± CPAP lowers work and may overestimate readiness (guidelines prefer PS for patients at cardiac risk)</li> <li><b>30 min</b> for most; up to <b>120 min</b> if high risk (elderly, COPD, heart failure, neuromuscular)</li> <li>Watch the first few minutes closely</li> <li>Pass → assess airway, cough and secretions, then <b>extubate</b> promptly</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>Pass</th><th>Fail</th></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">RR &lt; 35 · HR &lt; 140 · SpO₂ &gt; 90% or PaO₂ &gt; 60 mmHg on FiO₂ &lt; 0.4 · SBP 80–180 or &lt; 20% change · no distress</td><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">Sweating, nasal flaring, ↑ effort, HR ↑ &gt; 40, arrhythmia, hypotension, apnoea · PetCO₂ ↑ &gt; 10 mmHg, pH &lt; 7.32 or fall &gt; 0.07, PaO₂ &lt; 60 on FiO₂ &gt; 0.4, SpO₂ fall &gt; 5%</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"><li><b>Cuff leak test</b> before extubation if high risk of post-extubation stridor</li></ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"SBT failed → find the cause before the next trial","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Cause</th><th style=\"width:40%\">Work-up (ACE)</th><th>Treat (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Respiratory pump: load &gt; capacity</td><td>Flow/pressure waveforms, gas exchange, compliance, <b>PEEPi</b>, resistance; lung/abdomen US, CXR; tube/airway patency (bronchoscopy); MIP, Pdi max, phrenic stimulation</td><td>Treat underlying disease; bronchodilators, steroids; optimise sedation/analgesia; reduce asynchrony; muscle training; early mobilisation; adequate nutrition; tracheostomy rarely</td></tr> <tr><td>Cardiac: weaning-induced pulmonary oedema</td><td>ECG before/during SBT, <b>echo before and after SBT</b> (↑ LV filling pressure), SvO₂ during SBT, BNP, ↑ plasma protein/Hb, EVLW; exclude ischaemia (angiography), PAC. High risk: COPD + LV disease</td><td><b>Negative fluid balance</b> (diuretics, restriction); ↓ afterload; vasodilators / inotropes (nitrates, dobutamine, levosimendan; dose per CMH formulary); treat ischaemia (β-blocker, antiplatelet, Hb, PCI)</td></tr> <tr><td>Brain / psychological</td><td>Delirium (CAM-ICU), depression, anxiety, sleep disturbance</td><td>Optimise sedation, <b>dexmedetomidine</b>, early mobilisation, sleep-promoting environment</td></tr> <tr><td>Metabolic / endocrine</td><td>Electrolytes, glucose, hormone levels</td><td>Glucose control; electrolytes normal</td></tr> <tr><td>Respiratory centre</td><td>Sedative / opioid excess (rare)</td><td>Reduce sedatives</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"After extubation: NIV & HFNC","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Facilitate:</b> NIV as a weaning bridge in <b>hypercapnic COPD</b> not meeting criteria → fewer ventilator days, less pneumonia</li> <li><b>Prevent:</b> NIV or HFNC straight after extubation if high risk (&gt; 65 y, chronic heart or lung disease) → fewer reintubations; <b>NIV alternating with HFNC</b> beats HFNC alone in high risk</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>Rescue NIV</b> for established post-extubation failure: no benefit, ↑ ICU mortality (delayed reintubation). If used, <b>intubate if not better within 1–2 h</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Weaning groups (WIND)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Short ≤ 1 day (≈ 65%) · difficult 2–6 days · prolonged ≥ 7 days · ≈ 16% fail at day 90</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Tracheostomy","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Lowers airway resistance and RSBI, improves muscle function</li> <li><b>Early (≤ 10 days)</b>: shorter ventilation and ICU stay; no mortality or pneumonia benefit</li> <li>Consider when prolonged ventilation is anticipated; individualise to stability and goals of care</li> <li>Ventilated ≥ 14 days: tracheostomy linked to lower mortality and more weaning success (observational)</li> <li>Structured post-tracheostomy weaning protocol cut ventilator days 17 → 10.6</li> <li><b>Do not</b> perform early tracheostomy solely to prevent VAP</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Prevent ventilator-induced lung injury (VILI) (guardrails, not outcome-validated targets)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:16%\">Variable</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Target</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>VT</td><td class=\"tgt\">Start ≈ 6 mL/kg PBW (range 4–8)</td><td>Reduce towards 4 if Pplat / ΔP high; accept hypercapnia rather than high pressure</td></tr> <tr><td>Pplat</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 30 cmH₂O</td><td>Reduce VT, fix breath stacking, reassess PEEP / chest wall; ECMO in selected severe cases</td></tr> <tr><td>Driving pressure</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 14–15 cmH₂O, as low as feasible</td><td>Falls after PEEP ↑ = recruitment; rises = overdistension</td></tr> <tr><td>PEEP</td><td class=\"tgt\">No universal value; R/I &gt; 0.5 = recruitable</td><td>PEEP trial at constant VT: judge Crs, ΔP, dead space, BP, RV, not oxygenation alone</td></tr> <tr><td>Rate / FiO₂</td><td class=\"tgt\">RR often ≤ 25; SpO₂ 88–95% / PaO₂ 55–80 (ARDS)</td><td>Avoid auto-PEEP and hyperoxia</td></tr> <tr><td>Effort (assisted)</td><td class=\"tgt\">P0.1 1–4; Pmus / ΔPes 5–10 cmH₂O</td><td>Pmus &gt; 15 or dynamic ΔPL &gt; 20 = excessive: correct drive (hypoxaemia, acidosis, fever, pain, agitation) and support</td></tr> <tr><td>Mechanical power</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 16–18 J/min = warning</td><td>Reduce VT, ΔP, RR or flow</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Prone ≥ 16 h if P/F &lt; 150 despite PEEP. No routine recruitment manoeuvres (ART trial harm) or prolonged neuromuscular blockade; avoid positive fluid balance after stabilisation.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Who weans with difficulty?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Age, frailty, immunocompromise, multiple comorbidities</li> <li>Neurological events, high illness severity</li> <li><b>Rising PaCO₂ during SBT</b> → prolonged wean / failure</li> <li>Only ≈ 65% weaned by day 90 (WEAN SAFE)</li> <li>Prolonged weaning: tracheostomy, address mood and sleep</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Asynchrony & P-SILI","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Shape effort, don't just suppress it</li> <li>Too little support → vigorous effort; too much → high VT, ineffective efforts, diaphragm disuse</li> <li>Double triggering, breath stacking, reverse triggering, cycling mismatch → adjust flow, Ti, cycling, support, sedation</li> <li>Persistent injurious effort → short controlled ventilation ± NMBA</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"VAP prevention","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Avoid intubation when appropriate; shortest ventilation</li> <li>Light sedation, daily awakening + SBT, early mobilisation</li> <li>Head up, hand hygiene, <b>daily tooth brushing</b></li> <li>Minimise circuit disconnections; manage cuff pressure</li> <li>No routine probiotics or chlorhexidine mouthwash</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Ventilator-induced diaphragm dysfunction","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Suspect in every difficult wean; exclude other causes</li> <li>Weakness: MIP less negative than <b>−30 cmH₂O</b>; excursion <b>&lt; 10 mm</b> on SBT; TFdi &lt; 20% = low activity</li> <li>Avoid prolonged inactivity <b>and</b> excessive load: light sedation, NMBA only when indicated, assisted mode early</li> <li>Aim for quiet-breathing effort: <b>Pmus 3–12 cmH₂O, TFdi 15–30%</b></li> <li>Treat breath stacking / double triggering; mobilise; nutrition, electrolytes; inspiratory muscle training if weak</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"VAP treatment","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pneumonia ≥ 48 h after intubation: a clinical probability diagnosis</li> <li><b>Lower respiratory sample before antibiotics</b> if safe</li> <li>Empiric breadth by severity + MDR risk (local MDR &gt; 25%, IV antibiotics in 90 days, &gt; 5 days in hospital, MDR colonisation, ARDS, septic shock, RRT before VAP)</li> <li><b>De-escalate</b> to active monotherapy when improving</li> <li><b>7–8 days</b> default; stop earlier if stable and afebrile 48 h (REGARD-VAP)</li> <li>Not improving at 48–72 h: look for another cause, don't just add antibiotics</li> </ul>"}]},"04-circulatory-shock":{"slogan":"Treat the cause, not just the number · Flow matters more than pressure","legend":"CI = cardiac index · SVR = systemic vascular resistance","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Hypotension (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise shock","html":"<div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">Shock = globally impaired tissue/organ perfusion, inadequate for metabolic needs</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Hypotension:</b> SBP generally &lt; 90 mmHg, usually with tachycardia. Clinically relevant hypotension ≈ <b>&lt; 75% of the patient's normal resting SBP</b> (higher threshold in chronic hypertension)</li> <li><b>Kidney:</b> urine output &lt; 0.5 mL/kg/h</li> <li><b>Skin:</b> cold, clammy, cyanosis</li> <li><b>Brain:</b> syncope, altered mental state, confusion, ↓ GCS</li> <li><b>Lactate &gt; 1.5 mmol/L</b></li> <li>Rising RR comes first, then tachycardia, then hypotension and rapid collapse. <b>β-blocked patients may not become tachycardic.</b></li> <li>No single sign is diagnostic and early on all may be absent; use <b>early warning scores</b> to trigger escalation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Measure BP & flow","html":"<div class=\"kbox\"><b>MAP = CO × SVR</b> · <b>CO = HR × SV</b> · MAP = DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure<br/>A vasopressor can raise MAP while flow stays low if the circulation is under-filled.</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Arterial line</b> for severe hypotension in HDU/ICU: beat-to-beat BP, accuracy, frequent ABGs (risks rare: digital ischaemia, aneurysm, infection)</li> <li>NIBP: a <b>cuff too large</b> falsely suggests hypotension; arrhythmias and shivering cause errors</li> <li><b>Echo (TTE):</b> rapid function and CO, excludes <b>tamponade</b>, informs volume status and fluid responsiveness</li> <li>CO monitors: PAC (gold standard, thermodilution; routine use not favoured), pulse-contour (PiCCO, LiDCO), oesophageal Doppler (descending aorta carries only 70% of CO)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Classify the shock & treat the cause (the last three are low-output states: CI < 2.2 L/min/m²)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:12%\">Type</th><th style=\"width:18%\">Key features</th><th style=\"width:24%\">Causes</th><th>Cause-specific treatment (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Distributive <small>(≈ ⅔ of shock)</small></td><td>Low SVR; flushed, vasodilated; normal/high CO early</td><td>Sepsis (most), anaphylaxis, neurogenic (spinal cord injury), adrenal insufficiency, cyanide</td><td><b>Sepsis:</b> appropriate broad-spectrum <b>antibiotics are the most time-critical</b>; IV crystalloid <b>at least 30 mL/kg within 3 h</b>; complete care bundles (e.g. Sepsis 6); source control</td></tr> <tr><td>Hypovolaemic</td><td>Cold, peripherally shut down</td><td>Haemorrhage; GI loss (diarrhoea, vomiting); third-space loss; burns</td><td><b>Haemorrhage:</b> stop the bleeding (\"turn off the tap\") + <b>warmed blood products, RBC : plasma : platelets 1:1:1</b>; avoid hypothermia, hypocalcaemia, acidosis; <b>limit crystalloid</b> (dilutes clotting factors). <b>Burns:</b> Parkland formula for first 24 h. <b>Gastroenteritis:</b> fluid + electrolytes. <b>DKA:</b> IV insulin</td></tr> <tr><td>Obstructive</td><td>May have normal BP; venous congestion</td><td>Pulmonary embolus, tension pneumothorax, tamponade</td><td>Massive PE: thrombolytic + antithrombotic agents (surgery rarely possible when unstable); relieve tension/tamponade</td></tr> <tr><td>Cardiogenic</td><td>Normal or high BP; cold, shut down; pulmonary congestion</td><td>MI/ACS (most common), valve disease, arrhythmias, cardiomyopathy (e.g. HOCM)</td><td>Criteria: <b>CI &lt; 2.2 L/min/m² + SBP &lt; 90</b>. Recognise and treat the cause (e.g. MI) promptly to restore coronary perfusion; inotropes (box 6)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"General resuscitation: ABCDE, start now","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">Oxygen / airway</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Target <b>SpO₂ 94–98%</b> (<b>88–92% in COPD</b>); SpO₂ probes unreliable with poor perfusion</li> <li>Severe shock + hypoxia: <b>early intubation and ventilation</b> (↓ work of breathing and O₂ consumption)</li> <li>Intubation is high-risk: experienced team, <b>cardio-stable induction</b>, emergency drugs, equipment and <b>capnography ready</b> before induction</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Fluid challenge</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>500 mL crystalloid over 15 min</b> (NICE), reassess after every bolus; repeat until no longer fluid-responsive</li> <li>Fluid responsiveness: <b>passive leg raise</b> (with invasive CO monitoring if needed; never in isolation); PPV / SVV <b>&gt; 10%</b> predicts response (only in sinus rhythm + full ventilation at 8 mL/kg)</li> <li>Fluid non-responder → start vasoactive drugs; excess fluid is harmful</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Choice of fluid","html":"<div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">Crystalloid with Na⁺ 130–154 mmol/L in preference to colloid</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Balanced crystalloids</b> (Hartmann's, Plasma-Lyte) avoid hyperchloraemic acidosis of large-volume 0.9% saline</li> <li>Balanced vs saline (SPLIT, PLUS): no difference in mortality or AKI</li> <li>Albumin 4% vs saline (SAFE): no mortality difference; <b>higher mortality in trauma subgroup</b></li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>Hydroxyethyl starch:</b> more renal failure and blood product use (CHEST)</li> <li>Aggressive boluses in children with severe infection increased mortality (FEAST)</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Haemorrhage</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Blood products (1:1:1), warmed; limit clear fluids</li></ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Vasopressors & inotropes (ACE gives no infusion doses: dose per CMH formulary)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:16%\">Place</th><th>Key points (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Noradrenaline</td><td class=\"tgt\">1st-line vasopressor (SSC 2021)</td><td>Mainly α1 (↑ SVR, diverts flow to heart and brain), some β (limits reflex fall in CO). <b>Central line preferred</b> (extravasation necrosis). Start only once hypovolaemia is corrected</td></tr> <tr><td>Vasopressin</td><td class=\"tgt\">2nd-line vasopressor (SSC 2021)</td><td>V1 vasoconstriction; reduces noradrenaline requirement in resistant shock (VASST, VANISH)</td></tr> <tr><td>Adrenaline</td><td>Usually 2nd line</td><td>α + β; inotrope at low dose, α effects at higher dose; more arrhythmias than noradrenaline</td></tr> <tr><td>Dopamine</td><td>Not preferred</td><td>Mainly β1; inferior to noradrenaline, more arrhythmias</td></tr> <tr><td>Metaraminol / phenylephrine</td><td>Bridge</td><td>Less potent α1 agonists; short-term peripheral infusion to hold BP temporarily</td></tr> <tr><td>Dobutamine</td><td>Inotrope (low-CO / cardiogenic)</td><td>β1 inotrope with β2 vasodilation; offset BP fall with a vasopressor</td></tr> <tr><td>Milrinone (PDE-III)</td><td>Inotrope + vasodilator</td><td>Works independently of adrenoceptors: useful when receptors are down-regulated</td></tr> <tr><td>Levosimendan</td><td>Inotrope</td><td>Calcium sensitiser: ↑ contractility without ↑ O₂ demand</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"7","h":"Targets","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"big\">MAP 65 mmHg</div><div class=\"mid\">usual starting target; individualise</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Lower</b> may be acceptable: permissive hypotension in haemorrhagic shock; perioperative hypotensive anaesthesia</li> <li><b>Higher</b> needed: spinal cord injury, raised ICP, chronic hypertension</li> <li>Aim = adequate flow and O₂ delivery: urine output, mentation, skin, falling lactate</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Monitor & escalate","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Early warning score trigger → senior / critical care review quickly</li> <li>HDU/ICU + <b>arterial line</b>; frequent ABG and lactate</li> <li><b>Echo</b> early; add CO monitoring (pulse contour, Doppler, PAC) if not responding</li> <li>Reassess after every fluid bolus or drug change</li> <li>Use care bundles: <b>Sepsis 6</b>, <b>major haemorrhage protocol</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Pitfalls (ACE)","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Vasopressor before the circulation is filled: BP rises, organ flow does not</li> <li>Fluid after the patient stops responding: harm as fluid leaves the vessels</li> <li>Protocolised goal-directed therapy (Rivers) not confirmed by ARISE, ProMISe, ProCESS: individualise fluids and MAP</li> <li>Missing the cause: tamponade, tension pneumothorax, PE, bleeding, MI</li> </ul>"}]},"04b-hypertension":{"slogan":"Diagnosis = acute organ damage, not the BP number · Lower to safer levels, not to normal","legend":"Full detail: pages 2 onwards","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE module “Hypertension” (2024 ESC / 2025 ACC-AHA based)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise & confirm severe BP","html":"<div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">Severe BP: <b>SBP &gt; 180</b> and/or <b>DBP &gt; 110</b> mmHg (ESC) · DBP &gt; 120 (ACC/AHA)</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Rapid serial readings in BOTH arms</b> (screens for aortic dissection), then move to an <b>arterial line</b></li> <li>Correct cuff: bladder width 40% / length 80% of arm circumference (too small = falsely high)</li> <li>NIBP under-reads by up to <b>50/30 mmHg</b> when BP &gt; 180/100 in ICU/surgical patients</li> <li><b>Treat first:</b> pain, anxiety, cold/hypothermia, full bladder, hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, acidosis, hypervolaemia</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Look for ACUTE organ damage (HMOD)","html":"<div class=\"irow\"><div><div class=\"h\">Brain / retina</div>Headache, visual change, confusion, somnolence, seizures, cortical blindness, focal deficit (→ stroke work-up). <b>Fundoscopy is mandatory.</b> CT/MRI.</div></div> <div class=\"irow\"><div><div class=\"h\">Heart</div>Orthopnoea, dyspnoea at rest, crackles, angina. ECG, CXR, troponin, echo.</div></div> <div class=\"irow\"><div><div class=\"h\">Aorta / vessels</div>Migrating/tearing chest pain, arm BP difference, pulse deficits, new AR murmur. CT angiography.</div></div> <div class=\"irow\"><div><div class=\"h\">Kidney / blood</div>Proteinuria, rapid AKI. FBC + smear (schistocytes), urea, creatinine, electrolytes, urinalysis, urine toxicology.</div></div>"},{"n":"3A","h":"HMOD present = HYPERTENSIVE EMERGENCY","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>ICU / monitored bed</b> + <b>arterial line</b> (gold standard for titration)</li> <li><b>IV titratable agent</b>, a single agent preferred; dedicated infusion line</li> <li>Start <b>oral agents at the same time</b> to wean IV sooner</li> <li>Diuretics only if obvious fluid overload (most patients are volume depleted)</li> <li>Treat BP first; look for secondary causes later</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Malignant hypertension: grade III/IV retinopathy ± MAHA ± AKI.</div>"},{"n":"3B","h":"No acute HMOD = SEVERE HYPERTENSION","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Formerly “hypertensive urgency”; <b>no urgent BP lowering needed</b></li> <li>Short-stay unit / ward, non-invasive BP</li> <li><b>Oral therapy</b> to safer levels over <b>several hours to 24 h</b></li> <li>Asymptomatic: start or adjust regimen and arrange follow-up</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>No IV agents</b> for asymptomatic BP elevation; rapid lowering has no proven benefit and causes harm</li></ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"How much & how fast (general rule)","html":"<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1.25fr;gap:1.5mm;align-items:stretch\"> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"big\">MAP ↓ 20–25%</div><div class=\"mid\">or DBP to 100–110 mmHg over minutes to hours</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"big\">NOT to normal</div><div class=\"mid\">Autoregulation is shifted right in chronic hypertension</div></div> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\" style=\"text-align:left\">Over-lowering → stroke/TIA, confusion, myocardial or mesenteric ischaemia, worse renal function. <b>If urine output falls as BP drops, don't lower that much.</b></div></div> </div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Condition-specific targets & drug choice","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Condition</th><th style=\"width:27%\">BP target / time frame</th><th style=\"width:31%\">Preferred drugs</th><th>Avoid</th></tr> <tr><td>Hypertensive encephalopathy</td><td class=\"tgt\">↓ 25% in the first hour</td><td>Labetalol, esmolol (SNP, NTG, nicardipine, hydralazine: limited experience)</td><td class=\"av\">Clonidine and other sedating drugs</td></tr> <tr><td>Intracerebral haemorrhage</td><td class=\"tgt\">SBP 130–140 within 6 h, keep for 7 days (if initial SBP 150–220)</td><td>IV calcium channel blocker (e.g. nicardipine) ± urapidil; labetalol does not raise ICP</td><td class=\"av\">Fall &gt; 70 mmHg; SNP / NTG (↑ ICP, ↓ CPP)</td></tr> <tr><td>Ischaemic stroke: lysis / thrombectomy</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 185/110 before lysis, then &lt; 180/105 for 24 h. Thrombectomy: &lt; 180/105 before and for 24 h</td><td>Agents that do not raise ICP: labetalol, nicardipine</td><td class=\"av\">SNP / NTG (↑ ICP, ↓ CPP)</td></tr> <tr><td>Acute coronary syndrome</td><td class=\"tgt\">SBP &lt; 140 (common practice)</td><td>Relieve pain (NTG, morphine); IV NTG at higher dose; β-blocker / labetalol</td><td class=\"av\">Hydralazine, diazoxide; SNP (coronary steal)</td></tr> <tr><td>Acute aortic dissection</td><td class=\"tgt\">SBP &lt; 120 (as low as ≤ 110), HR 60–80, urgently</td><td>Esmolol first, then add CCB or nitrate</td><td class=\"av\">Thrombolytics, anticoagulants</td></tr> <tr><td>Catecholamine excess</td><td class=\"tgt\">Not specified in ACE</td><td>Phaeo: phentolamine. Drugs / alcohol withdrawal: benzodiazepines. Optimise volume</td><td class=\"av\">β-blockers acutely (unopposed α)</td></tr> <tr><td>Severe pre-eclampsia / eclampsia</td><td class=\"tgt\">DBP 90–100 mmHg</td><td>IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, oral IR nifedipine; alt. IV nicardipine. MgSO₄ + delivery</td><td class=\"av\">ACE-I, ARB, SNP (fetal toxicity)</td></tr> <tr><td>Peri-operative</td><td class=\"tgt\">Fix triggers first; usually lasts 2–6 h</td><td>Nicardipine, esmolol, labetalol; urapidil</td><td class=\"av\">—</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"6","h":"IV drug quick doses (ESC agents listed in ACE; full table on page 6)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:43%\">Dose</th><th style=\"width:15%\">Onset / duration</th><th>Key cautions</th></tr> <tr><td>Nicardipine</td><td>Infusion 5–15 mg/h, titrate by 2.5 mg/h every 5–15 min. No bolus</td><td>2–10 min / 2–6 h</td><td>Reflex tachycardia; ↑ ciclosporin/tacrolimus levels</td></tr> <tr><td>Labetalol</td><td>Bolus 0.25–0.5 mg/kg (or 20–80 mg) over 2 min; infusion 0.5–2 mg/min (or 0.1 mg/kg/h)</td><td>5–10 min / 3–6 h</td><td>Bradycardia, AV block, asthma/COPD, sympathomimetic states</td></tr> <tr><td>Esmolol</td><td>Load 500 µg/kg over 1 min; infusion 50–300 µg/kg/min</td><td>1–5 min / 10–20 min</td><td>Not alone in phaeochromocytoma</td></tr> <tr><td>Urapidil</td><td>Bolus 12.5–25 mg IV over 20 s; infusion 5–30 mg/h (max 60 mg/h)</td><td>3–5 min / 4–6 h</td><td>Orthostatic hypotension; IV ≤ 7 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Isosorbide dinitrate</td><td>Infusion 2–15 mg/h, titrate in 1 mg/h steps</td><td>1–5 min / 30–60 min</td><td>RV infarct; no PDE-5 inhibitors</td></tr> <tr><td>Nitroprusside</td><td>Avoid &gt; 3 µg/kg/min for &gt; 72 h; max rate 10 µg/kg/min for ≤ 10 min</td><td>Short-acting</td><td>Cyanide/thiocyanate toxicity, coronary steal</td></tr> </table>"}]},"04c-lv-heart-failure":{"slogan":"Wet or dry? Warm or cold? Find and fix the cause","legend":"AHF = acute heart failure · MCS = mechanical circulatory support","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Left sided Heart Failure (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise AHF (clinical diagnosis: no single test)","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <div><div class=\"sub l\">Symptoms</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Breathlessness: exertion, rest, <b>orthopnoea</b>, PND</li> <li>Fatigue, exercise intolerance, ankle swelling</li> <li>Cough with frothy (± blood-stained) sputum</li> <li>Cause-related: chest pain, palpitations</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Signs</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Tachypnoea, diaphoresis, tachycardia</li> <li>Raised JVP, 3rd/4th heart sound, murmur</li> <li>Bilateral crackles (large effusions may mask them), pleural effusions</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub l\" style=\"color:var(--red)\">Cardiogenic shock</div> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>SBP &lt; 90 mmHg</b></li> <li>Poor peripheral perfusion, ↓ capillary refill</li> <li>Skin mottling</li> <li>Oliguria / anuria</li> <li>Confusion</li> <li>ABG: <b>↑ lactate + metabolic acidosis</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"kbox\"><b>FIND-HF</b>: Fatigue, Increased water accumulation, Natriuretic peptide testing, Dyspnoea</div></div> </div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Immediate work-up","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>12-lead ECG:</b> arrhythmia (AF with fast rate), new ischaemia/infarction (ST change, new LBBB); compare with old ECG</li> <li><b>Troponin</b> (prognostic; ↑ also in stretch, arrhythmia, sepsis, renal dysfunction)</li> <li><b>BNP / NT-proBNP:</b> <b>NT-proBNP ≤ 125 pg/mL effectively excludes HF</b>; interpret in context</li> <li><b>Lung ultrasound:</b> more sensitive and specific than CXR for cardiogenic oedema</li> <li><b>CXR:</b> upper-lobe diversion, Kerley B, alveolar oedema, effusions, cardiomegaly (≈ 1 in 5 AHF has no congestion on CXR)</li> <li><b>Echo</b> = key imaging: LV/RV function, valves, wall motion, pulmonary hypertension</li> <li>Bloods: FBC, U&amp;E, LFT, glucose, TFT (anaemia, infection, renal, thyroid triggers)</li> <li><b>Emergent coronary angiography</b> in STEMI or ischaemic cardiogenic shock</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Profile the patient (ESC): congestion × perfusion · simultaneous ABC assessment and treatment","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Profile</th><th style=\"width:22%\">Features</th><th>Management (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Warm &amp; wet <small>(≈ 95% congested)</small></td><td>Congestion / pulmonary oedema, adequate perfusion</td><td><b>IV loop diuretic = 1st line</b> (bolus or infusion) → <b>vasodilator 2nd line</b> (esp. hypertensive: IV nitrates to <b>SBP &lt; 110 mmHg</b> while perfusion maintained) · O₂ to <b>SpO₂ ≥ 90%</b> · CPAP / NIV if dyspnoeic and tachypnoeic · IV opiates for symptomatic relief</td></tr> <tr><td>Cold &amp; wet</td><td>Congestion + poor perfusion and/or hypotension</td><td>Normal BP: start diuretics with close observation (on the verge of decompensation). Hypotension / hypoperfusion: <b>inotrope</b> (box 5) ± vasopressor; refractory → <b>MCS</b> (box 8)</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">SBP 90 mmHg is a common cut-off for diuretic/CPAP vs inotrope/MCS strategy, but there is no robust cut-off: judge against baseline BP, organ function, CRT, skin, urine output, lactate, mental state, ScvO₂.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Haemodynamic optimisation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Rate:</b> no target HR; compensatory tachycardia → treat the failure, use HR as a warning sign</li> <li><b>Rhythm:</b> atrial kick ≈ 20% of filling (more with tachycardia, diastolic dysfunction, MS). Cardiovert only <b>acute/paroxysmal</b> arrhythmias driving the decompensation (chronic AF: low success, embolic risk)</li> <li><b>Preload:</b> ↓ LVEDP with IV loop diuretics + nitrates; watch renal function and electrolytes; RRT if diuretic resistance</li> <li><b>Afterload:</b> non-shocked hypertensive → IV nitrates to SBP &lt; 110; as diuresis works BP may fall → reassess for relative hypovolaemia</li> <li><b>Pulmonary vascular resistance:</b> avoid hypoxia, acidosis, hypercapnia, high alveolar pressures, atelectasis; iNO (rapid, improves V/Q) or milrinone (systemic vasodilation)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Inotropes = salvage therapy (dose per CMH formulary)","html":"<div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">First correct K⁺, Mg²⁺, Ca²⁺, acid–base, oxygenation, coronary perfusion</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>\"Ino-dilators\" are the treatment of choice in cardiogenic shock (bridge to recovery, PCI or advanced therapy)</li> <li><b>Dobutamine:</b> short acting; β1 inotropy + β2 vasodilation</li> <li><b>Milrinone</b> (PDE-3 inhibitor) / <b>levosimendan</b> (Ca sensitiser): long acting, potent vasodilators (milrinone ↓ PVR)</li> <li><b>Adrenaline:</b> inotropy, chronotropy, vasoconstriction; causes <b>lactic acidosis</b> → lactate no longer tracks perfusion</li> <li>Vasoconstrictor may be needed to offset ino-dilator vasodilation; CO monitoring + SVR distinguish deterioration from drug effect</li> <li>Titrate with CO monitoring and repeated echo</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Respiratory & renal support","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>SpO₂ <b>&gt; 90%</b> pragmatic; <b>avoid hyperoxia</b> (coronary vasoconstriction, worse in ACS)</li> <li><b>CPAP</b>: recruits alveoli, ↓ lung water, shunt and work of breathing; BiPAP better CO₂ clearance but not superior</li> <li><b>Intubate</b> for worsening hypoxia, rising O₂ need or work of breathing, ↓ GCS, airway compromise</li> <li>PPV ↓ LV afterload but may ↓ RV filling and ↑ RV afterload: effects unpredictable</li> <li><b>RRT / ultrafiltration:</b> diuretic-refractory overload, electrolyte derangement, K⁺ &gt; 6.5 mmol/L or rising, acidosis, intoxication, uraemia</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\" style=\"color:var(--red)\">Intubation danger</div> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>High risk of cardiovascular collapse at induction (negative inotropy, vasodilation, intrathoracic pressure changes): experienced help + resuscitation drugs at hand</li></ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Monitoring","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>All:</b> continuous ECG, NIBP, RR, temperature, SpO₂</li> <li><b>Arterial line</b> if hypotensive or on vasoactive drugs</li> <li><b>CVC</b> for vasoactives; CVP trends useful in HF (indirect RVEDP), ScvO₂</li> <li><b>CO monitoring</b> if deteriorating or not responding: PAC (gold standard), LiDCO, PiCCO, FloTrac, oesophageal Doppler, non-invasive</li> <li><b>PAC</b> (wedge, CI, PVR, SvO₂) helps differentiate shock; complete haemodynamic profiling linked to lower mortality in cardiogenic shock</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Severity after MI: Killip-Kimball (30-day mortality)</div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>I</td><td>No HF signs</td><td>&lt; 6%</td></tr> <tr><td>II</td><td>Basal crackles / S3</td><td>&lt; 17%</td></tr> <tr><td>III</td><td>Florid pulmonary oedema</td><td>38%</td></tr> <tr><td>IV</td><td>Cardiogenic shock</td><td>81%</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"8","h":"Refractory cardiogenic shock → mechanical circulatory support (MDT: cardiology, surgery, ICU; discuss early with advanced HF centre)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:13%\">Device</th><th style=\"width:11%\">Support / flow</th><th style=\"width:35%\">Key effects</th><th>Contraindications / complications</th></tr> <tr><td>IABP</td><td>LV · 0.5–1 L/min</td><td>Diastolic inflation ↑ coronary flow; systolic deflation ↓ afterload. Weak with very poor LV, arrhythmia, tachycardia. <b>Not routine in cardiogenic shock</b> (IABP-SHOCK II)</td><td>≥ moderate AR; PVD (limb ischaemia); malposition; thrombocytopenia; infection</td></tr> <tr><td>Impella</td><td>LV or RV · 2.5–5 L/min</td><td>Axial pump across aortic valve; unloads LV. Needs adequate RV function; arrhythmias poorly tolerated</td><td>Mechanical AVR (absolute), AS/AR (relative), PVD; haemolysis, bleeding, limb ischaemia</td></tr> <tr><td>TandemHeart</td><td>LV or RV · 3.5–5 L/min</td><td>LA → femoral artery via transseptal puncture (expertise needed)</td><td>Highest limb-ischaemia risk; full anticoagulation; haemolysis</td></tr> <tr><td>VA ECMO</td><td>LV + RV + gas exchange · &gt; 5 L/min</td><td>Modality of choice in severe cardiorespiratory failure; <b>↑ LV afterload</b> → may need LV unloading</td><td>≥ moderate AR, severe PVD, contraindication to anticoagulation (ICH, HIT)</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>Define the strategy before implanting:</b> bridge to decision · bridge to bridge (long-term MCS) · bridge to transplant · bridge to recovery (e.g. myocarditis). Escalate to higher-flow devices if deteriorating.</div>"},{"n":"9","h":"Look for the cause","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Ischaemia</b> (unstable left main → profound AHF)</li> <li>Arrhythmia (fast AF, PVCs, RV pacing)</li> <li>Endocarditis with acute valve regurgitation</li> <li>Myocarditis, cardiomyopathy, sepsis myocardial depression, chemotherapy</li> <li>Alcohol, cocaine, methamphetamine</li> <li>Phaeochromocytoma; bilateral renal artery stenosis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Chase an arbitrary heart-rate target</li> <li>Cardiovert chronic AF (embolic risk)</li> <li>Give hyperoxia</li> <li>Use routine IABP in cardiogenic shock</li> <li>Start MCS with no route to recovery, transplant or long-term device</li> </ul>"}]},"04d-rv-failure":{"slogan":"Keep the RV perfused, not overfilled, and unload it","legend":"PVR = pulmonary vascular resistance · iNO = inhaled nitric oxide","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Right Ventricular Failure in ICU (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Suspect RV failure (classic signs often absent when sedated / ventilated)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Hypoperfusion: ↑ lactate, ↓ ScvO₂, deranged LFTs, ↑ urea / creatinine (non-specific)</li> <li><b>Rising CVP</b> = ominous; Kussmaul sign (RV infarct, constriction); dominant v-wave = TR</li> <li>CXR: relatively <b>clear lung fields with low cardiac output</b>; enlarged RV fills retrosternal space on lateral film</li> <li>ECG often normal: right axis, RBBB, RVH; S1Q3T3 in &lt; 10%</li> <li><b>BNP and troponin</b> rise in many causes (not specific)</li> <li>Echo \"empty, hyperdynamic LV\" may be <b>under-filled by a failing RV</b> (interdependence): look at the RV</li> </ul> <div class=\"kbox\"><b>Vicious cycle:</b> RV dilates → TR → septum bows into LV → ↓ LV output and BP → ↓ RCA perfusion + coronary sinus congestion → RV ischaemia → worse RV. Compensated can become decompensated fast: break the cycle at several points at once.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Confirm & measure","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Early TTE</b> = first line: RV size vs LV, TAPSE, FAC, strain, PASP, IVC (RAP); TEE if PPV/high PEEP/chest trauma limit views</li> <li>PE: <b>RV/LV diameter ≥ 1.0</b> and <b>TAPSE &lt; 16 mm</b> predict adverse outcome</li> <li><b>Arterial line:</b> ABGs (RV sensitive to ↑ CO₂ and acidosis), serial lactate</li> <li><b>CVP mandatory</b>, read as a trend in response to fluid and inotropes</li> <li><b>PAC</b> in complex cases. Acute RV dysfunction: <b>CVP &gt; PCWP</b>, low CI and SVI, <b>SvO₂ &lt; 55%</b>; allows PVR</li> <li><b>PH</b> = mPAP <b>&gt; 20 mmHg</b>; precapillary: PAWP ≤ 15 + PVR &gt; 2 WU</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Treat the cause","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Cause</th><th>Key ICU actions (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Pulmonary embolism</td><td><b>High-risk</b> (arrest; SBP &lt; 90 or vasopressor + hypoperfusion; SBP &lt; 90 or drop ≥ 40 for &gt; 15 min) → <b>immediate reperfusion: systemic thrombolysis</b>; if contraindicated/failed → surgical embolectomy or catheter therapy; VA-ECMO as bridge. <b>UFH preferred</b> (titratable). <b>Intermediate-high</b> (RV dysfunction + troponin): anticoagulate + monitor in HDU, <b>no routine full-dose lysis</b> (PEITHO); rescue reperfusion if decompensates. PE response team</td></tr> <tr><td>ARDS</td><td>Acute cor pulmonale ≈ 22–23%. Risk: pneumonia, <b>DP ≥ 18</b>, P/F &lt; 150, PaCO₂ ≥ 48 → echo. <b>RV-protective ventilation:</b> Pplat <b>&lt; 27</b>, DP <b>&lt; 18 cmH₂O</b>, correct hypoxia and CO₂, <b>prone</b> (≥ 12 h/day in severe ARDS), VV-ECMO in severe</td></tr> <tr><td>COPD / OHS</td><td>Collapse after intubation from <b>auto-PEEP</b>: long expiratory time, caution with PEEP, volume + vasopressor ready at induction. PAH drugs not recommended in COPD-PH</td></tr> <tr><td>Decompensated PH</td><td>Look for triggers (sepsis, pregnancy, anaemia, PE, arrhythmia, thyroid). <b>Intubation very high-risk.</b> Keep systemic/coronary pressure (noradrenaline, vasopressin), careful preload, <b>inhaled NO or prostacyclin</b>; early specialist input</td></tr> <tr><td>Cardiac surgery / LVAD / transplant</td><td>RV failure up to 30% after surgery (stunning, air embolism, ↑ PVR); 20–40% after LVAD. Vasopressor for perfusion, inhaled vasodilator, milrinone, AV synchrony, adjust LVAD speed; severe PGD → <b>VA-ECMO</b></td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Haemodynamic optimisation (stepwise)","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">Rate / rhythm</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li><b>Keep the RV beating faster</b>, ideally sinus (post-transplant often paced at 110/min): ↓ distension, ↓ TR. Cardiovert only acute/paroxysmal arrhythmias</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Coronary perfusion</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li><b>Maintain systemic BP</b>: noradrenaline or vasopressin. Hypotension starves the RV; IABP may help RCA perfusion</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Preload</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Hypotension without high filling pressures (<b>CVP &lt; 12–15</b>): <b>small</b> bolus, guided by echo/CVP/CO</li> <li>Congested (<b>CVP &gt; 15</b>, oedema, hepatic engorgement): <b>diurese</b> or ultrafiltration</li> <li>Hypotensive + congested: <b>vasopressor / inopressor before more fluid</b></li> <li>RV infarct (normal afterload) needs higher filling pressure</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Contractility</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Inotrope only for <b>confirmed low output</b> once BP restored; withdraw early. No agent superior</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Afterload (PVR)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Avoid hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis; low airway pressures; judicious PEEP (atelectasis and excess PEEP both ↑ PVR)</li> <li><b>Inhaled NO</b> preferred: fast, improves V/Q and RVEF. IV vasodilators worsen V/Q and drop SVR</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Drugs & doses (ACE Table 3)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:36%\">Dose</th><th>Notes</th></tr> <tr><td>Noradrenaline</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.02–0.2 mcg/kg/min IV</td><td>α1 β1; ↑ PVR at high dose</td></tr> <tr><td>Vasopressin</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.01–0.04 units/min IV</td><td>Less ↑ PVR; bradycardia, splanchnic ischaemia</td></tr> <tr><td>Dobutamine</td><td class=\"tgt\">2.5–10 mcg/kg/min IV</td><td>↓ SVR and PVR; tachyarrhythmia</td></tr> <tr><td>Milrinone</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.375–0.75 mcg/kg/min IV</td><td>t½ 2.5 h; hypotension. Loading dose ↑ hypotension risk</td></tr> <tr><td>Levosimendan</td><td class=\"tgt\">Load 6–12 mcg/kg over 10 min, then 0.1 mcg/kg/min</td><td>No ↑ O₂ demand; effect up to 1 wk</td></tr> <tr><td>Sildenafil</td><td class=\"tgt\">IV 10 mg TDS · PO 20–100 mg TDS</td><td>t½ 4–18 h, ↓ SVR: stable disease</td></tr> <tr><td>Epoprostenol</td><td class=\"tgt\">IV 1–2 ng/kg/min · neb 0.2–0.3 mL/min of 10–20 µg/mL</td><td>As effective as NO; IV hypotension</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">ACE lists levosimendan load as \"6–12 mcg/kg/min over 10 min\" (= 6–12 mcg/kg total). Inhaled NO: dose per CMH formulary.</div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">Ino-dilators drop SVR → often need a vasopressor alongside</div></div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Refractory → short-term mechanical support (early referral to cardiac centre; MDT)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:14%\">Device</th><th style=\"width:17%\">Flow / anticoagulation</th><th>Points</th></tr> <tr><td>Impella RP</td><td>≤ 4 L/min · ACT 160–180 s</td><td>Femoral vein, across pulmonary valve; ↓ RAP, ↑ LV preload; no oxygenation</td></tr> <tr><td>TandemHeart RVAD</td><td>2–4 L/min</td><td>RA → PA; two venous cannulae or dual-lumen (ProtekDuo)</td></tr> <tr><td>VA-ECMO</td><td>2–6 L/min · ACT 180–220 s</td><td>RV + gas exchange; ↑ LV afterload (LV distension, pulmonary oedema); AR and PVD contraindicate; limb ischaemia, coagulopathy</td></tr> <tr><td>Surgical RVAD</td><td>&gt; 5 L/min</td><td>Best unloading, mobilise; too unstable patients excluded</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>Wean</b> when organs recover (urine, lactate normal, stable SvO₂): CVP ideally <b>&lt; 14 mmHg</b>, PAPi, echo RV size/function, low or no drug support. Contraindications to MCS: advanced age, major comorbidity, established organ failure.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"RV vs LV: why the RV fails","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th></th><th>RV</th><th>LV</th></tr> <tr><td>Wall</td><td>2–5 mm</td><td>8–10 mm</td></tr> <tr><td>Systolic P</td><td>15–30 mmHg</td><td>90–140 mmHg</td></tr> <tr><td>Afterload</td><td>PVR &lt; 250 dyn</td><td>SVR 800–1200 dyn</td></tr> <tr><td>Tolerates</td><td>↑ preload</td><td>↑ afterload</td></tr> <tr><td>Coronary flow</td><td>Systole + diastole</td><td>Mainly diastole</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">RCA supplies RV in 80%. Falling PA pressure with persistently high PVR = RV exhausted (ominous).</div>"},{"n":"8","h":"PE risk classes (ESC/ERS 2019)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>High:</b> haemodynamically unstable → reperfusion now (mortality 20–30%)</li> <li><b>Intermediate-high:</b> RV dysfunction <b>and</b> ↑ troponin → anticoagulate, HDU, rescue reperfusion</li> <li><b>Intermediate-low:</b> one or neither positive</li> <li><b>Low:</b> sPESI 0 / PESI I–II, no RV dysfunction</li> <li>Catheter-directed lysis reduced 7-day deterioration in intermediate-high risk (HI-PEITHO)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Other at-risk RVs","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>OHS / OSA:</b> sedation may collapse airway; CPAP (OSA), NIV (OHS)</li> <li><b>Adult congenital:</b> Eisenmenger, repaired Fallot, systemic RV: involve ACHD centre</li> <li><b>LV failure</b> is the commonest cause of PH; RV dysfunction in ≈ 40% HFpEF</li> <li><b>Tricuspid repair:</b> pre-op TR flatters RV function; afterload rises after repair</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk cols\"> <li>Give repeated fluid boluses to a dilated, congested RV</li> <li>Be reassured by an empty, hyperdynamic LV</li> <li>Let the patient become hypotensive, hypoxic, hypercapnic or acidotic</li> <li>Intubate without volume, vasopressor and an experienced operator ready</li> <li>Give routine full-dose lysis in intermediate-high-risk PE</li> <li>Use IV pulmonary vasodilators acutely when inhaled options exist</li> </ul>"}]},"04e-acute-myocardial-ischaemia":{"slogan":"ECG within 10 minutes · Time is muscle","legend":"FMC = first medical contact · DAPT = dual antiplatelet therapy","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Acute Myocardial Ischaemia Terminology, Pathophysiology and Recognition (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise (often atypical or silent in ICU)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Typical MI pain: severe, constant, retrosternal, radiating to throat/jaw/arms/back, <b>&gt; 20 min</b>, sweating, nausea, pallor</li> <li>Also: dyspnoea, syncope, palpitations, abdominal/epigastric pain, sudden death; diabetics, women, elderly may be silent</li> <li>Examine: HR, BP, perfusion, RR, SpO₂, lungs, pulses, neuro screen; new MR or VSD murmur</li> <li><b>RV infarct:</b> hypotension + high JVP + <b>clear lungs</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\" style=\"color:var(--red)\">Exclude life-threatening mimics</div> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Aortic dissection (unequal arm BP), PE, pericarditis (rub), tension pneumothorax, oesophageal rupture</li></ul> <div class=\"kbox\"><b>\"Think A.C.S.\"</b> (ESC 2023): <b>A</b>bnormal ECG · <b>C</b>linical context · <b>S</b>table?</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"ECG, troponin, echo","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>12-lead ECG ≤ 10 min</b> of FMC; repeat every 15–30 min if non-diagnostic and symptoms persist; continuous monitoring</li> <li><b>STEMI:</b> new J-point ST↑ in ≥ 2 contiguous leads ≥ 1 mm; <b>V2–V3:</b> ≥ 2 mm men &gt; 40 y, ≥ 2.5 mm men ≤ 40 y, ≥ 1.5 mm women. New LBBB / paced rhythm = treat as STEMI equivalent</li> <li>ST↓ V1–V3 → posterior leads <b>V7–V9</b>; inferior MI → right leads <b>V3R–V4R</b></li> <li><b>hs-troponin 0 h / 1 h</b> (ESC; or 0 h/2 h): rule-in, observe, rule-out; cut-offs are assay-specific</li> <li>Troponin rises in sepsis, renal failure, stroke, vasopressors: <b>injury ≠ infarction</b></li> <li><b>Urgent TTE</b> if unstable: RWMA, LV/RV function, MR, rupture/VSD, effusion</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Immediate management (A–I)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Step</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Dose / action (ACE)</th><th>Key cautions</th></tr> <tr><td>Monitor + access</td><td>ECG, arterial pressure, SpO₂; venous access (<b>keep right arm</b> for radial PCI); troponin, coagulation, renal</td><td>Do not delay treatment for detailed history</td></tr> <tr><td>Oxygen</td><td class=\"tgt\">Only if SpO₂ &lt; 90% or respiratory distress</td><td>Routine O₂ in normoxaemia <b>not recommended</b> (DETO2X, AVOID); lowest mortality SpO₂ 94–96%</td></tr> <tr><td>Nitrates</td><td class=\"tgt\">GTN SL 0.4 mg every 5 min × 3, then IV titrated to symptoms</td><td>Only for symptoms. <b>Avoid in inferior MI with RV involvement</b> (severe hypotension)</td></tr> <tr><td>Morphine</td><td class=\"tgt\">2–4 mg IV, repeat 2–8 mg every 5–15 min</td><td>Only if nitrates + anxiolysis fail; delays P2Y12 absorption, linked to ↑ mortality/MACE</td></tr> <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td class=\"tgt\">150–325 mg chewed, then ≤ 100 mg daily</td><td>−23% mortality (ISIS-2)</td></tr> <tr><td>P2Y12 inhibitor</td><td>Prasugrel or ticagrelor preferred over clopidogrel for PCI (dose per CMH formulary)</td><td>Prasugrel: <b>not after stroke/TIA</b>; reduce if ≥ 75 y or &lt; 60 kg. No routine pretreatment in NSTE-ACS. DAPT ≥ 12 months</td></tr> <tr><td>Anticoagulant</td><td>UFH, enoxaparin, bivalirudin or fondaparinux (dose per CMH formulary)</td><td>All confirmed ACS; choose by strategy and bleeding risk</td></tr> <tr><td>β-blocker</td><td>Oral within 24 h if stable</td><td><b>No IV β-blocker</b> in HF, hypotension, shock risk (COMMIT)</td></tr> <tr><td>Pulmonary oedema</td><td>Sit up, IV furosemide, IV GTN, CPAP / NIV</td><td>Doses per CMH formulary</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"STEMI: reperfusion","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Symptoms ≤ 12 h + persistent ST↑ (or new LBBB) → <b>primary PCI</b></div></div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>FMC → ECG / diagnosis</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 10 min</td></tr> <tr><td>Diagnosis → wire (PCI hospital)</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 60 min</td></tr> <tr><td>Diagnosis → wire (transferred)</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 90 min</td></tr> <tr><td>If PCI delay &gt; 120 min</td><td class=\"tgt\">Fibrinolysis ≤ 10 min of diagnosis</td></tr> <tr><td>Assess lysis success</td><td>60–90 min</td></tr> <tr><td>Angiography after lysis</td><td>2–24 h (immediately if failed)</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Tenecteplase</b> preferred (alteplase, reteplase alternatives); <b>half dose if ≥ 75 y</b> (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>&gt; 12 h: PCI if ongoing ischaemia, instability, arrhythmia</li> <li>Radial access preferred</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"NSTE-ACS: timing of angiography","html":"<div class=\"sub l\" style=\"color:var(--red)\">Immediate (&lt; 2 h) if very high risk</div> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Haemodynamic instability / cardiogenic shock</li> <li>Refractory ongoing chest pain</li> <li>Life-threatening arrhythmia / cardiac arrest</li> <li>Mechanical complication</li> <li>Acute HF from ongoing ischaemia</li> <li>Recurrent dynamic ST/T changes (intermittent ST↑)</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Early (&lt; 24 h)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>High risk, e.g. <b>GRACE &gt; 140</b></li><li>Otherwise invasive strategy during admission if intermediate/high risk</li></ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">Fibrinolysis is NOT used in NSTEMI</div></div> <div class=\"sub l\">High-risk AMI features</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Killip ≥ II, SBP &lt; 100, HR &gt; 100, anterior ST↑ &gt; 2 mm in ≥ 2 leads, RV involvement, EF &lt; 35%, ↑ lactate / renal dysfunction</li></ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Cardiogenic shock (SCAI A–E)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Definition:</b> SBP &lt; 90 for &gt; 30 min or drugs/MCS needed + hypoperfusion; 5–10% of AMI, 30-day mortality ≈ 40%</li> <li><b>Emergency revascularisation of culprit</b> = only proven mortality benefit (SHOCK); <b>culprit-only PCI</b> (CULPRIT-SHOCK)</li> <li><b>Noradrenaline</b> 1st-line vasopressor, minimum dose for <b>MAP &gt; 65</b>; dobutamine most used inotrope; levosimendan if no response</li> <li><b>Impella CP</b> ↓ 180-day mortality in STEMI shock (DanGer) but more bleeding, limb ischaemia, RRT</li> <li><b>Routine IABP no longer recommended</b>; routine VA-ECMO no benefit (ECLS-SHOCK)</li> <li><b>RV infarct:</b> judicious fluid; avoid nitrates and diuretics</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Troponin in the ICU: type 1 or type 2?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Type 1</b> (plaque rupture/thrombus): sharp rise and fall, ischaemic symptoms/ECG, new RWMA → <b>DAPT + angiography</b></li> <li><b>Type 2</b> (supply–demand: shock, anaemia, tachyarrhythmia, hypoxia): gradual rise/plateau, diffuse hypokinesis → <b>fix the cause</b>; DAPT not indicated</li> <li>Sepsis can cause troponin rise + low EF that fully recovers</li> <li>Other types: 3 sudden death, 4a–c PCI/stent, 5 CABG. Mechanical complications (VSD, papillary rupture, free wall rupture) → urgent echo</li> <li>MINOCA (no stenosis ≥ 50%): working diagnosis → <b>CMR</b> (myocarditis, takotsubo, spasm, SCAD)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"SCAI shock stages","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>A At risk</td><td>Large MI, prior HF; no compromise</td></tr> <tr><td>B Pre-shock</td><td>SBP 60–90 / MAP 50–65 without hypoperfusion, or lactate 2–5 / ALT 200–500 without drugs</td></tr> <tr><td>C Classic</td><td>Hypotension + hypoperfusion, or 1 drug / 1 device</td></tr> <tr><td>D Deteriorating</td><td>Lactate 5–10 / ALT &gt; 500, or 2–5 drugs/devices, or failing on 1</td></tr> <tr><td>E Extremis</td><td>SBP &lt; 60 / MAP &lt; 50, lactate &gt; 10 or pH ≤ 7.2, &gt; 3 drugs or 3 devices; all OHCA</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"9","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Delay the ECG or reperfusion for a detailed history</li> <li>Give routine oxygen to normoxaemic patients</li> <li>Give nitrates in RV infarction or hypotension</li> <li>Give IV β-blocker in HF, hypotension or shock</li> <li>Give fibrinolysis in NSTEMI; give DAPT for type 2 MI</li> <li>Use routine IABP or multivessel PCI in shock</li> <li>Give prasugrel after stroke/TIA</li> </ul>"}]},"05a-arrhythmia-tachy":{"slogan":"Unstable = electricity · Every antiarrhythmic is pro-arrhythmic · Unsure if VT? Amiodarone, never verapamil","legend":"SVT = supraventricular tachycardia · VT = ventricular tachycardia · DCCV = synchronised DC cardioversion","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Arrhythmia Part I; Part II: Diagnosis of Rhythm Disturbances; Part IV (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Is the patient compromised?","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">YES (hypotension, low output, ischaemia, collapse) → <b>act now: synchronised DC cardioversion</b> (pace if slow)</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Likely to decompensate</b> (e.g. AF with poor LV function): wait and watch closely</li> <li><b>Stable:</b> do you need to intervene at all? Not all arrhythmias need treatment</li> <li><b>\"Electricity\" is safer than drugs</b>: drugs are pro-arrhythmic, negatively inotropic, long-acting</li> <li>12-lead ECG whenever possible; rhythm strip with adenosine; pacer pads on</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Correct triggers (simultaneously if unstable)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>ABG + electrolytes:</b> keep K⁺ in the <b>upper-normal range</b> in cardiac disease / after cardiac surgery (not after transplant)</li> <li><b>Magnesium</b>: deficiency common, levels don't reflect tissue; replace generously (care in renal failure). <b>4–8 mmol IV over 5 min</b> for torsade even if Mg normal</li> <li><b>Ischaemia:</b> β-blocker, nitrates, normalise pre/afterload, PCI, IABP/MCS, pacing</li> <li><b>Catheters:</b> CVC/PAC tip in RA/RV → withdraw a few cm; reposition pacing wires</li> <li><b>Drugs:</b> inotropes (esp. with low preload), β-agonists, antiarrhythmics, antidepressants, anticonvulsants, QT drugs; reduce pro-arrhythmic drugs</li> <li>Pain, anxiety, fever, sepsis, hypovolaemia/overfilling, hypoxia, ↑ PaCO₂, acidosis, thyrotoxicosis, suction/bronchoscopy</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2B","h":"Read the rhythm","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Rate:</b> &gt; 100 bpm = tachycardia (often appropriate in the critically ill – put it in context)</li> <li><b>Regular?</b> Irregularly irregular = AF (or flutter with variable block)</li> <li><b>P waves?</b> Present = coordinated atrial activity; relation to QRS; chaotic = AF</li> <li><b>QRS width:</b> narrow (supraventricular) vs broad (VT, aberrancy, pre-excitation)</li> <li>Helpers: CVP trace (no a-wave in AF, cannon waves in AV dissociation); atrial wire recording; adenosine strip</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2C","h":"Who needs ECG monitoring","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Known cardiopulmonary disease: prior arrhythmia, cardiothoracic surgery, advanced heart failure</li> <li>Pro-arrhythmic setting: electrical injury, cardiac procedures, acute neurological disease, drug/metabolic toxicity, endocrine disease, severe electrolyte disturbance, risk of profound bradycardia</li> <li>Unexplained collapse / syncope</li> <li>Pitfalls: single lead misses P waves and ischaemic ST in unmonitored zones; burns, prone position; alarm software lacks sensitivity/specificity</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2D","h":"Antiarrhythmic classes","html":"<ul class=\"dt\"> <li><b>0</b> ivabradine · <b>Ia</b> procainamide, quinidine · <b>Ib</b> lidocaine · <b>Ic</b> flecainide, propafenone</li> <li><b>II</b> β-blockers (esmolol, landiolol, metoprolol…); atropine, isoprenaline, digoxin, adenosine in subclasses</li> <li><b>III</b> amiodarone, sotalol, ibutilide, vernakalant · <b>IV</b> verapamil, diltiazem</li> </ul> <div class=\"hl\">Choose the drug for the diagnosis. In significant heart disease <b>amiodarone</b> has the safest profile. All are pro-arrhythmic; most are negatively inotropic.</div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Narrow-complex (supraventricular) tachycardias","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:12%\">Rhythm</th><th style=\"width:30%\">ECG clue</th><th>Management (ACE doses)</th></tr> <tr><td>Sinus tachycardia</td><td>Normal P before every QRS, constant PR</td><td>Treat cause (pain, hypovolaemia, sepsis, fever, anaemia, β-agonists, thyrotoxicosis); β-blocker rarely, if ever; exclude PAT / flutter 2:1. Severe diastolic dysfunction: cautious short-acting β-blocker</td></tr> <tr><td>Atrial tachycardia (PAT) / MAT</td><td>Abrupt onset, abnormal P axis; changing P = MAT</td><td>Adenosine (unmasks); IV β-blocker, or verapamil/diltiazem; DCCV if resistant and unstable. Digoxin-toxic junctional tachycardia: stop digoxin</td></tr> <tr><td>Atrial flutter</td><td>Rate a division of 300 (150, 100), saw-tooth</td><td>Rate control: β-blocker, CCB, digoxin; <b>synchronised DCCV</b> converts easily; overdrive atrial pacing via wire; <b>anticoagulate as for AF</b>. Think PE, under/over-filling</td></tr> <tr><td>AVNRT</td><td>Regular, &gt; 150 bpm, P hidden or retrograde after QRS</td><td>Carotid sinus massage (equal pulses, no bruit) or <b>adenosine</b>; then β-blocker or CCB</td></tr> <tr><td>AVRT / WPW</td><td>Short PR + delta wave; orthodromic narrow, antidromic wide</td><td>Orthodromic: as AVNRT. Pre-excited wide: <b>procainamide, ibutilide, propafenone, flecainide</b>; adenosine may trigger AF. Unstable: DCCV. <span class=\"av\"><b>No digoxin; caution verapamil/β-blocker</b></span></td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Adenosine: termination = AV-node re-entry; transient slowing = sinus/atrial tachycardia; 2–3 s AV block unmasks flutter/atrial tachycardia. Risk of prolonged asystole with sinus node disease – monitor, pads on. ACE gives no adenosine dose (per CMH formulary).</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Atrial fibrillation (priorities: rate → embolism → cause → rhythm)","html":"<div class=\"split3\"> <div><div class=\"sub\">Rate control</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Amiodarone</b> rapidly effective in ICU</li> <li><b>Diltiazem 0.25 mg/kg</b> over 2 min, then <b>5–15 mg/h</b></li> <li><b>Verapamil 0.075 mg/kg</b> slow IV (not with β-blocker, accessory pathway or profound hypotension)</li> <li>IV β-blocker (dose per CMH formulary); CCB/β-blockers are negatively inotropic</li> <li>Digoxin: slowest onset, not first choice</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Rhythm control</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Unstable: <b>DCCV first</b>; if it fails, <b>amiodarone 300 mg/30 min</b> then repeat</li> <li>Stable chemical: <b>amiodarone 150 mg over 10 min → 1 mg/min × 6 h → 0.5 mg/min × 18 h</b> → oral</li> <li>Propafenone 2 mg/kg IV over 10 min (converts in 30 min–2 h)</li> <li>Vernakalant 3 mg/kg over 10 min, then 2 mg/kg after 15 min (not if SBP &lt; 100 or advanced HF)</li> <li class=\"av\"><b>Flecainide contraindicated</b> with LV dysfunction / ischaemic heart disease</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Embolism &amp; cause</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CHA₂DS₂-VASc ≥ 2</b>: anticoagulate (warfarin INR 2–3 or DOAC); 1: individualise; 0: none</li> <li>AF &gt; 48 h: cardioversion emboli 5.5% vs &lt; 1% after 3 weeks' anticoagulation → or <b>TOE</b> then cardiovert + 3 weeks' anticoagulation</li> <li>Causes: fever, <b>sepsis</b>, under/over-filling, thyrotoxicosis (always exclude)</li> <li>Often reverts once cause corrected; DCCV fails if cause persists</li> <li>Post-cardiac surgery: amiodarone prophylaxis (600 mg/day oral 1 week pre-op then 200 mg/day; or IV for 48 h post-op)</li> </ul></div> </div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Broad-complex tachycardia = VT until proven otherwise","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Prior MI outweighs ECG criteria</b> → VT. Favour VT: AV dissociation, QRS &gt; 160 ms, northwest axis, capture/fusion beats, RBBB-type with mono/biphasic V1 or R/S &lt; 1 in V6, LBBB-type with R &gt; 30 ms in V1–2, notched S, QR in V6</li> <li><b>Unstable sustained VT: synchronised DCCV</b></li> <li>Stable, awake: <b>amiodarone 150 mg IV over 10 min</b> (works in only 24–40%)</li> <li>NSVT: correct K/Mg, reposition catheters; LVEF &lt; 35% → cardiology (ICD); recurrent with compromise → amiodarone infusion or pacing</li> <li>Refractory VT/VF: overdrive pacing, revascularisation, intubation, IABP/MCS, electrophysiologist (ablation, electrical storm)</li> <li>VT/VF &gt; 48 h after MI without ischaemia → cardiology referral; VF → resuscitation protocol</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>No verapamil</b> when origin uncertain (severe collapse in VT)</li></ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Torsade de pointes & devices","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Causes: electrolytes (<b>esp. ↓Mg</b>), amiodarone/sotalol, haloperidol, quetiapine, TCAs, quinolones, macrolides, ketoconazole, ondansetron, bradycardia, ischaemia, stroke/ICH, congenital long QT</li> <li>Stop drug, correct K/Mg, <b>Mg 4–8 mmol IV over 5 min</b>; then ↑ rate with <b>isoprenaline or overdrive pacing</b></li> <li><b>ICD:</b> magnet deactivates shocks; external shocks allowed (pads away from device). Repetitive shocks: treat triggers, intensify antiarrhythmics; deactivate if inappropriate (SVT, T-wave oversensing)</li> <li>Overdrive (antitachycardia) pacing at 20–30% above tachycardia rate may terminate flutter, AVNRT, AVRT, VT – but can accelerate or cause VF</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Ventricular ectopics","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Signal cardiac disease or mechanical/chemical irritability</li> <li>Correct electrolytes rigorously; supplement K⁺ and Mg²⁺ after cardiac surgery or with IHD</li> <li>Reposition intracardiac catheters; β-blocker in coronary artery disease</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"ICD – indications & problems","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Post-MI or dilated cardiomyopathy with LVEF &lt; 35%; inducible VT/VF with syncope; structural disease + sustained VT. CRT-D if NYHA III–IV, LVEF &lt; 35%, QRS &gt; 120 ms</li> <li>No therapy during VT: external DCCV / drugs, then interrogate. Diathermy: reprogram/disable shocks. Device infection: treat sepsis, full system replacement</li> </ul>"}]},"05b-arrhythmia-brady":{"slogan":"Correct drugs, K⁺, ischaemia, hypothermia first · Infra-Hisian block: isoprenaline or pace, not atropine","legend":"SND = sinus node dysfunction · AVB = AV block","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Arrhythmia Part III: Management of the patient with Bradyarrhythmia (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Bradycardia (HR < 60) or block: ask three questions","html":"<div class=\"split3\"> <div><div class=\"sub red\">Compromised?</div><ul class=\"ck\"><li>Hypotension, altered consciousness, signs of low cardiac output → treat now (box 4)</li><li>12-lead ECG, continuous monitoring, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, ABG, troponin, drug review</li></ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub red\">Reversible?</div><ul class=\"ck\"><li><b>Hyperkalaemia, hypothermia, drug toxicity, ischaemia, electrolytes</b>, hypoxia, acidosis, hypothyroidism, vagal reflex (suction), sleep apnoea</li><li>In the general ICU <b>extrinsic causes dominate</b>; correct them before any permanent pacing</li></ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub red\">Level of block?</div><ul class=\"ck\"><li><b>Supra-Hisian</b> (narrow escape, AV node): usually responds to <b>atropine</b></li><li><b>Infra-Hisian</b> (wide escape, Mobitz II, LAD MI): less atropine-responsive → <b>isoprenaline or pacing</b></li></ul></div> </div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Recognise the rhythm (ESC 2021)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Entity</th><th style=\"width:50%\">ECG definition</th><th>Significance / permanent pacing</th></tr> <tr><td>Sinus bradycardia · pause</td><td>Sinus rate &lt; 60 bpm · no P wave ≥ 2 s (≥ 3 s rare physiologically)</td><td>SND: correct cause; no permanent pacing if reversible</td></tr> <tr><td>Chronotropic incompetence</td><td>Fails to reach 80% of HR reserve (220 – age)</td><td>Universal after heart transplant (denervated)</td></tr> <tr><td>Brady-tachy syndrome</td><td>Alternating atrial tachyarrhythmia and slow rates; post-conversion pauses → syncope</td><td>AF coexists in 40–70%; rate-control drugs may worsen bradycardia</td></tr> <tr><td>1st-degree AVB</td><td>PR ≥ 200 ms, 1:1 conduction</td><td>Benign; PR &gt; 300 ms may cause \"pacemaker syndrome\"</td></tr> <tr><td>Mobitz I (Wenckebach)</td><td>Progressive PR lengthening then a dropped P</td><td>Usually benign; pace if symptomatic or block at/below His (IIa)</td></tr> <tr><td>Mobitz II · 2:1 · high-degree</td><td>Dropped P with constant PR · every 2nd P blocked · ≥ 2 consecutive blocked P</td><td class=\"tgt\">Class I pacing (infranodal 2:1, high-degree), regardless of symptoms</td></tr> <tr><td>Complete (3rd-degree) AVB</td><td>Complete AV dissociation; in AF suspect if ventricular rate &lt; 50 and regular</td><td class=\"tgt\">Class I permanent pacing</td></tr> <tr><td>Alternating BBB</td><td>RBBB alternating with LBBB</td><td class=\"tgt\">High risk of complete AVB → Class I pacing</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Escape pacemakers: junctional 40–60 bpm, Purkinje &lt; 40 bpm. BBB hides STEMI: new LBBB/RBBB with strong suspicion of ACS = manage as STEMI.</div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Look for the cause","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">Drugs (most common reversible ICU cause)</div> <div>β-blockers, non-DHP CCB, <b>amiodarone</b>, sotalol, flecainide, propafenone, procainamide, adenosine, <b>digoxin</b>, ivabradine, clonidine, lithium, opioids, phenytoin, SSRI, TCA, carbamazepine, muscle relaxants, <b>propofol</b>, ticagrelor, high-dose steroids, H2 blockers, PPIs, cannabis</div> <div class=\"sub l\">Intrinsic / other</div> <div>Degenerative (commonest overall), MI, cardiac surgery / TAVR, sarcoid / amyloid / haemochromatosis, myocarditis, Lyme, diphtheria, Chagas, toxoplasmosis, connective-tissue disease, neuromuscular disease, radiation. Young patient: <b>cardiac MRI</b> (myocarditis, sarcoid).</div> <div class=\"hl\"><b>Endocarditis + new AVB or BBB = aortic root abscess.</b> Aortic valve IE: continuous ECG + daily 12-lead (abrupt progression).</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Drugs to raise the rate (ACE gives no doses: per CMH formulary)","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">Atropine – first line</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Symptomatic sinus bradycardia, supra-Hisian AVB (narrow escape), transient vagal bradycardia (tracheal suction)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>May <b>worsen high-degree infra-Hisian block</b>; ineffective after heart transplant</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Isoprenaline – second line</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Atropine failure, esp. infra-Hisian block; sotalol toxicity (long QT, torsade)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Avoid in suspected acute ischaemia (pro-arrhythmic, ↑ ischaemia, vasodilation – AHA)</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Poisoning</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Atropine / isoprenaline often fail; TVP inconsistent</li> <li><b>β-blocker:</b> glucagon, high-dose insulin, vasopressors; VA-ECMO if refractory</li> <li><b>CCB:</b> IV calcium + high-dose insulin (all classes become bradycardic + vasodilating at overdose)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Temporary pacing","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Haemodynamically unstable bradyarrhythmia not responding to drugs (also: bridge to recovery – ischaemia, toxicity; post-cardiac-surgery epicardial wires)</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Transcutaneous:</b> immediate, in extremis; painful (sedate/analgese), unreliable capture, not sustained</li> <li><b>Transvenous (reference standard):</b> <b>internal jugular preferred</b> (mobilisation; femoral in emergency); balloon-tip lead to <b>RV apex</b> under fluoroscopy or <b>TTE guidance</b>; intensivists can insert safely</li> <li>Rate <b>10 bpm above intrinsic</b>; capture threshold ideally <b>≤ 1 mA</b>, output <b>2–3 × threshold</b></li> <li>Sensing (VVI): default 2 mV, raise until lost, set at <b>half the threshold</b>; check generator battery daily</li> <li>Prolonged need: active-fixation lead + externalised generator</li> <li>Complications: haematoma, pneumothorax, thrombosis, malposition, VT, <b>RV perforation / tamponade</b>, dislodgement, infection</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Specific settings","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Inferior STEMI:</b> sinus brady (Bezold-Jarisch) and nodal AVB are transient, atropine-responsive, resolve after reperfusion. High-grade AVB in ~3% of STEMI; LAD-related AVB is infra-Hisian and worse</li> <li>Only ~10% of post-MI AVB need a pacemaker: <b>pace if high-grade block persists &gt; 5 days</b> (earlier: anterior STEMI, heart failure); none if it resolves after revascularisation</li> <li><b>Cardiac surgery:</b> temporary pacing ~20% (valve &gt; CABG); permanent if high-grade AVB &gt; 5 days; SND observe up to 6 weeks; transplant SND &gt; 6 weeks</li> <li><b>TAVR:</b> new LBBB ~20%, PPM 10–15%; pre-existing RBBB = strongest risk. Pace for complete/high-degree AVB persisting <b>24–48 h</b> or alternating BBB</li> <li>PA catheter in pre-existing LBBB can cause complete AVB (no routine prophylactic pacing)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Patient with a pacemaker: malfunctions","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Problem</th><th style=\"width:40%\">Causes</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>Failure to pace (no spike)</td><td>Battery depletion, lead fracture/disconnection, oversensing</td><td>Temporary TVP / transcutaneous if compromised; reprogram, replace battery/lead</td></tr> <tr><td>Failure to capture (spike, no QRS)</td><td>Lead dislodgement/fracture, ↑ threshold (MI, fibrosis, electrolytes, acidosis, hypoxia, antiarrhythmics)</td><td>Temporary pacing if compromised; correct reversible causes; magnet for asynchronous pacing</td></tr> <tr><td>Undersensing</td><td>Dislodged lead, sensitivity too low, local fibrosis</td><td>Increase sensitivity, reposition lead (risk of R-on-T)</td></tr> <tr><td>Oversensing</td><td>Myopotentials, electromagnetic interference</td><td>Reduce sensitivity, shield, reprogram</td></tr> <tr><td>Inappropriate fast pacing</td><td>AF/flutter tracking, pacemaker-mediated tachycardia</td><td><b>Magnet</b> over generator acutely; then reprogram</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"8","h":"Bundle branch block in the ICU","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>BBB prevents standard STEMI diagnosis: with strong suspicion of ACS, treat <b>new LBBB or RBBB like STEMI</b> (40–60% of chest pain + LBBB = MI)</li> <li>New LBBB = extensive anterior/anteroseptal injury; new RBBB = proximal LAD; both worse prognosis</li> <li>RBBB causes: PE / acute cor pulmonale, pulmonary hypertension, Brugada, ARVC, rate-related, septal ablation</li> <li>Identify conduction delay even without bradycardia: may qualify for CRT</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Pacing-induced dysfunction & CRT","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>RV pacing burden &gt; 20–40% → pacing cardiomyopathy in 12–20% (septal flash, functional MR, ↓ CO)</li> <li>CRT (sinus rhythm, symptomatic HF on optimal therapy, <b>LVEF ≤ 35%</b>): LBBB QRS &gt; 150 ms <b>Class I</b>; LBBB 130–149 or non-LBBB &gt; 150 IIa; non-LBBB 130–149 IIb</li> <li><b>QRS &lt; 130 ms: CRT harmful</b> (III); aim BiV pacing ≥ 90% (AV-node ablation if needed)</li> </ul>"}]},"06a-aki-diagnosis":{"slogan":"Creatinine and urine output are not kidney-specific – always read them in clinical context","legend":"SCr = serum creatinine · UO = urine output","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Acute Kidney Injury Part I: Definition and diagnosis; Part II: Epidemiology; Part III: Pathophysiology; Part IV: Diagnostic work up (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Diagnose & stage AKI (KDIGO 2012)","html":"<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr;gap:1.4mm\"> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">SCr ↑ ≥ 0.3 mg/dL (≥ 26.5 µmol/L) within 48 h</div></div> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">SCr ≥ 1.5 × baseline (known/presumed) within 7 days</div></div> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">UO &lt; 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 h</div></div> </div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:10%\">Stage</th><th style=\"width:48%\">Serum creatinine</th><th>Urine output</th></tr> <tr><td>1</td><td>1.5–1.9 × baseline OR ↑ ≥ 0.3 mg/dL (≥ 26.5 µmol/L)</td><td>&lt; 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6–12 h</td></tr> <tr><td>2</td><td>2.0–2.9 × baseline</td><td>&lt; 0.5 mL/kg/h for ≥ 12 h</td></tr> <tr><td>3</td><td>3 × baseline OR SCr ≥ 4.0 mg/dL (≥ 353.6 µmol/L) OR <b>starting RRT</b> (automatic stage 3)</td><td>&lt; 0.3 mL/kg/h for ≥ 24 h OR anuria ≥ 12 h</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Stage = worst of SCr or UO criterion. Oliguria &gt; 12 h carries diagnostic and prognostic weight even without a creatinine rise. Unknown baseline: no agreed method (back-calculation from eGFR 75 mL/min/1.73 m² can over- or under-call AKI).</div>"},{"n":"1B","h":"Act as soon as AKI is detected","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Restore haemodynamics and correct hypovolaemia; avoid hypotension (MAP &lt; 65 mmHg) and fluid overload</li> <li>Stop / avoid nephrotoxins; review every drug dose (renally cleared)</li> <li>Treat the cause: sepsis (early appropriate antibiotics, source control), obstruction, abdominal compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis</li> <li>Hourly UO (ideal weight), daily SCr, K⁺, acid–base, fluid balance; avoid hyperglycaemia</li> <li>Screen for absolute RRT indications (refractory K⁺, diuretic-resistant pulmonary oedema, dialysable poison) → RRT page</li> </ul>"},{"n":"1C","h":"Kidney biomarkers (adjuncts)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>TIMP-2 × IGFBP7</b> (tubular stress): &gt; 0.3 high risk, &gt; 2 very high risk of stage 2–3 AKI within 12 h</li> <li><b>NGAL:</b> early ATN; separates pre-renal from ATN; urine NGAL &gt; 220 µg/g predicts non-response to terlipressin</li> <li><b>penKid:</b> real-time GFR marker, predicts stage 2–3 and RRT · <b>CCL-14:</b> persistent AKI, may support RRT decision</li> <li>IL-18 (peaks 12–18 h), L-FABP (peaks 6 h after cardiac surgery), KIM-1: less established</li> <li>E-alerts alone suffer \"alert fatigue\": embed in care bundles</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Creatinine pitfalls","html":"<ul class=\"wk\"> <li><b>False rise:</b> cimetidine, trimethoprim (block secretion); creatine / cooked meat; assay interference (5-fluorocytosine, cefoxitin, bilirubin); creatinine buffer in drugs (dexamethasone, azasetron) = pseudo-AKI</li> <li><b>Late / missed:</b> low production (muscle wasting, liver disease, <b>sepsis</b>); <b>fluid resuscitation/overload</b> dilutes SCr; pregnancy (↑ GFR)</li> <li><b>CKD:</b> high baseline → over-diagnosis (0.3 mg/dL may be daily variation); SCr 3.9 + 0.3 = \"stage 3\"</li> <li>SCr lags hours behind GFR changes and says nothing about cause</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Urine output pitfalls","html":"<ul class=\"wk\"> <li><b>Obesity:</b> use <b>ideal body weight</b> for mL/kg/h (actual weight over-diagnoses AKI)</li> <li><b>Transient ADH release</b> (post-op, pain, nausea) + salt/volume depletion → physiological oliguria</li> <li>With normal kidneys UO can fall to ~500 mL/day (0.28 mL/kg/h at 70 kg)</li> <li>Some experts favour 12 h or &lt; 0.3 mL/kg/h for better specificity</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Add-on functional tests</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Cystatin C:</b> shorter half-life, earlier steady state</li> <li><b>Furosemide stress test</b> (euvolaemic): <b>1.0 mg/kg IV</b> (1.5 mg/kg if furosemide in last 7 days); <b>&lt; 200 mL in 2 h</b> in stage 1–2 = high risk of stage 3 and RRT</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Find the cause: minimum work-up = serum biochemistry + urine dipstick + renal ultrasound","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Test</th><th>What to look for / when (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Urine dipstick (all AKI)</td><td>Haematuria + proteinuria → glomerulonephritis; pyuria + nitrites → pyelonephritis; (eosinophiluria) → interstitial nephritis</td></tr> <tr><td>Renal ultrasound (1st line)</td><td><b>Obstruction (~10% of cases, easily treated – never miss)</b>, chronic structural disease. Doppler resistive index / contrast US estimate renal perfusion</td></tr> <tr><td>Urine microscopy</td><td>Fresh non-catheter sample, skilled operator: red-cell casts / dysmorphic RBC = glomerular; oxalate crystals = ethylene glycol; urate crystals = tumour lysis</td></tr> <tr><td>Urine electrolytes</td><td>Low FENa / FEurate / FEurea + bland sediment support functional AKI, but a single value has limited value (low FENa also in early sepsis)</td></tr> <tr><td>Intra-abdominal pressure</td><td>Serial IAP if compartment syndrome suspected; <b>sustained &gt; 20 mmHg</b> = significant contributor</td></tr> <tr><td>Autoimmune screen</td><td>ANCA, ANA, anti-GBM, C3/C4 – <b>mandatory</b> with haemolysis/thrombocytopenia, pulmonary–renal syndrome/haemoptysis, or features of vasculitis/CTD (polyarthritis, purpura, ulcers, alopecia, photosensitivity)</td></tr> <tr><td>Context tests</td><td>LDH + fragmentocytes (TTP/HUS); CK + myoglobin (rhabdomyolysis); NT-proBNP + troponin (cardio-renal); serum/urine protein electrophoresis (myeloma)</td></tr> <tr><td>Renal biopsy</td><td>Rare; consider if parenchymal/glomerular disease suspected, persistent loss of function, <b>proteinuria &gt; 3 g/day</b>, systemic disease (half show a non-ATN diagnosis that changes treatment)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"5","h":"Risk factors & common ICU causes","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <div><div class=\"sub\">Patient</div><ul class=\"dt\"><li>Older age, male, ethnicity</li><li>Hypertension, diabetes, <b>CKD/proteinuria</b>, heart failure, CAD/PAD, COPD, obesity, malignancy, liver failure, hypoalbuminaemia</li></ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Acute (modifiable)</div><ul class=\"dt\"><li><b>Sepsis</b> (~50% of severe ICU AKI)</li><li>Hypovolaemia AND fluid overload (renal capsule oedema)</li><li>Shock, major/cardiac/abdominal surgery, trauma, burns, rhabdomyolysis, abdominal compartment syndrome, ventilation, anaemia, COVID-19</li></ul></div> </div> <div class=\"hl\"><b>Nephrotoxins</b> (drugs ≈ 30% of hospital AKI; 19% of RRT-AKI): aminoglycosides, penicillins, glycopeptides, antifungals, antivirals, PPIs, NSAIDs, immunosuppressants, chemotherapy, myoglobin/haem, uric acid → <b>nephrotoxin stewardship</b>. Contrast risk overestimated: keep euvolaemic, <b>don't withhold a needed contrast scan</b>.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Course, recovery & follow-up","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Rapid reversal</b>: recovery within 48 h (best outcome) · <b>Recovery</b>: no KDIGO criteria within 7 days</li> <li><b>AKD</b>: AKI persisting at day 7 (~40% of stage 2–3) · <b>CKD</b>: AKD still present at day 90</li> <li>Recovered patients can \"slip back\" (relapse ↑ 1-year mortality): <b>keep monitoring</b></li> <li>Promote recovery: avoid nephrotoxins and hyperglycaemia, avoid hypoperfusion, match RRT modality to haemodynamics and fluid status</li> <li>Non-recovery risk: CKD, age, comorbidity, severity and duration of AKI</li> <li>Assess recovery with <b>measured urinary CrCl</b> (longer collections more reliable) rather than eGFR</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Contrast-associated AKI","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Risk smaller than previously thought; <b>CKD</b> is the main risk factor</li> <li>At-risk patient: weigh toxicity against diagnostic gain and treatment consequences; consider alternative imaging</li> <li>Before contrast: <b>correct dehydration, hypovolaemia, hypotension</b>; stop other nephrotoxins</li> <li>KDIGO suggests <b>1 mL/kg/h for 12 h before and 12 h after</b>; no one-size protocol (over-hydration harms)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Sepsis & surgery AKI","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Sepsis-AKI</b> can occur with normal or high renal blood flow: inflammation, microcirculatory and tubular dysfunction, medullary hypoxia</li> <li>Key prevention: <b>optimise haemodynamics, early appropriate antibiotics, avoid nephrotoxins</b></li> <li><b>Surgery:</b> pre-existing CKD is the strongest risk factor; intra-op <b>MAP &lt; 65 mmHg</b> predicts AKI with rising duration and depth</li> <li>Pure haemodynamic dysfunction should reverse when circulation is corrected</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Why it matters","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Up to 50% of ICU patients; 10–15% of them need RRT</li> <li>↑ duration of ventilation, ICU/hospital stay, 90-day readmission, long-term care</li> <li>1-year mortality: 10% with sustained recovery within a week vs 58% with relapse and no recovery</li> <li>Stage 2–3: ~40% not fully recovered at discharge → arrange renal follow-up</li> </ul>"}]},"06b-aki-prevention-treatment":{"slogan":"No drug treats AKI – protect perfusion, stop nephrotoxins, treat K⁺, acid and fluid","legend":"IAP = intra-abdominal pressure","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Acute Kidney Injury Part V: Prevention and Conservative Treatment; Part VI: Long term outcome (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"KDIGO care bundle by risk / stage","html":"<div class=\"mini-tbl\" style=\"grid-template-columns:1.25fr 1fr 1fr 1fr\"> <div><b>High risk + every stage</b><br/>Stop all nephrotoxins where possible · ensure volume status and perfusion pressure · consider <b>functional haemodynamic monitoring</b> · monitor SCr and UO · avoid hyperglycaemia · consider alternatives to radiocontrast</div> <div><b>Stage 1</b><br/>Non-invasive diagnostic work-up; consider invasive work-up</div> <div><b>Stage 2</b><br/>Check / adjust drug dosing · consider RRT · consider ICU admission</div> <div><b>Stage 3</b><br/>Avoid subclavian catheters if possible (preserve future fistula)</div> </div> <div class=\"note\">Post-operative KDIGO bundle reduced AKI frequency and severity after cardiac and major non-cardiac surgery (PrevAKI) – mainly via haemodynamic optimisation and fewer nephrotoxins. Even short hypotensive episodes promote AKI.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Fluids","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Controlled, closely monitored</b> resuscitation when volume-depleted (absolute or vasodilatory)</li> <li>Sepsis: SSC ≥ 30 mL/kg initially (low-quality evidence); restrictive vs liberal no different (CLASSIC, CLOVERS); <b>don't restrict peri-operatively</b> in high-risk abdominal surgery (more AKI)</li> <li><b>Crystalloids first-line.</b> Saline or balanced both acceptable (SMART: less MAKE30 with balanced, esp. sepsis, high volume; BaSICS: balanced worse in TBI)</li> <li>Fluid overload worsens survival and renal perfusion (capsule oedema, venous congestion); cardiac failure may be overloaded from the start</li> <li>Albumin 20% added to crystalloid: no outcome benefit in sepsis</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>No hydroxyethyl starch</b> (EMA: not in sepsis, burns, critical illness)</li> <li>Albumin: no proven benefit, costly; worse in TBI (SAFE)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Perfusion pressure & vasoactives","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"big\">MAP 65–70</div><div class=\"mid\">individualise; 80–85 in chronic hypertension (less RRT, more AF – SEPSISPAM) · 60–65 acceptable in vasodilatory shock, age &gt; 65 (OVATION, 65 trial)</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Also mind <b>venous pressure (CVP)</b> and <b>IAP</b>: abdominal perfusion pressure = MAP – IAP</li> <li><b>Noradrenaline first-line</b>; raising MAP below the autoregulatory threshold increases renal blood flow (fear of renal vasoconstriction unfounded)</li> <li>Vasopressin (0.02–0.04 U/min) alternative; terlipressin for <b>hepatorenal syndrome</b>; angiotensin II spares noradrenaline</li> <li>Adrenaline not 1st line (lactate, glucose, arrhythmia, splanchnic)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>No dopamine</b> (incl. \"renal-dose\"), no continuous phenylephrine, <b>no fenoldopam</b> or <b>levosimendan for renal protection</b></li></ul>"},{"n":"3B","h":"Vasoactive drugs and the kidney (ACE gives no infusion doses except vasopressin: dose per CMH formulary)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:14%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Renal effect</th><th style=\"width:20%\">Systemic effect</th><th>ACE position</th></tr> <tr><td>Noradrenaline</td><td>↑ RBF, ↑ UO (when restoring MAP)</td><td>↑ MAP</td><td class=\"tgt\">First-line vasopressor</td></tr> <tr><td>Vasopressin</td><td>↑ RBF, ↑ UO</td><td>↑ MAP</td><td>Low dose 0.02–0.04 U/min alternative; watch mesenteric/coronary flow, ↓ CO</td></tr> <tr><td>Terlipressin</td><td>Reverses hepatorenal syndrome</td><td>↑ MAP, ↓ CO</td><td>Long-acting (t½ 6 h); HRS. Urine NGAL &gt; 220 µg/g predicts non-response</td></tr> <tr><td>Angiotensin II</td><td>Efferent &gt; afferent constriction (keeps GFR)</td><td>↑ MAP</td><td>Reduces noradrenaline need; no proven renal benefit</td></tr> <tr><td>Adrenaline</td><td>Uncertain</td><td>↑ MAP, CO, HR</td><td>Not first-line (metabolic, arrhythmic, splanchnic effects)</td></tr> <tr><td>Phenylephrine</td><td>Uncertain</td><td>↑ CO only if preload-dependent</td><td class=\"av\">No continuous infusion</td></tr> <tr><td>Dopamine / fenoldopam</td><td>Renal vasodilation – no protection</td><td>Fenoldopam ↓ MAP</td><td class=\"av\">Not recommended (incl. \"renal-dose\" dopamine)</td></tr> <tr><td>Dobutamine · milrinone · levosimendan</td><td>No effect on AKI or RRT need</td><td>↑ CO (inodilators)</td><td>Use for low cardiac output, not for kidney protection</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Established AKI – the 3 immediate questions","html":"<div class=\"split3\"> <div><div class=\"sub\">Volume overload?</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Diuretic trial</b>; higher-than-usual doses may be needed (resistance often = no response to furosemide 100–250 mg/day)</li> <li>Diuretics only to <b>treat fluid overload</b> – they do not prevent or treat AKI</li> <li>Relieve symptoms with high-flow O₂; refractory pulmonary oedema → <b>CPAP/NIV</b> while arranging RRT</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Hyperkalaemia? <span style=\"font-size:6.3pt;text-transform:none\">(drug doses per CMH formulary)</span></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ECG: peaked T, flat P, long PR, wide QRS → sine wave → VF/asystole; BBB, AV block</li> <li><b>Calcium</b> (gluconate or chloride) if ECG changes – acts in minutes, short-lived</li> <li><b>Insulin + glucose</b>: K⁺ ↓ 0.5–1.5 mmol/L in 15 min (max ~60 min) – monitor glucose</li> <li>β2-agonist (avoid in active coronary disease); bicarbonate (volume load); binders (SPS, sodium zirconium cyclosilicate)</li> <li>Shifts are temporary → <b>remove K⁺</b> (diuresis or RRT); stop shifting agents during RRT (rebound)</li> <li>K⁺ &lt; 6.5 + normal ECG: low-K diet, stop K sources and potentiating drugs; correct acidosis, ↓Na, ↓Ca</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Metabolic acidosis?</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Severe (<b>pH &lt; 7.2</b> or <b>HCO₃⁻ 10–15 mmol/L</b>): sodium bicarbonate IV or oral</li> <li>4.2% bicarbonate to pH &gt; 7.30 (BICAR-ICU, BICARICU-2): no mortality change but <b>less RRT in AKI stage 2–3</b></li> <li>Watch volume expansion and hypernatraemia</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub\">Uraemia</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Uraemic pericarditis</b> (rub, pleuritic pain) = start RRT unless tamponade (drain)</li> <li>Encephalopathy: confusion, asterixis, myoclonus</li> </ul></div> </div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Drugs: what works / doesn't","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Statins to prevent cardiac-surgery AKI (do not start)</li> <li>Loop diuretics for AKI prevention/treatment</li> <li>Routine contrast hydration, bicarbonate, NAC (no benefit vs none)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Contrast: base fluids on volume status</li> <li>Remote ischaemic preconditioning: may consider in high risk (safe)</li> <li>Propofol, dexmedetomidine: possible renoprotection, unproven</li> <li>Treat hyperglycaemia (nephrotoxic)</li> <li>Alkaline phosphatase (septic AKI): investigational only</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"General supportive care","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Treat the cause; <b>treat sepsis aggressively</b></li> <li>Blood + protein on dipstick: look for intrinsic renal disease</li> <li>Nutrition: adequate calories, limit nitrogenous waste and K⁺ intake</li> <li>Stress-ulcer prophylaxis with PPI</li> <li>Adjust all renally cleared drugs</li> <li>Abdominal compartment syndrome: measure IAP, restore abdominal perfusion pressure</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"After AKI: follow-up","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Follow <b>all</b> AKI survivors for CKD, whatever the recovery</li> <li><b>KAMPS</b>: Kidney function, Advocacy, Medication review, blood Pressure, Sick-day rules</li> <li>Recheck renal function at <b>3, 6 and 12 months</b>; proteinuria = CKD marker</li> <li>RRT survivors: up to 30% dialysis-dependent at day 30; up to 35% of these recover by 1 year</li> <li>Outcome driven by recovery in first 3 months</li> <li>Quality of life lower but acceptable; 71% would accept RRT again</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Who will progress to stage 3 / need RRT?","html":"<ul class=\"ck cols\" style=\"grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr\"> <li><b>Furosemide stress test</b> (euvolaemic, stage 1–2): 1.0 mg/kg IV (1.5 mg/kg if furosemide in last 7 days); <b>&lt; 200 mL in 2 h</b> = high risk of stage 3 and RRT</li> <li><b>Urinary CCL-14</b>: predicts persistent stage 3 AKI &gt; 72 h (AUC 0.83); FST + CCL-14 together best identify patients who will develop an absolute RRT indication</li> <li>Even FST-positive patients often never need RRT (13.6% in the FST trial) → use with the delayed-start strategy</li> <li>AI/ML prediction models (AUC 0.73–0.96) not yet validated for bedside use</li> </ul>"}]},"07a-rrt-indications-access":{"slogan":"No absolute indication? Monitor closely and wait – but don't postpone excessively","legend":"RRT = renal replacement therapy · IHD = intermittent haemodialysis · CRRT = continuous RRT","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Renal Replacement Therapy Part I & Part II (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Severe AKI: is there an ABSOLUTE (life-threatening) indication?","html":"<div class=\"split3\"> <div><div class=\"sub red\">Refractory hyperkalaemia</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Severe, life-threatening K⁺ <b>despite medical treatment</b> (bicarbonate, glucose–insulin, β2-agonist), especially with anuria</li><li>No fixed threshold (depends on rate of rise, acidosis, CKD); RRT <b>rarely urgent if K⁺ &lt; 6.0 mmol/L</b></li></ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub red\">Diuretic-resistant pulmonary oedema</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Severe respiratory deterioration (oedema with <b>hypoxaemia</b>) + anuria <b>despite diuretics</b> → start RRT for ultrafiltration</li><li>Preserved urine output: try diuretics alone</li></ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub red\">Dialysable poisoning</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Urgent RRT in severe poisoning: <b>metformin, lithium, ethylene glycol</b></li><li>Overt uraemic complications (pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding) are rare in AKI</li></ul></div> </div> <div class=\"hl\"><b>Two undisputed criteria:</b> severe hyperkalaemia (with anuria) and severe pulmonary oedema from diuretic-resistant fluid overload.</div>"},{"n":"2A","h":"No absolute indication: delayed strategy","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Monitor kidney function closely; <b>early RRT gives no survival benefit</b> (AKIKI, IDEAL-ICU, STARRT-AKI)</li> <li>With a delayed strategy, <b>~50% of stage 3 AKI patients never need RRT</b> and recover faster; less RRT dependence at day 90</li> <li><b>Start when</b> (potential indications, STARRT-AKI delayed-arm triggers): <b>pH &lt; 7.20</b> (intractable acidosis &lt; 7.15 often quoted), <b>HCO₃⁻ &lt; 12 mmol/L</b>, <b>K⁺ &gt; 6 mmol/L</b>, hypoxaemic respiratory failure from fluid overload, AKI persisting at 72 h</li> <li><b>Urea &gt; 40–50 mmol/L</b> without recovery (no return of diuresis) → consider RRT</li> <li><b>Don't wait too long:</b> a very-delayed strategy (AKIKI-2) was independently linked to higher day-60 mortality</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2B","h":"NOT an indication on their own","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>Septic shock</b> per se</li> <li><b>Oliguria/anuria duration</b> alone in the first 48 h of severe AKI</li> <li><b>Biomarkers</b> (plasma/urine): predict RRT need no better than creatinine</li> <li>Fluid overload in a <b>diuretic-responsive</b> patient (don't expose to RRT risks)</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Fluid accumulation before RRT is linked to higher mortality: keep fluid balance tight while waiting. RRT = bridge, not cure; does not replace EPO, vitamin D activation or gluconeogenesis.</div>"},{"n":"2C","h":"Timing evidence behind the delayed strategy","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:14%\">Trial</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Patients / early arm</th><th>Result → practice</th></tr> <tr><td>AKIKI · IDEAL-ICU</td><td>Mixed ICU, KDIGO 3 / RIFLE-F; immediate vs delayed</td><td>No mortality difference (ELAIN's early benefit: mostly fluid-overloaded cardiac surgery); more RRT-free days with delay; 38–49% never needed RRT; earlier renal recovery with delay (AKIKI)</td></tr> <tr><td>STARRT-AKI</td><td>2927 (mainly septic), stage 2–3 AKI; early = within 12 h</td><td>Same day-90 mortality; RRT dependence at day 90 6% (delayed) vs 10% (early) → <b>prefer delayed strategy</b></td></tr> <tr><td>AKIKI-2</td><td>Stage 3 AKI; delayed (urea &gt; 40 mmol/L or oliguria &gt; 72 h) vs very-delayed</td><td>No difference in RRT-free days, but very-delayed independently linked to higher day-60 mortality → <b>don't postpone excessively</b></td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"3","h":"Choose the modality (no modality improves survival)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:13%\">Modality</th><th style=\"width:27%\">Use it when</th><th style=\"width:27%\">Settings (ACE)</th><th>Watch-outs</th></tr> <tr><td>IHD</td><td class=\"tgt\">Dialysable poisoning (lithium, ethylene glycol), extreme life-threatening K⁺ / metabolic derangement (e.g. K⁺ with conduction block, bradycardia), centres without CRRT experience</td><td>Blood 200–400 mL/min, dialysate 500–800 mL/min; fast, cheap, allows mobilisation &amp; procedures</td><td>Hypotension (more than CRRT); rapid urea/K⁺/acid–base shifts with rebound between sessions; needs good access (box 7)</td></tr> <tr><td>CRRT</td><td class=\"tgt\">All other situations (usually preferred), esp. haemodynamic instability</td><td>24 h/day; better haemodynamic tolerance, no rapid osmolar shifts, easier fluid removal, allows full nutrition</td><td>Possibly better renal recovery (observational)</td></tr> <tr><td>CVVHD</td><td>Poor access / hypovolaemia; regional citrate</td><td>Blood 80–150 mL/min; clearance independent of blood flow if &gt; 40–50 mL/min; dose ≈ dialysate + UF flow</td><td>Lower blood flow = longer filter life, less citrate needed</td></tr> <tr><td>CVVH</td><td>Good access; middle-molecule clearance</td><td>Blood 200–300 mL/min; dose ≈ effluent rate; replacement ⅓ pre- / ⅔ post-filter</td><td><b>Filtration fraction &lt; 20–25%</b> (clotting)</td></tr> <tr><td>PIRRT / SLED</td><td>Hybrid option</td><td>HD-based, 8–12 h sessions</td><td>No survival benefit shown</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Principles at the bedside","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Diffusion (dialysis):</b> down a concentration gradient; dialysate runs <b>counter-current</b>. IHD limited by blood flow, CVVHD by dialysate flow (25–50 mL/min) → in CVVHD <b>effluent flow ≈ small-solute clearance</b></li> <li><b>Convection (filtration):</b> solute moves with water; pore cut-off ~<b>30 kDa</b>; needs replacement fluid</li> <li><b>Pre-dilution</b> lowers efficiency but prevents filter clotting; <b>post-dilution</b> more efficient</li> <li>Choice of CVVH / CVVHD / CVVHDF: equivalent outcomes; choose by device and team experience</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Prevent IHD hypotension","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Dialysate <b>Na⁺ &gt; 145 mmol/L</b></li> <li>Low initial blood flow <b>&lt; 250 mL/min</b></li> <li>Biofeedback machines (UF adjusted to relative blood volume)</li> <li>Avoid vasodilating drugs</li> <li>Longer sessions; more frequent sessions</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">IHD needs blood flow 200–400 mL/min, so optimum access is essential.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Circuit safety checks","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Air embolism</b> is the most feared complication: bubble trap + bubble detector + <b>safety clamp</b> on return line</li> <li><b>Blood leak detector</b> on effluent line: filter membrane rupture</li> <li>Pressures monitored: access (negative), pre-filter, return → TMP and filter pressure drop</li> <li>Catheter dysfunction is a leading cause of alarms and downtime (lost dose)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Vascular access: double-lumen dialysis catheter","html":"<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:1.2mm\"> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">1st: Right IJV</div><div class=\"big\">15 cm</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">2nd: R or L femoral</div><div class=\"big\">25 cm</div></div> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\">3rd: Left IJV</div><div class=\"big\">20 cm</div></div> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Last: subclavian (dominant side)</div><div class=\"big\">R 15 · L 20</div></div> </div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Wide bore <b>12–16 Fr</b> (1 Fr = 0.33 mm); tip in high-flow zone (<b>SVC–RA junction</b>, or IVC for femoral)</li> <li>Femoral catheter must be <b>≥ 25 cm</b> (too short = recirculation, poor dose)</li> <li><b>BMI &lt; 24:</b> femoral may suit better (jugular dressing seal poor) · <b>BMI &gt; 28:</b> prefer jugular (femoral infection risk)</li> <li><b>Ultrasound-guided</b> insertion; <b>CXR to confirm tip</b> before use for SVC-territory lines</li> <li>Step-tip (\"shotgun\"), no side holes, 2–3 cm between inflow and outflow tips; uncoated polyurethane/silicone</li> <li>Guidewire exchange possible (no more infection, but more dysfunction); 3rd lumen can be used for drugs or post-filter calcium with citrate</li> <li>Locks: 4% citrate no better than heparin (VERROUREA); only low-concentration (4%) citrate is safe</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Access do-nots","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>Subclavian</b>: venous stenosis, threatens future AV fistula</li> <li><b>Left IJV</b>: more catheter dysfunction</li> <li><b>Line reversal</b> (causes recirculation)</li> <li>Short femoral catheter (&lt; 25 cm)</li> <li>Leaving it in: <b>remove as soon as possible</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Circuit alarms</div> <ul class=\"dt\"> <li>Very negative access pressure: kinked/clotted line, hypovolaemia, tip against vessel wall</li> <li>Rising pre-filter pressure: filter clotting</li> <li>Isolated ↑ return pressure: distal clotting</li> </ul>"}]},"07b-rrt-prescription-anticoag":{"slogan":"Prescribe 25–30 to deliver 20–25 mL/kg/h · Citrate first · Check iCa, Mg, PO₄ daily","legend":"FF = filtration fraction · RCA = regional citrate anticoagulation · UF-NET = net ultrafiltration","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Renal Replacement Therapy Part III, IV, V & VI (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Write the CRRT prescription","html":"<div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"big\">Prescribe 25–30 mL/kg/h</div><div class=\"mid\">effluent → to DELIVER 20–25 mL/kg/h (AKI)</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Prescribe: <b>dose</b>, <b>pre:post-dilution ratio</b> (CVVH), <b>blood flow</b> (matched to modality and dose), <b>UF<sub>NET</sub></b></li> <li>Higher doses: no benefit; lose phosphate, antibiotics, heat. <b>High-volume HF not recommended</b> outside research</li> <li>Delivered dose falls with interruptions, pre-dilution, membrane protein cake</li> <li>Pre-dilution reduces dose: <b>add 10–15% to substitution flow</b> when part is given pre-filter</li> <li><b>FF = (Q<sub>pre</sub> + Q<sub>post</sub> + Q<sub>net</sub>) / (Q<sub>b</sub>×(1–Hct) + Q<sub>pre</sub>)</b> — keep <b>&lt; 25%</b> with heparin (<b>&lt; 30%</b> with efficient citrate); lower it with pre-dilution and higher blood flow</li> <li>Access pressure <b>&lt; –100 mmHg</b>: real catheter flow is below the set flow</li> <li>Filters: high-flux (K<sub>UF</sub> &gt; 25 mL/h/mmHg/m²), 1.2–2 m²; cuprophan no longer recommended</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Set net ultrafiltration (UFNET)","html":"<div class=\"mini-tbl\" style=\"grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr)\"> <div><b>0 mL/h</b> positive balance in anuria</div> <div><b>50–100 mL/h</b> ≈ neutral balance (ventilated)</div> <div><b>&gt; 100 mL/h</b> negative balance</div> </div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Set by fluid overload (esp. ARF / ARDS) and haemodynamics (lactate, vasopressor dose, preload dependence, low CO)</li> <li>Respiratory failure from fluid overload: <b>&gt; 200 mL/h for a few hours</b></li> <li>Lowest mortality with moderate <b>1.0–1.75 mL/kg/h</b>; both &lt; 1.0 and &gt; 1.75 mL/kg/h linked to more deaths</li> <li>On vasopressors: moderate UF<sub>NET</sub> (≈ <b>100 mL/h, ~1.4 mL/kg/h</b>) guided by functional haemodynamic monitoring is feasible (GO NEUTRAL)</li> <li><b>Delay UF<sub>NET</sub></b> in overt hypovolaemia (bleeding, early shock resuscitation); reassess and re-adjust systematically</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Anticoagulate the circuit","html":"<div class=\"split3\"> <div><div class=\"sub\">1st line: Regional citrate (KDIGO)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Longer filter life, fewer bleeds and transfusions vs heparin; same mortality</li> <li>Citrate pre-filter chelates Ca²⁺; <b>CaCl₂ infusion</b> replaces effluent Ca loss</li> <li><b>Post-filter iCa 0.20–0.35 mmol/L</b> (adjust citrate : blood-flow ratio)</li> <li><b>Systemic iCa 1.1–1.2 mmol/L</b> (adjust Ca : effluent ratio)</li> <li>Follow ONE strict local protocol; low blood flow (CVVHD) = less citrate</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Citrate problems</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Overload:</b> metabolic alkalosis (± hypernatraemia) – easily managed</li> <li><b>Accumulation</b> (shock, mitochondrial block, severe liver failure): metabolic acidosis, low iCa, rising Ca needs, <b>tCa/iCa &gt; 2.5</b> → ↑ citrate clearance / ↓ citrate dose; <b>stop RCA</b> if severe or uncontrolled</li> <li><b>Hypomagnesaemia:</b> check Mg daily, supplement</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Systemic heparin (citrate contraindicated)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>UFH bolus (dose per CMH formulary) then <b>800–1200 IU/h</b></li> <li>Target <b>aPTT 1.5–2 × baseline</b> or <b>anti-Xa 0.3–0.6 IU/mL</b> (lower if bleeding risk)</li> <li>Risks: bleeding, HIT; real filter life often &lt; 20–30 h. LMWH unpredictable in AKI</li> <li><b>HIT:</b> argatroban or danaparoid (specific protocol; dose per CMH formulary)</li> </ul></div> </div> <div class=\"hl\"><b>No anticoagulation</b> (all contraindicated): citrate is superior to none; minimise haemoconcentration (FF &lt; 25%, pre-dilution, high blood flow), good catheter, less blood–air contact, larger/heparin-coated (AN69ST) filters, saline flushes, <b>answer alarms fast</b>. Early clotting ≠ automatically more anticoagulant – check access and FF first.</div>"},{"n":"3B","h":"Daily CRRT checklist","html":"<ul class=\"ck cols\" style=\"grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr\"> <li><b>Delivered vs prescribed dose</b> (interruptions, downtime)</li> <li><b>FF</b> on screen &lt; 25% (&lt; 30% citrate)</li> <li><b>Filter life</b>, pre-filter / return / access pressures</li> <li><b>Post-filter iCa</b> 0.20–0.35 and <b>systemic iCa</b> 1.1–1.2 mmol/L (RCA)</li> <li><b>tCa/iCa</b> ratio (&gt; 2.5 = accumulation), pH / HCO₃⁻</li> <li><b>K⁺, PO₄, Mg, Na⁺</b> at least daily; adjust fluid content</li> <li><b>aPTT / anti-Xa</b> and platelets (heparin)</li> <li><b>Fluid balance</b> vs UF<sub>NET</sub> target; preload dependence if unstable</li> <li><b>Temperature</b>; catheter site; drug levels (TDM); urine output for weaning</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Complications to prevent & manage","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Problem</th><th>Prevention / action (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Haemodynamic instability (HIRRT)</td><td>Causes: UF<sub>NET</sub> &gt; refilling, osmotic shifts, K⁺/Ca²⁺ arrhythmias, stunning, vasopressor clearance, heat transfer, hypocalcaemia (RCA), bio-incompatibility. <b>Prefer CRRT</b> in circulatory failure, standard blood flow and effluent 20–25 mL/kg/h, delay UF<sub>NET</sub> in hypovolaemia, neutral/mild hypothermia. <b>IHD:</b> low blood/dialysate flow, <b>UF<sub>NET</sub> &lt; 500 mL/h</b>, mild hypothermia, high Na conductivity, daily sessions</td></tr> <tr><td>Dialysis disequilibrium</td><td>Rapid urea/Na correction → brain oedema (headache to seizures, coma). At risk: stroke, TBI, cardiac arrest, hyponatraemia, high urea CKD. Use <b>CRRT (CVVH) or low-dose, short first IHD</b>, monitor urea</td></tr> <tr><td>Electrolytes</td><td>↓K⁺, ↓PO₄ (<b>&lt; 0.35 mmol/L</b>: weakness, ventilation), ↓Mg (RCA). Check <b>at least daily</b>; add K/PO₄ to fluids. Dysnatraemia: adjust fluid Na (130–160) to avoid rapid correction</td></tr> <tr><td>Citrate metabolic</td><td>Hypocalcaemia, metabolic alkalosis, hypernatraemia; accumulation in 4–17% (linked more to severe shock than to liver dysfunction alone) → protocolised delivery, monitor iCa, tCa/iCa, acid–base</td></tr> <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>Hypothermia in up to 50% (masks fever, coagulopathy): line warmers, blankets. Fever &gt; 37.5 °C on CRRT: think infection first, then check heater</td></tr> <tr><td>Thrombocytopenia</td><td>On heparin with filter clotting: suspect <b>HIT</b> (4Ts intermediate/high); exclude sepsis, DIC, drugs</td></tr> <tr><td>Nutrient loss</td><td>Protein <b>1.3–1.5 g/kg/d (IHD)</b>, <b>1.5–1.7 g/kg/d (CRRT)</b>; trophic EN within 3 days, full (80–100% of measured EE) by day 3–7; replace Se, Zn, Cu, thiamine, folate; watch euglycaemic ketoacidosis with glucose-free fluids</td></tr> <tr><td>Access / circuit</td><td>US-guided insertion, remove early (CRBSI); air embolism and balance errors prevented by alarms but <b>examine circuit regularly</b>; vasodilatory shock from membrane → change filter material</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"5","h":"Drug dosing on RRT","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Loading dose unchanged</b> (often higher: ↑ Vd); first 48 h of septic shock: full loading + maintenance</li> <li>Maintenance: adapt to RRT intensity, urine output, <b>TDM</b>. Time-dependent: change dose/mode; concentration-dependent: change interval</li> <li>Effluent rate ≈ CrCl (~30–40 mL/min) as a rough guide</li> <li>Removal matters if &gt; 25% of total clearance; little for high Vd / protein-bound drugs</li> <li>Fluconazole, colistin (tubular reabsorption): RRT removal can exceed normal kidney clearance</li> <li>Convection removes large drugs (vancomycin, teicoplanin, colistin) better; filter sequestration: polymyxin, aminoglycosides</li> <li>Titrate sedatives and vasoactives to effect</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"When to stop RRT","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Cause of AKI resolved? Renal recovery? Fluid overload resolved?</li> <li><b>Urine output</b> is the key test; persisting oliguria → postpone</li> <li>UO <b>&gt; 400 mL/day</b> (~30 mL/h): do a urine collection (e.g. 2 h CrCl &gt; 23 mL/min; 24 h urinary creatinine ≥ 5.2 mmol)</li> <li>Best predictors of success: no pre-existing CKD, shorter CRRT, higher UO at cessation</li> <li>Steady CRRT clearance stabilises creatinine after ~48 h, so native CrCl can be measured</li> <li>Diuretics raise UO but don't speed recovery</li> <li>Remove the catheter as soon as possible</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Non-renal indications","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fluid overload (with or without AKI); refractory congestion in heart failure despite optimal diuretics (selected)</li> <li>Dialysable toxins: &lt; 500 Da, low Vd, low protein binding (lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate)</li> <li>Tumour lysis: lower threshold, CRRT preferred</li> <li>With ECMO (integrated circuit: faster start, longer filter life; &gt; 95% of survivors wean off RRT) or ECCO₂R</li> <li>Inborn errors of metabolism (disease-specific)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Not for sepsis \"blood purification\", hyperthermia, or isolated dysnatraemia</li></ul>"}]},"08a-tbi-early-icp":{"slogan":"Prevent secondary brain injury: no hypoxia, no hypotension, normocapnia · Herniation = minutes count","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Traumatic Brain Injury Parts I–III; ESICM Emergency Response (TBI lessons) (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Airway & breathing (never delay for history)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>High-flow O₂ to all</b> TBI patients, whatever the severity</li> <li><b>Intubate</b> if airway reflexes impaired, oxygenation/ventilation inadequate or GCS falling; also if higher GCS but injuries risk hypoxia / hypercapnia / hypotension (chest crush, PTX, ongoing bleeding)</li> <li><b>RSI</b> by experienced staff; low-cardiodepressant induction (midazolam, <b>ketamine is safe</b>, good if unstable) + fentanyl + suxamethonium or rocuronium; <b>vasopressor ready</b>; manual in-line C-spine stabilisation; <b>capnography</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">SpO₂ <b>&gt; 94–95%</b> (&gt; 90% acceptable if poor lung mechanics) · PaCO₂ <b>35–38 mmHg</b> with continuous EtCO₂ · avoid hypercapnia</div></div> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>No routine/early hyperventilation</b> (cerebral ischaemia); lung-protective ventilation once stable; prevent and treat aspiration</li></ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Circulation & fluids","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">SBP <b>≥ 110</b> (elderly / chronic HTN <b>120–130</b>, older &gt; 130) · MAP <b>80–85</b> (up to 90) until ICP known · then <b>CPP 60–70</b></div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>≥ 2 large-bore IV lines. Hypotension → <b>look for extracranial bleeding</b> (isolated TBI rarely causes it, except near-terminal with fixed dilated pupils)</li> <li>SBP &lt; 110 doubles mortality; even <b>one episode</b> worsens outcome</li> <li><b>Norepinephrine first-line</b> if volume fails within minutes or hypotension from sedation/ventilation</li> <li>Active bleeding: SBP ~<b>90</b> until haemorrhage controlled; <b>no prolonged permissive hypotension</b></li> <li>Fluids: <b>0.9% NaCl preferred</b>; aim normovolaemia; use PPV/SVV, CO, SvO₂, lactate, not CVP alone; monitor electrolytes</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Avoid <b>hypotonic / glucose</b> fluids, <b>albumin</b> (SAFE-TBI mortality 24.6 vs 16%), synthetic colloids</li></ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Assess, investigate, image","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>GCS after resuscitation</b>: severe 3–8 · moderate 9–12 · mild 13–15; pupils (size, symmetry, direct/consensual), brainstem reflexes, 4-limb motor (supraorbital pressure / trapezius pinch)</li> <li><b>Deterioration</b> = GCS ↓ ≥ 2, new pupil abnormality, or CT evolution</li> <li>History: GCS motor &amp; pupils at scene, prehospital SBP/SpO₂, mechanism, <b>anticoagulant / antiplatelet + time of last dose</b>, alcohol/drugs, medical cause</li> <li>Labs: FBC, Na/K, glucose, coagulation, group, U&amp;E, LFT, ± CK, troponin, amylase, pregnancy; moderate/severe: <b>ABG + cross-match ≥ 4 units</b></li> <li><b>Trauma CT</b> (head, C-spine, CT angio chest/abdomen/pelvis); assume spine injury until excluded</li> <li><b>Repeat CT within 6 h</b> in moderate/severe TBI; low threshold if coma, tSAH, fracture, antithrombotics; on warfarin/DOAC with mild TBI repeat at 12–24 h</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Coagulation, reversal, TXA","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Coagulopathy on arrival: <b>9×</b> mortality, 30× unfavourable outcome; unstable polytrauma → <b>1:1:1</b> plasma:platelets:RBC without waiting for labs; <b>TEG/ROTEM</b> early (first 72 h)</li> <li><b>VKA + intracranial bleed</b>: reverse with <b>PCC</b> (weight + INR based) + <b>vitamin K</b>; unstable polytrauma: FFP</li> <li><b>Dabigatran</b>: idarucizumab. <b>Xa inhibitors</b>: PCC ± TXA; haematology advice. Clearance ≈ 5 half-lives (dabigatran 12–17 h, rivaroxaban 5–9 h)</li> <li>Antiplatelets: platelet transfusion <i>may be considered</i> (no consensus)</li> <li>Hb: no benefit from target &gt; 10 vs 7 g/dL (more adverse events); mean 7-day Hb &lt; 9 g/dL linked to higher mortality</li> <li><b>TXA</b>: mild–moderate TBI <b>within 3 h</b>; <b>no benefit in severe isolated TBI</b>; not routine ICU therapy</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Signs of herniation (new dilated / unreactive pupil, Cushing response): buy time for CT / theatre","html":"<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr 1fr 1.1fr;gap:1.4mm;align-items:stretch\"> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"big\">Mannitol 1–2 g/kg</div><div class=\"mid\">over minutes, or hypertonic saline</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"big\">HTS rescue</div><div class=\"mid\">3% / 5%, or 23.4% NaCl ~30 mL bolus (experienced centres)</div></div> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\">Short hyperventilation, <b>FiO₂ 1.0</b>, PaCO₂ 25–30 (≥ 22) mmHg, <b>≤ 30 min</b>; stop if hypotension</div></div> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\" style=\"text-align:left\">Intubate, sedate, ventilate for transfer; <b>urgent neurosurgery</b> (EDH emergency; SDH: ICP may be extreme before evacuation)</div></div> </div>"},{"n":"6","h":"ICP monitoring","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Indicated</b>: severe TBI (GCS &lt; 9) + abnormal CT; higher yield with evolving lesion or diffuse pattern with obliterated 3rd ventricle / compressed cisterns (even moderate TBI)</li> <li><b>Exclusions</b>: bilateral fixed pupils, untreatable CT lesion, coagulopathy, GCS motor ≥ 5, unmanageable extracranial burden (age/comorbidity: controversial)</li> <li><b>EVD</b> = gold standard, drains CSF (contraindicated: uncorrected coagulopathy). <b>Parenchymal</b>: reliable, no recalibration. Epidural/subdural: not recommended</li> <li>Normal adult ICP &lt; 15; <b>CPP = MAP − ICP</b>, transducer at tragus</li> <li><b>A (plateau) waves</b> 50–100 mmHg for 5–20 min and B waves need treatment</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"ICP rising: check before you escalate","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Treat ICP <b>&gt; 22 mmHg</b> (≥ 55 y and women may have lower thresholds ~18) · CPP <b>60–70 mmHg</b> (CPPopt by PRx where available)</div></div> <div class=\"split\"> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>New pupil dilation / GCS fall</b> → treat at once + urgent CT</li> <li><b>Is the reading real?</b> Pulsatile waveform, rise with brief jugular compression, falls with CSF drainage, fits the clinical picture</li> <li><b>Venous outflow</b>: head position, tight collar/dressings; ventilator dyssynchrony, PTX/effusion, PEEP (usually &lt; 15 is fine); <b>intra-abdominal pressure</b></li> </ul> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Vasodilation</b>: EtCO₂ ↑, pain (check pumps, test bolus), fever, shivering, MAP swings, ↓ SpO₂</li> <li><b>Seizures / posturing</b> → EEG, deepen sedation</li> <li><b>Slow rise over hours</b>: fever, hyponatraemia, status epilepticus, growing contusion oedema (peaks ~day 5–7), post-traumatic infarction → repeat CT</li> </ul> </div>"},{"n":"8","h":"Tiered treatment of intracranial hypertension","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:12%\">Tier</th><th>Measures (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Basic</td><td>Sedation + analgesia, mechanical ventilation, <b>head-up 30°</b>, nothing compressing jugular veins, normocapnia, normothermia, normoglycaemia, SBP/CPP targets</td></tr> <tr><td>Standard</td><td><b>Sedation</b>: likely quick recovery → propofol + remifentanil/fentanyl; severe CT + high ICP → midazolam (lorazepam) + fentanyl. <b>CSF drainage</b> via EVD for ICP 20–25. <b>Mannitol 0.5–1.5 g/kg</b> PRN or 6–12-hourly (stable, normal kidneys; keep <b>osmolar gap &lt; 20</b>; risks hypovolaemia, AKI, rebound). <b>Hypertonic saline</b> bolus or infusion (preferred if hypotensive / hypovolaemic): <b>Na 145–155</b>, monitor osmolality</td></tr> <tr><td>Second-line (≤ 20–25% of monitored pts)</td><td><b>Hyperventilation</b> PaCO₂ <b>not &lt; 30</b>, ideally with SjvO₂ / PbtO₂ monitoring. <b>Barbiturates</b> (thiopental, pentobarbital) dosed to ICP with cEEG; no more once burst suppression reached (hypotension, infection, long t½). <b>Secondary decompressive craniectomy</b>: last tier (DECRA: not early; RESCUEicp: more survivors but more severe disability; inform family). <b>Hypothermia</b>: not early/prophylactic (POLAR, Eurotherm harm); refractory ICP only</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"9","h":"Read the CT fast","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Mass &gt; ~25 mL? midline shift? ipsilateral / bilateral <b>cistern compression</b> (= high ICP / coning)?</li> <li><b>EDH</b>: lucid interval possible, most within 8 h, emergency surgery, good outcome if fast</li> <li><b>Acute SDH</b>: usually comatose from start, worst prognosis</li> <li>Contusions grow for days; oedema peaks ~end of week 1</li> <li>Warning of evolution: thin temporal / posterior-fossa EDH/SDH, vault tSAH</li> <li>Deep coma + near-normal CT → <b>TAI</b>: MRI (FLAIR, SWI). Carotid/vertebral dissection risk → CT angiography</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Monitoring & lines","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>All: ECG, NIBP, SpO₂; <b>capnography if ventilated</b>; obs every 15 min if unstable</li> <li>Moderate/severe: arterial + central line, core temperature</li> <li><b>Orogastric tube</b> if facial / basal skull fracture (NG can perforate skull base)</li> <li>CO monitoring if high vasopressor need for CPP</li> <li>GCS unusable under sedation / paralysis: rely on pupils, ICP, imaging</li> <li>Advanced: PbtO₂ (BOOST-2 goal &gt; 20 mmHg), SjvO₂, PRx, microdialysis (LPR &gt; 25–40), TCD PI, cEEG</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"ICU admission & referral","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>ICU</b>: coma with diffuse injury, post-neurosurgery, altered consciousness needing intubation, multiply injured / cardiorespiratory unstable</li> <li><b>Refer to neurosurgical centre</b>: mass lesion, abnormal pupils, diffuse swelling, evolving contusion, vault/Sylvian tSAH, fractures, combined intra + extracranial injury (once stable)</li> <li><b>Neurogenic pulmonary oedema / stress cardiomyopathy</b>: early echo; avoid fluid excess; norepinephrine for CPP; inotrope only if echo-proven dysfunction</li> <li><b>Open / penetrating injury</b>: surgery best within 8 h; depressed fracture: prophylactic antibiotics 5–7 days (2C)</li> <li>Senior update to family; guarded prognosis</li> </ul>"}]},"08b-tbi-icu-outcome":{"slogan":"Care for the whole patient · A seizure in a comatose patient is an emergency · Prognosis: guarded, never from day 1","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Traumatic Brain Injury Parts IV–V; ESICM Emergency Response (TBI lessons) (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Sedation: deep only while ICP needs it","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Sedation = first-tier ICP therapy (↓ CMRO₂, ↓ CBV). <b>ICP stable + no evolving CT lesion → reduce/stop</b> as in general ICU; daily sedation review</li> <li>Once ICP controlled: daily interruption or switch infusions to PRN doses</li> <li>Over-sedation → tolerance, withdrawal, cardiovascular depression, longer ventilation</li> <li><b>Withdrawal</b>: CNS activation, GI upset, sympathetic overactivity, hyper-reflexia, clonus, yawning, ICP instability</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub\">Paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity (PSH)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Paroxysms of ↑ HR, BP, RR, temperature, sweating + posturing; 8–32% of severe TBI; appears on sedation weaning, lasts weeks; mimics withdrawal</li> <li>Prevent: opioids, IV sedatives; treat: <b>β- or α-blockers, benzodiazepines</b> (dose per CMH formulary); nutrition, physiotherapy</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Seizures","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>5–15% after TBI; risk ↑ after haematoma evacuation, penetrating injury / depressed fracture with dural breach, focal signs, intracranial sepsis</li> <li><b>Early</b> &lt; 7 days, <b>late</b> &gt; 7 days (permanent epilepsy risk). Status = ≥ 5 min continuous or ≥ 2 seizures without recovery between</li> <li>Seizures ↑ CMRO₂ and ICP, ↓ CPP. <b>Comatose patient: treat every seizure</b>; cEEG for NCSE</li> <li>Phenytoin / carbamazepine ↓ early (not late) seizures; levetiracetam prophylaxis: no outcome difference</li> <li>Review ICU drugs that lower seizure threshold</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Abort: <b>IV lorazepam</b> (dose per CMH formulary) → <b>phenytoin load 20 mg/kg at ≤ 50 mg/min</b>, then <b>300 mg/day</b> guided by levels (free level if low albumin)</div></div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Lungs & airway","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Head-up 30–45°</b> (↓ aspiration, ↓ ICP); turn back / right / left</li> <li>Pneumonia expected <b>day 3–7</b> (fever, purulent secretions, ↓ oxygenation); use hospital VAP protocol; <b>no broad-spectrum prophylaxis</b></li> <li>Hypothermia and barbiturates ↑ pneumonia risk</li> <li>Tracheostomy: early (4–6 d) / ultra-early (1–3 d) may shorten sedation and ventilation; timing unresolved. Delay after anterior C-spine fixation</li> <li>Lung contusion: balance gas-exchange strategy vs ICP control</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Gut & glucose","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Start feeding soon after admission: <b>full intake by day 3</b>; enteral preferred; avoid PN if gut works (line infection)</li> <li>Stress ulcer ~10% (2× others): early EN + analgesia/sedation; H2RA or PPI (↑ pneumonia, C. difficile)</li> <li><b>Glucose 150–200 mg/dL (8.3–11 mmol/L)</b>; <b>no tight control</b> (hypoglycaemia, low brain glucose)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"DVT & other injuries","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>High DVT risk; lesion growth unlikely after <b>day 3</b> → <b>LMWH after 72 h + mechanical compression</b>, individualised</li> <li>Stabilise spine early; fix pelvis / long bones early (external devices)</li> <li>Abdominal surgery, haemoperitoneum, ileus → ↑ IAP → ↑ ICP</li> <li>Fever raising ICP: antipyretics (continuous use masks infection)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Sodium disorders (~60% have electrolyte disturbance)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Problem</th><th style=\"width:33%\">Diagnosis</th><th style=\"width:32%\">Treatment</th><th>Notes</th></tr> <tr><td>Central diabetes insipidus</td><td>Na &gt; 150 + UO <b>&gt; 3 mL/kg/h</b>, SG <b>1.001–1.005</b>, U-osm <b>&lt; 150</b>, U-Na <b>&lt; 50</b></td><td><b>Desmopressin or argipressin</b> as soon as confirmed (dose per CMH formulary); replace fluid, K⁺, free-water deficit (enteral)</td><td>Also exclude mannitol osmotic diuresis; avoid further Na rise; correct gradually</td></tr> <tr><td>SIADH</td><td>Na &lt; 135 (140 = borderline in TBI); high urine Na, <b>low</b> urine volume</td><td>Fluid restriction ± loop diuretic</td><td>Commonest causes: low Na intake, hypotonic fluids, GI losses</td></tr> <tr><td>Cerebral salt wasting</td><td>High urine Na, <b>high</b> urine volume</td><td><b>Fludrocortisone 0.1–0.2 mg/day</b> + hypertonic saline; close Na monitoring</td><td>Young pts on high BP for CPP: “aldosterone escape” natriuresis → re-target CPP</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Monitor hourly UO, BP, CVP, serum electrolytes &amp; osmolality, urine SG &amp; osmolality.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Intracranial infection & meningitis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Risk: open / contaminated wounds, <b>CSF leak</b> (basal fracture), <b>EVD</b>; incidence 5–15% (&lt; 1% in good centres)</li> <li>Fever of unknown origin + pneumocephalus / rhinorrhoea / EVD → think meningitis. CSF: glucose <b>&lt; 2.8 mmol/L</b>, WBC high (&gt; 75% neutrophils), lactate ↑, protein 100–500 mg/dL</li> <li>Blood + CSF cultures, then <b>immediate high-dose empirical antibiotics</b>; discuss LP safety with neurosurgeon (herniation risk)</li> <li><i>S. pneumoniae</i>: IV penicillin 20–30 MU/day. Staph: flucloxacillin/oxacillin, vancomycin if MRSA ± rifampicin. Gram-neg: ceftriaxone/cefotaxime high dose or carbapenem</li> <li>Remove / replace infected drains; intrathecal vancomycin / gentamicin or colistin in selected cases</li> <li>Prevention: debridement, dural repair, surgical repair of persistent CSF leak</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>No antibiotic prophylaxis for basal skull fracture; no routine EVD changes; positive CSF culture without inflammation may be contamination</li></ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Late complications","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Hydrocephalus</b> (true in 1–5%; 25% within 2 wk, 90% within 6 wk; after meningitis or decompression): plateau or decline in recovery + enlarging ventricles → CSF withdrawal test → VP / VA shunt</li> <li><b>Sunken skin flap syndrome</b> weeks–months after craniectomy: postural headache, new deficits, seizures → <b>cranioplasty</b>; <b>no lumbar puncture / lumbar drain</b> (paradoxical herniation)</li> <li><b>Carotid-cavernous fistula</b>: pulsating exophthalmos, chemosis, bruit, CN II–VI palsy → TCD / angiography → endovascular closure</li> <li>Post-traumatic venous sinus thrombosis: atypical haemorrhage + oedema → CTV/MRV; heparin usual</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Fever, lines & catheter infections","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fever raises ICP: antipyretic, but avoid hypotension from bolus antipyretics and shivering from physical cooling; <b>low-dose continuous diclofenac infusion</b> has been suggested (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Central lines (catecholamines, sedation): <b>review daily, remove when not strictly needed</b></li> <li>Long-term urinary catheter: urinary catheter care bundle; diagnose UTI with WBC, systemic signs + colony count; IV antibiotics unless gut absorption assured</li> <li>EVD ventriculitis organisms: <i>P. aeruginosa</i>, <i>S. aureus</i>, <i>S. epidermidis</i>, <i>Acinetobacter</i>; post-traumatic meningitis: nasopharyngeal flora (pneumococcus, <i>H. influenzae</i>, meningococcus, staph)</li> <li>Refractory / resistant infection: microbiology / ID advice</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Family communication & decisions","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Early meeting led by a <b>senior</b>, with junior doctor and bedside nurse; may reveal medications and history</li> <li>Keep early comments short, focused on immediate plans; <b>guarded prognosis</b> (situation changes fast); promise regular updates</li> <li>Phone: respect privacy rules</li> <li>Refractory ICP: explain DECRA / RESCUEicp trade-off (more survivors, more vegetative / dependent) before decompression</li> <li>MRI (brainstem / dorsal TAI) can inform decisions on tracheostomy, decompression and continuing or withdrawing life-sustaining therapy; it is only part of the picture</li> <li>Balance prognosis at every step: avoid both under- and over-treatment</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Outcome, prognosis & audit","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Severe TBI: roughly <b>⅓ die, ⅓ recover, ⅓ disabled</b>; &gt; 90% reach final GOS category by <b>6 months</b>; gains up to 2 years in young adults</li> <li>Outcome drivers: extent of intra/extracranial damage, depth and duration of coma, age, comorbidity, quality of care</li> <li>Models (IMPACT, CRASH) ~80% individual accuracy: <b>do not use to set limits on acute care</b></li> </ul> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>GOS</b>: dead, vegetative, severe, moderate disability, good recovery (GOS-E 8 levels); favourable = good recovery + moderate disability</li> <li>Follow patients <b>≥ 6 months</b>, not “outcome at discharge”; IMPACT-adjusted observed/expected ratios; non-judgemental mortality meetings</li> <li>Support families through prolonged uncertainty</li> </ul> </div>"}]},"09a-stroke":{"slogan":"Time is brain: a 30-min delay to lysis cuts good 3-month outcome by 10% · Malignant MCA: decompress within 48 h","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Acute Ischaemic Stroke (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise & stabilise (in parallel)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ABC first; IV access, crystalloid, ECG, SpO₂, <b>immediate glucose</b> (hypoglycaemia mimics stroke)</li> <li><b>Onset = last seen well</b> (not time found / woke up); focused history: evolution, prior stroke, risk factors, drugs (anticoagulants), lysis contraindications</li> <li>Focused exam with <b>NIHSS</b>; dysphagia screen (nil by mouth until safe); cardiac (AF, murmurs, bruits) and chest</li> <li>Favour ICH: onset in hypertensive crisis, progression in minutes, early vomiting, early coma, sudden headache. <b>Clinical exam cannot exclude ICH → image</b></li> <li>Mimics (up to ⅓): hypo/hyperglycaemia, SOL/SDH, seizure/Todd’s, migraine, encephalopathy, hypoNa, sepsis, drugs (lithium, carbamazepine, phenytoin), functional</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Emergency tests & door times","html":"<div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\">Door → physician <b>≤ 10 min</b> · stroke team <b>≤ 15</b> · CT start <b>≤ 25</b> · CT read <b>≤ 45</b> · drug <b>≤ 60</b> · stroke unit <b>≤ 3 h</b></div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Non-contrast CT</b> (rule out bleed) → <b>CTA</b> (occlusion, collaterals; mCTA) ± <b>CT perfusion</b> (core/penumbra); MRI DWI/FLAIR for wake-up / late window. Imaging must not delay lysis</li> <li><b>ASPECTS</b> (10 MCA regions, −1 each): <b>≤ 7</b> = worse outcome, more lysis-related bleeding</li> <li>All: glucose, SpO₂, U&amp;E, FBC + platelets, troponin, PT/INR, aPTT, ECG. Selected: TT/ecarin time (DOAC), LFT, tox, alcohol, pregnancy, ABG, CXR, LP (CT-negative SAH), EEG, echo</li> <li>Do not wait for labs before lysis <b>unless</b> suspected thrombocytopenia or heparin / warfarin / DOAC use</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Reperfusion (anterior circulation)","html":"<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.15fr 1fr 1fr;gap:1.5mm;align-items:stretch\"> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"big\">Alteplase 0.9 mg/kg</div><div class=\"mid\">max 90 mg over 60 min, 10% as 1-min bolus · ≤ 4.5 h, or later / unknown onset if DWI lesion &lt; ⅓ MCA + FLAIR-negative (WAKE-UP) or CTP-selected 4.5–9 h (EXTEND)</div></div> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\" style=\"text-align:left\"><b>Thrombectomy</b> (stent retriever ± aspiration) for proximal LVO ≤ 6 h; <b>6–24 h</b> if distal ICA / proximal MCA with core <b>≤ 25 mL</b>, penumbra <b>≥ 1.8 × core</b>, <b>NIHSS ≥ 10</b>. Give lysis even if EVT planned</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\" style=\"text-align:left\"><b>Basilar occlusion</b>: emergency, transfer to interventional centre; EVT often considered (possible benefit NIHSS 10–20) though not proven standard; IV lysis rarely effective in LVO</div></div> </div> <ul class=\"ck\" style=\"margin-top:.7mm\"> <li>Eligibility: NIHSS, BP lability, anticoagulant/antiplatelet use or bleeding diathesis, seizures, recent major surgery, prior stroke / ICH / AVM / tumour. <b>Any deterioration after lysis → immediate CT</b>; <b>routine CT at 24 h</b> after reperfusion</li> <li>Avoid arterial puncture / invasive procedures up to 24 h after lysis (local rules)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Physiological targets","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:13%\">Variable</th><th style=\"width:30%\">During thrombectomy / lysis</th><th style=\"width:30%\">ICU</th><th>How</th></tr> <tr><td>Oxygen</td><td class=\"tgt\">SpO₂ &gt; 92%</td><td class=\"tgt\">SpO₂ ≥ 94%; PaO₂ &gt; 80 mmHg if ventilated</td><td>O₂ <b>only if SpO₂ &lt; 94%</b> (routine O₂ harmful); confirm with ABG; normocapnia</td></tr> <tr><td>Blood pressure</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 185/110 before lysis and for 24 h after; BP every 3 min during EVT</td><td class=\"tgt\">No lysis: treat only &gt; 220/120; lower ≤ 15% in first 24 h</td><td>IV <b>labetalol, nicardipine, urapidil, clevidipine</b> (dose per CMH formulary). Arterial line if unstable / ventilated; BP every 10–15 min first 24 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Hypotension</td><td class=\"tgt\" colspan=\"2\">SBP &lt; 90 mmHg (relative to usual BP)</td><td>IV fluid → <b>norepinephrine</b> (or metaraminol)</td></tr> <tr><td>Glucose</td><td class=\"tgt\">70–140 mg/dL (4–8 mmol/L)</td><td class=\"tgt\">140–180 mg/dL (8–10 mmol/L)</td><td>Hourly checks, <b>IV insulin infusion</b>; treat hypoglycaemia</td></tr> <tr><td>Temperature</td><td class=\"tgt\">35–37 °C</td><td class=\"tgt\">Treat &gt; 37.5 °C</td><td>Find infection; paracetamol → IV metamizole, cold saline 4 °C, cooling device. Therapeutic hypothermia: no proven benefit</td></tr> <tr><td>Fluids / Hb</td><td colspan=\"2\">Euvolaemia, <b>0.9% saline</b>; no dextrose (unless hypoglycaemic), no haemodilution, no albumin benefit</td><td>Transfusion: avoid aggressive; ~7 g/dL used in general ICU</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"5","h":"ICU admission & basic care","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Admit</b>: need for intubation, <b>GCS ≤ 8</b> / brainstem signs / airway risk, ↑ ICP or major oedema, respiratory failure, seizures / status, apnoea, <b>NIHSS &gt; 17</b>, reperfusion with complications or MOF, <b>MCA infarct &gt; 145 cm³</b>, uncontrolled BP, organ support, post-craniectomy, planned organ donation</li> <li>Head <b>midline, 20–30° up</b>; avoid IJ lines (neck rotation): femoral / subclavian</li> <li>Intubation: modified RSI, stable BP, normocapnia; <b>propofol 2–4 mg/kg</b> or <b>etomidate 0.15–0.3 mg/kg</b> + <b>rocuronium 1.0 mg/kg</b>; high aspiration risk</li> <li>Continuous ECG, echo once; troponin if ECG / echo changes (insular strokes: arrhythmia, sudden death)</li> <li>Tracheostomy if MV &gt; 2 weeks expected or severe dysphagia / bulbar palsy (≤ 3 days may reduce mortality)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Antithrombotics & VTE","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Aspirin 160–325 mg within 48 h</b>; <b>not within 24 h of lysis</b>; never instead of reperfusion</li> <li>Early heparin for cardioembolic stroke: no benefit; <b>no anticoagulation for 24 h after rt-PA</b></li> <li>Stop therapeutic anticoagulation in large stroke (&gt; ⅓ MCA); restart with specialists</li> <li><b>Prophylactic LMWH ≥ 24 h after lysis</b> + <b>intermittent pneumatic compression</b>; stockings ineffective; IVC filter if high PE risk and anticoagulation contraindicated</li> <li>Secondary prevention ASAP: aspirin 160–325 mg/day, clopidogrel / ticagrelor; by aetiology</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Oedema & malignant infarct","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Oedema peaks <b>24–48 h</b>; young, large hemispheric or cerebellar infarcts at highest risk → centre with neurosurgery. ICP may read normal despite large infarct</li> <li>Temporising only: HTS (more effective than mannitol) / mannitol, short hyperventilation, thiopental (ICP + EEG + invasive monitoring)</li> <li><b>Decompressive craniectomy ≤ 48 h</b>: age 18–60 mortality 78 → 29%; 61–82 y 70 → 33% but more severe disability</li> <li><b>Cerebellar</b>: suboccipital craniectomy; EVD alone insufficient</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>No steroids; no prophylactic osmotherapy; osmotherapy must not delay surgery</li></ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Haemorrhagic transformation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Symptomatic in <b>5–6%</b> after lysis / anticoagulation</li> <li><b>Stop thrombolytic immediately</b>; cryoprecipitate + FFP + rFVIIa (doses per CMH formulary / haematology)</li> <li>Repeat CT; neurosurgical review: evacuation can be lifesaving for large haematoma with shift</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Seizures & rehab","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Treat as other seizures: IV levetiracetam (most used), valproate, phenytoin (status), lacosamide</li> <li><b>No routine prophylactic anticonvulsants</b></li> <li><b>EEG</b> if unexplained / persistent altered consciousness</li> <li>Early (passive) rehabilitation even if unconscious: contractures, pressure sores, pneumonia, DVT</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Localise & find the mechanism","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>MCA</b>: contralateral face/arm &gt; leg weakness, aphasia (dominant) or neglect, gaze deviation, hemianopia. <b>ACA</b>: leg &gt; arm, incontinence, apathy / mutism if bilateral</li> <li><b>Posterior</b>: crossed signs, bilateral deficits, gaze palsy, ataxia, ↓ consciousness, dysphagia, Horner, cortical blindness. <b>Basilar</b>: quadriparesis with preserved consciousness (proximal) or somnolence / oculomotor signs (distal)</li> <li><b>Embolic</b>: maximal at onset, during activity, TIAs in different territories (AF, valve, LV thrombus, MI &lt; 4 wk). <b>Lacunar</b>: stuttering course, pure motor / sensory. <b>Dissection</b>: young, neck pain / headache, Horner, recent trauma</li> <li>ECG for AF / MI; carotid duplex (occlusion → keep BP higher-normal); TTE/TEE within 24 h; young cryptogenic: thrombophilia, antiphospholipid, vasculitis, toxicology</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Sedation, ventilation & neuromonitoring","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Hypoxaemia causes: aspiration, pneumonia, ARDS, PE, pulmonary oedema, central apnoea / sleep apnoea, muscle weakness</li> <li>If sedated: midazolam or propofol ± clonidine / dexmedetomidine + opioid; sedation scales, cEEG where available; high PEEP may raise ICP (less than thought)</li> <li>Neuromonitoring = <b>repeated clinical exam + imaging</b>; no proven role for routine ICP/CPP targets</li> <li><b>TCD</b>: occlusion / stenosis, response to lysis, autoregulation, micro-emboli; vasoreactivity (Diamox test: acetazolamide 1000 mg IV, normal CBF ↑ 30–60%); 5–10% no window</li> </ul>"}]},"09b-coma":{"slogan":"Glucose first, thiamine before glucose if at risk · Think non-convulsive status · Never prognosticate early on one sign","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Coma and disorders of consciousness (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise & resuscitate","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Coma = <b>no arousal + no awareness</b> (eyes closed, no response to voice, shaking or noxious stimulus)</li> <li><b>Not breathing → cardiac arrest algorithm</b>; otherwise ABC; <b>GCS ≤ 8</b> usually warrants intubation; any deteriorating consciousness → ICU</li> <li>Examine <b>before sedation</b> when possible (diagnostic value)</li> <li>Prevent secondary injury: no hypoxaemia, hypercapnia, hypotension, hypo/hyperglycaemia, hyponatraemia, fever</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Thiamine 100 mg IV</b> before any glucose if alcohol / malnutrition · No glucose result available → <b>25 g glucose (50 mL of 50%) IV push</b></div></div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Reverse the treatable now","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Status epilepticus</b>: IV benzodiazepine (e.g. lorazepam; IM also effective) at once (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li><b>Opioid</b>: <b>naloxone titrated to effect</b>; short half-life → repeat boluses or infusion (IV ± SC)</li> <li><b>Benzodiazepine</b>: <b>flumazenil</b> IV; <b>avoid</b> in epilepsy, chronic BZD use, pro-convulsant co-ingestion (e.g. TCA) – precipitates seizures</li> <li>Overdose ≈ 30% of non-traumatic coma: treat first unless TBI or other obvious cause</li> <li><b>CNS infection suspected</b>: blood cultures → <b>IV antibiotics immediately</b>; <b>aciclovir</b> if any suspicion of HSV; do not wait for CT/LP</li> <li>Structural cause → tertiary centre (neurosurgery); non-structural → ICU</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"History & neuro examination","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Witnesses; <b>sudden</b> (thunderclap → SAH) vs <b>progressive</b> (toxic / metabolic); headache, vomiting, fever, seizures; epilepsy, diabetes, cardiac, psychiatric history; recent surgery, infection, drugs; empty bottles, CO source</li> <li>General: trauma, meningism, fever, purpura, signs of ↑ ICP; <b>subtle eyelid myoclonus</b> → NCSE</li> <li><b>Motor</b>: noxious stimulus = trapezius squeeze → supraorbital → jaw thrust (<b>no</b> sternal rub / nipple; nail bed discouraged). Describe extension / abnormal flexion, lateralisation</li> <li><b>Pupils</b>: unilateral mydriasis = uncal herniation; bilateral miosis / mydriasis usually drugs (opioid / anticholinergic)</li> <li><b>Eyes</b>: doll’s eyes (not if C-spine risk) or cold calorics (intact drum): no deviation = pontine lesion; fast phase present = conscious / psychogenic</li> <li><b>Corneal</b> (saline drops), cough on suction, oculocardiac only if others absent; breathing pattern (Kussmaul = metabolic acidosis) and ventilator triggering</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Structural vs non-structural","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <div><div class=\"sub\">Structural</div> <ul class=\"dt\"><li>Focal signs: fixed unilateral pupil, asymmetric motor, disconjugate gaze</li><li>Brainstem / bilateral infarcts, haematoma, herniation, abscess / empyema, tumour, TBI, ADEM</li></ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub\">Non-structural (≈ 20% metabolic)</div> <ul class=\"dt\"><li>Reactive pupils, no focal signs</li><li>Glucose, Na, Ca, PO₄, Mg; DKA / HHS, myxoedema, Addison; uraemia, liver, CO₂ narcosis, HIE; sepsis, meningitis / encephalitis, malaria; PRES, TTP, DIC; ethanol, drugs, CO, lead; NMS, serotonin, anticholinergic; thiamine / niacin deficiency; NCSE / post-ictal; hypo/hyperthermia; psychogenic</li></ul></div> </div> <div class=\"note\">Exceptions: hypoglycaemia and Todd’s paresis can give focal signs. Locked-in syndrome mimics coma: test vertical eye movements / blinking.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Investigations","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Test</th><th style=\"width:43%\">When / what</th><th>Look for</th></tr> <tr><td>All patients</td><td>Na, Ca, glucose, FBC + differential, LFT, coagulation, creatinine/urea, <b>ABG</b>, urine toxicology, <b>serum osmolality</b> (osmolar gap)</td><td>Dysnatraemia, hypercalcaemia, sepsis, liver / renal failure, CO₂ narcosis; BZD, opioids, TCA, cocaine, amphetamine, THC; ethanol / methanol / ethylene glycol / isopropanol</td></tr> <tr><td>Selected</td><td><b>Ammonia</b> (every unexplained coma), thyroid, cortisol, cultures, anti-neuronal Ab (anti-NMDA, VGKC)</td><td>Hepatic encephalopathy / urea-cycle defect, myxoedema / thyroid storm, Addison, septic encephalopathy, autoimmune encephalitis</td></tr> <tr><td>CT</td><td>Trauma, focal deficit, sudden headache or vomiting before coma</td><td>Haemorrhage, mass effect, herniation. <b>Look at the images yourself</b></td></tr> <tr><td>MRI</td><td>Unexplained coma when transportable</td><td>Early stroke, venous thrombosis, CT-negative SAH, PRES, early HSV temporal lesions; ASL (seizure), spectroscopy</td></tr> <tr><td>EEG (continuous)</td><td><b>Any ICU patient with persistent unexplained altered consciousness</b></td><td>NCS (~8% of coma; sepsis 60%, metabolic 19% of ICU seizures); triphasic / slowing = metabolic / drugs; PLEDs = destructive lesion, HSV</td></tr> <tr><td>Lumbar puncture</td><td>Fever, neck stiffness, sudden severe headache; <b>CT first</b> if papilloedema / focal signs</td><td>Opening pressure, cells, glucose, protein, Gram, culture, viral PCR; save extra CSF (autoimmune)</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Clue → action: fever / neck stiffness → CSF + early antimicrobials · transplant + ↑ BP / creatinine → MRI (PRES), lower BP · myoclonus → ammonia, EEG, drug levels · orofacial dyskinesia + psychiatric prodrome → anti-NMDA: steroids / immunotherapy.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Scales & states","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>GCS</b>: verbal unreliable when intubated; no brainstem reflexes</li> <li><b>FOUR score</b> (each 0–4): Eye, Motor, Brainstem (pupil, corneal, cough), Respiration (breathes above / at ventilator rate). Detects locked-in, herniation, brain death</li> <li><b>VS/UWS</b> = eyes open, no awareness. <b>MCS</b> = reproducible tracking (use a <b>mirror</b>), blink to threat, command following. Early MCS: 50% higher chance of recovery</li> <li><b>CRS-R</b> for standardised assessment; covert consciousness (CMD) in ~15–20% of DOC</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Prognosis: multimodal, after stabilisation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Post-cardiac arrest poor outcome</b>: absent pupillary reflex, motor ≤ extension, absent corneal at day 3; burst-suppression or <b>unreactive EEG</b>; <b>bilateral absent N20 SSEP</b> (from 24 h). Use <b>≥ 2</b> of: incomplete brainstem reflexes, unreactive EEG, absent SSEP. NSE adjunct</li> <li><b>Lance-Adams</b> (post-anoxic myoclonus while aware) ≠ myoclonic status: do not withdraw care</li> <li>Exclude sedatives, residual <b>neuromuscular block</b> (train-of-four), CIP (NCS/EMG) before concluding</li> <li>Uncertain → keep supporting and reassess over days; MRI DWI/DTI, PET, ERP (P3b) add value</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Ongoing ICU care","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Daily sedation stop + neuro assessment</b>; avoid deep prolonged sedation</li> <li>Delirium prevention: orientation, sleep, light/dark, pain control, early mobilisation</li> <li>Early enteral feeding (post-pyloric if needed); stress ulcer prophylaxis; <b>LMWH/UFH + IPC</b></li> <li>Remove unneeded lines / catheters, oral hygiene, early extubation</li> <li>Repositioning, passive range-of-motion ~10×/day, pressure-relief mattress; avoid NMBA and high-dose steroids (CIP)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Brain death & family","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Warning signs: loss of pupillary and cough reflexes, no motor activity, herniation features</li> <li>Diagnosis per national law: absent brainstem reflexes + <b>apnoea test</b> ± ancillary test (EEG, angiography) if sedation / hypothermia confounds</li> <li>Honest, consistent family communication: recovery uncertain, may take months, sequelae likely; involve MDT</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Distinguish the states","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">State</th><th style=\"width:17%\">Arousal</th><th style=\"width:17%\">Awareness</th><th>Motor / EEG / key point</th></tr> <tr><td>Brain death</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>No brainstem function, apnoea; electrical silence</td></tr> <tr><td>Coma</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>Reflex or posturing; breathing variable</td></tr> <tr><td>VS / UWS</td><td>+</td><td>–</td><td>Reflex, non-purposeful; needs time since injury to diagnose</td></tr> <tr><td>MCS</td><td>+</td><td>± fluctuating</td><td>Possible purposeful movement; emergence = functional object use / communication on 2 exams</td></tr> <tr><td>Locked-in</td><td>+</td><td>+++</td><td>Quadriplegia, brainstem signs, normal EEG: <b>not a DOC</b></td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"11","h":"Unfavourable factors by cause","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Cause</th><th>Factors</th></tr> <tr><td>TBI</td><td>High-energy injury, hypothermia, hypotension, mydriasis, herniation, prolonged high ICP, Marshall CT score</td></tr> <tr><td>Ischaemic stroke</td><td>Ischaemic volume (MRI / CT perfusion), basilar occlusion, malignant infarct</td></tr> <tr><td>ICH</td><td>Haematoma volume on CT</td></tr> <tr><td>SAH</td><td>WFNS grade, Fisher CT score</td></tr> <tr><td>Cardiac arrest</td><td>Unwitnessed, long no-flow / low-flow, epinephrine dose, non-shockable rhythm</td></tr> </table>"}]},"09c-neuromuscular":{"slogan":"Normal blood gases do not mean safe ventilation · Myasthenia can crash suddenly · Watch GBS autonomic storms","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Neuromuscular Conditions (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Assess four things in every weak patient","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Severity &amp; pattern</b>: proximal / distal, nerve / cord level / cortical; repeat often (GBS changes fast, MG fluctuates with activity)</li> <li><b>Bulbar</b>: voice change / hoarseness, coughing on eating, nasal regurgitation, palate / tongue deviation, fasciculation, weak cough → <b>aspiration risk</b></li> <li><b>Respiratory muscles</b>: diaphragm (C3–5) paradox, intercostal (T1–12) paradox on sniff, accessory muscles, nasal flaring; f/Vt (normal &lt; 50); restlessness = rising CO₂</li> <li><b>Cause</b>: so specific therapy starts early</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">Bedside: count 1–20 in one breath fails → VC &lt; 15–18 mL/kg. <b>ABG stays normal until sudden collapse</b>; SpO₂ is not a measure of ventilation</div></div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Intubate / admit to ICU when","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>FVC &lt; 20 mL/kg</b> (or &lt; 1 L, or fall &gt; 50%) · <b>MIP &lt; 30 cmH₂O</b> (weaker than −20 predicts ventilation) · <b>MEP &lt; 40 cmH₂O</b></div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Rising PaCO₂, cannot manage secretions, worsening swallow / speech, falling spirometry (measure <b>hourly</b> in GBS)</li> <li>GBS high risk: onset → admission &lt; 7 days, bulbar signs, facial weakness (prevents FVC), low MRC, cannot cough / stand / lift elbows / lift head. <b>EGRIS</b> 0–2 = 4%, 3–4 = 24%, 5–7 = 65% risk of ventilation</li> <li><b>MG</b>: deterioration can be sudden → <b>FVC unreliable</b>; judge clinically</li> <li>Subglottic suction, oral care, chest physio; anticholinesterases ↑ secretions</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li><b>Suxamethonium</b>: hyperkalaemia in myopathy / established tetanus; non-depolarising NMBA prolonged in MG (short-acting preferred)</li></ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Localise the lesion","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:12%\">Feature</th><th style=\"width:22%\">Upper motor neuron</th><th style=\"width:22%\">Lower motor neuron</th><th style=\"width:22%\">Neuromuscular junction</th><th>Muscle</th></tr> <tr><td>Tone / reflexes</td><td>↑ / ↑ ± clonus (may be absent acutely, spinal shock)</td><td>↓ / absent or ↓</td><td>↓ / normal (absent under NMBA)</td><td>Normal / normal</td></tr> <tr><td>Sensation</td><td>Usually preserved</td><td>Often affected (nerve)</td><td>Normal</td><td>Normal</td></tr> <tr><td>Other</td><td>Babinski, Hoffman; focal signs; <b>no</b> respiratory weakness if pure UMN</td><td>Early wasting, fasciculation</td><td>Fatigability, ocular signs</td><td>Proximal; pain if inflammatory; skin (dermatomyositis)</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Flaccid paralysis + sensory level + sphincter disturbance = acute cord lesion → urgent MRI. Lateralised deficit = CNS (stroke). Sensory loss argues against MND, MG and myopathy.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Investigations","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CK / myoglobin</b> (rhabdomyolysis, myositis; urine dipstick misses up to 50%), ESR/CRP, antibodies: AChR, MuSK, LRP4 (MG), VGCC (Lambert-Eaton), anti-synthetase, GM1/GD1a (AMAN), GQ1b (Miller Fisher)</li> <li><b>MRI / CT</b>: cord compression, myelitis, abscess, demyelination</li> <li><b>CSF</b>: GBS protein 0.5–2.0 g/L, normal cells (&gt; 90% by week 2; may be normal week 1). <b>WBC &gt; 5/mm³</b>, sensory level or persistent asymmetry → doubt GBS</li> <li><b>NCS/EMG</b>: slow conduction = demyelination (AIDP); ↓ CMAP = axonal; <b>10% decrement on repetitive stimulation</b> = MG or residual NMBA; peroneal CMAP screens ICU-AW; DMS separates neuropathy vs myopathy</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Supportive care","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Ventilation: higher inspiratory flow / pressure support, sensitive trigger (NAVA option)</li> <li><b>Wean only as strength returns</b>: GBS VC gain &gt; 4 mL/kg predicts success; atelectasis, infection, autonomic instability → extubation failure; cough peak flow 35–60 L/min</li> <li>Feeding reviewed daily; avoid oral intake if bulbar; gastroparesis → <b>post-pyloric / jejunal</b> rather than PN</li> <li><b>LMWH + mechanical</b> prophylaxis (no full anticoagulation); adjust in AKI</li> <li>Light sedation, passive movements, foot-drop splints, protect peroneal nerve; postural hypotension limits mobilisation</li> <li>Check Na (SIADH in GBS), K, Mg, Ca, PO₄ (refeeding), thyroid, adrenal</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Specific conditions","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:12%\">Condition</th><th style=\"width:36%\">Recognise</th><th>Treat (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>Guillain-Barré</td><td>Post-infective (flu-like, <i>Campylobacter</i>), ascending symmetrical weakness, areflexia, paraesthesia, <b>facial weakness</b>; nadir ~2 wk (≤ 4 wk). Miller Fisher: ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, areflexia</td><td><b>IVIG or plasma exchange</b> early (PLEX may be better if ventilated); <b>steroids ineffective</b>. Autonomic: tachycardia, labile BP, <b>bradycardia / asystole on suctioning</b> (pacing reported); caution with metoclopramide. Pain: gabapentin, carbamazepine, short-term morphine. 20–30% ventilated, ≤ 5% die, 85% near-normal</td></tr> <tr><td>Myasthenic crisis</td><td>Fatigable ocular, bulbar, limb weakness; triggers: surgery / thymectomy, infection, trauma, post-partum, missed drugs. <b>Drugs that worsen</b>: aminoglycosides, polymyxin, clindamycin, quinolones, lidocaine, procainamide, phenytoin, CCB, Mg, β-blockers, diuretics, NMBA</td><td>Assess ventilation → <b>↑ anticholinesterase</b> (pyridostigmine) → start / ↑ steroids → <b>IVIG or PLEX</b> if no response. Wean only after PLEX/IVIG and VC improves (extubation failure 44%; atelectasis). Post-thymectomy: ventilate 24–48 h, restart anticholinesterase at lower dose after ~24 h. Other surgery: withhold 6 h pre-op. Residual rocuronium → <b>sugammadex</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Cholinergic crisis</td><td>Weakness + salivation, colic, diarrhoea, sweating, miosis (over-treatment)</td><td>Stop anticholinesterase ≥ 24 h, support, restart lower dose</td></tr> <tr><td>Botulism</td><td>Descending symmetrical paralysis, alert, no sensory loss, afebrile; food 6 h–8 days, wound 4–18 days (injecting drug use)</td><td><b>ICU immediately</b>, <b>early antitoxin</b> (dose per CMH formulary), wound debridement</td></tr> <tr><td>Tetanus</td><td>Trismus, neck stiffness → generalised rigidity, opisthotonus, laryngospasm; autonomic storms (leading cause of death)</td><td>Wound debridement, <b>metronidazole</b>, <b>tetanus immunoglobulin 500 IU IV once</b>; deep benzodiazepine sedation ± NMBA; <b>Mg to 2–4 mmol/L</b>; storms: epidural / spinal bupivacaine, atropine infusion reported. Support 4–6 weeks</td></tr> <tr><td>Rhabdomyolysis</td><td>Pain, weakness, dark urine, CK ↑, dipstick blood without RBC; drugs (propofol, statins + macrolide), compression, exertion, hyper/hypothermia</td><td><b>Restore volume and BP early</b>; K⁺ binders, no K⁺ fluids → insulin/dextrose, bicarbonate; treat Ca only if symptomatic or severe hyperK; fasciotomy / debridement; RRT if no urine flow; alkalinisation unproven</td></tr> <tr><td>MND / myotonic dystrophy</td><td>UMN + LMN signs, tongue fasciculation; may present as failure to wean. Myotonic: arrhythmias, LV failure, prolonged post-anaesthetic depression</td><td>NIV if VC &lt; 50%, SNIP &lt; 40, orthopnoea, nocturnal desaturation; riluzole; early end-of-life planning before invasive ventilation</td></tr> <tr><td>Organophosphate</td><td>Muscarinic + nicotinic (fasciculation, paralysis) within 12 h</td><td>Ventilation may be needed (see Major Intoxication)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"7","h":"ICU-acquired weakness (CIP / CIM)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>¼ of prolonged MV, up to 60% of sepsis: symmetrical flaccid limb &gt; proximal weakness, reflexes ↓ or normal, <b>face spared (grimace)</b>, failure to wean</li> <li><b>MRC sum score &lt; 48</b> (&lt; 36 severe); handgrip &lt; 11 kg men, &lt; 7 kg women (needs cooperation; &gt; 30% cannot test)</li> <li>Peroneal nerve test screens; EMG / biopsy not needed acutely</li> <li>Exclude other weaning failures first (heart, mechanics, abdomen); profound weakness can mimic brain injury</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Prevent ICU-AW","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ABCDEF bundle, <b>mobilise within 72 h</b>, in-bed cycling, minimise sedation</li> <li>Review steroids (lowest dose; stop when shock not steroid-dependent); minimise NMBA, use TOF and paralysis holds</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>No high-protein / energy push (harm signal); no intensive insulin 4.4–6.1 mmol/L (hypoglycaemia)</li><li>PRIS: high-dose propofol → acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, cardiac failure</li></ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Confirming MG & hypoventilation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>MG: AChR antibodies (specific; 10–15% seronegative), MuSK, LRP4; repetitive stimulation 10% decrement; <b>SFEMG jitter</b> if other tests normal</li> <li><b>Edrophonium test</b> only with obvious ocular signs, unclear diagnosis and <b>full resuscitation available</b> (collapse risk; caution IHD, asthma)</li> <li>Sleep study: AHI mild 5–15, moderate 15–30, severe &gt; 30/h; severe AHI or &gt; 20% sleep with SaO₂ &lt; 90% → difficult airway, post-op respiratory failure; check right heart</li> <li>Nocturnal hypoventilation in chronic NM disease = progression → home NIV, discuss future intubation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Safe discharge & recovery","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Assess falls risk (weakness, orthostatic intolerance), airway protection / tracheostomy need, swallow (speech &amp; language therapy), nutrition, physiotherapy plan</li> <li>ICU-AW: longer MV and stay, higher mortality; &gt; half of working-age survivors not back at work at 1 year, ⅓ need help with daily activities</li> <li>Screen for post-intensive care syndrome (e.g. PICUPS); physical + occupational therapy also helps delirium</li> </ul>"}]},"09d-neuro-monitoring":{"slogan":"No single monitor tells the whole story · Integrate imaging, EEG and TCD with the bedside exam","legend":"ACE content only · thresholds as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Neuroimaging; Electrophysiology in Neurocritical Care; Transcranial Doppler (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Which scan, when","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CT first</b>: acute head trauma, suspected bleed, acute deficit, CNS infection, hydrocephalus, raised ICP / herniation; + CT angiogram (aneurysm), CT venogram (sinus thrombosis)</li> <li><b>Trauma: CT if GCS &lt; 13</b>; if GCS ≥ 13 consider CT with headache, vomiting, LOC / amnesia, seizure, trauma above clavicle, alcohol / drugs, anticoagulants</li> <li><b>MRI</b>: early ischaemia (DWI bright / ADC dark while CT normal), DAI, hypoxic injury, encephalitis, sinus thrombosis, metabolic / inflammatory</li> <li>MRI in stable severe TBI with persistent impaired consciousness not explained by CT or seizures after <b>1–2 weeks</b></li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>MRI contraindicated: ferromagnetic pacemaker / ICD, aneurysm clips, cochlear implants, <b>high cardiorespiratory support</b> (transfer unsafe)</li></ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Read the CT: act on","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Midline shift ≥ 5 mm → urgent neurosurgical review</b> (line between anterior / posterior falx attachments)</li> <li>Mass effect: 3rd ventricle / sulcal effacement → subfalcine, uncal transtentorial, tonsillar, external herniation</li> <li><b>EDH</b> lens-shaped, crosses midline not sutures; <b>SDH</b> crescent, crosses sutures not midline; contusions inferior frontal / anterior temporal; DAI punctate at grey-white junction (MRI more sensitive)</li> <li>Blood: hyperdense acute → isodense 2–3 wk → hypodense &gt; 1 month</li> <li>Ischaemia: grey-white loss / swelling (hours) → hypodense by 12 h → volume loss 3–4 wk; hyperdense MCA sign</li> <li>Hydrocephalus: periventricular low density = raised ICP. Post-arrest: <b>average GWR &lt; 1.21</b> predicted poor outcome (CT ≤ 24 h)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"CT grading scales","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Marshall</th><th>Definition</th></tr> <tr><td>Diffuse I</td><td>No visible pathology</td></tr> <tr><td>Diffuse II</td><td>Cisterns present, shift 0–5 mm; no high / mixed lesion &gt; 25 cm³</td></tr> <tr><td>Diffuse III</td><td>Cisterns compressed / absent, shift 0–5 mm (swelling)</td></tr> <tr><td>Diffuse IV</td><td>Shift &gt; 5 mm; no lesion &gt; 25 cm³</td></tr> <tr><td>V / VI</td><td>Mass evacuated / non-evacuated lesion &gt; 25 cm³</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>Rotterdam</b> (1–6): cisterns compressed 1 / absent 2; shift &gt; 5 mm 1; EDH absent 1; IVH / tSAH 1; +1. <b>Helsinki</b> (−3 to 14): SDH 2, ICH 2, EDH −3, mass &gt; 25 cm³ 2, IVH 3, suprasellar cisterns compressed 1 / obliterated 5.</div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Mod. Fisher</th><th>SAH</th></tr> <tr><td>0 / 1 / 2</td><td>None · thin SAH no IVH · thin SAH + IVH</td></tr> <tr><td>3 / 4</td><td>Thick SAH no IVH · thick SAH + IVH</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Perfusion CT & MRI sequences","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Infarct core</b>: matched ↓ CBF + ↓ CBV, ↑ MTT. <b>Penumbra</b>: ↓ CBF, <b>maintained CBV</b>, prolonged MTT (CBV/MTT mismatch); Tmax better predictor</li> <li>CT perfusion: DCI / vasospasm after SAH; can test the MAP that avoids delayed ischaemia</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Sequence</th><th>Use</th></tr> <tr><td>T1 / T2</td><td>Anatomy / oedema, gliosis, acute-subacute bleed</td></tr> <tr><td>FLAIR</td><td>White matter lesions, oedema, DAI (bright)</td></tr> <tr><td>GRE / SWI</td><td>Micro-haemorrhage, DAI (dark)</td></tr> <tr><td>DWI / ADC</td><td>Acute ischaemia, cytotoxic oedema, abscess, HIBI (DWI bright, ADC dark)</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Hypoxic injury: symmetrical basal ganglia high T2/FLAIR + DWI, low ADC.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"EEG: indications","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Strong</b>: all acute brain injury with unexplained persistent altered consciousness</li> <li><b>Strong</b>: urgent EEG if CSE not back to baseline <b>within 60 min</b> of seizure drugs; urgent (≤ 60 min) in refractory SE</li> <li><b>Strong</b>: comatose post-arrest, during TTM and ≤ 24 h of rewarming (usually spot EEG at 24 and 72 h)</li> <li>Weak: unexplained encephalopathy (renal / hepatic failure); DCI in comatose SAH; cEEG over routine EEG in coma without primary brain lesion</li> <li>Also: confirm burst suppression, paroxysmal events (video-EEG), NCSE diagnosis / monitoring</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\">Spot 30–60 min EEG finds NCS in 45–58% · <b>cEEG 24–48 h finds 80–95%</b> · NCSE follows CSE in up to 43%</div></div>"},{"n":"6","h":"EEG: read it","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Seizure</b>: epileptiform discharges &gt; 2.5 Hz for &gt; 10 s with evolution and clear onset / offset; &lt; 10 s = BIRDs; prolonged = SE. NCSE: Salzburg criteria</li> <li>Ictal-interictal continuum (LPDs, GPDs, BIPDs, LRDA, GRDA) ↑ seizure risk</li> <li><b>Post-arrest</b>: suppressed &lt; 10 µV; discontinuous = 10–40% suppression; <b>burst suppression at 24 h without confounders = highly malignant</b> (worse if identical / epileptiform bursts); reactive continuous / discontinuous = good; GPDs + suppressed background or status myoclonus = unfavourable</li> <li>Sedation: propofol / barbiturates / volatiles → slowing → burst suppression; dexmedetomidine sleep-like spindles; ketamine theta</li> <li>Brain death ancillary: 30 min good-quality EEG, nothing &gt; 2 µV after excluding sedatives, metabolic causes, hypothermia (EEG flat &lt; 25 °C → use evoked potentials)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"TCD: technique & normal values","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Low-frequency probe <b>≤ 2 MHz</b> (cardiac); <b>transtemporal</b> above zygoma, depth 16–20 cm to find peduncles → 10 cm; MCA (M1) at sphenoid wing, flow towards probe; PW gate 4–5 mm</li> <li>Beam–flow angle &lt; 30° (ideally ≤ 10°); no angle correction intracranially</li> <li>Other windows: <b>submandibular</b> (extracranial ICA for Lindegaard; when temporal window fails), <b>suboccipital</b> (VA / BA, 7–10 cm, flow away), <b>transorbital</b> (siphon, ophthalmic, ONSD)</li> <li>Window may fail in elderly (women), Asian and African patients; 50–90% have Circle of Willis variants</li> <li>Know MAP, HR, CO, temperature, PaCO₂ when interpreting (↑ temperature → ↑ velocities)</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">MCA <b>mFV 60–80 cm/s</b> · <b>dFV &gt; 20</b> · <b>PI ≤ 1.25</b> · mFV = (sFV + 2 dFV)/3 · PI = (sFV − dFV)/mFV</div></div>"},{"n":"8","h":"Raised ICP: TCD & ONSD","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>dFV ≤ 25 cm/s + PI ≥ 1.25</b> → high resistance / hypoperfusion; suggests ICP &gt; 15–20; sudden one-sided ↓ velocity + ↑ PI = expanding lesion / shift</li> <li>Mild–moderate TBI (Marshall II): normal TCD <b>NPV 98%</b> (PPV 18%)</li> <li>Compare PI only with same MAP, CO and PaCO₂; PI unusable in vasospasm</li> <li><b>ONSD</b>: 3 mm behind globe, average 2 planes: &lt; 5 mm normal · &gt; 5 mm specific · &gt; 5.7 mm good for ICH · &gt; 7.5 mm very high ICP; confirm with another test</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Transorbital: power to minimum (17 mW / 10%) — cataract risk</li></ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Vasospasm (SAH / TBI)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:16%\">Artery</th><th>Mild mFV / LR</th><th>Moderate</th><th>Severe</th></tr> <tr><td>MCA / ICA / ACA</td><td>&gt; 120 / 3–6</td><td>&gt; 150 / 3–6</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 200 / &gt; 6</td></tr> <tr><td>VA / BA</td><td>60–79</td><td>80–99</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 100 / &gt; 6</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>Lindegaard ratio</b> = mFV MCA / mFV ipsilateral extracranial ICA (submandibular); modified = BA / extracranial VA. High mFV + LR &lt; 3 = hyperaemia; ≥ 3 vasospasm; &gt; 6 severe. MCA mFV &gt; 200 → start therapy + confirm; 120–200 grey zone, always investigate; rise &gt; 50 cm/s in 24 h suspicious. Onset: TBI day 2 (peak 5–7); SAH day 4 (peak 5–14). Daily TCD.</div>"},{"n":"10","h":"Autoregulation & CO₂","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Intact range MAP 60–160 / CPP 50–150 (shifted right in chronic hypertension)</li> <li><b>Mx &gt; 0.3 = impaired</b> → set MAP / CPP actively</li> <li>CO₂ test: +1 mmHg PaCO₂ for ≥ 2 min → mFV +3–5% if intact</li> <li>PaCO₂ target <b>35–40 mmHg</b> often suggested</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Carotid compression test: embolism / hypotension risk</li></ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"EEG in encephalopathy","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Toxic-metabolic: EEG grades severity and excludes NCSE but <b>not the cause</b>; sedation confounds</li> <li>Hepatic: theta → delta slowing with West Haven grade; consider in ALF</li> <li>Sepsis: periodic / rhythmic patterns common, non-specific</li> <li><b>Viral encephalitis</b>: seizures 32–36%; HSV → lateralised temporal LPDs (42.5%) → keep cEEG</li> <li>Unconscious + <b>reactive EEG</b> → more likely favourable</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Stroke & brain death on TCD","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>TCD detects ICA / MCA / VA / BA occlusion (sens 79%, spec 94% vs CTA); follow recanalisation during IVT</li> <li>Rising ICP: diastolic flow falls → systolic only → <b>to-and-fro (oscillating)</b> → isolated systolic spikes → no signal = cerebral circulatory arrest</li> <li>TCD for brain death: sens 90%, spec 98%; helps time angiography</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Use TCD to tell ischaemic from haemorrhagic stroke or to guide thrombolysis</li> <li>Declare brain death on TCD alone; arrest patterns can reverse if raised ICP is treated quickly</li> <li>Leave EEG caps on &gt; 3–4 h (use glued cup electrodes for cEEG)</li> <li>Do suboccipital TCD with possible cervical instability</li> <li>Rely on one ONSD cut-off; it varies with age and pathology</li> </ul>"}]},"10a-trauma-resuscitation":{"slogan":"Stop the bleeding first · Blood, not crystalloid · TXA within 3 hours · Bay to CT / OR in ≤ 30 min","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Trauma: Epidemiology & Pathophysiology; Initial Management of Severe Trauma (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Prepare & receive","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pre-alert minimum: <b>age, GCS, mechanism (penetrating?), shock, BP, HR</b></li> <li>Huddle: roles, likely deterioration, airway kit, access, <b>blood products</b>, OR / IR on standby, fluid warmer-infuser primed</li> <li>Handover with everyone silent: <b>ATMISTER</b> (Age, Time, Mechanism, Injuries, Signs, Treatment, ETA, Requirements)</li> <li>Leader stays hands-off; closed-loop talk; time-outs ≤ 2 min</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">High-energy mechanism → full trauma imaging even if asymptomatic. ISS = sum of squares of 3 worst AIS regions; <b>ISS &gt; 15 = major trauma</b>.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Primary survey: xABCDE","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>X</b>: exsanguinating external bleeding → pressure, haemostatic packing, limb tourniquet, junctional device, scalp clips, <b>pelvic binder</b></li> <li><b>A</b>: obstruction, tube / SGA position, <b>EtCO₂</b></li> <li><b>B</b>: ventilation, deformity, surgical emphysema, asymmetry; RR &gt; 30 = warning</li> <li><b>C</b>: radial pulse, HR, SBP, CRT, skin, pulses</li> <li><b>D</b>: GCS + pupils; <b>E</b>: abdomen, pelvis, limbs, back (all wounds)</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Peri-arrest with hypovolaemia / obstruction: <b>C-A-B</b>. Survey takes minutes → plan for the next 10 min</div></div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Bay tests → where next","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>eFAST</b> (&lt; 3 min): fluid sens 90% / spec 98% (shock 62% / 83%); PTX 91% / 99%; pubic diastasis</li> <li>CXR (PTX sens only 47%), pelvic XR (68%) — use in grade A, unclear FAST, ? diaphragm</li> <li>TCD in moderate–severe TBI: <b>PI &gt; 1.2, dFV &lt; 20 cm/s</b> = low CBF</li> <li>Priorities: <b>breathing → bleeding → brain</b></li> <li>Whole-body CT preferred (↓ mortality); <b>very unstable → OR / IR directly</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Trauma bay stay <b>≤ 30 min</b></div></div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Haemorrhagic shock: pressure targets","html":"<div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>SBP 80–90 / MAP 60–65 mmHg until bleeding controlled</b> · TBI: SBP &gt; 110 (100–120) · <b>MAP ≥ 80 if severe TBI (GCS ≤ 8)</b></div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Shock = sustained SBP fall ≥ 30% + hypoperfusion (mottling, oliguria); late vagal phase: sudden hypotension + paradoxical bradycardia</li> <li>Rule out haemorrhage first; then vasoplegia (spinal), obstruction (tamponade, PTX), cardiac contusion</li> <li><b>Blood products early</b>; restrict crystalloid; <b>noradrenaline</b> for life-threatening hypotension (peripheral IV safe up to <b>2 mg/h</b>)</li> <li>Perfusion check: lactate, ScvO₂</li> <li>Fluids: balanced crystalloid, but <b>not hypotonic (Ringer lactate) in severe TBI</b>; avoid colloids</li> <li>Permissive hypotension: caution elderly / chronic hypertension; contraindicated TBI, spinal cord injury; &gt; 90 min ↑ organ damage</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Massive transfusion: predict & activate","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Red Flag</b> (≥ 2 of 5 prehospital): unstable pelvis, <b>shock index ≥ 1</b>, POC Hb ≤ 13 g/dL, MAP &lt; 70, intubated → MTP / immediate haemostasis</li> <li><b>Vandromme</b>: lactate ≥ 5, HR &gt; 105, INR &gt; 1.5, Hb ≤ 11, SBP &lt; 110. Also TASH, ABC, PWH</li> <li>MT = ≥ 10 RBC in 24 h · ≥ 4 RBC in 1 h with ongoing need · 50% blood volume in 3 h</li> <li><b>DCR: RBC : FFP : platelets 1:1:1</b>; crystalloid only to keep lines open; O-neg / O-pos RBC if needed (no Hb trigger)</li> <li>ATC within 30 min: base deficit &gt; 6 + ISS &gt; 15; hyperfibrinolytic early, prothrombotic later (VTE, LMWH resistance)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Haemostatic resuscitation (start if SBP < 90 and / or HR > 110)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Item</th><th style=\"width:40%\">Dose / target</th><th>Notes</th></tr> <tr><td>Tranexamic acid</td><td class=\"tgt\">1 g IV bolus within 3 h of injury, then 1 g over 8 h</td><td>CRASH-2: ↓ bleeding deaths by ⅓; greatest benefit ≤ 1 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Plasma</td><td>Plasma : RBC ≥ 1:2 initially; then keep <b>PT / aPTT &lt; 1.5 × normal</b></td><td>1 L plasma ≈ 4 g fibrinogen; thawing delay. No plasma without significant bleeding</td></tr> <tr><td>Fibrinogen</td><td class=\"tgt\">2–4 g (concentrate or cryoprecipitate) if &lt; 1.5–2.0 g/L or viscoelastic deficit</td><td>Low in 41% hypotensive on arrival; &gt; 70% if Hb &lt; 100 g/L. Repeat per lab / ROTEM / TEG</td></tr> <tr><td>RBC</td><td><b>Hb 8–10 g/dL</b> while bleeding</td><td>Warmed via rapid infuser</td></tr> <tr><td>Platelets</td><td><b>&gt; 100 × 10⁹/L</b> with ongoing bleeding and / or TBI</td><td>Initial 4–8 single units or 1 apheresis pack</td></tr> <tr><td>Calcium</td><td>Keep <b>ionised Ca normal</b> during MT</td><td>Hypocalcaemia common in severe trauma</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">TIC: PT / aPTT prolonged 50%, <b>INR ≥ 1.5</b>, PT ratio &gt; 1.2 or <b>fibrinogen &lt; 1.5 g/L</b>. Standard tests are slow and miss platelets / fibrinolysis → use viscoelastic (TEG / ROTEM) when available (run at 37 °C, misses hypothermia and anticoagulant effects).</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Airway & chest","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Every trauma intubation is difficult</b> (C-spine, blood, vomit; poor preoxygenation): MACOCHA, <b>video laryngoscope</b></li> <li>Induction: <b>ketamine or etomidate</b> (dose per CMH formulary) — <b>reduce dose in shock</b></li> <li><b>Tension PTX = clinical</b>: finger thoracostomy 4th–5th ICS anterior axillary line, no X-ray wait; chest drain asap</li> <li>Drain <b>&gt; 1000–1500 mL</b> or <b>&gt; 150–200 mL/h</b> → damage-control thoracotomy</li> <li>Flail / multiple rib fractures: intubation + MV usually stabilise</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Brain in multitrauma","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>GCS + pupils, repeat often</li> <li>Avoid hypotension and hypoxaemia — even one episode worsens outcome</li> <li>Intubate severe TBI; <b>EtCO₂ 35–40 mmHg</b>, no swings</li> <li>Mannitol / hypertonic saline only as <b>bridge to surgery</b> (pupils, TCD PI &gt; 1.2–1.4); no prophylactic osmotherapy</li> <li><b>ICP &lt; 20, CPP 60–70 mmHg</b></li> <li>CT angio supra-aortic vessels: skull base / C-spine fracture, Le Fort II–III, neck soft tissue, or CT not explaining neurology</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Access & devices","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>2 large-bore peripheral IVs</b> (faster than CVC)</li> <li>CVC + arterial line: <b>femoral</b>, ultrasound-guided; stop after 10–15 min of difficulty; never delay OR / CT</li> <li>No IV: <b>humeral IO</b> (tibial 2nd); arrest with no access: landmark subclavian</li> <li>Urethral blood → no urinary catheter (suprapubic, urology)</li> <li>Basal skull fracture → <b>orogastric</b>, not nasogastric</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Damage control","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>For the <b>lethal triad</b> (coagulopathy, acidosis, hypothermia) or low reserve: short surgery to stop bleeding / contamination (liver or retroperitoneal packing, splenectomy)</li> <li>Pelvic girdle closure / stabilisation; packing + <b>embolisation</b>; early long-bone stabilisation</li> <li>Phases: resuscitation → definitive haemostasis → ICU restoration of physiology; multidisciplinary decision with clear goals</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Blast injury","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Primary (lungs, gut, ears), secondary (fragments), tertiary (thrown), quaternary (burns, inhalation, crush)</li> <li>Severe injury possible <b>without visible wounds</b>: blast lung, tension PTX, air embolism, bowel rupture</li> <li>FAST + CXR now, CT when stable; O₂ and <b>early intubation</b> for blast lung; decompress PTX</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"ICU phase: what kills later","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>~50% deaths &lt; 2 h, 25–30% &lt; 6 h; <b>~20% die later in ICU</b>: MOF, ARDS, pneumonia, PE</li> <li>Coagulopathy turns <b>prothrombotic</b> → high VTE risk, LMWH resistance (low antithrombin)</li> <li>Endotheliopathy → secondary sepsis (mostly pneumonia); persistent inflammation-immunosuppression-catabolism (PICS)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Traumatic cardiac arrest","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Survival 7–17%: treat causes <b>simultaneously</b>: hypoxaemia, tension PTX, massive haemothorax, external / non-compressible bleeding, tamponade</li> <li><b>Bilateral finger thoracostomy</b> first; compressions must not delay this</li> <li>Tourniquet, packing, pelvic binder (even without signs), rapid blood</li> <li>Tamponade: eFAST; penetrating + witnessed arrest → <b>clamshell thoracotomy</b></li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Needle pericardiocentesis (rarely drains clot); REBOA outside trials</li></ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Pregnant & elderly","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Pregnancy</b>: ≥ 20 wk → <b>left lateral tilt</b>; obstetric team, fetal check; adapted whole-body CT acceptable; well mother ≠ well fetus (abruption, uterine rupture)</li> <li><b>Elderly</b> (&gt; 65): often under-triaged; frailty / baseline function, drugs (anticoagulants); C1–C2 injuries; early coagulopathy correction; <b>reassess at 72 h</b>; delirium, infection, electrolytes</li> </ul>"},{"n":"15","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Rely on needle decompression (fails ~50%); it must not delay finger thoracostomy</li> <li>Give large-volume crystalloid or colloid</li> <li>Keep permissive hypotension &gt; 90 min, or use it in TBI / cord injury</li> <li>Wait for an Hb value to give emergency blood</li> <li>Let lines, X-rays or POCUS delay haemorrhage control or decompression</li> </ul>"}]},"10b-trauma-icu-regions":{"slogan":"Reassess every injury · Time surgery to physiology · Prevent VTE, compartment syndrome and AKI","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Trauma ICU Management; Extremity Injury (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"First 24–48 h in ICU","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fresh reassessment: <b>confirm all injuries</b> (complete imaging), look for raised ICP and <b>delayed bleeding</b></li> <li>Support ventilation, perfusion, kidneys, coagulation; plan staged surgery</li> <li>Daily multidisciplinary round: intensivist, trauma surgeon, neurosurgeon, orthopaedics, anaesthetist</li> <li>Perfusion: lactate, base deficit, ScvO₂ / SvO₂, POCUS; PiCCO / PAC if complex</li> <li><b>Balanced crystalloid</b> (limit 0.9% NaCl), blood products if shocked, <b>noradrenaline</b> for persistent hypotension; daily weight, <b>de-escalate fluid early</b></li> <li>Non-TBI active bleeding: SBP 80–90 until controlled; <b>TBI: SBP &gt; 110</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Brain targets (TBI in up to 40%)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:30%\">Variable</th><th>Target</th></tr> <tr><td>PaO₂ / SpO₂</td><td class=\"tgt\">60–80 mmHg / &gt; 94%</td></tr> <tr><td>PaCO₂ / EtCO₂</td><td class=\"tgt\">35–40 mmHg, no swings</td></tr> <tr><td>SBP · CPP · ICP</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 110 · 60–70 · &lt; 20 mmHg</td></tr> <tr><td>Glucose</td><td>1.1–1.8 g/L, no hypoglycaemia</td></tr> <tr><td>Hb</td><td>&gt; 9 g/dL (HEMOTION)</td></tr> <tr><td>Na / temp</td><td>No hyponatraemia; no fever</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">ICP up: head 30° midline, sedation, mannitol / hypertonic saline dosed by osmolality and Na, EVD, decompression. Multimodal monitoring (PbtO₂, TCD, EEG) in selected. Delay non-vital surgery while ICP high.</div>"},{"n":"3","h":"When is the patient fit for theatre?","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Domain</th><th style=\"width:28%\">Stable → definitive surgery</th><th style=\"width:28%\">Intermediate</th><th>Unstable → damage control only</th></tr> <tr><td>Circulation</td><td>No noradrenaline; lactate normal</td><td>Responding to resuscitation</td><td class=\"av\">High-dose noradrenaline, ≥ 5 RBC units, lactate &gt; 4 mmol/L</td></tr> <tr><td>Temperature</td><td>&gt; 35 °C</td><td>32–35 °C</td><td class=\"av\">&lt; 32 °C</td></tr> <tr><td>Lungs</td><td>P/F &gt; 300</td><td>P/F 150–300</td><td class=\"av\">Severe ARDS</td></tr> <tr><td>Readiness</td><td colspan=\"3\"><b>Temp &gt; 34 °C, pH &gt; 7.2 (normal lactate), fibrinogen &gt; 1 g/L</b> → early total care; else operate day 1 or in the <b>day 4–10 window</b> (between SIRS and CARS)</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>Life → limb → function.</b> ETC = definitive fixation &lt; 24 h; early safe definitive &lt; 48 h; damage control orthopaedics (ex-fix / traction) then definitive after 48 h. TBI: defer orthopaedics until <b>ICP &lt; 20 and CPP &gt; 60–70</b>. Spine fixed &lt; 24 h. Restore limb perfusion (shunt / repair) before fixation. Chest drains before long cases with bilateral contusion / PTX. Open abdomen does not prevent early fixation if stable.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Coagulation & VTE","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Ongoing bleeding: <b>RBC : plasma : platelets 1:1:1</b>, fibrinogen, <b>TXA within 3 h</b>, TEG / ROTEM-guided; higher targets with intracranial injury</li> <li>VTE up to <b>30%</b> despite prophylaxis: CVC, cord injury, initial SBP &lt; 80, pelvic / femur fracture</li> <li><b>IPC immediately</b>; <b>LMWH within 24–48 h</b> if bleeding allows</li> <li>TBI: LMWH after <b>72 h</b> shown safe; &lt; 48 h ↓ VTE but ↑ neurosurgery</li> <li>Doppler surveillance in high-risk</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Prophylactic IVC filter (only if both LMWH and IPC impossible)</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Lungs","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>VAP</b> = commonest infection: head up, oral care, least sedation; late-onset → MDR</li> <li>Contusions worsen over <b>24–48 h</b>; rib fractures in elderly → pneumonia</li> <li>Analgesia: <b>regional</b> (erector spinae, epidural, serratus anterior)</li> <li>ARDS: <b>6 mL/kg PBW</b>, limit plateau, PEEP; NMB; prone (caution unstable spine / pelvis); <b>VV-ECMO</b> possible even early</li> <li>Fat embolism (&lt; 5% of long-bone / pelvic fractures): hypoxaemia 90%, confusion 60%, petechiae 30%; <b>early fixation ↓ 70%</b>; supportive</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Rhabdomyolysis & AKI","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Risk: ISS &gt; 16, crush, mangled limb, compartment syndrome, BMI &gt; 30, limb ischaemia. <b>Severe: CK &gt; 5000 IU/L</b>; myoglobin &gt; 1217 µg/L predicts AKI</li> <li><b>Fluids ~400 mL/h initially</b> → UO 1–3 mL/kg/h (≥ 200 mL/h), urine pH &gt; 6.5; any crystalloid</li> <li>K⁺ ↑, PO₄ ↑, Ca ↓: treat Ca only if hyperkalaemic arrhythmia</li> <li>AKI 20–40%: MAP &gt; 65, KDIGO, limit contrast / nephrotoxins; RRT if anuric despite fluids</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Loop diuretics; bicarbonate / mannitol add nothing; no prophylactic RRT</li></ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Limb: compartment & vessels","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Compartment syndrome</b>: pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, tense, paraesthesia, paresis (pallor / pulselessness = too late); tibia, forearm, crush, &lt; 35 y</li> <li><b>Pressure &gt; 30 mmHg or DBP − compartment pressure &lt; 30</b>; continuous monitoring if sedated → <b>fasciotomy &lt; 24 h</b> (all 4 leg compartments)</li> <li>Blocks: low-concentration, single shot, keep checking</li> <li>Vascular hard signs → surgery / CTA; soft signs → <b>ABI</b>: &lt; 0.9 = injury (sens 87%, spec 97%)</li> <li>Warm ischaemia &gt; 6 h ↓ limb salvage; MESS alone must not decide amputation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Infection prevention","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Tetanus</b> status: immunoglobulin ± vaccine</li> <li><b>Open fracture</b>: antibiotics <b>≤ 3 h</b> — aminopenicillin + β-lactamase inhibitor (± aminoglycoside if heavy contamination); stop at <b>72 h</b> if no infection. Allergy: <b>clindamycin 900 mg + gentamicin 6–7 mg/kg</b></li> <li>Debride / irrigate / stabilise ≤ 24 h (open fracture ideally ≤ 2 h); infection 1% closed vs up to 25% open (50% Gustilo IIIC)</li> <li>Open facial fracture: prophylaxis ≤ 24 h. None for CSF leak, pneumocephalus, nasal packing</li> <li>Post-splenectomy: penicillin 2 years + pneumococcal, meningococcal, Hib vaccines from day 14</li> <li>Necrotic wound in prolonged illness: think <b>mucormycosis</b> → amphotericin B + debridement</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Sedation & analgesia","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Light sedation / daily interruption where injuries allow</li> <li><b>Ketamine</b>: stable haemodynamics, analgesia; short low-dose (cholestasis if prolonged high dose)</li> <li><b>Dexmedetomidine</b>: ↓ delirium, MV time; ↑ bradycardia, hypotension</li> <li>Volatiles: caution in ARDS (SESAR ↑ mortality); propofol first line</li> <li>Barbiturates only for refractory ICP (hypotension, infection, slow wake)</li> <li>Regional / nerve blocks; neuropathic pain → pregabalin, amitriptyline early (dose per CMH formulary)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Pain after limb trauma","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Severe pain (NRS ≥ 7): <b>IV morphine 0.1 mg/kg</b>; moderate (NRS 4–6): ketoprofen or paracetamol; inhaled methoxyflurane / intranasal ketamine act faster</li> <li><b>Ultrasound-guided nerve blocks</b> (complications 0.4%); hip fracture in elderly → <b>fascia iliaca block</b> (↓ opioids, delirium)</li> <li>Multimodal with ketamine, clonidine; uncontrolled pain → chronic pain / CRPS</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Sepsis after trauma","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Sepsis in <b>23%</b> ventilated ≥ 3 days; mostly <b>airway</b>, &lt; 10% wound / injury site</li> <li>Laparotomy for trauma: surgical-site infection 35%, independent of prophylaxis length; avoid <b>intra-operative hypothermia</b></li> <li>Fungal infection ~4% (dose-related with PN); fracture infection: staph / strep then Gram-negatives, weeks of therapy (IV 1–2 wk then oral)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Long ICU stay","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Young patients, abrupt illness; a subgroup develops <b>persistent critical illness</b> (inflammation, immunosuppression, catabolism) → late deaths, most bed-days</li> <li>Delayed / prolonged MOF recovery = high mortality</li> <li>Early physiotherapy and mobilisation after stabilisation; ↑ nutrition as patient stabilises, mobilises and is hungry</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Nutrition","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Stabilise first; then <b>some EN</b>, not early full feeding (bowel ischaemia if still on vasopressors)</li> <li>Abdominal trauma: withhold EN a few days; no early PN just because EN is impossible</li> <li><b>Daily phosphate</b>: &lt; 0.65 mmol/L or fall &gt; 0.16 → restrict intake + replace</li> <li>At-risk (alcohol, bariatric, poor intake): <b>thiamine 50–100 mg IV</b></li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Glutamine / arginine / high-dose micronutrients</li></ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Family & uncertainty","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Structured family meetings: team agrees first, private room, listen, prognosis in plain words, patient's values, shared decision</li> <li><b>Time-limited trial (TIME)</b>: Truth, Interval, Measurable outcomes, End or extend</li> <li>Bad news: <b>SPIKES</b>; consistent message from whole team</li> <li>Conflict: prevent, recognise, reframe, communicate, support; ethics / palliative input</li> </ul>"},{"n":"15","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do long definitive surgery on a cold, acidotic, coagulopathic patient (second hit)</li> <li>Wait for pallor or absent pulse to diagnose compartment syndrome</li> <li>Keep a pelvic binder on &gt; 6 h (trochanter pressure sores)</li> <li>Over-screen fever and over-treat with antibiotics (most fever non-infectious)</li> <li>Let fluid balance run positive once perfusion goals are met</li> </ul>"}]},"10c-major-intoxication":{"slogan":"ABC first · Check glucose in every altered patient · Always screen for paracetamol · Call the poisons centre when unsure","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Major Intoxications (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"History: what, how much, when","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Sources: patient, relatives, paramedics / police, containers, paraphernalia, notes; accept statements with caution</li> <li>Route, <b>time</b>, quantity, <b>slow-release</b> form, chronic use, co-ingestants (alcohol common), vomiting</li> <li>&lt; <b>10 × single therapeutic dose</b> rarely life-threatening — <b>except</b> opioids, paracetamol, chloroquine, lithium, digoxin, coumarins</li> <li>Life-threatening effects usually within <b>6 h</b>; slow-release cardiac drugs up to <b>24 h</b>; paracetamol 1–2 days</li> <li>Chronic users (lithium, salicylate): toxic at lower levels</li> <li>Worse with liver dysfunction (↑ first-pass drugs), low albumin (phenytoin), ↓ elimination; slow gastric emptying delays symptoms</li> <li>Overestimate rather than underestimate severity; pill imprints + <b>clinical pharmacist</b>; ask about herbal / OTC products</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Immediate actions (ABCD)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>O₂ if <b>SpO₂ &lt; 94%</b>; give O₂ anyway if breathing slow / irregular (opioid, GHB)</li> <li>Not intubated + altered → <b>recovery position</b>, nasopharyngeal airway</li> <li>RCT: <b>conservative strategy</b> (withholding intubation) in depressed consciousness ↓ mortality and stay</li> <li>Intubate for respiratory acidosis or hypoxaemia refractory to O₂</li> <li>Shock index (SBP/HR) <b>≥ 0.7–0.8</b> = shock; BP may be normal while compensated</li> <li>Vasodilatation most common → fluids, then <b>noradrenaline</b>; vasopressin in severe cases; avoid indirect catecholamines</li> <li>Young, vasodilated, no hypoperfusion / normal lactate → monitor only</li> <li><b>Glucose</b> in every altered mental state; GCS to track</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Toxidromes","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:12%\">Syndrome</th><th style=\"width:48%\">Signs</th><th>Common causes</th></tr> <tr><td>Anticholinergic</td><td>Delirium, tachycardia, <b>dry flushed skin</b>, mydriasis, myoclonus, hyperpyrexia, urinary retention, ↓ bowel sounds; seizures, arrhythmias</td><td>Antihistamines, scopolamine, antiparkinsonian, antipsychotics, antidepressants, antispasmodics, plants</td></tr> <tr><td>Sympathomimetic</td><td>Agitation, paranoia, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperpyrexia, <b>diaphoresis</b>, mydriasis, hyperreflexia; seizures, arrhythmias</td><td>Cocaine, amphetamines, bath salts, synthetic cannabinoids, decongestants, PCP, caffeine, theophylline</td></tr> <tr><td>Opioid</td><td>Coma, <b>respiratory depression, miosis</b> (pethidine: mydriasis), hypotension, bradycardia, hypothermia</td><td>All opioids, tramadol</td></tr> <tr><td>Sedative-hypnotic</td><td>Coma, respiratory depression, hypotension, hypothermia, hyporeflexia</td><td>Barbiturates, benzodiazepines, GHB, valproate, alcohols</td></tr> <tr><td>Serotonergic</td><td>Confusion, agitation, tremor, myoclonus, <b>hyperreflexia (legs), ocular clonus</b>, hyperpyrexia, sweating, tachycardia</td><td>SSRIs, tramadol, synthetic opioids</td></tr> <tr><td>Cholinergic</td><td>Salivation, lacrimation, <b>bronchorrhoea</b>, incontinence, cramps, emesis, miosis, brady/tachycardia, seizures</td><td>Organophosphates, carbamates, nerve gas, physostigmine, mushrooms</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Bedside clues","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Coma</b>: ~all improve within <b>12 h</b>; not improving / negative screen → look for other cause (CT if lateralising)</li> <li><b>Seizures</b> often precede arrhythmia, coma, arrest</li> <li>Slow RR → opioid; shallow normal RR → sedative; hyperventilation → salicylate, stimulants</li> <li>Cyanosis not fixed by O₂ → <b>methaemoglobin</b></li> <li>Bradycardia: β-blocker, CCB, clonidine, digoxin, organophosphate</li> <li>QT↑ / torsade: neuroleptics, TCAs, citalopram, methadone, antiarrhythmics</li> <li>Skin: track marks, patches, <b>pressure sores → compartments, CK</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Investigations","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ABG, electrolytes, <b>anion gap</b>, renal / liver, CK, ECG</li> <li><b>Osmolar gap</b>: calc. osm = 2 Na + urea + glucose (mmol/L)</li> <li>High-AG acidosis: CO, cyanide, ethylene glycol, methanol, iron, isoniazid, metformin, NRTIs, paracetamol, propofol, propylene glycol, toluene, salicylates, valproate</li> <li>Urine screens: limited, false +, miss fentanyl, GHB, Z-drugs</li> <li><b>Quantitative levels</b> guide therapy: paracetamol, salicylate, lithium, digoxin, iron, methanol, EG, theophylline, CO (whole blood for CO, cyanide, lead)</li> <li><b>Paracetamol screen always justified</b></li> <li>X-ray / CT: iron, batteries, body packs</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"ICU / HDU admission","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Suspected life-threatening dose</li> <li>Airway protection, respiratory failure, ventilation</li> <li>Arrhythmia or ECG conduction change</li> <li>Invasive monitoring, vasopressors / inotropes</li> <li>Coma or convulsions</li> <li>Antidote needing ICU</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Most recover in <b>12–24 h</b> with support. PPE for staff (blood-borne virus risk). Call the <b>poisons information centre</b> if unfamiliar.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Coma: is it really poisoning?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Headache, behaviour change, vomiting before coma → primary brain cause</li> <li>Mimics: encephalitis, SAH, brainstem stroke, subdural / extradural haematoma</li> <li>Cocaine / amphetamines themselves cause stroke and bleeds; a fall may accompany the overdose</li> <li>Found on hard ground → rhabdomyolysis, compartments, flexion myelopathy</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Lungs, gut & pupils","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Non-cardiogenic oedema: heroin / opioids, cocaine, EG, methanol, salicylates, chlorine, ammonia, paraquat</li> <li>Several patients with secretions, bronchospasm, miosis → <b>nerve gas</b></li> <li>Early severe abdominal pain → corrosives or iron; oral ulcers → acids / alkalis, paraquat</li> <li>Pinpoint: opioids, sedatives, PCP, pesticides; dilated: anticholinergic, sympathomimetic, pethidine, LSD, hypoxia</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Prevent absorption","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Skin: remove clothes, wash with soap + warm water; protect yourself</li> <li><b>Induced emesis: no.</b> Gastric lavage not routine (aspiration, perforation, hypoxaemia); endoscopy only for massive iron / slow-release / batteries, airway protected</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Activated charcoal 25–100 g</b> (usually <b>50 g</b>) within <b>1–2 h</b>; up to 4–6 h if severe or slow gastric emptying; charcoal ≥ 10 × drug mass</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Not adsorbed: iron, lithium, alcohols, hydrocarbons, electrolytes, cyanide, acids / alkalis</li> <li><b>Repeat doses every 2–4 h</b>: carbamazepine, theophylline, quinine, dapsone (+ enterohepatic, slow-release); ensure gut motility</li> <li><b>Whole bowel irrigation</b>: PEG <b>1–2 L/h</b> (child 250–500 mL/h) until clear (2–6 h) — lithium, iron, slow-release</li> <li>Body packers: conservative if asymptomatic; surgery if obstruction, perforation or toxicity; imaging must confirm clearance</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Enhance elimination & antidotes","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Haemodialysis</b> (most effective): salicylates, ethylene glycol, methanol, lithium, theophylline, potassium; CVVH less effective</li> <li><b>Alkaline diuresis</b>: urine pH <b>≥ 8.0</b>, flow <b>2–3 mL/kg/h</b> — salicylate, barbiturate</li> <li>Forced diuresis (3–5 mL/kg/h): no role</li> <li>Rhabdomyolysis: UO <b>1.5–2 mL/kg/h</b> with balanced crystalloid</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Naloxone</b> 100–400 µg IV every 1–2 min (to 2 mg) — aim for breathing, not full arousal</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Physostigmine 2 mg</b> slow IV for anticholinergic syndrome with altered mental state</div></div> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Routine <b>flumazenil</b> in unknown coma (seizures, re-sedation; contraindicated with TCAs, epilepsy, head injury, ↑ ICP)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Sodium bicarbonate & ventilation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>For <b>wide-QRS tachycardia / AV block</b> from Na-channel blockers: TCAs, class I antiarrhythmics (flecainide, propafenone, quinidine…), chloroquine, quinine, cocaine, bupivacaine, diphenhydramine, carbamazepine, thioridazine</li> <li>TCA / neuroleptic on MV: mild hyperventilation, <b>PaCO₂ 34–38 mmHg (4.5–5.0 kPa)</b>; correct hypercapnia before metabolic acidosis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Before discharge & do not","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Senior psychiatrist / social worker after recovery; psychiatric admission if suicidal crisis persists</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Gastric emptying after <b>hydrocarbons / corrosives</b> (absolute)</li> <li>Charcoal with unprotected airway, obstruction, planned endoscopy</li> <li>Use a qualitative urine screen to name the culprit or order screens that will not change management</li> </ul>"}]},"10d-poisoning-specific-agents":{"slogan":"QRS > 0.10 s → bicarbonate · Osmolar gap > 10 → fomepizole · Complete every NAC bag · Benzodiazepines for stimulants","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Major Intoxications (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Paracetamol","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Toxic single dose <b>150 mg/kg</b> (~8–10 g); lower if glutathione depleted (liver disease, malnutrition, alcohol, AIDS)</li> <li>GI symptoms 2–8 h → AST/ALT peak 48–72 h (severe &gt; <b>1000 IU/L</b>) → liver / kidney failure by 72–96 h</li> <li>Charcoal if ≤ 1–2 h, unknown time, or delaying co-ingestants</li> <li>Level <b>≥ 4 h</b> → Rumack-Matthew nomogram (single acute ingestion only)</li> <li>Staggered, chronic, or &gt; 24 h: treat if level <b>&gt; 10 µg/mL</b> or AST/ALT <b>&gt; 50 IU/L</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>NAC 200 mg/kg</b> (max 22 g) in 500 mL D5 over <b>4 h</b> → <b>100 mg/kg</b> (max 11 g) in 1000 mL D5 over <b>16 h</b><br/>Double-dose: second bag <b>200 mg/kg</b> (max 22 g) over 16 h</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Give all doses even if level falls; massive overdose → continue until level ↓ / transaminases falling</li> <li>Late presentation / ALF: NAC still ↓ cerebral oedema, ↑ survival; early hepatology (transplant)</li> <li>Anaphylactoid reaction → antihistamine (adrenaline if severe); CT head if altered</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Methanol & ethylene glycol","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Drunk-like, then latent (up to 24 h) → <b>high-AG acidosis + osmolar gap</b>; gap may be normal if late</li> <li>Methanol: visual loss, seizures (lethal ~1.2 mL/kg). EG: stage 1 (0.5–12 h) CNS → 2 (12–24 h) cardiopulmonary → 3 (24–72 h) AKI; ↓ Ca, oxalate crystals, falsely high POC lactate</li> <li>Antidote if known ingestion + <b>osmolar gap &gt; 10</b>, or suspicion + acidosis + gap &gt; 10 (level &gt; 200 mg/L)</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Fomepizole 15 mg/kg</b> over 30 min → <b>10 mg/kg every 12 h</b> until methanol undetectable or acidosis resolved</div></div> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\">Ethanol alternative: <b>50 g</b> load (50 mL absolute in 1 L D5) → <b>10–12 g/h</b>; target 100–150 mg/dL; ↑ dose on dialysis</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Haemodialysis</b>: &gt; 0.5 mL/kg, level &gt; 500 mg/L, <b>pH &lt; 7.15</b> / AG &gt; 24, vision loss, AKI, seizures, coma; stop when &lt; 200 mg/L + better; re-dose antidote</li> <li><b>Folinic / folic acid 50 mg every 4–6 h</b> (methanol); thiamine if alcohol misuse</li> <li>Treat only symptomatic ↓ Ca in EG; bicarbonate as temporiser</li> <li>Isopropanol: osmolar gap, no acidosis, ketones — supportive. Propylene glycol (lorazepam &gt; 0.1 mg/kg/h): stop drug</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Tricyclics & other antidepressants","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Signs within 2 h, life-threatening ≤ 6 h; coma, seizures (10–25%), anticholinergic, Na-channel block</li> <li>ECG: <b>QRS &gt; 0.10 s → seizures</b>, <b>&gt; 0.16 s → VT</b>; R in aVR &gt; 3 mm</li> <li>Early intubation if comatose; ventilate to mild alkalosis</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Sodium bicarbonate 8.4%</b> boluses to <b>pH 7.50–7.55</b> while QRS &gt; 0.10 s (target &lt; 0.10–0.12 s); may need 200–300 mL+; infusion; stop 4–6 h after resolution</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Hypotension: fluids → noradrenaline → <b>vasopressin 2–4 IU/h</b>; refractory: hypertonic saline, lipid, VA-ECMO</li> <li>Seizures: benzodiazepines. QRS &gt; 0.10 s at 6 h → monitor 24 h; normal 6 h → may leave ICU</li> <li><b>SSRIs</b>: QT/QRS (citalopram), hyponatraemia; ECG + 6 h observation. <b>Serotonin syndrome</b>: stop drugs, cool, benzodiazepines (cyproheptadine unproven); triggers in ICU: linezolid, ondansetron, opioids, tramadol</li> <li><b>Bupropion</b>: seizures, refractory shock / arrhythmia → lipid, VA-ECMO</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Physostigmine or flumazenil in TCA poisoning</li></ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"β-blockers & calcium channel blockers","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Brady + hypotension; CCB → <b>hyperglycaemia</b>; β-blocker → hypoglycaemia, seizures (lipophilic), sotalol QT/TdP</li> <li>Delay: sustained-release CCB, sotalol (up to 20 h)</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:21%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Initial IV</th><th>Maintenance / note</th></tr> <tr><td>Calcium chloride</td><td class=\"tgt\">10 mL 10% (1 g) slow</td><td>20–50 mg/kg/h · first line</td></tr> <tr><td>Glucagon</td><td class=\"tgt\">2–5 mg</td><td>2–10 mg/h · often not useful</td></tr> <tr><td>Insulin (HIET)</td><td class=\"tgt\">1 IU/kg</td><td>0.1–10 U/kg/h + glucose 10–75 g/h; watch K⁺</td></tr> <tr><td>Catecholamines</td><td>—</td><td>titrate; often little response</td></tr> <tr><td>Levosimendan / milrinone</td><td>—</td><td>may worsen vasodilatation</td></tr> <tr><td>Lipid 20%</td><td class=\"tgt\">1.5 mL/kg</td><td>0.25 mL/kg/min over 30–60 min; lab interference</td></tr> <tr><td>VA-ECMO / pacing</td><td>—</td><td>non-responders, ECMO centres</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">CaCl 10 mL 10% = 272 mg Ca (13.6 mEq) vs gluconate 93 mg. Atropine usually ineffective; not dialysable.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Opioids","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Coma + bradypnoea (4–6/min) + miosis; methadone QT; tramadol / pethidine seizures</li> <li>Non-cardiogenic oedema clears 24–48 h; <b>no diuretics</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Naloxone 100–400 µg IV</b> every 1–2 min (to 2 mg); synthetic: <b>2 mg</b> every 3–5 min, up to 10–20 mg</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Half-life 45–60 min → infusion <b>⅔–½ reversal dose per hour</b>, or intubate</li> <li>No response to 10 mg → rethink diagnosis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Sedatives & antipsychotics","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Flumazenil</b> only selected: <b>0.2 mg/min</b> to 3–5 mg (&gt; 1 mg rarely); re-sedation (t½ 0.7–1.3 h) → observe ≥ 2 h</li> <li>Barbiturate: MDAC, alkaline diuresis; <b>HD</b> (EXTRIP) for prolonged coma, shock, ventilation, rising level</li> <li>Antipsychotics: QT, AV block, hypotension → noradrenaline ± vasopressin; <b>Mg 2–4 g IV</b> for TdP / VT; HD ineffective</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Salicylates","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Resp. alkalosis + high-AG acidosis; <b>sepsis mimic</b>; acute-on-chronic mortality &gt; 25%</li> <li>Level at ~6 h, repeat 2-hourly until falling; fluids to UO 1–1.5 mL/kg/h</li> <li>Alkalinise (urine pH &gt; 7.5, keep K⁺ normal) if symptomatic or &gt; 35 mg/dL</li> <li><b>HD</b>: coma, pulmonary oedema, <b>pH ≤ 7.20</b>, &gt; 80–100 mg/dL acute or &gt; 40–50 chronic</li> <li>Intubation: bicarbonate first, high minute ventilation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Opioid & sedative pearls","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fentanyl analogues missed by screens; xylazine (“tranq”) → skin necrosis</li> <li>Bag-mask until breathing; naloxone IN / IM / SC or external jugular if no IV</li> <li>Charcoal even hours later if airway secure (slow gut)</li> <li>Arrest = hypoxaemia + hypercapnia → poor neuro outcome</li> <li>Benzodiazepine alone: severe depression rare (elderly, co-ingestion)</li> <li>Barbiturate: bullae in ~50% of lethal cases; asymptomatic at 6 h → discharge</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Antipsychotic pearls","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Symptoms within <b>6 h</b>; charcoal if early and awake</li> <li>Phenothiazines: α-block → hypotension resistant to catecholamines</li> <li>Seizure-prone: chlorpromazine, clozapine, loxapine (&gt; haloperidol); seizures precede coma, arrhythmia → benzodiazepines, often intubation</li> <li>Acute dystonia → anticholinergic or benzodiazepine</li> <li>Hypo-/hyperthermia; rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Stimulant complications","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Cocaine: seizures, ICH, stroke (stroke pathway incl. aspirin), MI, arrhythmia, bowel ischaemia, AKI, rhabdomyolysis; alcohol potentiates</li> <li>Amphetamines last up to 24 h; chronic → dilated cardiomyopathy</li> <li>MDMA: hyperthermia, paradoxical hypotension, DIC, liver necrosis</li> <li>Bath salts / synthetic cannabinoids: not on urine screens; may last &gt; 24 h</li> <li>Psychosis despite benzodiazepines → haloperidol (↓ seizure threshold)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Methaemoglobinaemia","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Local anaesthetics (prilocaine, benzocaine, EMLA), nitrites (“poppers”), dapsone, nitroprusside</li> <li>Cyanosis despite O₂, chocolate blood, <b>saturation gap</b> → co-oximetry</li> <li>20–30% headache · 30–50% dyspnoea · &gt; 60% coma, seizures · &gt; 70% lethal</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Methylene blue 1–2 mg/kg</b> over 5–10 min if symptomatic &gt; 20% or &gt; 30%</div></div> <div class=\"note\">G6PD deficiency: avoid; vitamin C or exchange.</div>"},{"n":"12","h":"Stimulants","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Cocaine, amphetamines, MDMA (↓ Na, seizures), bath salts, synthetic cannabinoids</li> <li><b>Benzodiazepines</b> first: agitation, hypertension, chest pain, seizures</li> <li>BP still high: clonidine, nicardipine, urapidil</li> <li>Seizure + ↓ Na → <b>3% saline</b>; temp <b>&gt; 40 °C</b> → aggressive cooling</li> <li>ACS: aspirin, nitrates; avoid β-blockers if sympathomimetic</li> <li>Check CK, renal, liver, coagulation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Others & do not","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>GHB</b>: fluctuating coma / agitation, apnoea; hours. Ketamine, LSD, PCP: agitation → benzodiazepines</li> <li>Herbal: aconite, glycosides → arrhythmia; kava → liver failure; ginkgo / ginger → bleeding</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Stop NAC early because the level fell</li> <li>Give diuretics for opioid pulmonary oedema</li> <li>Rely on atropine or heart rate in β-blocker / CCB shock</li> <li>Let pH fall at intubation (TCA, salicylate)</li> </ul>"}]},"11a-burns-initial-icu":{"slogan":"Parkland is only a starting point · Titrate to urine output · Keep them warm · Intubate early if the airway is burnt","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Burns: Initial Assessment & Management; Burns: ICU Management (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Assess size & depth","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ABCDE, then secondary survey; <b>failed to escape the fire</b> → look for other pathology (head injury, intoxication, CO / cyanide)</li> <li><b>%TBSA</b> (exclude superficial / erythema): Lund &amp; Browder (most accurate), rule of nines, <b>palm + fingers = 1%</b></li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:34%\">Depth</th><th>Looks like</th></tr> <tr><td>Superficial</td><td>Blanching erythema, painful; heals</td></tr> <tr><td>Superficial partial</td><td>Moist, pink, blisters; heals</td></tr> <tr><td>Deep partial</td><td>Dry, cherry-red; may need surgery</td></tr> <tr><td>Full thickness</td><td>White, waxy, charred, painless; always surgery</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"2","h":"Airway: decide early","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Suspect with face / neck burns, closed-space fire, prolonged extrication</li> <li><b>Supraglottic</b>: singed nasal hair, lip / tongue swelling, hoarseness, dysphagia, <b>stridor</b></li> <li><b>Infraglottic</b>: carbonaceous sputum, dyspnoea, wheeze, hypoxia</li> <li>Swelling peaks <b>after fluid resuscitation</b> → low threshold to intubate early</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Large-bore, UNCUT</b> endotracheal tube (bronchoscopy, debris; won't pull back as face swells)</div></div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Cyanide (smoke from plastics)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Smoke inhalation + <b>lactic acidosis not responding to fluids</b> or with <b>high venous SO₂</b>; seizures, ↓ consciousness, CV collapse</li> <li>Levels too slow → treat on clinical grounds</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Hydroxocobalamin 5 g IV over 15–30 min</b> (child 70 mg/kg); repeat if needed</div></div> <div class=\"note\">Orange-red skin / urine ~48 h; interferes with lab tests.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Burn shock: fluids (first 24–48 h)","html":"<div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">IV fluids if <b>≥ 15% TBSA</b> (child ≥ 10%) · <b>Parkland 4 mL × kg × %TBSA</b> balanced crystalloid (Hartmann's) in 24 h: <b>½ in first 8 h from injury, ½ over next 16 h</b></div></div> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">Titrate hourly to <b>urine output 0.5–1 mL/kg IBW/h</b> — too little ↑ rate, too much ↓ rate (avoid fluid creep)</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Also: peripheral perfusion, lactate, haematocrit, acid-base; cardiac output monitoring improves DO₂ (no mortality benefit)</li> <li><b>Albumin rescue</b>: add <b>4.5% human albumin</b> if 24-h volume projected at 12 h to exceed <b>6 mL/kg/%TBSA</b>; continue to 48 h</li> <li>Low UO not always hypovolaemia: ATN, <b>rhabdomyolysis</b> (electrical), ADH, vasoplegia, low CO, <b>abdominal compartment</b></li> <li>Hypotension despite fluids → <b>noradrenaline / vasopressin</b> to keep renal perfusion (dose per CMH formulary)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>0.9% saline as main fluid (hyperchloraemic acidosis); rigid formula adherence; over-resuscitation (lung water, IAH, graft failure, hyponatraemia)</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Circumferential burns","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Deep circumferential burn of limb, neck, chest, abdomen → eschar limits swelling → ischaemia / poor ventilation</li> <li><b>Early escharotomy</b> (fasciotomy if muscle, e.g. electrical)</li> <li><b>Abdominal compartment</b>: IAP normal 5–7 mmHg; measure <b>bladder pressure</b>; escharotomy, loosen dressings, sedation, NMB, GI decompression, diuresis / ultrafiltration</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Temperature","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Each 1 °C lower on arrival → <b>+5% odds of death</b></li> <li><b>Room 28–33 °C</b>, minimal exposure, remove wet linen, foil / hats, forced-air warmers, heat lamps, warm fluids, intravascular warming; core temperature</li> <li>Later fever is usual (reset set-point); <b>&gt; 39.5 °C</b>: reduce dressings, paracetamol, ice to unburnt skin, cold IV fluids / bladder / gastric lavage, cooling catheter</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Refer & transfer","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Burn centre: <b>&gt; 25% TBSA</b> adult (&gt; 15% child), needs ICU / early surgery, excision &gt; 10%, face / hands / feet / perineum, circumferential, chemical, electrical</li> <li>Transfer once stable and monitored; <b>active warming</b>; cling film dressing, <b>never circumferential</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Prognosis","html":"<div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Revised Baux = age + %TBSA (+ 17 if inhalation injury)</b></div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>&lt; 65 rarely die · <b>LD50 ≈ 110</b> · <b>≈ 160 = futility</b> · &gt; 165 usually die</li> <li>Frailty (CFS) independently ↑ mortality → early goals-of-care talk</li> <li>Mass casualty: triage by age + TBSA; no transfer for outpatient or expectant (&gt; 90% mortality)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Sepsis: diagnose & treat","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Infection causes <b>42–65% of burn deaths</b>; SIRS universal if &gt; 30% → follow <b>trends</b></li> <li><b>ABA: infection + ≥ 3</b>: T &gt; 39 or &lt; 36.5 °C · HR &gt; 110 · RR &gt; 25 (MV &gt; 12 L/min) · platelets &lt; 100 (&gt; 3 days after resuscitation) · glucose &gt; 11.1 mmol/L or insulin &gt; 7 U/h · feed intolerance</li> <li><b>PCT rise &gt; 2 ng/mL from baseline</b> → consider sepsis; WCC useless</li> <li>Regular wound-swab surveillance guides therapy; <b>continuous infusion</b> + drug levels (high Vd)</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:30%\">Organism</th><th>ACE drug choice</th></tr> <tr><td>Staph / Strep / Enterococcus</td><td>Penicillins; MRSA → vancomycin; VRE → linezolid</td></tr> <tr><td>Pseudomonas</td><td>Piperacillin-tazobactam; MDR → colistin</td></tr> <tr><td>Acinetobacter / ESBL</td><td>Carbapenems</td></tr> <tr><td>Enterobacteriaceae</td><td>Cephalosporins</td></tr> <tr><td>Candida / Aspergillus</td><td>Azoles, amphotericin / echinocandins</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"10","h":"Feed the hypermetabolism","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>REE 2–3× normal (&gt; 2× if &gt; 40% TBSA), lasts years</li> <li><b>EN within 6–12 h</b> (NG, continuous); post-pyloric if gastric stasis / repeated anaesthesia; PN rarely</li> <li>Keep weight loss <b>&lt; 10%</b> of admission weight; dietitian; indirect calorimetry best (formulae: Harris-Benedict, Toronto)</li> <li><b>Protein 1.5–2 g/kg/day</b>; glucose ≤ 55% of energy (insulin as needed); <b>lipid ≤ 30%</b> (count propofol)</li> <li>Early, often IV, Cu, Se, Zn, vitamins B, C, D, E</li> <li><b>Propranolol</b> (non-selective) started ≤ 72 h in &gt; 20% TBSA ↓ REE; hypotension in 11.9% (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Oxandrolone 10 mg twice daily from day 5 if ≥ 30% — FDA approval withdrawn 2023, seldom available</li> <li>Debridement + grafting, exercise / physiotherapy</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Glutamine (no benefit); overfeeding (↑ glucose, TG, CO₂, steatosis)</li></ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Why timing matters","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Zones: coagulation (dead) · <b>stasis (salvageable</b> — lost with hypotension, oedema, infection) · hyperaemia (recovers)</li> <li><b>Ebb phase 48–72 h</b>: capillary leak + myocardial depression = burn shock</li> <li><b>Flow phase</b>: hyperdynamic, CO 2–3× baseline, catabolism, insulin resistance, immunoparesis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Wound infection clues","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Invasive infection: surrounding cellulitis, deepening burn, eschar separation, new necrosis</li> <li>Early Gram-positives; <b>Pseudomonas</b>: green-yellow, foul smell, necrosis</li> <li>MDR risk: broad-spectrum antibiotics, colonised on admission, escharotomy, long stay, many operations</li> <li>Fungal wound infection (Candida, Aspergillus, moulds) independently ↑ mortality</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"After discharge","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Effects last <b>years</b>: infections, ischaemic heart disease, heart failure, stroke, fractures, joint stiffness, epilepsy, cancer, all-cause mortality ↑</li> <li>Mental-health admissions ~5× (self-harm, alcohol / drugs)</li> <li>Peer support, charities, follow-up pathways for patient and family</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Pain plan","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:26%\">Pain</th><th>IV phase → enteral</th></tr> <tr><td>Background</td><td>Regular paracetamol + opioid ± <b>ketamine</b> infusion → oral, gabapentinoid</td></tr> <tr><td>Breakthrough</td><td>IV opioid bolus → immediate-release</td></tr> <tr><td>Procedural</td><td>Short-acting opioid bolus, N₂O / methoxyflurane, ketamine</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">CPOT if sedated. NSAIDs rarely (bleeding, AKI). Dexmedetomidine: brady, hypotension, pyrexia. Stop opioids early (dependence).</div>"},{"n":"15","h":"Lungs, kidneys, mind","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ARDS: lung-protective ventilation, conservative fluids, NMB; <b>prone only with surgeons</b> (grafts); ECMO for refractory hypoxaemia; steroids only after risk–benefit</li> <li>AKI: hypovolaemia, ATN, myoglobin, abdominal compartment</li> <li><b>PTSD up to 45%</b>, anxiety / depression 7–46%; pain at discharge predicts suicidal ideation → routine psychology screening</li> </ul>"},{"n":"16","h":"Cardiac arrest & do-nots","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Arrest: scene (asphyxia, toxic gas, electrocution) · early 24–48 h (under-resuscitation, K⁺, airway) · late (sepsis, MOF)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Count superficial (erythema) burns in %TBSA</li> <li>Cut the endotracheal tube</li> <li>Expose the whole patient for long, or transfer cold</li> <li>Rely on silver sulfadiazine (poorer healing)</li> </ul>"}]},"11b-burns-inhalation":{"slogan":"SpO₂ lies in CO poisoning · Normal gases do not clear the airway · Leave the tube uncut · Inhalation = +17% TBSA","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Burns: Inhalation Injury (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise the four injuries","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Type</th><th style=\"width:22%\">Cause</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Features</th><th>Diagnose with</th></tr> <tr><td>Upper airway</td><td>Heat (thermal)</td><td>Hoarseness, odynophagia, <b>stridor</b>, soot / singed hair, facial burns</td><td>Look in mouth, <b>flexible nasendoscopy</b>, laryngoscopy</td></tr> <tr><td>Tracheobronchial</td><td>Chemical irritants, soot (steam = thermal)</td><td>Dyspnoea, cough, wheeze, carbonaceous sputum; casts, bronchorrhoea</td><td><b>Fibreoptic bronchoscopy</b> (gold standard)</td></tr> <tr><td>Parenchymal</td><td>Inflammation → leak</td><td>Hypoxaemia, ARDS (CXR may be normal early)</td><td>Imaging, P/F ratio</td></tr> <tr><td>Systemic toxins</td><td><b>CO</b>, <b>hydrogen cyanide</b></td><td>↓ consciousness, haemodynamic lability</td><td><b>COHb (co-oximetry)</b>, lactate, high venous SO₂</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Risk: <b>enclosed space</b>, poor ventilation, long exposure / entrapment, coma. Inhalation injury needs up to <b>50% more resuscitation fluid</b> and adds the mortality of <b>+17% TBSA</b> (revised Baux).</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"First hour bundle","html":"<div class=\"split\" style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(4,1fr);gap:1.2mm\"> <div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Airway first</b>: risk of upper-airway burn? Doubt → nasendoscopy → intubate early, uncut standard tube</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>100% O₂</b> from the start; send <b>COHb (co-oximetry)</b>, lactate, venous SO₂</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">Smoke + lactate not clearing with fluid or high venous SO₂ → <b>hydroxocobalamin 5 g</b></div></div> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\">Once intubated: <b>bronchoscopy</b> (AIS grade); plan fluids for up to <b>+50%</b> requirement</div></div> </div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Upper airway: secure it in time","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Oral / supraglottic burn: throat pain, tongue swelling, drooling secretions, dysphonia, stridor → obstruction can be sudden</li> <li><b>Normal gas exchange is NOT reassuring</b> (late sign)</li> <li>Unclear? <b>Nasendoscopy</b> to decide on intubation</li> <li>Intubate with a <b>standard-diameter, UNCUT tube</b>; re-secure deeper as the face swells over the first <b>48 h</b></li> <li>Supportive: <b>sit up</b>, anti-reflux medication → oedema settles in days</li> <li>Tracheostomy if facial burns need healing or usual indications; within 10 days ↓ ICU / hospital stay</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Before extubation: oedema gone on <b>repeat laryngoscopy / nasendoscopy</b> or a positive <b>cuff-leak test</b></div></div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Lower airway: bronchoscopy & toilet","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">AIS grade</th><th>Bronchoscopic appearance</th></tr> <tr><td>0 none</td><td>No soot, erythema, oedema, bronchorrhoea</td></tr> <tr><td>1 mild</td><td>Patchy erythema, soot in bronchi</td></tr> <tr><td>2 moderate</td><td>Moderate erythema, soot, bronchorrhoea ± bronchial compromise</td></tr> <tr><td>3 severe</td><td>Friable, copious soot, bronchorrhoea, obstruction</td></tr> <tr><td>4 massive</td><td>Mucosal sloughing, necrosis, obliteration</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Any injury on bronchoscopy → longer MV, ICU stay, ↑ mortality</li> <li>Toilet: regular suction, chest physio, mobilisation; <b>BAL only for proximal obstruction</b> with poor gas exchange</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Prophylactic antibiotics or steroids</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Damage by level","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Heat is dissipated above the cords → <b>thermal injury below cords rare</b> (except superheated steam)</li> <li>Below cords: chemicals + particulates → hyperaemia, bronchorrhoea</li> <li>Clotting activation → <b>fibrin casts</b> + oedema + soot → obstruction</li> <li>Mucociliary failure → <b>lower respiratory infection</b></li> <li>Severe: ulceration, sloughing</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Who to suspect","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fire, chemical spill, industrial accident</li> <li>Severity ↑ with substance, small particles, gas solubility, <b>duration of exposure</b></li> <li>Enclosed, poorly ventilated space → incomplete combustion</li> <li>Lower airway: dyspnoea, wheeze, cough, <b>carbonaceous secretions</b>; CXR often normal early → later consolidation / bilateral infiltrates</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Toxin clues at the bedside","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CO</b>: anaemic hypoxia — O₂ displaced from Hb; most common toxin</li> <li><b>Cyanide</b>: histotoxic hypoxia — lactic acidosis, high venous SO₂, seizures, altered mental state (NMDA)</li> <li>Unexplained coma or CV collapse after smoke → treat both</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Nebulised therapy (evidence weak)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Heparin 5000–10,000 units nebulised every 4 h</b>, alternating with N-acetylcysteine and bronchodilator (observational); RCT of <b>5000 units 4-hourly</b> + NAC + albuterol: more ventilator-free days, same mortality</li> <li><b>N-acetylcysteine</b>: mucolytic, antioxidant — can cause bronchospasm → give with bronchodilator (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li><b>Salbutamol</b> (β-agonist; benefit in animal models) only for potentially reversible airflow obstruction</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">HEPBURN RCT stopped early (poor recruitment); flagged <b>nebuliser filter saturation</b> and <b>bleeding risk</b>. Older heparin studies non-randomised, historical controls.</div> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Routine β-agonists in ARDS (linked to harm)</li></ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"ARDS in burns","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Lung-protective ventilation</b>: limit Vt and plateau; circumferential chest-wall burns or dressings → poor compliance, high airway pressures</li> <li>Burn hypermetabolism → hypercapnia on low Vt → adjust RR and/or Vt</li> <li><b>Prone</b>: no burn-specific evidence; risks tube loss (swollen airway), graft failure, ↑ IAP, feed intolerance</li> <li><b>NMB</b>: no burn-specific evidence; consider with high minute volume (high RR ± larger Vt to limit respiratory acidosis)</li> <li>Severity by P/F ratio (Berlin); ARDS can follow burns without inhalation (inflammation + big fluid volumes)</li> <li><b>ECMO</b> rescue: outcomes similar to non-burn ARDS; weigh anticoagulation vs planned surgery</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Carbon monoxide","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Enclosed fire, entrapment, exhaust suicide attempt, faulty heater</li> <li><b>Pulse oximetry overestimates oxygenation</b> → measure <b>COHb by co-oximetry</b></li> <li>Level at arrival may look falsely low after delay / O₂; smokers baseline 3–10%; COHb 60% → cardiorespiratory collapse</li> <li>Worse with high minute ventilation, metabolic rate, anaemia</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>100% O₂</b> until <b>COHb &lt; 5%</b> · half-life 4–5 h → 40–80 min (100% O₂) → 23 min (hyperbaric 2 atm)</div></div> <div class=\"note\">Hyperbaric O₂ in severe cases (↓ late neuro sequelae) — often impractical in critical burns. <b>Pregnancy</b>: fetus at greater risk (higher affinity, longer half-life) → hyperbaric may have a role.</div>"},{"n":"11","h":"Hydrogen cyanide","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Burning plastics / synthetics; blocks cytochrome oxidase → cells cannot use O₂</li> <li>Suspect: smoke exposure + <b>lactic acidosis not responding to fluids</b> or with <b>high venous SO₂</b>; seizures, coma, CV collapse</li> <li>Levels too slow → <b>treat clinically</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Hydroxocobalamin 5 g IV over 15–30 min</b> (child 70 mg/kg) · repeat if needed</div></div> <div class=\"note\">Benign orange-red skin and body fluids for ~48 h.</div>"},{"n":"12","h":"Recovery & follow-up","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Allied health (speech / swallow) to <b>rehabilitate bulbar function</b></li> <li>Long-term: none → mild scarring → <b>laryngotracheal stenosis</b> needing surgery</li> <li>Early-tracheostomy RCT (single centre): no benefit; registry of 9173 patients: ≤10 days ↓ ICU / hospital stay</li> <li>VOCs and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons: effects beyond the lung incl. carcinogenesis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Trust SpO₂ or a normal blood gas to rule out airway or CO injury</li> <li>Delay intubation until stridor is obvious and the face has swollen</li> <li>Cut the endotracheal tube or use a small tube</li> <li>Repeated saline / bicarbonate lavage to clear soot routinely (surfactant loss, atelectasis)</li> <li>Wait for a cyanide level before giving hydroxocobalamin</li> </ul>"}]},"12-infection-antimicrobials":{"slogan":"Sample first · treat fast and hard · de-escalate · stop","legend":"PCT = procalcitonin · TDM = therapeutic drug monitoring · MDR = multidrug-resistant","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Rational use of antimicrobials in the ICU (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Is it infection? Start or wait?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fever, tachycardia, vasoplegia, sputum, ↑/↓ WCC, lactate, consolidation can all be <b>non-infectious</b>; CRP/PCT cannot reliably separate sterile from septic inflammation</li> <li>Of presumed sepsis admissions, ≈ 25% are <b>sepsis mimics</b></li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>Start antibiotics now</th><th>Watchful waiting is reasonable</th></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">Proven infection (targeted)</td><td>Undifferentiated fever, <b>not hypotensive</b></td></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">Immediately life-threatening (suspected bacterial meningitis, meningococcal sepsis)</td><td>Catheter colonised by low-virulence organism (catheter removed)</td></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\"><b>Hypotension</b> from suspected infection</td><td>Ventilator-associated condition not infection-related</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Watchful waiting = close monitoring in HDU/ICU (ideally 1:1 nursing) while investigating and searching for a source.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Timing","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"big\">≤ 1 hour</div><div class=\"mid\">septic shock or high likelihood of sepsis (SSC 2021): the earlier the better</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Possible sepsis without shock:</b> rapid assessment infectious vs not; if suspicion persists start within <b>3 h</b></li> <li><b>Cultures before antibiotics</b> whenever this does not delay treatment</li> <li><b>Source control within 12 h</b>: drain collections/abscesses, debride, excise necrosis, remove infected devices</li> <li>Remove a suspected infected line <b>after other access is established</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Sample the right thing","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Blood cultures:</b> ≈ 20 mL per set split aerobic + anaerobic; <b>2–4 sets</b> → sensitivity &gt; 80%</li> <li>Sample relevant sites: superficial wound swabs mostly grow colonisers; nasopharyngeal swabs essential for viruses</li> <li>VAP: tracheal aspirate (sensitive) vs BAL / PSB (more specific). Thresholds <b>10⁵–10⁶ (TA), 10⁴ (BAL), 10³ (PSB)</b></li> <li>Talk to the microbiologist about test choice, handling and transport</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">When results arrive</div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>0–2 h</td><td>Gram stain (CSF, ETA, ascites, pleural fluid)</td></tr> <tr><td>2–8 h</td><td>Antigens (urine <i>Legionella</i>/pneumococcus, galactomannan, cryptococcal), multiplex PCR</td></tr> <tr><td>24–48 h</td><td>Gram stain / MALDI-TOF / PCR on positive culture</td></tr> <tr><td>&gt; 48 h</td><td>Susceptibility (AST) → escalate / de-escalate; 16S PCR, serology</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Choose the empirical regimen","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Appropriate</b> = covers the pathogen; <b>adequate</b> = appropriate + early + optimal dose and interval. Inappropriate initial therapy independently ↑ mortality</li> <li>Base on <b>local guidelines and unit ecology</b> (differs between wards), site of infection (penetration), patient factors (allergy, organ failure, devices, immune defects, drug interactions, prior colonisation)</li> <li>ICU-acquired / hospital ≥ 5 days → more MDR</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">MDR risk in VAP</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>IV antibiotics within 90 days (also for MDR HAP, MRSA, <i>Pseudomonas</i>), septic shock at onset, preceding ARDS, ≥ 5 days in hospital, RRT before VAP</li></ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Two or more drugs</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Broaden coverage in septic shock</b> (e.g. add vancomycin for MRSA)</li> <li><b>Synergistic combination</b> (β-lactam + aminoglycoside / quinolone / macrolide): unproven; consider in shock, not routinely in sepsis; advised with polymyxins, tigecycline, fosfomycin</li> <li>Clindamycin (toxin) for streptococcal shock; macrolide for severe CAP</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Dose it right: PK/PD (standard product doses often under-dose the critically ill; higher initial doses especially for hydrophilic drugs)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Drug / class</th><th style=\"width:16%\">PD driver</th><th style=\"width:28%\">Target (ACE)</th><th>Dosing (ACE)</th></tr> <tr><td>β-lactams</td><td>Time (fT&gt;MIC)</td><td class=\"tgt\">100% fT&gt;MIC in ICU; 1–4 × MIC (4× for resistant)</td><td><b>Extended or continuous infusion</b> (loading dose recommended with continuous infusion); adjust to changing renal function</td></tr> <tr><td>Aminoglycosides</td><td>Concentration</td><td class=\"tgt\">Cmax/MIC 8–10</td><td>High-dose, once-daily / extended interval (dose per CMH formulary)</td></tr> <tr><td>Vancomycin</td><td>AUC/MIC</td><td class=\"tgt\">AUC/MIC ≥ 400 · trough 15–20 mg/L</td><td><b>Load 25–30 mg/kg</b>, then <b>15–20 mg/kg every 8–12 h</b>; loading also for continuous infusion; TDM (nephrotoxicity)</td></tr> <tr><td>Fluoroquinolones</td><td>AUC24/MIC</td><td class=\"tgt\">Cmax/MIC 8–10:1; AUC 125–250</td><td>Dose per CMH formulary</td></tr> <tr><td>Colistin</td><td>AUC/MIC</td><td class=\"tgt\">Trough &lt; 2.4 mg/L (neuro/nephrotoxicity)</td><td><b>Loading dose</b> by weight and target MIC (CMS prodrug converts slowly); 60% renal clearance; combine with another agent</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Hydrophilic (↑ Vd in sepsis/fluids, renal clearance): β-lactams, aminoglycosides, glycopeptides, daptomycin. Lipophilic: quinolones, macrolides, linezolid, tigecycline, clindamycin, metronidazole, tetracyclines. Use TDM and renal/weight nomograms where available.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"48–72 h review: de-escalate","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Actively chase cultures</b> (usually 24–72 h) and reassess: falling vasopressor, shock reversal, falling inflammatory markers?</li> <li>Empiric therapy active → <b>narrow to the narrowest effective agent</b> (e.g. carbapenem → 3rd-gen cephalosporin for susceptible Enterobacterales; vancomycin → oxacillin for MSSA)</li> <li>Pathogen not covered → change: escalate or switch mechanism</li> <li>Review every day like sedation or ventilation; use TDM and prolonged infusions</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"How long?","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>5–7 days</td><td>Most pneumonias, unobstructed UTI, intra-abdominal infection with source controlled (even with sepsis)</td></tr> <tr><td>7–14 days</td><td>Meningitis, complicated soft tissue, UTI/abdominal with delayed source control; <i>S. aureus</i> bacteraemia, candidaemia (look for focus)</td></tr> <tr><td>&gt; 14 days</td><td>Endocarditis, bone/joint, brain abscess, lung/pleural/renal abscess</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Longer in immunocompromise (HIV, chemotherapy, neutropenia)</li> <li><b>PCT:</b> measure every 2–3 days; <b>stop when ≥ 80% fall from peak</b> if improving; needs ≥ 2 values; never replaces clinical review</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Stewardship in the ICU","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>≈ 70% of ICU patients receive an antibiotic: the ICU is the stewardship front line</li> <li>Team: <b>pharmacist, clinical microbiologist, ID specialist</b> + intensivist</li> <li>Goals: right drug, dose, route and duration → cure, less resistance, fewer adverse events, lower cost</li> <li><b>Restrictive</b> tools (prior authorisation for last-resort agents) + <b>enabling</b> tools (education, audit and feedback)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Spectrum ladder (narrow → very broad)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Narrow</td><td>Penicillin, oxacillin, cefazolin, aztreonam, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, macrolides, clindamycin, linezolid, metronidazole</td></tr> <tr><td>Moderate</td><td>Ampicillin, piperacillin, cefuroxime, cefoxitin, ciprofloxacin, co-trimoxazole</td></tr> <tr><td>Broad</td><td>Amoxicillin-clavulanate, ampicillin-sulbactam, ceftriaxone, ceftazidime, cefepime, levofloxacin, doxycycline</td></tr> <tr><td>Very broad</td><td>Piperacillin-tazobactam, imipenem, meropenem, ertapenem, moxifloxacin, colistin</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"10","h":"Not improving?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Microbiological failure:</b> resistance → repeat blood and site cultures; escalate according to local resistance patterns</li> <li><b>Uncontrolled focus:</b> abscess, empyema, obstruction → <b>image</b> and call surgeons / interventional drainage</li> <li>Under-dosing → check levels (TDM)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Start antibiotics on a CRP or PCT value alone</li> <li>Give standard product-label doses to septic patients without thinking about PK</li> <li>Continue broad-spectrum therapy once cultures allow narrowing</li> <li>Treat colonisation (e.g. superficial swab growth)</li> <li>Use synergistic combinations routinely in sepsis without shock</li> </ul>"}]},"12b-infection-prevention":{"slogan":"Clean hands · bundles every day · remove every device you don't need","legend":"HAI = healthcare-associated infection · CRBSI / CAUTI / VAP","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Infection prevention and control (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Know the target","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>HAI</b> = not present or incubating on admission; may appear after discharge; often MDR organisms</li> <li>Mostly <b>device-associated</b>: VAP (tube), CRBSI (vascular lines), CAUTI (urinary catheter)</li> <li>Preventable with consistent evidence-based practice: <b>CRBSI and CAUTI up to 65–70%</b>, <b>VAP up to 55%</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Colonisation ≠ infection</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Colonisation = multiplying organisms without tissue damage, host response or symptoms (e.g. tracheal colonisation, catheter bacteriuria)</li> <li>Every infection is preceded by colonisation, but most colonisation never becomes infection</li> <li><b>Antibiotics do not clear colonisation or prevent infection</b>; they select resistance</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Five pillars for every ICU","html":"<div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">Strict hand hygiene = non-negotiable baseline</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Regular, thorough <b>environmental disinfection</b></li> <li><b>Care bundles</b> (VAP, CRBSI, CAUTI) with <b>daily review of every invasive device</b></li> <li>Structured <b>education</b> for all staff with patient contact</li> <li><b>Surveillance</b> of HAI rates (e.g. per 1000 device-days) for early detection of rising rates</li> <li><b>Adequate nurse staffing</b>: poor nurse:patient ratio independently ↑ HAI risk</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>Routine prophylactic antibiotics (e.g. before intubation) to prevent HAI: <b>not advised</b> (drives resistance)</li></ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"VAP bundle (VAP = commonest ICU infection: up to 50% of ventilated patients; 3%/day week 1, 2%/day week 2, then 1%/day)","html":"<div class=\"split3\"> <div><div class=\"sub l\">Essential practices</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Avoid intubation</b> when safe: HFNO / NIV (but don't delay needed intubation); prevent reintubation</li> <li><b>Minimise sedation</b>: avoid benzodiazepines, sedation + weaning protocols, <b>daily SBT</b></li> <li><b>Head of bed 30–45°</b>; early mobilisation</li> <li><b>Daily tooth brushing, without chlorhexidine</b></li> <li><b>Early enteral nutrition</b> (within 48–72 h) rather than early PN</li> <li>Change ventilator circuit <b>only if soiled or malfunctioning</b></li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub l\">Additional approaches</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Subglottic secretion drainage</b> tube if IMV expected &gt; 48–72 h</li> <li><b>Early tracheostomy</b> (&lt; 7 days): ≈ 40% less VAP, no mortality change</li> <li><b>Post-pyloric feeding</b> if gastric intolerance or high aspiration risk</li> <li><b>SOD / SDD</b> only where resistance is low (<b>&lt; 5% BSI due to ESBL</b>)</li> </ul></div> <div><div class=\"sub l\" style=\"color:var(--red)\">Not recommended for VAP prevention</div> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Chlorhexidine oral care / bathing (possible ↑ mortality)</li> <li>Probiotics</li> <li>Silver-coated / ultrathin / tapered cuff tubes; automated or frequent cuff-pressure checks</li> <li>Kinetic beds; closed suction systems</li> <li>Stress-ulcer prophylaxis, gastric residual monitoring, early PN, prone positioning (not for VAP)</li> </ul></div> </div> <div class=\"note\"><b>Diagnosis</b> needs clinical + microbiological data; no gold standard; 20–30% false positives. <b>VAE</b> surveillance (CDC): <b>VAC</b> = ↑ daily min PEEP ≥ 3 cmH₂O or FiO₂ ≥ 20 points for ≥ 2 days after ≥ 2 stable days → <b>IVAC</b> (+ T, WBC ≤ 4000 or ≥ 12 000, new antibiotics ≥ 4 days) → <b>possible VAP</b> (+ positive respiratory culture / purulence).</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"CRBSI bundle","html":"<div class=\"sub l\">Insertion</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Avoid femoral</b> (subclavian lowest CRBSI)</li> <li>Hand hygiene + <b>maximal barrier precautions</b> (gloves, gown, large drapes)</li> <li><b>Chlorhexidine 2%</b> skin prep (better than povidone-iodine)</li> <li>Ultrasound with sterile probe cover and gel</li> <li>Nurse <b>empowered to stop</b> unsafe practice</li> <li>Antimicrobial-impregnated CVC (minocycline-rifampicin, silver-chlorhexidine): long-term / high risk when other measures maximised</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Maintenance &amp; removal</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Minimise handling; hand hygiene before every access; hub care; dedicated CVC team</li> <li><b>Dedicated lumen for PN</b>; chlorhexidine washing</li> <li><b>Daily documented review</b>; remove at earliest opportunity (risk ↑ after 5–7 days)</li> <li>Lines inserted unsterile → replace as soon as possible</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>No routine scheduled CVC replacement</li><li><b>No guidewire exchange</b> for CRBSI or exit-site infection</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"CAUTI bundle","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Catheter only with indication</b>; reassess daily; remove early (e.g. surgical patients 24–48 h post-op)</li> <li>Oliguric/anuric AKI/CKD: consider <b>intermittent catheterisation</b> guided by bladder ultrasound</li> <li>Aseptic insertion: hand hygiene, sterile gloves, drapes, periurethral antisepsis; <b>smallest size</b>; secure it</li> <li><b>Closed drainage</b>, no kinks, <b>bag below bladder</b> at all times</li> <li>Replace system only if sterility broken (no fixed-interval changes)</li> <li>Document indication + insertion date; <b>nurse-driven removal protocols</b></li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">Suspected CAUTI: how to sample</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Not needed → <b>remove</b>, then midstream sample</li> <li>Needed but in place &gt; 10–14 days → <b>replace</b>, then sample</li> <li>Recently placed → disinfect and aspirate from sampling port</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>Never sample from the bag</b></li> <li>No surveillance urine cultures or pyuria screening; smell/appearance not diagnostic</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"How VAP happens","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Micro-aspiration</b> of secretions pooling above the cuff = main mechanism</li> <li>Tube bypasses airway defences; ICU therapies (sedation, supine, antibiotics)</li> <li>Microbiome shift (dysbiosis) + altered immunity (alveolar macrophages)</li> <li>Each VAP: longer ICU stay, ≈ 25% ↑ relative mortality risk, attributable 3–10%</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"How CRBSI happens","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Commonest route: <b>skin organisms along the insertion tract</b>; then hub contamination</li> <li>1.8–5.2 BSI / 1000 catheter-days (CDC)</li> <li>Arterial and <b>peripheral</b> lines also cause BSI (low rate, high numbers)</li> <li>PN through a CVC lumen ↑ risk</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"CAUTI risk factors","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Modifiable:</b> dwell time (bacteriuria → ≈ 100% by 30 days), unnecessary catheter, poor insertion, poor maintenance, late removal</li> <li><b>Non-modifiable:</b> age, female sex, diabetes, immobility, urological abnormality / instrumentation</li> <li>CAUTI is a <b>clinical diagnosis</b>: bacteriuria / pyuria alone insufficient</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Daily device round (ask at every ward round, document the answer)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Device</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Daily question</th><th style=\"width:28%\">Check</th><th>Do not</th></tr> <tr><td>Endotracheal tube</td><td>Ready for SBT / extubation? Sedation the minimum?</td><td>Head up 30–45°, tooth brushing, enteral feed, subglottic drainage</td><td class=\"av\">Chlorhexidine mouthwash; routine circuit change</td></tr> <tr><td>CVC / arterial line</td><td>Still indicated? Can it come out today?</td><td>Site, dressing, hub care, dedicated PN lumen, insertion date</td><td class=\"av\">Scheduled replacement; guidewire exchange for infection</td></tr> <tr><td>Urinary catheter</td><td>Hourly output still needed?</td><td>Closed system, bag below bladder, no kinks</td><td class=\"av\">Bag samples; routine urine cultures</td></tr> <tr><td>Antibiotics</td><td>Infection or colonisation?</td><td>Cultures taken correctly before starting</td><td class=\"av\">Prophylaxis to \"prevent\" HAI</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"10","h":"Blood cultures done right","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Suspected CRBSI: culture <b>from the CVC + 2 peripheral sets</b> simultaneously (IDSA)</li> <li>Antiseptic technique: skin, hands, sterile gloves, mask; <b>alcohol on bottle diaphragm</b></li> <li><b>8–10 mL blood per bottle</b>; adequate number of sets</li> <li>Incubate within <b>12 h</b>; catheter tip culture when at risk</li> <li>Staphylococci, Enterobacterales, <i>Candida</i>: treat as real even in one set; low-pathogenicity organism in one set = likely contaminant</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Six steps that cut CRBSI to zero","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Antiseptic hand wash + alcohol rub</li> <li>Full aseptic precautions</li> <li>Chlorhexidine 2% skin preparation</li> <li>Avoid the femoral route</li> <li>Minimise duration of placement</li> <li>Empower the nurse to stop unsafe practice</li> </ul>"}]},"12c-antimicrobial-pkpd":{"slogan":"Full loading dose always · then dose to the kidney you see today","legend":"Vd = volume of distribution · CL = clearance · ARC = augmented renal clearance","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Antimicrobial PK and PD and need for dose optimisation in critically ill patients (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Why standard doses fail in ICU","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Standard dose = <b>starting point</b>, not the right dose for every patient; many ICU patients miss PK/PD targets on guideline doses (DALI)</li> <li><b>Vd ↑</b> (capillary leak + fluid resuscitation) for <b>hydrophilic</b> drugs: β-lactams, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, fluconazole, daptomycin, colistin → low early levels. Vd sets the <b>loading dose</b></li> <li><b>Clearance</b> sets the <b>maintenance dose</b>: swings between ARC, AKI and recovery</li> <li><b>Hypoalbuminaemia</b>: ↑ free fraction of highly bound drugs; faster distribution and elimination; total levels mislead</li> <li><b>Gut absorption</b> unreliable in shock (vasopressors, opioids, oedema, PPIs) → <b>IV first</b>; oral switch once stable</li> <li>Sanctuary sites (CNS, bone, prosthesis, abscess, prostate, lung ELF): choose a drug that penetrates; <b>more dose may not fix poor penetration</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Know the PK/PD driver","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>Driver</th><th>Drugs</th><th>Dosing logic</th></tr> <tr><td>Cmax/MIC (concentration)</td><td>Aminoglycosides</td><td>High-dose, <b>once daily</b>; long post-antibiotic effect</td></tr> <tr><td>fT&gt;MIC (time)</td><td>β-lactams</td><td><b>Prolonged / continuous infusion</b> after loading dose</td></tr> <tr><td>AUC24/MIC (exposure)</td><td>Fluoroquinolones, vancomycin, most triazoles</td><td>Once or twice daily by half-life</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Only <b>free (unbound) drug</b> is active; measure free levels where possible</li> <li>MIC varies by one two-fold dilution: <b>use EUCAST breakpoints / ECOFF</b>, not a single patient MIC, to individualise</li> <li>Regimens should reach the target in <b>≥ 90% of patients</b> (PTA) without toxic exposure</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Bedside dosing pathway","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Phase</th><th style=\"width:38%\">Action (ACE)</th><th>Why</th></tr> <tr><td>Loading dose (first dose)</td><td class=\"tgt\">Full or even increased dose; <b>never reduce for AKI</b></td><td>Vd expanded in early sepsis (e.g. gentamicin 24 → 30–40 L; piperacillin 15 → 28 L in an 80 kg patient)</td></tr> <tr><td>Early maintenance (0–48 h)</td><td class=\"tgt\">Do not cut doses of wide-window drugs (β-lactams) in first 24–48 h unless severe toxicity expected</td><td>AKI may resolve; premature reduction → prolonged under-exposure</td></tr> <tr><td>β-lactam infusion</td><td class=\"tgt\">Loading dose → <b>prolonged or continuous infusion</b></td><td>BLING III + meta-analysis: better target attainment and modest ↓ mortality; recommended in sepsis guidelines</td></tr> <tr><td>Later maintenance</td><td class=\"tgt\">Dose to the elimination route; <b>reassess renal function daily</b></td><td>Patients move between ARC, AKI and recovery</td></tr> <tr><td>Renal function</td><td class=\"tgt\"><b>Measured CrCl</b> (8 h or 24 h urine) rather than eGFR</td><td>Detects ARC and recovering function; equations mislead</td></tr> <tr><td>TDM</td><td class=\"tgt\">Standard for <b>vancomycin, aminoglycosides, voriconazole</b>; β-lactam TDM in ARC, RRT, ECMO, obesity, burns, high MIC</td><td>Narrow window or high variability</td></tr> <tr><td>Change in support</td><td class=\"tgt\">Re-dose when RRT / ECMO / plasma exchange starts, stops or changes</td><td>Clearance and Vd change abruptly</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Augmented renal clearance (ARC)","html":"<div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Measured CrCl &gt; 130 mL/min/1.73 m²</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>30–75% of ICU patients; often lasts several days</li> <li>At risk: <b>age &lt; 50–60</b>, trauma, neurosurgery, burns, early sepsis, no CKD</li> <li>Under-exposure to β-lactams, vancomycin, aminoglycosides, fluconazole</li> <li>→ <b>higher maintenance dose, shorter interval or prolonged infusion</b>; TDM</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub l\">AKI (up to 50% of ICU)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"><li>Keep the loading dose; adjust <b>maintenance</b>. Accumulation toxicity: nephro (vancomycin, aminoglycosides, aciclovir, ganciclovir), <b>neuro (cefepime, β-lactams)</b>, marrow (ganciclovir, flucytosine)</li></ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Extracorporeal support","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CRRT:</b> removes small, low protein-bound, low-Vd drugs; usually lower doses than normal kidneys suffice, but <b>delivered dose &lt; prescribed</b> (downtime, clotting, transport); count residual renal function</li> <li><b>ECMO:</b> circuit sequestration of lipophilic / highly bound drugs (voriconazole, caspofungin); priming haemodilution; ECMO alone does not mandate dose change</li> <li><b>ECMO + RRT:</b> no formal guidance → PK principles + TDM (aminoglycosides, glycopeptides, triazoles, β-lactams)</li> <li><b>Plasma exchange:</b> give drug <b>after</b> TPE or after distribution phase; consider supplemental dose for high-risk drugs (e.g. ceftriaxone); time TDM samples around TPE</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Vancomycin","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Target <b>AUC/MIC 400–600</b> (MIC 1 mg/L), Bayesian AUC-guided dosing preferred</li> <li>Trough 15–20 mg/L = older surrogate; more nephrotoxicity</li> <li>Continuous infusion (after load): faster target, less nephrotoxicity</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Aminoglycosides","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Extended-interval (once-daily)</b> = standard: high peak, low trough, less nephrotoxicity</li> <li>Peak = efficacy; trough = toxicity (TDM essential)</li> <li>Obese: dose on <b>adjusted body weight</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Antifungals","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Fluconazole</b> (renal): under-exposed in ARC, obesity, RRT → higher load and maintenance</li> <li><b>Voriconazole:</b> routine TDM (non-linear, CYP2C19, interactions, inflammation); low = failure, high = liver/neuro toxicity</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"PK terms that change the dose","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Bioavailability (F)</td><td>IV = 100%; ↓ with poor gut absorption → decides IV vs oral</td></tr> <tr><td>Cmax</td><td>Peak level: key for aminoglycosides</td></tr> <tr><td>AUC</td><td>Total exposure: quinolones, vancomycin, azoles</td></tr> <tr><td>Vd</td><td>Sets the <b>loading dose</b>; ↑ with leak, fluids, oedema</td></tr> <tr><td>Clearance (CL)</td><td>Renal + non-renal; sets the <b>maintenance dose</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Liver</td><td>↓ flow (shock, high PEEP), IL-6 suppresses CYP3A4/2C19 → slower metabolism (e.g. voriconazole)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"10","h":"Ways to individualise (learn & confirm)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Step 1:</b> is optimisation needed? (high PK variability expected)</li> <li><b>Higher dose for all:</b> rarely optimal (toxicity)</li> <li><b>Change mode</b> of administration (prolonged infusion) without changing total dose</li> <li><b>Stratified dosing / nomograms</b> by renal function or weight: works even with limited resources</li> <li><b>TDM:</b> useful if done well; errors from sample timing, assay, delayed reports, trough-only targets</li> <li><b>MIPD:</b> Bayesian model + 1–2 levels predicts best dose; ↑ target attainment (DOLPHIN), outcome benefit not yet proven</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Special patients & interactions","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Obese:</b> hydrophilic drugs modest ↑ Vd, lipophilic large ↑ Vd; choose weight descriptor per drug; maintenance by renal function</li> <li><b>Burns:</b> ↑ Vd early, then weeks of ARC → higher load, higher maintenance, frequent TDM</li> <li><b>Pregnancy:</b> GFR ↑ ≈ 50% → under-exposure with standard doses</li> <li><b>Children</b> are not small adults: dose by age, size, development, organ function</li> <li><b>Carbapenem + valproate:</b> valproate levels fall rapidly → avoid, or add levetiracetam</li> <li>Triazoles ↑ tacrolimus; rifampicin/phenytoin ↓ triazoles</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Administration pitfalls","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Start a continuous infusion <b>without a loading dose</b> (delays target by several half-lives)</li> <li>Leave drug in long lines, bags, syringes: <b>flush the line</b></li> <li>Ignore stability: meropenem, amoxicillin-clavulanate degrade after reconstitution</li> <li>Co-infuse incompatibles: <b>ceftriaxone + calcium</b>, <b>piperacillin-tazobactam + vancomycin</b></li> <li>Reduce the first dose because of AKI</li> </ul>"}]},"13-drug-dosing-aki":{"slogan":"Sepsis + AKI: do NOT cut antibiotic doses in the first 48 h · Reassess every 24 h · Use TDM","legend":"ACE content only · ACE gives no drug-specific doses for this topic","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Drug Dosing during Acute Kidney Injury and Renal Replacement Therapy (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Assess kidney function at the time of prescribing","html":"<div class=\"dose green\"><div class=\"mid\">AKI (KDIGO 2012, stages 1–3): stage 1 = creatinine <b>1.5–1.9 × baseline</b> or rise <b>≥ 0.3 mg/dL (≥ 26.5 µmol/L)</b>, or urine-output criterion</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Preferred bedside method = MEASURED creatinine clearance (UV/P)</b>: U = urine creatinine (µmol/L), V = urine volume per unit time (mL/min), P = serum creatinine (µmol/L)</li> <li>Collect <b>≥ 1 h of urine</b> (“flash creatinine clearance”); <b>repeat</b> whenever GFR is changing fast</li> <li>Cockcroft-Gault, MDRD, CKD-EPI: <b>not validated in critically ill AKI</b>; use only for <b>baseline</b> function on admission, not during the ICU stay</li> <li>De-index eGFR if needed: <b>GFR (mL/min) = eGFR × BSA / 1.73</b></li> <li><b>Sarcopenia</b> (pre-existing or ICU-acquired) → low creatinine production → <b>falsely “normal” eGFR</b></li> <li>GFR is dynamic: <b>re-evaluate regularly</b> and re-dose as it changes</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"How AKI / ARC change PK → how to adapt","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Change</th><th style=\"width:36%\">Consequence</th><th>Adapt</th></tr> <tr><td>AKI: ↑ Vd (fluid overload, ↓ albumin)</td><td>↓ Cmax, sub-therapeutic levels (hydrophilic, low-MW, low protein-binding drugs most affected)</td><td class=\"tgt\">↑ LOADING dose</td></tr> <tr><td>AKI: ↓ clearance</td><td>↑ Cmin, accumulation, toxicity</td><td class=\"tgt\">↓ MAINTENANCE dose</td></tr> <tr><td>AKI: ↑ half-life (t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL)</td><td>↑ Cmin, ↑ AUC</td><td class=\"tgt\">↑ interval and/or intermittent regimen</td></tr> <tr><td>ARC: CrCl &gt; 130 mL/min/1.73 m²</td><td>↓ Cmin, under-dosing, treatment failure (sepsis, trauma, fluids, vasopressors)</td><td class=\"tgt\">↑ maintenance dose; ↓ interval or continuous infusion</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"dt\" style=\"margin-top:.6mm\"> <li><b>Active metabolites accumulate</b> in AKI (e.g. <b>morphine, midazolam</b>), with therapeutic or toxic effect</li> <li>Enteral absorption unpredictable: gut oedema, ↓ splanchnic flow, catecholamines, PPI / enteral feed pH effects; prefer IV in the sickest</li> <li>↓ non-renal (CYP-450) clearance may also occur</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Sepsis / septic shock with AKI: the critical first 48 h","html":"<div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1.1fr 1fr 1fr;gap:1.5mm;align-items:stretch\"> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"big\">FULL doses</div><div class=\"mid\">No dose reduction of renally eliminated antibiotics in the first ~48 h (high Vd, high risk of under-dosing)</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\" style=\"text-align:left\">When starting antibiotics in sepsis / septic shock, <b>kidney function is NOT the primary consideration</b></div></div> <div class=\"dose navy\"><div class=\"mid\" style=\"text-align:left\">Aggressive PD targets (e.g. empirical <b>Pseudomonas at 4 × MIC</b>) need extra vigilance, especially on RRT</div></div> </div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Dose by PK/PD class","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:19%\">Class (examples)</th><th style=\"width:16%\">PD target</th><th style=\"width:33%\">Strategy</th><th>On RRT</th></tr> <tr><td>Concentration-dependent (aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones)</td><td class=\"tgt\">Cmax / MIC</td><td>High initial dose to reach Cmax above MIC, then adjust doses to keep the trough in range (avoid toxicity)</td><td>IHD: give the dose <b>after the session</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Time-dependent (β-lactams)</td><td class=\"tgt\">fT ≥ MIC</td><td><b>Loading dose + continuous infusion</b>; consider switching to intermittent infusion after <b>24–48 h</b> (continuous infusion risks supratherapeutic levels)</td><td>High risk of sub-therapeutic levels, especially on CRRT: <b>continuous infusion</b> usually recommended</td></tr> <tr><td>Concentration + time (vancomycin)</td><td class=\"tgt\">AUC<sub>0–24</sub> / MIC</td><td>Dose to AUC target; TDM</td><td>Adjust to modality and settings; TDM</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"5","h":"RRT modality and drug removal","html":"<div class=\"sub\">IHD (diffusion, small molecules &lt; 500 Da)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>4–6 h sessions; Qb <b>250–450 mL/min</b>, Qd <b>500–800 mL/min</b>; ICU patients often need more frequent sessions</li> <li>Temporary catheters give Qb only <b>150–300 mL/min</b> (fistula &gt; 400) → delivered dose lower than in chronic HD</li> <li>Poorly tolerated in unstable patients (large fluid removal in a short time)</li> </ul> <div class=\"sub\">CRRT (CVVH convection · CVVHD diffusion · CVVHDF both)</div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Prescribe 30–35 mL/kg/h</b> effluent to <b>deliver 20–25 mL/kg/h</b> (KDIGO); <b>&gt; 35 mL/kg/h not recommended</b></li> <li>Lower dose acceptable if the goal is fluid control only</li> <li>Prescribed clearance <b>over-estimates</b> delivered: <b>adjust drug doses if CRRT is interrupted</b> (clotting, access, transport)</li> <li><b>Pre-filter</b> replacement dilutes blood → <b>less drug removal</b></li> <li>Hybrid (SLED, EDD): little PK data</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Estimate extracorporeal clearance","html":"<div class=\"kbox\" style=\"text-align:center;line-height:1.5\"> CL<sub>CVVH</sub> = Q<sub>eff</sub> × SC · CL<sub>CVVHD</sub> = Q<sub>d</sub> × SA<br/> CL<sub>CVVHDF</sub> = (Q<sub>eff</sub> + Q<sub>d</sub>) × SA · CL<sub>IHD</sub> = Q<sub>d</sub>/Q<sub>b</sub> × SA </div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>SA / SC</b> (0 = not removed, 1 = freely removed); without levels estimate <b>SA ≈ 1 − protein binding</b></li> <li><b>Drug factors</b>: molecular weight, Vd, protein binding, SA/SC</li> <li><b>RRT factors</b>: modality, Qb, Qd, pre/post-dilution, duration, membrane (type, pore size, surface), residual urine output</li> <li>RRT replaces filtration only: <b>no tubular secretion or metabolism</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Monitoring & re-dosing loop","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Initial dose from published tables once RRT modality and settings are known; note these mostly derive from <b>CKD</b>, not AKI</li> <li><b>Re-evaluate the regimen at least every 24 h</b> (PK changes are dynamic)</li> <li>On RRT, <b>serum creatinine / estimated clearance are unreliable</b>; residual kidney function is hard to estimate</li> </ul> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Antibiotic TDM</b>: particularly indicated in AKI and RRT</li> <li><b>RRT-specific nomograms</b> now exist for <b>meropenem</b> and <b>piperacillin/tazobactam</b> (by modality, intensity, urine output, PK/PD target)</li> <li>Prescription software / model-informed precision dosing where available</li> </ul> </div>"},{"n":"8","h":"Baseline eGFR formulas (admission only)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:22%\">Formula</th><th style=\"width:40%\">Strength</th><th>Limitation</th></tr> <tr><td>Cockcroft-Gault (mL/min)</td><td>Simple, familiar; good in normal-weight patients</td><td>Ignores body size, age extremes; needs weight</td></tr> <tr><td>MDRD (mL/min/1.73 m²)</td><td>Advanced CKD</td><td>Biased at high GFR</td></tr> <tr><td>CKD-EPI (mL/min/1.73 m²)</td><td>More accurate than MDRD at higher GFR; all adult ages</td><td>Derived in &lt; 66 y, BMI 27–28; imprecise at very low / high GFR</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Creatinine production follows muscle mass, excretion follows kidney function: they diverge in critical illness.</div>"},{"n":"9","h":"Key do-nots (ACE)","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Do not reduce renally cleared antibiotic doses in early sepsis / septic shock</li> <li>Do not use creatinine-based formulas to dose during the ICU stay or on RRT</li> <li>Do not set CRRT effluent &gt; 35 mL/kg/h (no benefit; electrolyte and nutrient losses)</li> <li>Do not assume the prescribed RRT dose was delivered</li> <li>Do not treat ARC as normal function: it needs higher / optimised dosing</li> <li>Do not rely on CKD-derived dosing tables alone: reassess daily and use TDM</li> </ul>"}]},"14a-nutrition":{"slogan":"No feeding in shock · Start low, go slow · No PN in days 1–3/7 · Check phosphate daily","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Nutrition Parts I–V (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Screen & assess on admission","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Everyone staying &gt; <b>48 h</b> in ICU = at risk of malnutrition (ESPEN)</li> <li>High risk: <b>NUTRIC ≥ 5</b> (&gt; 5 if IL-6 used) or NRS-2002 — ICU cut-off <b>5</b> (most ICU patients score 3 for severity); no tool picks who benefits from more feeding</li> <li>History: low intake, alcohol, drugs, chronic diarrhoea; weight loss <b>≥ 5% in 1 month or ≥ 10% in 3 months</b></li> <li>Exam: muscle wasting (clavicle, deltoid), fat loss (triceps, eye pads), oedema, glossitis / mucosal lesions (SGA)</li> <li>BMI &gt; 30 → ideal / adjusted weight; IBW (kg) = 2.2 × BMI + 3.5 × BMI × (height − 1.5 m); sarcopenic obesity is high risk</li> <li>Albumin, transferrin, prealbumin, trace elements, vitamins: <b>not</b> for initial assessment (inflammation, fluids)</li> <li>Note propofol 1% = <b>1.1 kcal/mL</b> (prefer 2%), citrate, CRRT losses, drain protein up to 6 g/L</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"When to start — timeline","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Phase</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>Shock / resuscitation</td><td class=\"tgt\">No feeding — may cause non-occlusive bowel necrosis</td></tr> <tr><td>Stable, &lt; 48 h</td><td>Oral, or <b>low-dose EN within 48 h</b> if oral not expected within 3 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Days 1–3/4</td><td>Ramp up gradually; accept moderate deficit; acute-phase target <b>≤ 70% of EE</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Days 3/4–7</td><td>EN insufficient → <b>add PN only after day 3–7</b>; supplemental PN if EN &lt; 60% target</td></tr> <tr><td>After day 7</td><td>Full dose via EN, or supplemental / total PN</td></tr> <tr><td>Any time</td><td>Oral / EN &lt; <b>80%</b> of target → give IV vitamins, trace elements, minerals</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Early high-dose feeding ↑ metabolic damage, blocks autophagy and does not prevent muscle loss (anabolic resistance). Early EN ≈ isocaloric early PN (CALORIES, NUTRIREA-2).</div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Targets","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Item</th><th>ACE target</th></tr> <tr><td>Energy (after acute phase)</td><td class=\"tgt\">20–25 or 25–30 kcal/kg/day</td></tr> <tr><td></td><td>Higher end: young, recovering, male; lower: old, immobile; IBW if BMI &gt; 30</td></tr> <tr><td>Protein</td><td class=\"tgt\">Restrict in acute phase; later ≤ 1.3 g/kg/day</td></tr> <tr><td></td><td>Basal 0.8–1.3 g/kg/day; early 2.0 g/kg/day no benefit / harm; more in burns, GI / urine losses</td></tr> <tr><td>Glucose (PN)</td><td>≤ <b>6 g/kg/day</b>, ≤ <b>5 mg/kg/min</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Lipid</td><td>9 kcal/g; excess (&gt; 23 mg/kg/min or 55% of energy) → steatosis; give lipid ≥ weekly</td></tr> <tr><td>Micronutrients</td><td>PN has none — add IV until EN ≥ 80% target</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Indirect calorimetry most accurate; not proven superior — <b>EE ≠ need</b> (matching EE early = overfeeding)</li> <li>Fever ↑ EE ~10% per °C — don't add calories for it; deep sedation / burst suppression → may lower target</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Enteral nutrition — can I feed the gut?","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>Absolute CI</b>: oral &gt; 80% target; GI perforation / obstruction without distal access; uncontrolled shock; bowel ischaemia; abdominal compartment syndrome</li> <li><b>Relative</b>: fast ≤ 3 days; uncontrolled hypoxaemia / hypercapnia / acidosis; gastric aspirate &gt; 500 mL/6 h; active upper GI bleed; high aspiration risk; bowel &lt; 70 cm; high-output fistula without distal access</li> </ul> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Do NOT delay EN for</b>: NMBA, hypothermia (low dose, ↑ after rewarming), ECMO, prone, TBI, stroke, spinal injury, severe pancreatitis, GI / aortic surgery, open abdomen, abdominal trauma (once continuity restored, perfusion OK), IAP &lt; 20 mmHg (watch trend), ALF (after correcting life-threatening metabolic problems), no bowel sounds, diarrhoea</li> <li>High-risk EN: severe shock, vomiting, sphenoid fracture (no blind tube), severe ileus</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"EN practice","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fine-bore 6–12 Fr NG; length xiphoid → ear → nostril (stomach ≈ 60 cm)</li> <li><b>Confirm by X-ray</b> (UK: aspirate pH &lt; 5.5 else X-ray); <b>no NG in basal skull fracture</b></li> <li>Start <b>10–25 mL/h</b>, ↑ by 10–25 mL/h to target; &gt; 100 mL/h → aspiration, ischaemia, distension</li> <li>Head up <b>30–45°</b></li> <li>GRV: working limit <b>500 mL</b>, ~twice daily; can be omitted in established EN</li> <li>Prokinetics: <b>erythromycin 250 mg IV 6-hourly</b>; <b>metoclopramide 10 mg IV 8-hourly</b></li> <li>Post-pyloric (NJ) if gastric fails; blind bedside success 70–&gt; 90%; X-ray to confirm</li> <li>PEG if EN ≥ 4–6 weeks; wait 7 days afebrile; antibiotic prophylaxis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Formula & GI problems","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Standard <b>polymeric</b>, ~300 mOsm/L, 1–2 kcal/mL (CHO 45–60%, fat 20–35%, protein 15–20%); fibre-enriched more physiological</li> <li>Semi-elemental only for persistent diarrhoea on polymeric, malabsorption, short bowel</li> <li>Disease-specific (renal, hepatic, pulmonary) formulas: no convincing data</li> <li>500 mL containers; EN-only connectors; change tubing daily</li> <li>Diarrhoea &gt; 3 days (other causes excluded) → stool culture + <i>C. difficile</i> toxin</li> <li>Colonic pseudo-obstruction / NOMI → <b>stop EN</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Parenteral nutrition","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Central line; dedicated single lumen / port; change tubing daily</li> <li>Peripheral PN: ≤ 900 mOsm/L, 2.5–3.5 L/day; cannula ≤ 72 h; midline ≤ 6 days; PICC possible</li> <li>All-in-one bags preferred; run continuously</li> <li><b>Binary bag</b> (no lipid): steatosis, ↑ LFTs / TG, high-dose propofol</li> <li>No routine glutamine (harm) or BCAA</li> <li>Suspected line sepsis with shock → stop PN, central + peripheral cultures, remove catheter, antibiotics</li> <li>No routine CVC change</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Refeeding syndrome","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Risk: little / no intake &gt; 10 days, <b>BMI &lt; 18.5</b>, weight loss, abnormal electrolytes, alcohol, insulin / chemo / antacids / diuretics — can follow only 4–5 days off feeds</li> <li>Signs: ↓ phosphate (± K, Mg) within hours of glucose (even 5% dextrose diluent); lactic acidosis, Wernicke, heart failure / VF, weakness</li> <li>Define: phosphate fall ≥ 0.16 to <b>&lt; 0.65 mmol/L</b> within 72 h of feeding</li> <li>Prevent: <b>phosphate on admission + daily</b>; thiamine <b>100–300 mg IV</b> if at risk; IV micronutrients; slow increase</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>If it occurs:</b> restrict to <b>max 20 kcal/h for 2 days</b>, then back to normal over 2–3 days, while correcting phosphate, K, Mg, vitamins, trace elements (13% more survived)</div></div>"},{"n":"9","h":"Glucose control","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:26%\">Strategy</th><th>Start insulin when glucose &gt;</th></tr> <tr><td>Liberal</td><td>10.3–11.9 mmol/L (185–215 mg/dL) — no protocol, eating, no art / CVC line, inexperienced team</td></tr> <tr><td>Intermediate</td><td class=\"tgt\">8.3–10.0 mmol/L (150–180 mg/dL)</td></tr> <tr><td>Tight</td><td>Target 4.4–6.1 mmol/L only if achievable without severe hypoglycaemia</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Safe control: accurate meter, arterial blood, IV infusion — <b>no insulin boluses</b>; computer algorithm (LOGIC-Insulin)</li> <li>NICE-SUGAR: tight control → 14× severe hypoglycaemia, ↑ mortality</li> <li>Hypoglycaemia on PN: sudden stop of glucose (kinked line), excess insulin, sepsis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Monitoring","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Daily: phosphate, K, Mg, glucose; GI tolerance (abdomen, vomiting, distension, diarrhoea)</li> <li>Overfeeding: severe hyperglycaemia, ↑ triglycerides, ↑ urea : creatinine</li> <li>LFTs (AST, ALT, bilirubin) ≥ twice weekly on PN (&gt; 60% abnormal after 2 weeks); amylase / lipase regularly</li> <li>TG &gt; 4 mmol/L (350 mg/dL) → reduce lipid; &gt; 6 mmol/L → look for sepsis, hyperglycaemia, pancreatitis, excess energy / lipid, propofol</li> <li>Re-assess at least weekly and with CRRT, surgery, new sepsis, fever / cooling, ventilation or sedation change, rehab</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Don't feed during resuscitation or shock</li> <li>Don't give full calories / high protein in the first week</li> <li>Don't start PN in the first 3–7 days</li> <li>No routine immunonutrients (glutamine, arginine, omega-3, high-dose vitamin C / E, selenium, zinc) — harm with glutamine in MOF, vitamin C in septic shock</li> <li>Don't use ventilator-built EE readings (not validated)</li> <li>Avoid vitamin A and manganese in severe liver failure</li> <li>Glucose maintenance fluids are probably not needed</li> </ul>"}]},"14b-abdomen":{"slogan":"Examine the abdomen daily · Measure IAP if at risk · CT with IV contrast · Source control is time-critical","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – The Abdomen in Critical Care Parts I–IV (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Daily GI assessment (COSMOGI)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Pain</b>: NRS / VAS; BPS on abdominal palpation if non-communicating. Sedated: distension, feeding intolerance, unexplained organ failure, ↑ lactate, shock or sepsis may be the only signs</li> <li><b>Gastroparesis</b>: GRV &gt; 500 mL or vomiting from slow emptying; GRV not routine — use if vomiting, distension, aspiration risk, recent surgery</li> <li><b>Bowel dilatation “3–6–9”</b>: small bowel &gt; 3 cm, colon &gt; 6 cm, caecum &gt; 9 cm (&gt; 12 cm high risk)</li> <li><b>Lower GI paralysis</b>: no stool ≥ 3 days despite laxatives / prokinetics, no obstruction — think peritonitis, ischaemia</li> <li><b>Diarrhoea</b>: ≥ 3 loose stools/day + ≥ 200–250 g/day + Bristol 6–7</li> <li><b>Clinically important GI bleed</b>: overt + haemodynamic change, ≥ 2 RBC units/24 h, Hb fall ≥ 2 g/dL, or intervention needed</li> <li>CT abdomen with <b>IV contrast</b> is the main test (non-contrast ~30% less accurate); don't withhold contrast for renal worry; CTA if ischaemia possible; plain film only for tubes / bowel diameter</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Grade it — acute GI injury (AGI)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Grade</th><th style=\"width:28%\">Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr> <tr><td>I</td><td>Risk — transient, known cause</td><td>PONV, absent bowel sounds after surgery</td></tr> <tr><td>II</td><td>GI dysfunction — needs intervention to meet needs</td><td>High GRV, lower GI paralysis, diarrhoea, IAP 12–15, blood in gastric content / stool</td></tr> <tr><td>III</td><td>GI failure — not restored despite treatment, MODS persists</td><td>EN intolerance despite erythromycin / post-pyloric tube, worsening dilatation, IAP 15–20</td></tr> <tr class=\"tgt\"><td>IV</td><td>Failure with distant organ impact, life-threatening</td><td>Bowel ischaemia / necrosis, haemorrhagic shock, Ogilvie, ACS needing decompression</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">GIDS (0–4) adds symptoms, prokinetic use and catastrophic events; 4 = GI bleed with shock, mesenteric ischaemia or ACS. ≥ 3 GI symptoms at once independently predicts death.</div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Intra-abdominal hypertension / ACS","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>IAH I</th><th>IAH II</th><th>IAH III</th><th>IAH IV</th><th>ACS</th></tr> <tr><td>12–15</td><td>16–20</td><td>21–25</td><td>&gt; 25</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 20 + new organ failure</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Bladder: ≤ <b>25 mL</b> room-temp saline, supine, end-expiration, zero at mid-axillary line / iliac crest, wait 30–60 s; normal 5–7 mmHg</li> <li>Measure <b>≥ 4-hourly</b> if unstable; at risk: pancreatitis, rAAA, abdominal trauma / surgery, obesity, ileus, ascites, sepsis + leak, big fluid load, burns</li> <li>Prevent: avoid fluid overload, negative balance when safe, analgesia</li> <li>IAH rising: NG decompression, prokinetics, enemas, colonoscopic decompression, drain collections, deeper sedation; reduce EN if IAP rises</li> <li><b>ACS</b>: NG decompression now, deep sedation, temporary NMB → <b>surgical decompression</b> if medical measures fail; no EN in uncontrolled ACS</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Acute abdomen & abdominal sepsis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Operate / intervene ASAP</b>: perforated ulcer, embolic or progressive ischaemia, volvulus, ruptured AAA, ruptured ectopic, ovarian torsion, haemoperitoneum / ruptured spleen, peritonitis</li> <li>Delayed: pancreatitis; variable: cholecystitis, appendicitis, diverticulitis, obstruction (surgery if ischaemia); no surgery: SBP, pyelonephritis, adrenal crisis, sickle crisis, FMF</li> <li>Sepsis: ≥ 2 blood cultures then <b>broad-spectrum antibiotics within 1 h</b></li> <li>Peritonitis is surgical: <b>source control</b> — drainage, decompression, debridement, restore anatomy — as soon as possible after resuscitation</li> <li>Resuscitate (fluids, vasopressors ± inotropes) <b>in parallel</b> — optimisation must not delay surgery</li> <li>Unexplained sepsis: think <b>acalculous cholecystitis</b> (wall &gt; 3 mm, GB &gt; 5 cm, pericholecystic fluid) → antibiotics + percutaneous cholecystostomy if unstable</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Mesenteric ischaemia","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Suspect: old, atherosclerosis, AF, smoker, post-rAAA / vascular surgery, high or rising vasopressors</li> <li>Signs: pain, feeding intolerance, distension, bloody diarrhoea, unexplained acidosis; <b>normal lactate does not exclude</b></li> <li><b>CTA now</b> (arterial + portal venous phase); don't wait for biomarkers; laparotomy if doubt + deteriorating</li> <li>Resuscitate, broad antibiotics, <b>systemic anticoagulation</b> (dose per CMH formulary) unless contraindicated</li> <li>Occlusive: <b>revascularise</b>; resection alone → very high mortality; second look 24–48 h</li> <li>NOMI: improve flow / CO, ↓ vasoconstrictors; EN only low-dose after stabilising</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Ogilvie (ACPO) & obstruction","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Exclude mechanical obstruction / perforation</b> (CT)</li> <li>Correct K, Mg, Ca, fluids; stop opioids / anticholinergics; NG + rectal tube; no oral osmotic / stimulant laxatives</li> <li><b>Neostigmine 2–2.5 mg IV over 3–5 min</b> (repeatable), monitored, atropine / glycopyrrolate ready</li> <li>Fails → colonoscopic decompression (61–95%; perforation 1–3%) → surgery</li> <li>Caecum &gt; 9 cm warning, <b>&gt; 12 cm</b> high risk</li> <li>Obstruction red flags: severe pain, peritonism, fever, ↑ HR / lactate, acidosis; CT pneumatosis, portal gas, closed loop, poor wall enhancement → surgery</li> <li>Bowel sounds don't separate ileus from obstruction</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"GI bleeding","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Instability matters more than first Hb; labs incl. fibrinogen if massive, lactate, crossmatch</li> <li>Upper non-variceal: endoscopy <b>within 24 h</b> after resuscitation</li> <li>Variceal: vasoactive drug + antibiotics + urgent endoscopy</li> <li>Lower, unstable: <b>CTA</b> → IR embolisation</li> <li>Surgery if endoscopy / IR fail, ischaemia, perforation, aorto-enteric fistula</li> <li><b>SUP (PPI / H2RA) only if at risk</b> (MV, coagulopathy, shock, RRT, liver disease); stop when risk gone or EN tolerated</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Motility drugs (ACE doses)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:22%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:36%\">Dose</th><th>Notes</th></tr> <tr><td>Erythromycin</td><td>1–3 mg/kg IV 2–4× daily</td><td>Stomach; tachyphylaxis 3–5 d; QT</td></tr> <tr><td>Metoclopramide</td><td>10 mg IV up to 3× daily (↓ in renal failure)</td><td>Extrapyramidal, QT</td></tr> <tr><td>Domperidone</td><td>10 mg oral 3× daily</td><td>QT; limited ICU use</td></tr> <tr><td>Methylnaltrexone</td><td>8–12 mg SC every 48 h</td><td>Opioid constipation; not if obstruction / perforation</td></tr> <tr><td>Naloxegol</td><td>25 mg oral daily</td><td>Opioid constipation; ↓ in renal impairment</td></tr> <tr><td>Neostigmine</td><td>2 mg slow IV over 3–5 min</td><td>ACPO; bradycardia, bronchospasm</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Laxatives early in high-risk (opioids): PEG 24–72 h; lactulose 24–48 h (caution if distended / Ogilvie); avoid bulk-formers.</div>"},{"n":"9","h":"High GRV & diarrhoea","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>GRV 200–500 mL</b>: don't stop EN routinely; <b>&gt; 500 mL/6 h</b> → reassess, check tube, ↓ rate, prokinetic</li> <li>Fix causes of slow emptying: opioids, sedatives, α2-agonists, hyperglycaemia, ↓ K / Mg, acidosis</li> <li>Post-pyloric not routine; continuous only; contrast ≤ 100 mL/h</li> <li>Diarrhoea: review antibiotics, laxatives, Mg / sorbitol drugs; test <i>C. difficile</i> if new, severe, febrile or post-antibiotic</li> <li><b>Don't stop EN</b> — reduce rate, avoid hyperosmolar feed, soluble fibre if stable</li> <li>Pancreatic insufficiency → enzymes; bile-acid → cholestyramine; loperamide only after infection excluded</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Ascites & SBP","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Tap new ascites and any deteriorating cirrhotic: cells, albumin (SAAG), protein, culture in blood bottles</li> <li>SAAG ≥ 1.1 g/dL = portal hypertension</li> <li><b>SBP: PMN ≥ 250/mm³</b> — treat without waiting for culture</li> <li>Tense ascites ↑ IAP / dyspnoea → paracentesis; <b>albumin if ≥ 5 L removed</b> (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>No indwelling drain in cirrhosis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"After aortic / abdominal surgery","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>rAAA: colonic ischaemia up to 10% (2% elective) — early diarrhoea, haematochezia, ileus; ask about IMA</li> <li>GRV + IAP 4–6× daily after rAAA; low threshold for CT / re-laparotomy</li> <li>EN within 48 h at <b>10 mL/h</b>, slow ↑; withhold if ischaemia / shock</li> <li>Oesophagectomy / gastrectomy: leak, mediastinitis; HIPEC: planned ICU</li> <li>Support ERAS: early feeding, mobilisation, opioid-sparing</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Trauma & open abdomen","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Unstable: <b>CT must not delay bleeding control</b> — eFAST, laparotomy, packing, angio, REBOA</li> <li>Damage control if shock, acidosis, coagulopathy, hypothermia</li> <li>Non-operative: serial exam, Hb / lactate, blood ready, escalation plan</li> <li>Open abdomen: monitor IAP, infection, fistula, losses; early low-dose EN if bowel viable; aim early fascial closure</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Don't let “optimisation” delay surgery or source control · don't withhold IV contrast for renal worry · don't wait for biomarkers when ischaemia is possible</li> <li>Don't stop EN for GRV 200–500 mL or diarrhoea alone · don't give routine SUP to everyone · no oral laxatives in marked colonic dilatation</li> </ul>"}]},"14c-severe-acute-pancreatitis":{"slogan":"Goal-directed fluids, not floods · No prophylactic antibiotics · Feed the gut · Step up, wait 4 weeks","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Severe Acute Pancreatitis (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise — diagnosis (2 of 3)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Constant upper abdominal pain (often to the back) ± nausea / vomiting</li> <li><b>Amylase or lipase &gt; 3 × ULN</b> — lipase more sensitive / specific, stays up longer; amylase normal in 20%, false-positive in renal failure</li> <li>Typical imaging (CT, MRCP, US)</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>Pitfalls</b>: painless presentations (diabetic coma, hypothermia, shock, GI bleed, respiratory distress); pancreas may look normal in first 2 days</li> <li>Exclude <b>perforation and mesenteric ischaemia</b> (CT); infected peritoneal tap (Gram stain) → urgent laparotomy</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Suspect pancreatitis in any unexplained remote organ failure — especially unconscious patients with gallstones or alcohol history.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Severity — revised Atlanta","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">Class</th><th>Definition</th></tr> <tr><td>Mild</td><td>No organ failure, no local / systemic complications — ward, home ≤ 7 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Moderately severe</td><td>No or <b>transient organ failure (&lt; 48 h)</b> ± local complications</td></tr> <tr><td>Severe</td><td class=\"tgt\">Persistent organ failure &gt; 48 h → ICU organ support</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Watch SIRS / organ failure for <b>≥ 48 h</b>; re-examine every few hours — clinical look misses ~50% on admission</li> <li>Mortality 1% overall, <b>20–40%</b> with necrosis / organ failure; age &gt; 60, obesity, comorbidity ↑ risk</li> <li>Cullen / Grey-Turner = retroperitoneal bleeding</li> <li>Ranson, Glasgow, APACHE II, CRP: modest value — no single lab beats clinical review; use SOFA / Marshall daily</li> <li>CT severity index 0–10 (Balthazar + necrosis): 0–3 mild, 4–6 moderate, 7–10 severe</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Imaging & cause","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Ultrasound on admission in all</b> (gallstones 70–90% detected)</li> <li>Contrast CT best at <b>≥ 72 h</b> (necrosis &lt; 30 HU, &gt; 90% accurate); earlier only if diagnosis unclear</li> <li>CT if no improvement after 72 h, or new deterioration (often day 7–14)</li> <li>MRI with gadolinium if contrast contraindicated</li> <li>Biliary: woman &gt; 40 y with <b>ALT &gt; 3 × ULN</b>; CBD stones → EUS or MRCP</li> <li>Alcohol: &gt; 50 g/day for &gt; 5 years; drugs (azathioprine, 6-MP, valproate, ACE-i, antiretrovirals); tumours; post-op (cardiac surgery 5–10%)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Fluids — goal-directed","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Restore O₂ and volume early (third-space loss)</li> <li><b>Moderate</b>: 10 mL/kg bolus if hypovolaemic (none if normovolaemic) → <b>1.5 mL/kg/h</b></li> <li>Aggressive (20 mL/kg + 3 mL/kg/h) → more fluid overload</li> <li><b>Balanced crystalloids</b>; no HES; no 250–500 mL/h “dilution” targets</li> <li>Reassess often; <b>early vasopressor</b> once volume adequate</li> <li>Overload → respiratory failure, IAH, splanchnic ischaemia</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"IAH / ACS","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Measure IAP regularly</li> <li><b>IAP &gt; 20 mmHg</b>: GI suction, limit fluids, drain ascites, deeper sedation, NMB</li> <li><b>ACS</b> (IAP &gt; 20 + new organ failure) → surgical decompression, or rectal tube if colonic distension</li> <li>Effects: ↑ airway pressure, ↓ venous return, hypotension, AKI, gut ischaemia</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Pain & biliary pancreatitis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>WHO ladder, IV; morphine allowed; <b>caution NSAIDs</b> (AKI); thoracic epidural (bupivacaine + opioid) cuts opioids — risk infection, haematoma, hypotension; clonidine / ketamine as adjuncts</li> <li><b>Urgent ERCP only with cholangitis</b>; consider if persistent cholestasis (APEC: routine ERCP &lt; 24 h no benefit)</li> <li>Confirm retained stone (EUS / MRCP) before sphincterotomy; if coagulopathic → biliary stent or percutaneous drainage</li> <li>With cholangitis: broad-spectrum antibiotics</li> <li><b>Cholecystectomy</b> within 2–4 weeks after discharge (recurrence 8% at 40 days) — delay if peripancreatic collections</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Infection — antibiotics only when infected","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li><b>No prophylactic IV antibiotics</b> (no benefit; resistant organisms, fungi); <b>no probiotics</b> (↑ mortality); SDD unproven</li> </ul> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Suspect infected necrosis: worsening organ failure, high fever, ileus, distension — peaks <b>week 3</b> (25% in first week)</li> <li>Contrast CT: <b>gas in necrosis</b> = infection (only 50% show it); PCT / CRP not specific</li> <li>Cultures → <b>empirical broad-spectrum antibiotics</b> → de-escalate; FNA false-negative ~25%</li> <li>Look for other sources: cholangitis, line, urine, pneumonia</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Step-up approach to infected necrosis","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:12%\">Step</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>1</td><td>Antibiotics + support — some need nothing more</td></tr> <tr><td>2</td><td>Drainage: CT-guided percutaneous, or <b>EUS-guided transgastric</b> (expert centres)</td></tr> <tr><td>3</td><td>Minimally invasive surgical or endoscopic necrosectomy</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Delay debridement ≥ 4 weeks</b> if possible → walled-off necrosis (WOPN)</li> <li>Endoscopic ≈ surgical step-up (TENSION): fewer fistulas, shorter stay</li> <li>Open necrosectomy: 70% morbidity, 25% re-operation</li> <li>Prolonged continuous drainage needed</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Late complications","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Haemorrhage</b> (pseudoaneurysm, weeks 3–4; splenic artery 30–50%): CT → <b>angio-embolisation</b> (70–90% success) → surgery; don't drain sterile necrosis at bleeding surgery</li> <li><b>Pseudocyst</b> (≥ 4 weeks, 10–20%): &lt; 6 cm asymptomatic → US watch; drain (endoscopic) if large / symptomatic, compression, rupture, bleeding, infection</li> <li><b>Colon perforation / necrosis</b> (left / transverse): sepsis, diarrhoea, rectal bleeding → colectomy, or loop ileostomy if viability unclear</li> <li>GI fistula: 1 in 6 necrotising; colonic usually needs surgery</li> <li>Pancreatic fistula (minor): drain + somatostatin / octreotide (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Splanchnic vein thrombosis: anticoagulation only if bleeding risk acceptable</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Feeding","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>No “pancreas rest”; <b>EN after haemodynamic stabilisation</b> — fewer infections, organ failure, deaths than PN</li> <li>Start tube feeding if intake insufficient after <b>72 h</b> (PYTHON: &lt; 24 h no better); continuous, not bolus</li> <li><b>NG feeding is fine</b>; NJ only for gastric outlet obstruction or NG intolerance (blind NJ with caution; separate gastric drain)</li> <li>Polymeric feed (semi-elemental no better); MCT may help tolerance</li> <li>PN only if EN fails / no access; avoid overfeeding &gt; 110% of measured EE</li> <li>Oral: gradual low-fat diet once ileus, inflammation, organ failure settle</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Monitor & targets","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Glucose: IV short-acting insulin, keep <b>&lt; 10 mmol/L</b> (7–9)</li> <li>Triglycerides ≥ <b>twice weekly</b>, keep <b>&lt; 4.5 mmol/L (400 mg/dL)</b>; count propofol lipid</li> <li>Flare on feeding (↑ TG, NG feed, tube slipped, early oral): amylase / lipase + CT; use PN if major gut intolerance</li> <li>Daily organ-failure score; IAP; CT for deterioration</li> <li>Multidisciplinary team: intensivist, surgeon, endoscopist, IR, dietitian; psychology support for patient / family</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Course & prognosis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>~20% of admitted cases become severe; necrotising in 5–10%</li> <li><b>Early “toxaemic” phase (0–15 days)</b>: sterile SIRS → remote organ failure (mortality up to 50%)</li> <li><b>Late “necrotic” phase</b>: infected necrosis, local complications (weeks–months)</li> <li>Outcome = inflammatory surge + extent of necrosis + infection of necrosis</li> <li><b>Persistent organ failure at 48 h</b> = strongest predictor of death; early, multiple, evolving failures worst</li> <li>“Predicted mild” turns severe in &lt; 5%; “predicted severe” is severe in ~50%</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Collections — know the type","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Acute fluid collection</b>: early, no defined wall</li> <li><b>Pseudocyst</b>: homogeneous fluid, no solid part, fibrous wall, ≥ 4 weeks; ⅓ resolve (mostly &lt; 4 cm)</li> <li><b>Walled-off necrosis</b>: encapsulated, liquefied necrosis — the target for delayed drainage / necrosectomy</li> <li>Check contents with US or MRI — don't mistake organised necrosis for a pseudocyst</li> <li>Pseudocyst bleeding 5%, infection ~10% (often after percutaneous attempt), rupture &lt; 5%</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Don't give aggressive or “dilution” fluids, or HES</li> <li>Don't give prophylactic antibiotics or probiotics</li> <li>Don't do routine early ERCP without cholangitis</li> <li>Don't CT for severity before 72 h unless diagnosis unclear</li> <li>Don't operate on necrosis early — wait ≥ 4 weeks if possible</li> <li>Don't keep the patient nil by mouth / on routine TPN</li> <li>Don't over-feed — watch glucose and TG</li> </ul>"}]},"15a-bleeding-massive":{"slogan":"Declare early · TXA within 3 h · Fibrinogen > 1.5 g/L · Ionised Ca²⁺ · Keep warm · Find the source: floor + four more","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Bleeding and Thrombosis: Massive Transfusion; Anticoagulant Medications (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise massive haemorrhage","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Bleeding with <b>SBP &lt; 90 mmHg</b> and/or <b>HR &gt; 110/min</b></li> <li>Classic: 1 blood volume in 24 h (70 mL/kg man, 65 mL/kg woman), 50% in 3 h, or <b>150 mL/min</b></li> <li>Loss is often occult and underestimated; take care in children, pregnancy, β-blockers</li> <li>Causes: trauma, obstetric, surgery, GI; trauma shock = 80% of OR deaths, 50% of deaths within 24 h</li> <li>Coagulopathy tracks with shock and temperature; haematocrit unreliable while bleeding</li> <li><b>Lethal triad</b>: hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Immediate actions","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ATLS <b>ABCDE</b>; secure airway, ventilation</li> <li>Most senior clinician <b>declares</b> → activate the major haemorrhage protocol</li> <li><b>Communication lead</b> talks only to blood bank: state how fast blood is needed; involve haematology early</li> <li><b>Large-bore</b> access (central or intraosseous if needed)</li> <li>Group &amp; cross-match; consider going <b>straight to theatre</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Stop the bleeding","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Tourniquet, direct pressure, topical haemostatic dressings, fracture stabilisation</li> <li>Unknown source → <b>“on the floor and four more”</b>: thorax, abdomen, pelvis, long bones; rapid imaging</li> <li><b>Interventional radiology</b>: pelvic / rectus sheath haematoma, obstetric bleeding</li> <li>Early surgery for definitive control</li> <li><b>Cell salvage</b> if loss expected &gt; 1000 mL (risks: haemolysis, air embolus, dilution, contamination)</li> <li>STOP: <b>S</b>earch for risk, <b>T</b>reat early, <b>O</b>bserve response, <b>P</b>revent rebleeding</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Haemostatic resuscitation","html":"<div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Tranexamic acid 1 g IV over 10 min</b> within <b>3 h</b> → <b>1 g over 8 h</b> (in 500 mL saline); possible harm &gt; 3 h</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Crystalloid may start; <b>no hypertonic solutions</b>; red cells instead of crystalloid / colloid</li> <li>Foundation ratio <b>1:1:1</b> (RBC : plasma : platelets); RCT 1:1:1 vs 2:1:1 → same 24 h / 30 d mortality</li> <li>Then <b>goal-directed</b> by clinical course + labs</li> <li>Monitor shock with <b>lactate / base deficit</b></li> <li>Review and reverse antithrombotics (aspirin, warfarin) — see box 9</li> <li>Early plasma + platelets prevent dilutional coagulopathy; red cells also aid haemostasis (platelet margination)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Targets during massive transfusion","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Parameter</th><th style=\"width:27%\">Target</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>Blood pressure</td><td class=\"tgt\">SBP 80–90 mmHg · MAP &gt; 80 with brain injury</td><td>Initial phase, no brain injury: permissive</td></tr> <tr><td>Haemoglobin</td><td class=\"tgt\">70–90 g/L (90–100 cardio-resp. disease)</td><td>RBC: likely needed after sudden 30–40% loss; rarely if &gt; 10 g/dL; <b>&lt; 6 g/dL obligatory</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Platelets</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 50 × 10⁹/L (100 if TBI / ongoing bleeding)</td><td>Give 1 pool when &lt; 75 × 10⁹/L; dedicated giving set</td></tr> <tr><td>PT / APTT ratio</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 1.5</td><td>Plasma (FFP / Octoplas) <b>12–15 mL/kg</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Fibrinogen (Clauss)</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 1.5 g/L</td><td>Fibrinogen concentrate <b>50 mg/kg</b> (usually 3–4 g) or cryoprecipitate <b>two 5-pools</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Ionised calcium</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 0.9 (keep &gt; 1) mmol/L</td><td><b>Calcium chloride 10% 10 mL over 10 min</b> (citrate binds Ca²⁺, worse with liver failure)</td></tr> <tr><td>Temperature</td><td class=\"tgt\">&gt; 35 °C</td><td>Warm all fluids / blood, warm-air blanket</td></tr> <tr><td>Urine · acid–base</td><td class=\"tgt\">UO &gt; 1 mL/kg/h · normal BE, lactate</td><td>Monitor K⁺ / ECG: stored RBC may hold 5–10 mmol K⁺ per pack</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"6","h":"Which red cells?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Immediate: <b>group O</b>; O RhD-neg for women &lt; 50 y; O RhD-pos acceptable in men / older women</li> <li><b>Group-specific</b> preferred: ready in 10–15 min; full cross-match up to 45 min</li> <li>Unknown group plasma: <b>AB</b> (or A low anti-B)</li> <li>Transfuse within 4 h of leaving storage</li> <li>Two samples / electronic ID: avoid “wrong blood in tube”</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Reading the coagulation tests","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Fibrinogen falls first</b> (&lt; 1 g/L after ~150% loss); Clauss more sensitive than PT / APTT</li> <li>Normal PT / APTT does not reassure; PT more sensitive than APTT</li> <li><b>INR is not valid</b> in massive bleeding</li> <li>APTT falsely long with heparin, lupus anticoagulant</li> <li>Platelets &lt; 50 after ~2 blood volumes replaced</li> <li>TEG / ROTEM: agree protocol in advance; no proven outcome benefit</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Special groups","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Obstetric</b>: uterotonics + obstetric control first; fibrinogen 4–6 g/L at term → measure, replace early</li> <li><b>GI bleed</b>: restrictive, Hb 70–90 g/L (↑ survival, ↓ rebleed)</li> <li><b>Children</b>: blood volume &gt; 70 mL/kg; prescribe in mL/kg; early intubation</li> <li>Bleeding disorders → haematologist</li> <li>Platelets do not reverse clopidogrel</li> <li>Empirical platelets if dysfunction suspected: bypass, renal failure, antiplatelet drugs</li> <li>Most societies: platelets &gt; 75 × 10⁹/L while actively bleeding</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Bleeding on antithrombotics: emergency reversal","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:37%\">Reversal</th><th>Notes</th></tr> <tr><td>Warfarin / VKA</td><td class=\"tgt\">4-factor PCC 25–50 IU/kg + vitamin K 5 mg IV</td><td>PCC immediate but short → may need repeat until vitamin K acts; no PCC → plasma + vitamin K (suboptimal)</td></tr> <tr><td>UFH</td><td class=\"tgt\">Stop infusion; protamine 1 mg per 80–100 U UFH (last 2 h)</td><td>Give slowly &gt; 5 min (anaphylaxis; excess is anticoagulant); t½ 7 min → repeat; “heparin rebound” after cardiac surgery</td></tr> <tr><td>LMWH</td><td class=\"tgt\">≤ 8 h: protamine 1 mg / 100 anti-Xa U (max 50 mg); then 0.5 mg / 100 U</td><td>Only partial reversal; &gt; 8 h → smaller dose; offset 12 h (prophylactic) – 24 h (therapeutic)</td></tr> <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td class=\"tgt\">Idarucizumab (dose per CMH formulary)</td><td>Charcoal if taken &lt; 2 h; <b>haemodialysis</b> removes it (35% protein bound); TT best measure</td></tr> <tr><td>Rivaroxaban / apixaban</td><td>General measures</td><td>Rivaroxaban &gt; 90% protein bound → not dialysable</td></tr> <tr><td>Antiplatelets</td><td>No antidote: stop, TXA, plasma / fibrinogen</td><td>Platelets for life-threatening bleeding (PATCH: harm in spontaneous ICH); aspirin effect 7–10 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Fondaparinux · danaparoid</td><td>No antidote</td><td>Fondaparinux t½ 17–20 h (72 h if CrCl &lt; 30); danaparoid: plasmapheresis if critical</td></tr> <tr><td>Argatroban · bivalirudin</td><td>Stop (t½ 45 min / 25 min)</td><td>General haemostatic measures usually enough</td></tr> <tr><td>Thrombolysis (rtPA)</td><td class=\"tgt\">Stop; plasma 12 mL/kg; TXA 1 g every 8 h</td><td>Cryo / fibrinogen if low; repeat coagulation tests</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"10","h":"Product facts","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>Component</th><th>Storage</th><th>Shelf-life</th><th>Fibrinogen</th></tr> <tr><td>Red cells</td><td>4 ± 2 °C</td><td>35 days</td><td>—</td></tr> <tr><td>Plasma (FFP)</td><td>−25 °C</td><td>2 years</td><td>2–5 g/L</td></tr> <tr><td>Platelets</td><td>22 ± 2 °C</td><td>5 (7) days</td><td>—</td></tr> <tr><td>Cryoprecipitate</td><td>−25 °C</td><td>2 years</td><td>3–4 g / 200 mL</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Cryo also has FVIII, FXIII, VWF. Fibrinogen concentrate: pathogen-inactivated, 3–4 g; neither source proven superior. Fresh (&lt; 14 d) red cells: no mortality benefit. Never carry platelets with thawed or cold products. Intraosseous blood under pressure may haemolyse.</div>"},{"n":"11","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Wait for the lab before declaring and releasing blood</li> <li>Resuscitate with large-volume crystalloid / colloid or hypertonic fluid</li> <li>Give TXA beyond 3 h after injury</li> <li>Steer by INR or by haematocrit</li> <li>Forget calcium, potassium and temperature</li> <li>Return blood to stock after &gt; 30 min outside storage</li> </ul>"}]},"15b-dic":{"slogan":"Treat the cause · Score only with a risk factor · Keep VTE prophylaxis · Don't transfuse numbers · Exclude TTP","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Who gets DIC? (always secondary)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Sepsis / severe infection</b> (Gram −/+, viral, fungal): cytokines, NETs, tissue factor, endothelial VWF</li> <li><b>Solid tumours, haematological malignancy</b> (cancer: thrombosis &gt; bleeding)</li> <li><b>Obstetric</b>: abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, pre-eclampsia / eclampsia, HELLP</li> <li>Acute pancreatitis, trauma (often called trauma-induced coagulopathy), severe transfusion reaction</li> <li>Snake venom / severe toxic reaction, heat stroke</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">ISTH: acquired intravascular activation of coagulation with loss of localisation, causing microvascular damage and organ dysfunction.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Recognise it at the bedside","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Bleeding</b>: petechiae, ecchymoses, oozing from wounds, lines, mucosa, GI tract</li> <li><b>Thrombosis</b>: DVT / PE (often silent), acute limb ischaemia, intra-abdominal thrombosis</li> <li><b>Organ failure</b>: kidney, liver, lung, brain, skin (microthrombi ↓ perfusion)</li> <li>Bleeding and thrombosis can occur <b>at the same time</b></li> <li>Sepsis-DIC: fibrinolysis suppressed (PAI-1) → microthrombi, organ failure. Non-septic / haematological DIC: more fibrinolysis → bleeding</li> <li>A continuum: mild thrombocytopenia → sepsis-induced coagulopathy → overt DIC</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Laboratory pattern","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>No single test: <b>PT ↑, APTT ↑, platelets ↓, fibrinogen ↓, D-dimer / FDP ↑</b></li> <li>Same pattern as <b>advanced liver disease</b></li> <li>Dynamic → <b>repeat tests</b> and trend</li> <li>PT and platelets predict sepsis mortality; fibrinogen and D-dimer do not</li> <li>TEG / ROTEM: hyper- or hypo-coagulable; <b>not for diagnosis</b> (not in any score)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Score it (only if a risk factor is present)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:18%\">Score</th><th style=\"width:40%\">Components</th><th>Positive</th></tr> <tr><td>ISTH overt DIC</td><td>Platelets, PT, D-dimer / fibrin marker, fibrinogen (0–8)</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 5 (high specificity, low sensitivity)</td></tr> <tr><td>JAAM</td><td>SIRS criteria, platelets, PT, fibrin marker (no fibrinogen)</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 4 (sensitive, less specific)</td></tr> <tr><td>SIC (2017)</td><td>Platelets, PT-INR, SOFA</td><td>Screen for sepsis coagulopathy</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Legible ACE points: platelets &gt; 100 = 0, 50–100 = 1; fibrinogen &gt; 1 g/L = 0, &lt; 1 g/L = 1 (ISTH). JAAM: platelet fall ≥ 30% in 24 h = 1, ≥ 50% = 3; SIRS 0–2 = 0, ≥ 3 = 1. SIC: platelets &gt; 150 = 0, 100–150 = 1; SOFA 0 / 1 / ≥ 2 = 0 / 1 / 2 points. Rest of the ACE table is not legible. <b>ISTH 2019: two steps</b> — SIC screen, then ISTH score. ISTH 1–4 = non-overt DIC.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Low platelets: is it DIC or something else?","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:23%\">Alternative</th><th>Clue / test</th></tr> <tr><td>TTP / HUS</td><td class=\"tgt\">Schistocytes → <b>ADAMTS-13 now</b>: &lt; 10% = TTP → immediate plasma exchange (sepsis usually &gt; 20–30%). HUS rarely has very low platelets; TTP rarely severe AKI</td></tr> <tr><td>HIT / HITT</td><td>Fall after heparin / LMWH; PF4 ELISA often falsely + in sepsis → functional test (serotonin release assay)</td></tr> <tr><td>Sepsis without DIC</td><td>Consumption, sequestration, ↓ production — score does not meet DIC</td></tr> <tr><td>RRT filter / devices</td><td>Filter or mechanical circulatory support consumes platelets; anticoagulation prolongs tests</td></tr> <tr><td>HLH</td><td>Ferritin, triglycerides, fibrinogen, LFTs → NK activity, soluble CD25; haemophagocytosis on marrow / node</td></tr> <tr><td>Others</td><td>VITT, haematological malignancy (smear, LDH, marrow), drugs, alcohol, ITP (lupus, viral), post-transfusion purpura, hypersplenism</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"6","h":"Treat the cause + prevent clots","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Remove the trigger</b> → DIC resolves (source control, antibiotics, delivery…)</li> <li>Not bleeding → <b>start / continue prophylactic UFH or LMWH</b>, even with low platelets (cut-off debated); ↓ further thrombocytopenia</li> <li>Add / use <b>pneumatic compression</b></li> <li><b>Stress ulcer prophylaxis</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Blood products","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Platelets: same <b>restrictive</b> triggers as other ICU patients (prophylactic &lt; 10 × 10⁹/L)</li> <li>DIC alone is no reason for a higher trigger or for platelets before a procedure</li> <li>On anticoagulation: many use higher trigger, e.g. <b>30–50</b> (no data)</li> <li>Plasma / cryo / fibrinogen <b>only if bleeding</b> — follow bleeding guidelines</li> <li>PCC: thrombosis risk → only after careful thought</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Therapeutic anticoagulation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>DIC + thromboembolism, not bleeding → <b>UFH</b> (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Active bleeding + leg thrombus → <b>caval filter</b></li> <li>Antithrombin, activated protein C, thrombomodulin: no proven mortality benefit; EU / US sepsis guidance against AT, aPC (Japan recommends AT or sTM)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Shock, schistocytes, falling platelets","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>30-year-old, joint pains, fever, confusion, shock, lactate 6; platelets 80 → <b>15</b> next day, schistocytes; on RRT</li> <li>Think: DIC · sepsis thrombocytopenia · RRT filter consumption · <b>TTP / HUS</b> (lupus link)</li> <li>Send <b>ADAMTS-13 without delay</b>; &lt; 10% → plasma exchange (removes antibodies + ultralarge VWF, restores enzyme)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Septic, on heparin for CRRT","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pneumonia, MV, vasopressors, CRRT on continuous UFH, low platelets since admission</li> <li>Order: <b>DIC score labs</b>; HIT ELISA → functional test if positive; HLH screen if a risk factor; smear, LDH, aspirate if malignancy suspected</li> <li>No specific test for drug-induced thrombocytopenia or ITP</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"DIC with a DVT: platelet trigger?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Consumption + low platelets + anticoagulation = higher bleeding risk</li> <li>No data; prudent prophylactic trigger <b>30–50 × 10⁹/L</b> (experts disagree)</li> <li>Non-bleeding DIC: platelets may ↓ bleeding risk but may “fuel the fire” (more microthrombi) and cause reactions — size of each effect unknown</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Normal coagulation: three pillars","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Procoagulant</b>: tissue factor + FVIIa → FXa → thrombin → fibrin + activated platelets = stable clot; thrombin amplifies</li> <li><b>Anticoagulant</b>: protein C, protein S, antithrombin, TFPI (blocks TF–VII)</li> <li><b>Fibrinolytic</b>: tPA turns plasminogen → plasmin → FDPs; inhibited by PAI-1 and TAFI</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Mechanism by trigger","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Sepsis</b>: TNF-α, IL-6, NETs, TF on immune + endothelial cells, VWF shedding; ADAMTS-13 ↓ (usually &gt; 20–30%)</li> <li><b>Cancer</b>: TF, microparticles, tumour procoagulants; tumour cells ↑ plasminogen activation</li> <li><b>Obstetric</b>: placental TF leak (abruption); phosphatidylserine / TF vesicles (amniotic fluid embolism)</li> <li><b>Pancreatitis</b>: sterile inflammation; <b>trauma</b>: activation + accelerated fibrinolysis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"What is happening in the vessel","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Procoagulant ↑</b>: tissue factor on monocytes / endothelium (PAMPs, DAMPs: DNA, histones, HMGB1), VWF → factors and platelets consumed</li> <li><b>Anticoagulant ↓</b>: protein C, protein S, antithrombin, thrombomodulin all fall</li> <li><b>Fibrinolysis</b>: ↓ in sepsis (PAI-1 blocks tPA) · ↑ in haematological cancer (plasmin → bleeding)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"15","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Give plasma for a high PT / APTT, or cryo / fibrinogen for a low level, if not bleeding</li> <li>Stop VTE prophylaxis just because platelets are low</li> <li>Use DIC scores without a DIC risk factor, or TEG to diagnose</li> <li>Miss TTP: send ADAMTS-13 and call haematology for plasma exchange</li> <li>Forget that platelets / plasma may “fuel the fire”, and transfusion reactions</li> </ul>"}]},"16-pad":{"slogan":"Analgesia first · Light or no sedation · Screen for delirium every shift · Avoid benzodiazepines","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Pain, Agitation and Delirium in Intensive Care (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Assess pain","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pain in ~45% of ICU patients, at rest and in procedures</li> <li>Can talk: <b>NRS with visual support</b>; or functional activity scale</li> <li>Can't talk: <b>CPOT &gt; 2</b> or <b>BPS &gt; 5</b> = pain (presence, not severity)</li> <li>HR / BP changes unreliable — only a trigger to assess</li> <li>Most painful: chest drain / wound drain removal, arterial line, suctioning → <b>pre-emptive analgesia</b></li> <li>Sources: wound, back / limbs, abdomen, ETT and tubes</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Assess agitation & sedation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Agitation in 50–70%; look for causes: pain, withdrawal (alcohol, drugs), toxicity, CNS infection, delirium, hypoxia, full bladder / constipation, noise, worry</li> <li>Score with <b>RASS</b> or <b>SAS</b>; vital signs unreliable</li> <li>Goal (eCASH): <b>calm, awake, cooperative</b>, comfortable</li> <li>BIS / EEG: if deep sedation or <b>NMB</b>, non-convulsive seizures, burst suppression for ICP</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Screen for delirium","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>CAM-ICU</b> (sens 80%, spec 95%) or <b>ICDSC</b> (74% / 82%) — at least once a nursing shift</li> <li>Acute, fluctuating inattention + disorganised thinking / altered awareness (DSM-5)</li> <li><b>Hypoactive</b> delirium looks “quiet and fine” — same mortality</li> <li>Incidence up to 82% if ventilated; death RR 2.2; each day +10% risk; long-term cognitive decline</li> <li>Risks: age, dementia, hypertension, trauma / emergency surgery, coma, MV, acidosis, <b>benzodiazepines</b>, poor sleep</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Treat pain — analgesia-first","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>IV opioids</b> are first line in ventilated patients; titrate to a validated score; choose the agent staff know</li> <li>Analgosedation → less hypnotic, shorter ventilation and ICU stay</li> <li>Avoid unreliable routes (enteral, SC, transdermal) until absorption is sure; avoid modified-release opioids</li> <li>Remifentanil: plan analgesia before stopping (pain rebound)</li> <li>Adjuncts: <b>paracetamol</b> (max 1 g four times daily; may lower BP); <b>ketamine</b> low dose (&lt; 5–10 µg/kg/min); clonidine if high opioid need; gabapentin / carbamazepine for neuropathic pain (GBS)</li> <li>NSAIDs: not routine (kidney, GI bleed, platelets) — selected young / post-cardiac surgery only</li> <li><b>Regional</b>: thoracic epidural for open AAA, thoracic / upper abdominal surgery; rib fractures — epidural evidence limited; paravertebral, intercostal, serratus anterior, erector spinae blocks</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Sedate only if needed — lightly","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Not every ventilated patient needs a sedative; first fix the cause and use non-drug measures</li> <li><b>Light target</b> by protocol (RASS / SAS) or daily sedation break — stop infusions early (ideally within 4 h of admission if possible)</li> <li>Deep sedation only for: raised ICP, refractory status epilepticus, NMB, severe hypoxaemia / dyssynchrony, end of life</li> <li>Careful with breaks in ↑ ICP, unstable spine, airway problems; never during NMB</li> <li>Heavy sedation → ↑ ventilation time, ICU stay, death, delirium, weakness, ileus, infection, PTSD</li> <li>Light sedation does not increase psychological harm</li> <li>Choice: <b>propofol</b> or <b>dexmedetomidine</b> over benzodiazepines</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Drug doses given in ACE (others: dose per CMH formulary)","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:14%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:30%\">ACE dose</th><th>Key points</th></tr> <tr><td>Fentanyl</td><td class=\"tgt\">50–100 µg bolus; 100–200 µg/h</td><td>Rapid onset; long infusion → accumulation, slow waking, withdrawal</td></tr> <tr><td>Alfentanil</td><td>250–500 µg bolus; 1–2 mg/h</td><td>Onset &lt; 2 min, short acting</td></tr> <tr><td>Remifentanil</td><td>6–15 (up to 30) µg/kg/h; no bolus</td><td>Offset 3–10 min, no accumulation; bradycardia / hypotension with bolus</td></tr> <tr><td>Morphine</td><td>Dose per CMH formulary</td><td>Active M6G accumulates in renal failure; ↓ metabolism in cirrhosis</td></tr> <tr><td>Ketamine</td><td>25–50 mg IV bolus; 10–30 mg/h</td><td>Analgesia, bronchodilator; ↑ HR / BP / MVO₂; emergence hallucinations; avoid in severe coronary disease, stroke, brain trauma</td></tr> <tr><td>Propofol</td><td>Keep <b>&lt; 4 mg/kg/h</b></td><td>Onset 30 s; ↓ BP; 1.1 kcal/mL; <b>PRIS</b> if &gt; 4 mg/kg/h &gt; 48 h; not if egg / soya / peanut allergy, ≤ 16 y</td></tr> <tr><td>Dexmedetomidine</td><td class=\"tgt\">0.2–1.4 µg/kg/h; <b>no loading dose</b></td><td>Onset 30 min — cover acute agitation with another drug; bradycardia, hypotension; no respiratory depression; for RASS lighter than −3</td></tr> <tr><td>Clonidine</td><td>25–450 µg PO / IV bolus; 1–4 µg/kg/h</td><td>Hypotension, bradycardia; rebound hypertension if stopped abruptly; unlicensed for sedation</td></tr> <tr><td>Midazolam / lorazepam</td><td>Dose per CMH formulary</td><td>Delirium; accumulate (midazolam t½ up to 6×); lorazepam IV → propylene glycol acidosis</td></tr> <tr><td>Haloperidol</td><td>0.5–10 mg IV / PO</td><td>QTc, torsade, EPS; avoid in Parkinson's</td></tr> <tr><td>Quetiapine</td><td>Start 25 mg twice daily (enteral)</td><td>QTc; no CYP3A4 inhibitors (clarithromycin)</td></tr> <tr><td>Olanzapine / risperidone</td><td>5–20 mg/day / 2 mg/day (0.5 mg twice daily if elderly)</td><td>Risperidone: avoid in Lewy body dementia</td></tr> <tr><td>Naloxone / flumazenil</td><td>Titrate slowly (dose per CMH formulary)</td><td>Re-sedation; flumazenil can cause seizures / withdrawal</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"7","h":"Delirium — prevent & treat","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Find the cause — THINK</b>: Toxic (shock, CHF, new organ failure, drugs), Hypoxaemia / hypercarbia, Infection / Immobility, Non-drug measures, K⁺ / electrolytes</li> <li><b>ABCDEF bundle</b>: pain, SAT + SBT, choice of sedation, delirium checks, early mobility, family — better survival</li> <li>Glasses, hearing aids, reorientation, longer family visits (12 h/day halved delirium), earplugs / eye masks, night-time quiet</li> <li>Stop deliriogenic drugs (benzodiazepines, antihistamines, opioids — Dr DRE)</li> <li><b>No drug prophylaxis</b> (haloperidol: REDUCE negative)</li> <li>Agitated / dangerous hyperactive delirium: <b>haloperidol</b> or low-dose atypical (quetiapine add-on) for agitation</li> <li>Dexmedetomidine preferred over propofol when light sedation / less delirium is the priority (PADIS 2025); A2B: more agitation, bradycardia</li> <li>Not recommended: rivastigmine (harm), statins, melatonin</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Neuromuscular blockers & restraint","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>NMB</th><th>Dose (IBW)</th></tr> <tr><td>Suxamethonium</td><td>1–1.5 mg/kg (↑ K⁺)</td></tr> <tr><td>Rocuronium</td><td>600 µg/kg; up to 600 µg/kg/h</td></tr> <tr><td>Atracurium</td><td>300–600 µg/kg; 270–1770 µg/kg/h</td></tr> <tr><td>Cisatracurium</td><td>0.1–0.2 mg/kg; 30–600 µg/kg/h</td></tr> <tr><td>Vecuronium</td><td>0.02–0.1 mg/kg; 0.8–1.4 µg/kg/min</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Uses: intubation, severe hypoxia / hypercapnia, ↑ ICP, dyssynchrony; <b>always with sedation</b> (monitor depth), eye care, DVT and weakness risk</li> <li>Restraint: not routine; least restrictive, after risk–benefit, treat the cause; legality varies by country</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Propofol infusion syndrome","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Arrhythmia / cardiac failure, metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, ↑ TG, hepatomegaly, worsening AKI</li> <li>Risks: &gt; 48 h, &gt; 4 mg/kg/h, severe head injury, sepsis, high catecholamines, steroids, low carbohydrate / high lipid intake</li> <li>Check gas, <b>CK, lactate</b> regularly; no diagnostic test</li> <li>Treat: <b>stop propofol</b>, other sedative, correct acidosis, organ support (incidence ~1%, death 18–30%)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Special situations","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Benzodiazepines still for <b>seizures, alcohol withdrawal</b>, drug toxicity; long-term users — don't stop suddenly</li> <li><b>Catatonia</b> can mimic delirium (1 in 3 screen +): benzodiazepines, avoid antipsychotics</li> <li>Dementia overlap: prolonged delirium or a new cognitive baseline?</li> <li>~20% of survivors go home on opioids; chronic pain 33–73% — plan weaning</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Sedate deeply by default or before treating pain</li> <li>Trust HR / BP alone for pain or sedation depth</li> <li>Give dexmedetomidine as a loading bolus</li> <li>Run propofol &gt; 4 mg/kg/h or ignore rising lactate / CK</li> <li>Use benzodiazepine infusions as routine sedation</li> <li>Paralyse without sedation and depth monitoring</li> <li>Miss hypoactive delirium</li> <li>Give drug prophylaxis for delirium</li> </ul>"}]},"16b-epicc-perioperative":{"slogan":"Assess risk early · Ventilate protectively · Reverse block fully (TOF > 0.9) · Plan the airway","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – EPICC: Perioperative management of respiration, mechanical ventilation, and pulmonary function (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Pre-op respiratory assessment","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>History: lung disease and control, smoking, admissions, prior NIV / IMV, inhalers / steroids, <b>STOP-BANG</b> (3–4 intermediate, 5–8 high OSA risk), NYHA</li> <li><b>Function &lt; 4 METs</b> (can't climb a flight of stairs) → ↑ MI, stroke, PPC after high-risk surgery; <b>DASI</b> best predictor of 30-day death / MI</li> <li>Tests: SpO₂ (&lt; 90% or &gt; 4% drop on exercise = worse outcome), ABG (↑ HCO₃⁻ = chronic CO₂ retention), CXR, spirometry, CT, DLCO</li> <li>Spirometry: FEV₁/FVC &lt; 70% obstructive; &gt; 80% with ↓ FVC restrictive</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Functional capacity & thoracic surgery","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>CPET: AT &lt; 10–11 mL/kg/min → ↑ death; VO₂max &lt; 15 (= 4 METs) → post-op heart failure risk</li> <li>6MWT ~430 m ≈ AT 11 mL/kg/min</li> <li>Resection: FEV₁ &gt; 1.5 L lobectomy, &gt; 2 L pneumonectomy (or &gt; 80% predicted)</li> <li>DLCO &lt; 80% → ↑ PPC; &lt; 60% → ↑ death</li> <li>ppoFEV₁ = FEV₁ × remaining / 19 segments; ppoFEV₁ 0.7–0.8 L suits resection (V/Q scan more accurate)</li> <li>VO₂max &gt; 20 no ↑ risk; &lt; 15 ↑ complications; <b>&lt; 10 → mortality 40–50%</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"PPC risk — ARISCAT","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>Age 51–80 / &gt; 80</td><td>3 / 16</td></tr> <tr><td>SpO₂ 91–95 / ≤ 90%</td><td>8 / 24</td></tr> <tr><td>Resp infection last month</td><td>17</td></tr> <tr><td>Hb ≤ 100 g/L</td><td>11</td></tr> <tr><td>Upper abdo / intrathoracic</td><td>15 / 24</td></tr> <tr><td>Surgery 2–3 h / &gt; 3 h</td><td>16 / 23</td></tr> <tr><td>Emergency</td><td>8</td></tr> <tr><td><b>≥ 26 intermediate</b> (13% PPC) · <b>≥ 45 high</b> (38%)</td><td></td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Also ↑ resp failure: resp symptom, chronic liver disease, heart failure.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Prevent PPCs","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Robust evidence</b>: supervised pre-op physiotherapy, ERAS, minimally invasive surgery, regional over GA, low Vt and low driving pressure</li> <li><b>Some evidence</b>: stop smoking (even late), prehabilitation, inspiratory muscle training, nocturnal CPAP for OSA in the weeks before, chlorhexidine mouthwash, no residual NMB, prophylactic CPAP after surgery (thoracic)</li> <li>Avoid routine NG tubes (atelectasis, pneumonia); avoid liberal intra-op fluids</li> <li>Post-op: physio, early mobilisation, incentive spirometry; <b>ICOUGH bundle</b> (spirometry, physio, oral care, head-up, mobilise) ↓ pneumonia and re-intubation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Protective ventilation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Induction: FRC ↓ 15–20%; atelectasis in 87% with NMB; FiO₂ 1.0 → absorption atelectasis; FRC recovers in 5–7 days</li> <li>ICU: <b>Vt 6–8 mL/kg PBW, Pplat ≤ 30 cmH₂O</b> (PReVENT: no gain over intermediate Vt in non-ARDS)</li> <li>Theatre: Vt ≤ 8 mL/kg PBW → PPC RR 0.64; IMPROVE (6–8 mL/kg, PEEP 6–8, recruitment) PPC 17.5% vs 36%</li> <li>PROVHILO high PEEP 12: no fewer PPCs, more hypotension; iPROVE: no difference</li> <li>High <b>driving pressure</b> (Pplat − PEEP) → ↑ PPC</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Residual neuromuscular block","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Causes aspiration, PPC, awareness, distress; recovery = <b>TOF ratio &gt; 0.9</b> (normal FEV₁ needs &gt; 0.95)</li> <li>Use <b>quantitative</b> monitoring (acceleromyography); head-lift, grip, visual / tactile fade unreliable</li> <li><b>Neostigmine</b> only after some recovery (TOF count 3–4); needs ≥ 15 min; harmful if no block left</li> <li><b>Sugammadex</b> reverses rocuronium / vecuronium → TOF &gt; 0.9 within 5 min (dose per CMH formulary)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Post-op respiratory compromise — ABCDE","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:14%\">Step</th><th>Consider</th></tr> <tr><td>Airway</td><td>Residual anaesthesia / NMB, trauma, aspiration (vomit, blood), foreign body (tooth, throat pack), swelling, bleeding, vocal cord injury</td></tr> <tr><td>Breathing</td><td>Opioids, residual NMB, pneumothorax, oedema, bronchospasm, effusion, aspiration, pneumonia, anaphylaxis, collapse / mucus plug, pain or distension, atelectasis, PE, ARDS</td></tr> <tr><td>Circulation</td><td>Oedema from fluid excess, MI, arrhythmia, valve disease — check lactate, ECG</td></tr> <tr><td>Disability</td><td>Drug hypoventilation, stroke / bleed / ↑ ICP, myasthenia after thymectomy, diaphragm palsy — check glucose</td></tr> <tr><td>Exposure</td><td>Rash (anaphylaxis); metabolic acidosis as cause of tachypnoea</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Early = anaesthesia / surgery / exacerbation; later = pneumonia, oedema, ARDS. Imaging: CXR, CT / CTPA (PE gold standard), lung ultrasound + echo, EIT.</div>"},{"n":"8","h":"Treat the cause","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>ARDS</b> (Berlin; P/F ≤ 300): Vt 6–7 mL/kg IBW, Pplat &lt; 30, higher PEEP, <b>prone ≥ 16 h</b> if severe; early NMB less favoured after ROSE</li> <li><b>Pneumonia</b>: antibiotics per local protocol; NIV / CPAP only in ICU with escalation plan (delayed intubation ↑ death)</li> <li><b>Pulmonary oedema</b> (fluid, MI, neurogenic): <b>CPAP</b> (3CPO)</li> <li><b>HFNO</b>: ↓ intubation in type 1 failure with P/F &lt; 200; no benefit in cardiogenic oedema</li> <li>CPAP treats post-op respiratory failure; less proof it prevents it</li> <li>ARDS causes — direct: pneumonia, aspiration, contusion, fat embolism, drowning, inhalation; indirect: sepsis, trauma, massive transfusion, pancreatitis, bypass</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Airway assessment & ICU intubation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ICU intubation: complications <b>up to 40%</b> — hypotension 10–25%, hypoxaemia 25%, arrest 2%</li> <li>Predictors: prior difficulty, OSA, neck / ENT surgery, burns, abscess; Mallampati, mouth opening &lt; 3 cm, thyromental &lt; 6 cm, lip-bite test — use together</li> <li>Pre-oxygenate to EtO₂ 90%, sniffing position, vasopressors ready</li> <li>Document Cormack–Lehane; airway alert card if difficult</li> <li>Video laryngoscope: helps experienced users in difficult airways</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Airway obstruction","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Define level, degree, cause, fixed vs variable, worse supine?, speed; stridor = upper, wheeze = lower</li> <li>Nebulised <b>epinephrine and steroids</b> may buy time; CT / nasoendoscopy, plan with ENT</li> <li>Doubtful laryngoscopy → <b>awake fibreoptic</b> intubation (topical lidocaine)</li> <li><b>CICO</b>: cricothyroidotomy / tracheotomy first, then intubate; senior ENT ready</li> <li>Tracheal: small long tubes, rigid bronchoscopy rescue; bronchial: specialist centre / ECMO</li> <li>Neck haematoma (ENT, carotid, thyroid): sit 45°, call surgeon, usually intubate + evacuate; bilateral recurrent nerve palsy → airway emergency</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"At extubation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Laryngospasm</b>: 100% O₂, CPAP via tight mask → propofol bolus if BP normal → suxamethonium + re-intubate if severe (propofol worsens hypotension in hypoxic brady)</li> <li><b>Bronchospasm</b>: deepen anaesthesia, FiO₂ 1.0, salbutamol via spacer (↓ K⁺), check tube depth / aspiration; sevoflurane; IV magnesium, ketamine; long expiration, permissive hypercapnia</li> <li><b>Cuff-leak</b> &lt; 110 mL → ↑ stridor risk; high-risk patients only; don't delay extubation on it alone; early IV steroids ↓ oedema; backup airway plan</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Read the tests","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>GOLD (FEV₁/FVC &lt; 70%)</th><th>FEV₁ % pred</th></tr> <tr><td>I mild</td><td>&gt; 80</td></tr> <tr><td>II moderate</td><td>50–79</td></tr> <tr><td>III severe</td><td>30–49</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Flow–volume loop: <b>fixed</b> obstruction flattens both phases (goitre, tumour, subglottic stenosis); <b>variable extrathoracic</b> flattens inspiration (cord palsy); <b>variable intrathoracic</b> flattens expiration</li> <li>METs: 1–4 self-care; 4–10 stairs, brisk walk; &gt; 10 strenuous sport. Major surgery ↑ O₂ demand ~50%</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Extubation risk & plan","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>↑ airway oedema risk: long surgery head-down or prone, difficult / traumatic or prolonged intubation, fluid overload, neck surgery</li> <li>Weakness or poorly coordinated breathing after surgery → exclude residual NMB; reverse with neostigmine or sugammadex as per agent</li> <li>Deep extubation only with experience; <b>extubate awake</b> if difficult airway, severe COPD or aspiration risk</li> <li>Fibreoptic look for laryngeal oedema if unsure; delay extubation when conditions are poor — both delay and failed extubation carry harm</li> <li>Asthma / hyper-reactive airways: prophylactic bronchodilators before induction</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Rely on head-lift, grip or visual TOF fade</li> <li>Give neostigmine at deep block or after full recovery</li> <li>Use high FiO₂ or high driving pressure without need</li> <li>Insert NG tubes routinely</li> <li>Keep failing NIV / CPAP outside ICU or without escalation plan</li> <li>Use isoflurane / desflurane in bronchospasm</li> <li>Delay extubation on cuff-leak result alone</li> <li>Induce an obstructed airway without backup plan and front-of-neck team</li> </ul>"}]},"16c-epicc-perioperative-care":{"slogan":"Stratify early · Optimise before surgery · Clear handover · Watch the gut, glucose & temperature","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – EPICC: Perioperative GI management; Long term outcomes after surgery; Perioperative patient pathway and resource allocation; Perioperative care of the very old patient; Surgical stress response (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Pre-op risk stratification","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Assess physical reserve, cognitive / psychological reserve and <b>frailty</b> (strongest predictor)</li> <li>Outpatient review ≤ 30 days before, final check ≤ 48 h; selective, risk-based tests</li> <li>Self-reported METs weak → CPET more robust; NT-proBNP / BNP predict cardiac events</li> <li><b>RCRI &gt; 2 + high-risk surgery</b> → post-op troponin surveillance (+ DASI)</li> <li>Tools: ASA-PS, Surgical Mortality Probability Model, EuroSCORE, RCRI, Gupta, ARISCAT, Kheterpal AKI index, Edmonton frail scale</li> <li>Use scores to inform shared decisions — not as a final forecast</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Timing & optimisation","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td>MI</td><td>≥ 6–8 weeks</td></tr> <tr><td>Stent</td><td>BMS ≥ 1 month · DES 3–6 months</td></tr> <tr><td>Stroke</td><td>≥ 3 months (ideally 6–9)</td></tr> <tr><td>Pneumonia / ADHF</td><td>Until resolved / stable</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fast solids 6 h; clear (carbohydrate) fluids to <b>2 h</b></li> <li>Stop smoking / alcohol ≥ 2–4 weeks; correct anaemia; prehab (exercise, nutrition via GLIM / NRS-2002, IMT, psychology)</li> <li>Continue statins and CPAP; <b>stop SGLT2i days before</b> (euglycaemic DKA); no routine warfarin bridging (BRIDGE); DOAC pause without bridging</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"The very old (> 85 y)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Age alone poor guide — weigh disease, comorbidity, <b>frailty</b>, prognosis with / without surgery</li> <li>Frailty: <b>CFS</b> (9-point, used in VIP1), Fried ≥ 3 of 5, 11-item mFI</li> <li>Nutrition: <b>MNA-SF</b> 12–14 normal, 8–11 at risk, 0–7 malnourished; weight loss &gt; 10–15% predicts outcome</li> <li>Cognition (Mini-Cog); post-op delirium in 40–50%</li> <li>Hip fracture: 1-year mortality up to 29%</li> <li>“Start low, go slow”; avoid long-acting opioids / hypnotics; <b>regional</b> preferred; avoid fluid overload; geriatric input</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Stress response & glucose","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Alert (catecholamines) → adaptive (ADH, cortisol, RAAS: Na / water retention) → exhaustion</li> <li>Cortisol ~400 → &gt; 1500 nmol/L within 4 h of major surgery</li> <li>Hyperglycaemia → infection, MACE, delirium; target <b>&lt; 10 mmol/L</b>, diabetics <b>6–10</b>; avoid hypoglycaemia</li> <li>Long-acting insulin at <b>80% of evening dose</b>; no evidence to stop metformin; IV insulin + electrolytes if above target</li> <li>Glucose &gt; 11.5 peri-op → 28% later new diabetes</li> <li>Chronic steroids: continue usual dose (IV equivalent); no supraphysiological cover; extra if unwell; etomidate blocks 11β-hydroxylase</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"MINS & heart","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>MINS = ischaemic troponin &gt; 99th centile ≤ 30 days after non-cardiac surgery; ~8% of major surgery; <b>10% die in 30 days</b>; &gt; 90% silent</li> <li>Starting aspirin, β-blocker or clonidine does not prevent it</li> <li>Continue rate-limiting drugs and statins; avoid hypothermia; ensure oxygenation and analgesia</li> <li>Treat pain, cold, hypovolaemia first → then HR control with β-blocker</li> <li>Type 1 MI strategy (antiplatelets, anticoagulation, cath) not for most MINS; balance against bleeding</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Temperature & kidney","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Hypothermia &lt; 36 °C; falls 1–3 °C under GA or regional → wound infection, MI</li> <li>Monitor core temp if surgery &gt; 20 min; <b>pre-warm</b>; forced air + warmed fluids to keep &gt; 36 °C; rewarming takes ≥ 2 h</li> <li><b>eGFR &lt; 50</b> → ↑ complications and MACE (often silent)</li> <li>CKD: autonomic neuropathy (~65%), poor Na excretion → early fluid overload; saline → hyperchloraemic acidosis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Post-op GI problems — assess & act","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Problem</th><th style=\"width:28%\">Definition / check</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>PONV</td><td>Up to 1/3 after GA; Apfel score</td><td>Any risk factor → <b>≥ 2 preventive measures</b>: <b>dexamethasone 4–8 mg</b> at induction, <b>ondansetron 4 mg IV</b> at end; TIVA, no N₂O, fewer opioids. Rescue with a <b>different class</b> (metoclopramide 10 mg IV, low-dose propofol, amisulpride)</td></tr> <tr><td>Gastroparesis</td><td>GRV &gt; 500 mL / 6 h; vomiting; gastric ultrasound</td><td>Delay EN, exclude obstruction; <b>erythromycin 100–250 mg × 2–3/day</b> or <b>metoclopramide 10 mg × 3</b> (× 1–2 renal), ≤ 3 days; jejunal EN if no effect in 24 h. GRV ≤ 500 alone: keep feeding</td></tr> <tr><td>Bowel paralysis</td><td>No stool &gt; 3 days; exclude obstruction, leak, ischaemia</td><td>EN 10 mL/h, ↑ 10 mL/h every 12–24 h; correct K⁺ / Mg²⁺ / glucose; fewer opioids; mobilise; PEG laxative (preferred to lactulose)</td></tr> <tr><td>Dilatation / Ogilvie</td><td>Small bowel &gt; 3 cm, colon &gt; 6, caecum &gt; 9 cm; <b>caecum &gt; 12 cm → perforation risk</b></td><td>NG suction, imaging, surgeon; <b>neostigmine 0.4–0.8 mg/h IV (up to 2 mg)</b> if caecum 8–10 cm; endoscopic decompression if unresolved at 48 h</td></tr> <tr><td>Diarrhoea</td><td>≥ 3 liquid stools, &gt; 250 g/day</td><td>Early post-op → think ischaemia; test <i>C. difficile</i> (oral fidaxomicin or vancomycin); stop laxatives, keep EN</td></tr> <tr><td>Bowel oedema</td><td>Small bowel wall &gt; 3 mm; colon &gt; 2 mm distended / &gt; 5 mm contracted</td><td>Avoid fluid overload, negative balance when safe (oedema ↑ anastomotic leak); start EN cautiously</td></tr> <tr><td>Intra-abdominal bleed</td><td>Serial Hb, coagulation, haemodynamics, IAP, ultrasound</td><td>Conservative only if stable; plan embolisation or re-look early; watch for ACS and ileus</td></tr> <tr><td>IAH / ACS</td><td>IAP ≥ 12 / &gt; 20 + new organ failure</td><td>Avoid fluid overload, analgesia, open / suction NG, prokinetics, enemas, colonoscopic decompression, drain collections → deep sedation, NMB, decompression</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"8","h":"Theatre → ICU handover","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Procedure, resections and remaining bowel length; perfusion / viability, oedema, distension</li> <li>Contamination; site and quality of anastomoses, stomas</li> <li>Bleeding risk; abdominal closure / tension; drains (marked)</li> <li>Expected GI problems, time to diet / EN, NG risks, re-look plan</li> <li>NG tube only if MV &gt; 24 h, stomach must be emptied, or EN start</li> <li>Upper GI surgery: head up 45°</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Surgical complications","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Not improving / unexplained sepsis → suspect <b>leak</b>: contrast CT, source control</li> <li><b>Mesenteric ischaemia</b> (&gt; 50% death): pain &gt; signs; urgent CTA; revascularise; NOMI after cardiac surgery</li> <li>High-output stoma &gt; 2000 mL/day (or &gt; 1500 × 2 days); fistula &gt; 500 mL</li> <li>Pancreatic fistula: drain amylase &gt; 3× serum ≥ day 3 → octreotide (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>Short bowel &lt; 180–200 cm: IV fluids / PN, small EN early</li> <li>Liver surgery: lactate clearance, INR, bilirubin, ammonia; lactulose; adjust paracetamol</li> <li>Unclear infection in sedated patient → ultrasound for cholecystitis</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Monitoring","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>IAP</b>: bladder, ≤ 25 mL saline, supine, end-expiration, zero at mid-axillary line / iliac crest; every 4 h if unstable; normal 5–7 mmHg</li> <li>Drain / stool / stoma volume and look (blood, pus, bile); lactate</li> <li>Bowel sounds alone don't change management</li> <li>CT with enteral contrast: minimum volume; post-pyloric ≤ 100 mL/h; intubate first if high aspiration risk</li> <li>Nutrition order: oral → gastric → jejunal → PN (PN after 4–7 days); no aggressive early feeding</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"PACU & ICU flow","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>PACU fast track (ASA 1–2): Aldrete ≥ 8 and NRS ≤ 3; slow track (ASA 3–5) physician-led</li> <li>ICU admission delayed in up to 38% → ↑ mortality; avoid <b>night-time ICU discharge</b></li> <li>Prevent failure to rescue: repeated ward observations, outreach, timely ICU</li> <li>Planned ICU after surgery in the very old: individual decision</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Long-term outcomes","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>PICS</b> in 5–8 of 10 ICU survivors (physical, cognitive, mental; family PICS-F); worse after urgent surgery</li> <li>Post-op neurocognitive disorder: up to 40% first week after cardiac surgery; BIS-guided anaesthesia, regional, NIRS may help</li> <li><b>Chronic post-surgical pain</b> ≥ 3 months in 10–50%; risk: pre-op pain, anxiety, severe acute pain</li> <li>ERAS: hospital stay 3–8 days shorter; follow-up clinics may ↓ PTSD</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Rely on age or self-reported METs alone</li> <li>Starve patients overnight or stop statins / CPAP</li> <li>Give tight glucose control with hypoglycaemia risk</li> <li>Let patients get cold</li> <li>Insert NG tubes only to measure GRV</li> <li>Call it ileus before excluding obstruction, leak or ischaemia</li> <li>Repeat a failed antiemetic class</li> </ul>"}]},"17-acute-liver-failure":{"slogan":"Call the transplant centre early · Falling AST + rising INR = bad sign · Check glucose often","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Acute Liver Failure (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Find the cause","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Paracetamol</b> (40–50% in high-income countries): ≥ 150 mg/kg (~7.5–10 g); risk ↑ fasting, malnutrition, alcohol, enzyme inducers</li> <li><b>Viral</b> (40–70% in LMICs): HAV, HBV (incl. <b>reactivation on chemo / immunosuppression</b>, occult HBV), HDV, <b>HEV</b> (pregnancy: maternal deaths); HSV, VZV, CMV, EBV, adenovirus if immunocompromised</li> <li><b>Drugs (DILI)</b>: anti-TB, anticonvulsants, antibiotics, NSAIDs, checkpoint inhibitors, herbal / recreational — think when timing fits</li> <li><b>Toxins</b>: Amanita, phosphorus, CCl₄, stimulants. <b>Vascular</b>: ischaemia, Budd-Chiari, veno-occlusive disease; heat stroke</li> <li><b>Pregnancy</b> (3rd trimester): acute fatty liver, HELLP, rupture, pre-eclampsia</li> <li>Autoimmune, <b>Wilson</b>, malignant infiltration, HLH, trauma; indeterminate (15–30%)</li> <li>In ICU: <b>hypoxic hepatitis</b> 1–18% (shock: cardiogenic 49%, sepsis 30%) — 28-day mortality 40–60%; cholestasis 11–36%</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"ALF or a mimic?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Severe acute hepatitis</b>: jaundice + coagulopathy, <b>no encephalopathy</b> → good prognosis</li> <li><b>Wilson</b>: dysarthria, dystonia, tremor, parkinsonism (may precede liver signs) ≠ hepatic encephalopathy</li> <li><b>Alcoholic hepatitis</b> = ACLF: years of drinking, <b>AST:ALT ≈ 2:1</b>; steroids have a role there, not in ALF; alcohol does not exclude paracetamol</li> <li>Phenotype by jaundice → encephalopathy interval: <b>hyperacute</b> (paracetamol) more recovery but more <b>cerebral oedema</b>; subacute less recovery, harder to spot</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">Imaging: <b>ultrasound with Doppler</b> — parenchyma, bile ducts, <b>hepatic veins (Budd-Chiari)</b>, hepatic artery, portal vein. CT / MRI if US negative but Budd-Chiari / tumour suspected, and before transplant</div></div>"},{"n":"3","h":"Severity & prognosis in ALF","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Reassess repeatedly: age, cause, phenotype, encephalopathy grade, trend, extrahepatic organs</li> <li>Bad signs: advancing HE, worsening INR, rising bilirubin, <b>persistent acidosis / lactate</b>, hypoglycaemia, AKI, shock, multi-organ failure</li> <li><b>Falling AST/ALT + rising bilirubin / INR + shrinking liver</b> = hepatocyte loss, not recovery</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:24%\">King's College</th><th>Poor prognosis</th></tr> <tr><td>Paracetamol</td><td>Arterial pH criterion, or INR &gt; 6.5 + creatinine <b>&gt; 300 µmol/L</b> (3.4 mg/dL) + <b>grade III–IV HE</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Non-paracetamol</td><td><b>INR &gt; 6.5</b>, or ≥ 3 of 5: age criterion (40 y), unfavourable cause (indeterminate, idiosyncratic DILI), jaundice → HE &gt; 7 days, INR &gt; 3.5, bilirubin &gt; 300 µmol/L</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">ACG 2023: <b>KCC or MELD &gt; 25</b> → urgent transplant-centre assessment. KCC specific but not sensitive — not meeting it ≠ safe. Paracetamol: lactate / pH after resuscitation. pH cut-off and age range are not legible in the ACE table.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Acute-on-chronic liver failure","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ABC first. Grade with <b>EASL-CLIF</b>: liver, kidney, brain, coagulation, circulation, lungs (CLIF-C OF)</li> <li><b>CLIF-C ACLF</b> (OF + age + WBC, 0–100) beats Child-Pugh / MELD for prognosis</li> <li>MELD 90-day mortality: ≤ 9 → 1.9% · 10–19 → 6% · 20–29 → 20% · 30–39 → 53% · ≥ 40 → 71%</li> <li>Child-Pugh: A 5–6, B 7–9, C 10–15 — describes chronic disease only</li> <li><b>ACLF-3 is not a contraindication</b>: CLIF-C ACLF ≤ 64 → evaluate for LT; &gt; 64 or &gt; 3 organ failures → reassess daily</li> <li>No transplant option: after <b>3–7 days</b> full support, ≥ 4 organ failures or CLIF-C ACLF <b>&gt; 70</b> → consider limiting treatment / palliation</li> <li>Never declare futility on an admission score</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Call the liver-transplant centre when…","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Every established ALF</b> — early, before too unstable to move</li> <li>HE appears or progresses; worsening coagulopathy; rapidly rising bilirubin / INR</li> <li>Acidosis or lactate persisting after resuscitation; AKI / RRT</li> <li>Shock or rising vasopressors; hypoglycaemia; high ammonia; respiratory failure</li> <li>Cerebral oedema / neuro decline; shrinking liver on imaging</li> <li>ACLF with decompensation, infection, AKI, severe HE, GI bleed; need for TIPS / IR / transjugular biopsy</li> <li>Liver trauma with bleeding → IR + surgery centre; severe pregnancy liver disease → obstetric-hepatology centre</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Send: cause, neuro status, INR, bilirubin, creatinine, lactate, vasopressor / respiratory needs, infection, RRT, comorbidities, trends — and update repeatedly.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"Support & safe transfer","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Critical-care team with continuous monitoring; contact receiving liver + ICU teams directly</li> <li><b>Grade III–IV HE</b> or failing airway → <b>intubate before transfer</b></li> <li>Avoid hypoxaemia and unnecessary hypocapnia; hyperventilate only for suspected ↑ ICP</li> <li>Cerebral oedema risk → neuro monitoring + rescue <b>hypertonic saline or mannitol</b> (dose per CMH formulary)</li> <li>IV access, vasopressors as needed, correct electrolytes</li> <li><b>Glucose often</b>; IV glucose as needed</li> <li>RRT (continuous preferred): AKI, acidosis, <b>hyperammonaemia</b> (even early), electrolytes, fluid overload, temperature</li> <li>No plasma / factors for numbers — only bleeding or selected procedures</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Liver transplantation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Start assessment <b>before</b> criteria are met; list emergency candidates without delay; reassess on the list</li> <li>Window is narrow: 17% died waiting (median <b>2 days</b>); waiting &gt; 5 days ↑ post-LT mortality</li> <li>Contraindications: severe cardiopulmonary disease, extrahepatic cancer, uncontrolled psychiatric disease; irreversible brain injury, uncontrolled infection, refractory shock reduce benefit</li> <li>Infection, MV, RRT alone do not exclude; <b>age alone</b> is not a contraindication</li> <li>Worse outcome: age &gt; 50, creatinine &gt; 2 mg/dL, BMI ≥ 30, pre-LT life support</li> <li>Options: deceased whole liver; living donor; auxiliary (APOLT) if regeneration likely (paracetamol, HAV); ABO-incompatible → discuss, don't abandon; total hepatectomy + shunt only as rescue</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Extracorporeal liver support","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>High-volume plasma exchange</b> × 3 days: RCT (182 ALF) ↑ hospital and transplant-free survival — adjunct / bridge, never a delay to LT</li> <li>Plasma exchange alters INR, bilirubin, ammonia — not proof of regeneration</li> <li><b>CRRT</b>: clears ammonia (∝ effluent); associated with better transplant-free survival (observational)</li> <li><b>MARS</b>: no survival benefit (FULMAR, RELIEF); specialist centre bridge, refractory HE after 24–48 h, HRS bridge, cholestasis / pruritus</li> <li>SPAD, Prometheus, DIALIVE, ADVOS, bioartificial, pig livers: investigational</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Liver basics that matter","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>25% of cardiac output: ⅓ hepatic artery (SaO₂ 98%), ⅔ portal vein (SO₂ 70–85%)</li> <li><b>Zone 3</b> (centrilobular): least O₂, CYP450 → most injured (ischaemia, paracetamol)</li> <li>Functions lost: gluconeogenesis (hypoglycaemia), lactate clearance, ammonia, protein / clotting factor synthesis, drug metabolism, Kupffer-cell clearance of bacteria / endotoxin</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"After liver trauma","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Fever + pain on conservative care → sepsis screen, antibiotics, daily LFTs, tell surgeons, repeat US / CT</li> <li>Bile leak → <b>MRCP or ERCP</b></li> <li><b>Pseudoaneurysm</b>: sudden bleeding or melaena via bile duct → angio-embolisation; endoscopy must see the papilla</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"ACLF transplant judgement","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Early LT assessment for ACLF-2 / 3; not by MELD alone</li> <li>Weigh organ failures, CLIF-C ACLF, trend, lactate, vasopressors, lungs, infection control, RRT, age, frailty</li> <li>ARDS and high lactate → ↑ post-LT death</li> <li>Recovery of circulation / brain and extubation before LT → better outcome</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Evidence for liver support","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Cochrane 2026 (11 RCTs, 681 ALF): effect on mortality, LT, HE, organ failure <b>very uncertain</b></li> <li>MARS removes bilirubin, bile acids, ammonia, benzodiazepine-like toxins; RELIEF (ACLF) ↓ bilirubin / creatinine, better HE, same 28-day survival</li> <li>HepatAssist (171 ALF), ELAD (203 alcoholic hepatitis): no survival benefit</li> <li>DIALIVE (32 ACLF): ↓ endotoxin, faster ACLF resolution, same mortality</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Wait for King's criteria (or a full work-up) before calling the transplant centre</li> <li>Read falling transaminases as recovery when INR and bilirubin rise</li> <li>Give plasma / factors to correct INR without bleeding or a procedure</li> <li>Hyperventilate routinely; transfer grade III–IV HE without a secured airway</li> <li>Give steroids for ALF (they belong to alcoholic hepatitis)</li> <li>Let liver support or a high score delay or cancel transplant assessment</li> </ul>"}]},"18-organ-donation":{"slogan":"Recognise & refer early · Treatment decisions independent of donation · Dead donor rule","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Organ Donation and Transplantation (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Recognise & refer early","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Catastrophic brain injury</b> with deep coma / absent brainstem reflexes (NICE example: ≥ 1 absent cranial nerve reflex + <b>GCS ≤ 4</b> not explained by sedation)</li> <li><b>Planned withdrawal</b> of life-sustaining treatment expected to cause circulatory death</li> <li>Unexpected arrest where a uDCD programme exists</li> <li>Age, infection, cancer, organ dysfunction, long stay → <b>refer, don't self-exclude</b></li> <li>Referral ≠ acceptance or authorisation; don't wait for certainty</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"First actions","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Continue appropriate treatment; don't change goals because donation is possible</li> <li>Call donation co-ordinator; <b>document time and advice</b></li> <li>Clarify pathway: ongoing care, possible DNC, or independent withdrawal decision</li> <li>Share history: identity, infection, cancer, travel, exposures, CPR details, organ data</li> <li>Check authorisation framework / registry via the authorised process</li> <li>One lead for family communication</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"WHO pathway terms","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td><b>Possible</b></td><td>Devastating brain injury / circulatory failure → refer</td></tr> <tr><td><b>Potential</b></td><td>DBD: DNC may be met · DCD: withdrawal decided</td></tr> <tr><td><b>Eligible</b></td><td>Dead by law, no contraindication found</td></tr> <tr><td><b>Actual</b></td><td>Authorised; recovery begun / ≥ 1 organ recovered</td></tr> <tr><td><b>Utilised</b></td><td>≥ 1 organ transplanted</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Death by neurological criteria (DNC) — local code","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Prerequisites</b>: known catastrophic, permanent cause fitting imaging; temperature at code minimum and rewarming delay done after TTM; no residual sedatives (levels, half-lives, organ failure, metabolites); NMB excluded (nerve stimulator); no severe metabolic / endocrine cause; stable BP and oxygenation; known baseline PaCO₂</li> <li><b>Exam</b>: no cerebrally mediated motor response; absent pupillary, corneal, oculocephalic / oculovestibular, facial pain, gag and cough reflexes</li> <li><b>Apnoea test</b>: baseline ABG, pre-oxygenate, disconnect with O₂ / CPAP; watch chest and abdomen (not ventilator waveform). Endpoint: <b>PaCO₂ ≥ 60 mmHg and ≥ 20 above baseline, pH &lt; 7.30</b>, no breathing. Abort for breathing, hypoxaemia, hypotension, arrhythmia</li> <li>DNC = permanent loss of consciousness and all brainstem function incl. breathing; examiners, observation periods and number of exams vary by jurisdiction</li> <li><b>Ancillary tests</b> (if law requires or exam / apnoea test impossible): angiography, perfusion scan, TCD, CTA where validated; EEG not accepted everywhere; never to bypass a reversible confounder</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"DNC pitfalls","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Limb movement → may be spinal reflex; if unusual, stop, document, get expert review</li> <li>Heart beating, skin warm → normal after DNC with ventilation; death = loss of brain function</li> <li>Ventilator auto-triggering (cardiac oscillation, leak) → disconnect and observe</li> <li>Sedation / overdose with renal or liver failure → “5 half-lives” may be unsafe; use levels, wait</li> <li>After hypothermia / TTM → full rewarming and delay</li> <li>Facial trauma, ear disease, unstable C-spine → ancillary pathway</li> <li>Chronic hypercapnia / unknown baseline → protocol endpoint or ancillary test</li> <li>Family: “has died”, not “no hope”; prepare for warmth, heartbeat, spinal reflexes</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"DBD donor management — targets & actions","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Problem (frequency)</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Target</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>Catecholamine storm</td><td>—</td><td>Severe hypertension, brady / tachyarrhythmia → <b>short-acting antihypertensives</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Hypotension (80–97%)</td><td class=\"tgt\">SBP ≥ 100; MAP 60–75 mmHg; CVP 6–8; PCWP 6–10</td><td>Fluids if responsive (PPV ≥ 13%, SVV ≥ 10%); <b>norepinephrine</b> first; add <b>vasopressin</b> (esp. with DI); dopamine &lt; 4 µg/kg/min may help kidneys, avoid &gt; 10</td></tr> <tr><td>Why hypotension</td><td>—</td><td>Storm → catecholamine depletion, vasoplegia, SIRS; hypovolaemia from DI and mannitol diuresis; central hypothyroidism, adrenal insufficiency</td></tr> <tr><td>Myocardial dysfunction</td><td class=\"tgt\">LVEF ≥ 45%</td><td><b>Dobutamine</b> if cardiac index low (2 L/min/m² threshold)</td></tr> <tr><td>Arrhythmia (25–35%); arrest 5–10%</td><td class=\"tgt\">HR 70–100/min</td><td>Brady: <b>epinephrine 2–5 µg/min</b>. Tachy: <b>amiodarone 150–300 mg IV</b>; VT: <b>lidocaine 1–1.5 mg/kg IV</b></td></tr> <tr><td>Lungs (oedema 13–18%)</td><td class=\"tgt\">pH 7.35–7.45; SpO₂ ≥ 95%; Vt 6–8 mL/kg PBW; PEEP 8–10</td><td>Lung-protective ventilation (more lungs procured); <b>recruit after apnoea test</b>, suction, bronchoscopy, disconnection; humidify, oral care; cautious diuresis if overloaded</td></tr> <tr><td>Diabetes insipidus (46–86%)</td><td class=\"tgt\">UO 0.5–2 mL/kg/h</td><td>Suspect if UO &gt; 2.5 mL/kg/h (&gt; 3–4 L/day), Na &gt; 145, serum osm &gt; 305, urine osm &lt; 200 / SG &lt; 1.005 → vasopressin; desmopressin dose per CMH formulary</td></tr> <tr><td>Hypothermia (up to 100%)</td><td class=\"tgt\">35–37 °C (&gt; 35)</td><td>Warm blankets, hot-air devices, warmed IV fluids; investigate fever</td></tr> <tr><td>Endocrine / glucose</td><td>Standard ICU glucose range</td><td>Unstable donor: <b>methylprednisolone or hydrocortisone</b> (dose per CMH formulary); T3 not routine — consider if on several vasoactives</td></tr> <tr><td>Coagulopathy (28–55%)</td><td class=\"tgt\">Platelets ≥ 50 000/mm³; fibrinogen ≥ 100 mg/dL</td><td>Treat if bleeding or needed for retrieval: RBC, platelets, cryo / fibrinogen per retrieval plan</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Tell the family that care has shifted to organ support after death. Targets guide, not replace, judgement — discuss persistent instability early with the donation team.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Controlled DCD (Maastricht III)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>1 Withdrawal decision made and documented <b>independently</b> → 2 refer → 3 authorise and plan (place, time, family, retrieval team ready, stand-down plan)</li> <li>4 Withdrawal with symptom-led <b>comfort care</b> by ICU team → 5 agonal phase → 6 circulatory arrest + <b>no-touch period</b> (often 5 min; 20 min in Italy — local law)</li> <li>7 Death confirmed by clinician independent of retrieval; arterial line shows no pulsatility (ECG activity may persist) → 8 preservation / retrieval, or <b>stand down</b> and continue end-of-life care</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"DCD — record these times","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Withdrawal → significant hypotension / hypoxaemia → circulatory arrest → no-touch start / end → death declared → cold perfusion / NRP</li> <li><b>FWIT</b> starts at critical hypoperfusion (UK: SBP &lt; 50 mmHg ≥ 2 min)</li> <li>UK examples: kidney FWIT ~30 min; liver ~30 min; pancreas stand-down 60 min; lung FWIT &lt; 30 min, withdrawal-to-asystole &lt; 120 min — not universal cut-offs</li> <li><b>uDCD</b> (unexpected arrest): only within an organised regional programme; stopping CPR follows normal resuscitation standards</li> <li>Maastricht: I found dead · II failed CPR · III planned withdrawal · IV arrest in DNC · V assisted death</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Ethics — do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Let donation influence withdrawal or CPR-stop decisions</li> <li>Recover organs before death is determined (dead donor rule)</li> <li>Withhold analgesia / sedation to protect organs, or give it to hasten death</li> <li>Let the death-determining clinician join retrieval</li> <li>Pick the recipient or advocate — allocation is the system's job</li> <li>Assume opt-out removes family talk; use donation as pressure</li> <li>Use “heart-beating / non-heart-beating” labels</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Suitability — don't self-exclude","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Age</b>: no arbitrary cut-off</li> <li><b>Cancer</b>: give type, stage, treatment, interval</li> <li><b>Bacteraemia / sepsis</b>: organism, source, treatment — organs may still be usable</li> <li><b>HIV / HBV / HCV</b>: no bedside exclusion</li> <li><b>AKI, liver, heart, lung dysfunction</b>: optimise; machine perfusion may rescue</li> <li>Long stay, CPR, high vasopressors: give full trajectory</li> <li>Contraindications are applied by the specialist service with current guidance — not a memorised list</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Family communication","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Named senior clinician + donation professional; one consistent message</li> <li>Explain injury and prognosis <b>before</b> raising donation (unless family raises it)</li> <li>Death, authorisation and suitability are separate talks; check understanding, privacy, interpreter, spiritual care</li> <li>DCD: prepare for what they will see, uncertain timing, no-touch period, presence after death, possible stand-down</li> <li>Family asks to “keep machines on” after DNC: DNC is death, not prognosis; follow local policy; senior / ethics support</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Consent & preservation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Opt-in</b>: needs recorded consent / representative authorisation. <b>Opt-out</b>: presumed unless objection — still check wishes and talk to family</li> <li>No payment, coercion or trafficking; confidentiality and traceability</li> <li>Preservation: super-rapid recovery, ex situ machine perfusion, abdominal or thoraco-abdominal <b>NRP</b> (must prevent cerebral circulation; needs governance)</li> <li>Brief staff before; debrief after, especially a stand-down</li> </ul>"}]},"18b-ethics-communication":{"slogan":"Meet the family early · Ask–Tell–Ask · Name the uncertainty · Seek consensus, not a family verdict","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Communication Part I–IV; Ethics; EPICC: Communication and Decision Making in a Multidisciplinary Environment (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Inform the family early","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>First family meeting <b>within 72 h</b> of admission; same physician each time if possible</li> <li>Explain: diagnosis and organs involved, balanced cautious prognosis, treatments in plain words, team roles; you remain available; care never stops</li> <li><b>Half of families</b> don't understand diagnosis, prognosis or treatment (65.7% in one study)</li> <li>One team message (agree before meeting); one family spokesperson; interpreters; brochure / notebook</li> <li>Family = whoever the patient defines; offer participation in rounds</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Core skills","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Ask–Tell–Ask</b>: “What have you been told so far?” → short plain message → “Can you tell me in your own words…?” (teach-back, not “Do you understand?”)</li> <li>Open → values → concern → clarifying questions (“When you say ‘do everything’, what are you hoping for?”)</li> <li>Listen, paraphrase, reflect emotion</li> <li>Chunk, signpost, pause; use absolute numbers</li> <li>Close with summary: known, uncertain, what matters, plan</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Respond to emotion","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th colspan=\"2\">NURSE</th></tr> <tr><td>N</td><td>Name the emotion</td></tr> <tr><td>U</td><td>Understanding</td></tr> <tr><td>R</td><td>Respect</td></tr> <tr><td>S</td><td>Support</td></tr> <tr><td>E</td><td>Explore what it means</td></tr> <tr><th colspan=\"2\">VALUE (end-of-life meeting — ↓ PTSD, anxiety, depression in relatives)</th></tr> <tr><td colspan=\"2\">Value statements · Acknowledge emotions · Listen · Understand the person · Elicit questions</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"4","h":"Breaking bad news","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:50%\">SPIKES</th><th>PEWTER</th></tr> <tr><td><b>S</b>etting — private, quiet</td><td><b>P</b>repare — room, people, key message</td></tr> <tr><td><b>P</b>erception — what do they know?</td><td><b>E</b>valuate — what do they know?</td></tr> <tr><td><b>I</b>nvitation — how much detail?</td><td><b>W</b>arning shot — “I'm afraid I have very difficult news”</td></tr> <tr><td><b>K</b>nowledge — plain words, small chunks</td><td><b>T</b>ell — clearly, no euphemism</td></tr> <tr><td><b>E</b>motions — explicit empathy</td><td><b>E</b>motional response — allow silence</td></tr> <tr><td><b>S</b>trategy &amp; summary</td><td><b>R</b>egroup — next steps, support, follow-up</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Prepare families early for a bad outcome with repeated, consistent meetings; give bad news in a private room, not at the bedside.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Structure a difficult conversation","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Prepare</b>: facts, decisions, who attends, interpreter, private room</li> <li><b>Connect</b>: introduce team; ask their understanding</li> <li><b>Headline</b>: one sentence — “I am worried because…”</li> <li><b>Pause</b> for emotion — respond with NURSE</li> <li><b>Explore</b> the person: values, past statements, acceptable outcomes, fears</li> <li><b>Recommend</b>, linked to their values; admit uncertainty</li> <li><b>Close the loop</b>: summarise, teach-back, next step, document</li> <li>Uncertainty: “What we know… what we don't yet know… over the next 24–48 h we will look for… if no improvement, we will revisit whether this is still helping”</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Capacity, consent & surrogates","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Valid consent: <b>voluntary</b>, adequate <b>disclosure</b>, <b>competence</b></li> <li>Capacity = can <b>understand, retain, weigh, communicate</b>; assume capacity; decision- and time-specific; delay non-urgent decisions if capacity may return</li> <li>No capacity: advance directive, power of attorney, best interests, court (local law)</li> <li>Surrogates: substituted judgement — “What would your mother have wanted?”; unknown → best interests</li> <li>Complex or disputed: documented best-interests meeting</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Shared decision-making","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Default: <b>information exchange → deliberation → decision</b>; tailor to how much the family wants to decide</li> <li>Choice talk → option talk → decision talk</li> <li>Team first (interprofessional SDM), nurses involved, then family</li> <li><b>Seek consensus — don't ask the family for the decision</b>: PTSD symptoms in 1/3 of relatives, 81.8% if they shared end-of-life decisions</li> <li>Goals must be medically realistic and match the patient's values</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Admission & triage","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Admit those with a reasonable prospect of benefit; transparent, fair criteria; <b>age alone is not a reason to refuse</b></li> <li>Mid-range severity benefits most; benefit greatest early in stay</li> <li>APACHE / SAPS not for individual decisions</li> <li>DNACPR ≠ no ICU; consider <b>time-limited trial</b> (e.g. NIV)</li> <li>Mass casualty / pandemic: know the local triage plan</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Limiting life-sustaining treatment & comfort care","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Withholding = withdrawing (morally, ethically, legally in most countries); death foreseen, not intended; uncertain prognosis → <b>time-limited trial</b>, then comfort care if no improvement</li> <li>Use a unit protocol; stop drugs not serving comfort — <b>no need to taper</b> vasopressors / antibiotics</li> <li>Pain / dyspnoea: <b>morphine 2 mg IV bolus</b>, repeat to effect, then <b>2–10 mg/h</b> (or fentanyl / sufentanil)</li> <li>Agitation: <b>midazolam 2 mg IV bolus</b>, then <b>2–10 mg/h</b> (or propofol, lorazepam); titrate to effect — <b>no maximum dose</b></li> <li>Terminal extubation or FiO₂ 0.21 + PEEP 3–4 with tube in — per unit practice</li> <li>Secretions: suction; <b>scopolamine 1–2 mg three times daily</b> or 1 patch / 3 days; <b>glycopyrrolate 0.2 mg IV</b>; stridor → inhaled epinephrine</li> <li>Fluids for symptoms only; nutrition can stop (local law); private room, family, religious support; always consider <b>organ donation</b></li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"Conflict","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Conflict reported by up to 70% of ICU staff; causes: treatment and end-of-life decisions, poor communication, culture, resources, moral distress</li> <li>Keep calm; recognise your own bias; listen; know the law on surrogate rights</li> <li><b>Same message</b> from all team members before meeting</li> <li>Second opinion (ethics, clergy, mediator, palliative care); give family time</li> <li>Time-limited trial; court rarely needed</li> <li>Normalising statement: “Many people feel overwhelmed when their loved one is in intensive care”</li> <li>Prevent: family in rounds, early advance care planning, ethical team climate</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Patient who cannot speak","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Can't speak ≠ can't understand or decide — talk to the patient</li> <li>Treat pain, dyspnoea, secretions; glasses, hearing aids, interpreter</li> <li>Agree and test a reliable <b>yes / no</b> signal</li> <li>Mouthing, writing, letter / picture boards, tablet, eye-gaze; speaking valve after team assessment</li> <li>Document the method that works</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Team safety","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>SBAR</b> handover; closed-loop orders (name, task, read-back, confirm)</li> <li>Handoffs implicated in 28% of errors</li> <li>Speak up — <b>CUS</b>: “I'm Concerned… Uncomfortable… this is a Safety issue — senior review now”</li> <li>Think aloud, double-check key data, focus on solutions not blame; confidential reporting</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Use jargon or unexplained abbreviations</li> <li>Give conflicting messages from different staff</li> <li>Ask “Do you understand?” instead of teach-back</li> <li>Hide uncertainty or give false precision</li> <li>Make the family carry the decision alone</li> <li>Refuse ICU on age alone</li> <li>Under-treat symptoms at end of life</li> <li>Use children / untrained relatives as interpreters</li> <li>Restrict visiting without reason</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"Ethical principles","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice</li> <li>Autonomy → competent patient may consent to or refuse any intervention</li> <li>Disclose what a reasonable person would want to know</li> <li>Palliative care integration improves ethical climate, ↓ moral distress</li> </ul>"},{"n":"15","h":"Barriers in awake patients","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Intubation, hearing loss, language, illiteracy, culture</li> <li>Hearing: cerumen, middle-ear fluid, electrolytes — often reversible; aminoglycoside / furosemide ototoxicity may not be</li> <li>Professional interpreter + cultural liaison</li> </ul>"},{"n":"16","h":"Family-centred care","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Flexible visiting: ↓ delirium, ↓ family anxiety and depression; no evidence for restricting</li> <li>Greet the patient first on rounds</li> <li>Give clergy / social-work contacts</li> </ul>"}]},"19-patient-transport":{"slogan":"Clear benefit or don't go · Intubate in the ICU, not the lift · Check O₂, batteries, pumps · Always have a Plan B","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Patient Transportation I–III (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Decide: does the benefit beat the risk?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Indications: expertise not available (neurosurgery, IR, PCI, CRRT, transplant, prone / ECMO), no bed, step-down, CT / MRI / angio / OR</li> <li>Joint decision: <b>senior referring + receiving intensivists</b>; consider course, staff, logistics, distance, weather, mode</li> <li>Intrahospital: <b>will the result change management?</b> Can it wait until less support is needed? Send images by PACS instead</li> <li>Adverse events: up to <b>1 per transport</b>; major (desaturation, shock) <b>5–28%</b>; cardiovascular (hypotension, arrhythmia, arrest) ≈ 1–2%; IHT ≈ IFT risk</li> <li>Higher risk: children, nights / weekends, ↑ ICP (can't sit up), high PEEP / obese / severe ARDS, abrupt team change</li> <li>Bed shortage: “last in, first out” vs moving a less sick patient — senior decision, patient's interest first</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Stabilise enough — or go now?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Go now</b> when the fix is elsewhere: STEMI / cardiogenic shock → PCI, stroke thrombectomy, bleeding needing surgery, necrotising infection, abdominal compartment</li> <li><b>Optimise first</b> for long trips without a time-critical fix (septic, anuric; refractory hypoxaemia → ventilation, paralysis, inhaled vasodilator)</li> <li><b>Intubate before leaving</b> if airway / breathing likely to fail (e.g. snoring LVO stroke, GCS 8, SpO₂ 90%; obese + morphine + vomiting; HFNC 60 L/min FiO₂ 1.0)</li> <li>Start vasopressors, get central / arterial access, extra IV lines, blood products before departure</li> <li>Don't delay for a central line when several peripheral IVs work, or for CT / X-ray</li> <li><b>FORDEC</b>: Facts, Options, Risks, Decide, Execute, Check</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Team","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Experienced, trained</b> staff — never just the most junior doctor</li> <li>Airway skills matched to the patient; manage line loss, shock, ventilator failure</li> <li>Specialised transport teams ↓ morbidity</li> <li>Assign roles (airway / ventilator vs drugs); <b>closed-loop</b> communication</li> <li>IHT team 2–3 people able to give full ICU care; know the team's limits (drugs, doses, modes, weight)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"4","h":"Monitoring (minimum)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ECG ≥ 3 leads (12-lead capability), SpO₂, RR, temperature</li> <li>Invasive BP preferred; NIBP at least</li> <li><b>Waveform EtCO₂ in every ventilated patient</b></li> <li>TBI: avoid PaCO₂ &lt; 27 mmHg (3.5 kPa) — ↑ mortality; EtCO₂ &lt; 24 mmHg at 20 min after intubation in blunt trauma → 5% survival</li> <li>Pulmonary hypertension (esp. post-cardiac surgery): hypercapnia → acute RV failure — IBP + tight EtCO₂ alarms</li> <li>Defibrillator / pacer with pads; alarms loud + visible</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"Ventilator, O₂ & pumps","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Ventilator: PC + VC, I:E, PEEP, monitors Vt / Pplat; trial <b>10–15 min</b> on it before leaving; <b>bag-valve-mask</b> always at hand</li> <li>O₂ supply: litres = cylinder L × bar (5 L × 200 = 1000 L); 15 L/min at FiO₂ 0.5 = 7.5 L/min → <b>133 min</b>; add buffer + spare cylinder</li> <li>Pumps: enough drug for trip, labelled name / concentration / rate; double-check concentrations; long lines; batteries</li> <li>Suction ≥ 25 L/min (separate unit for chest drain)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Pre-departure “time-out” checklist","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Patient ID, diagnoses, reason for transfer; full notes, labs, imaging (copies + media)</li> <li>Exact destination unit, route, escort; weather; alternative route / hospital</li> <li>Names + phone numbers: responsible senior sender and receiver</li> <li>All equipment checked (batteries, cylinders, expiry); drugs and syringes drawn up in the ICU</li> <li>Trip duration → O₂, drug and blood supply; <b>Plan B</b> with its drugs / kit</li> <li>Final call: receiving department still ready; reserve lifts, clear corridors</li> <li>Give antibiotics / one-off infusions before departure to free pumps; next doses travel with the team</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Checklists caught up to 91% of avoidable IHT events. Every change of monitor / pump is a risk — close 3-way taps when swapping syringes.</div>"},{"n":"7","h":"Handover to the transport team","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Call the transport team early: weight / size, special kit, extra drugs or blood</li> <li><b>Face-to-face</b> report from the decision-maker; special targets; what failed already</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:25%\">PACE plan</th><th>Example (shock)</th></tr> <tr><td>Primary</td><td>Noradrenaline to MAP 65</td></tr> <tr><td>Alternate</td><td>At max dose → add second agent</td></tr> <tr><td>Contingency</td><td>Still failing → treat suspected cause empirically</td></tr> <tr><td>Emergency</td><td>Sudden collapse → immediate action + bolus</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Blood products: transport teams carry little — order extra from the blood bank; unmatched products acceptable rather than waiting.</div>"},{"n":"8","h":"Which vehicle?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>No proof one mode is best; decide by bed-to-bed time, distance, weather, availability</li> <li><b>Helicopter</b>: &gt; ~80 km (50 mi) with a time-critical fix (STEMI, major trauma); ~50 km without helipad → road usually as fast</li> <li><b>Fixed wing</b>: &gt; 160–240 km (100–150 mi), stable, bulky kit, bad weather; add ground legs (45 min flight ≈ 90 min total)</li> <li>Mobile ICU (road) for IABP / bulky devices; helicopter can't stop to do procedures</li> <li>Air-medical steps: patient severity → care needed → time-sensitivity → logistics</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Altitude & flight physiology","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pressurised cabin ≈ <b>2000 m</b> → hypoxia without O₂; helicopters unpressurised, lower</li> <li><b>Boyle's law</b>: trapped gas expands — pneumothorax, ileus / megacolon, intracranial air, decompression sickness (fly low)</li> <li>Pneumothorax: only 6% deteriorated, all fixed by needle decompression</li> <li><b>ETT cuff &gt; 30 cmH₂O in &gt; 80%</b> in helicopters → check with manometer</li> <li>Acceleration shifts blood (ICP, preload); noise hides alarms / breath sounds; vibration may auto-trigger the ventilator; protect from cold and wet</li> <li>Helicopter ceilings: gravity drips fail → pressure bags / pumps (air embolism risk)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"10","h":"En route & pitfalls","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Keep resuscitating; give scheduled doses (e.g. antibiotics) on time</li> <li>Plan for tube loss, IV loss, loading phase with poor access</li> <li>Unmanageable crisis → divert to <b>nearest capable ICU</b>; know exit points</li> <li>Prone transport is possible with preparation</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Don't take over unfamiliar drug concentrations — redraw your own</li> <li>Don't leave with unchecked cylinders / batteries or ignored alarms</li> </ul>"},{"n":"11","h":"Receiving the patient","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Treating doctor meets the team face-to-face</li> <li>Report: demographics, work-up, diagnosis, treatments, changes in transit, transport interventions, contacts</li> <li>Examine, check drips / ventilator, imaging media</li> <li><b>No abrupt changes</b> — ask why first; titrate gradually</li> </ul>"},{"n":"12","h":"Special situations","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>MRI</b>: no ferromagnetic items (patient + staff pockets); MRI-safe kit; long lines add dead space / delay; arrest plan</li> <li>Cath lab: lead + thyroid shields</li> <li><b>ECMO</b>: team (cannulator, assistant, ECMO nurse) + checklist; O₂ for ventilator + oxygenator; mount, power (inverter), weight, family counselling</li> <li>Infectious: mask / filters, PPE, isolation units</li> </ul>"},{"n":"13","h":"Prehospital scene","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>ABCDE: serious A, B or C problem? Fix on scene what is simply reversible (foreign body, tension PTX)</li> <li>No on-scene fix (abdominal bleeding) → go, treat en route; decide on scene / in transit / at hospital</li> <li>Haemorrhage: tourniquet, haemostatic dressing, blood, TXA, less crystalloid, permissive low normotension; prevent hypothermia</li> <li>Before leaving: cool burns (beware hypothermia); flush alkali eye with water; crush → early generous crystalloid</li> <li>Spinal restriction must not compromise airway / ICP; not for isolated penetrating trauma</li> <li>BP goal individual: MAP 65 may do after MI; ≥ 100 considered in stroke</li> <li>When in doubt → tertiary / trauma centre; call ahead</li> </ul>"},{"n":"14","h":"(D)MIST handover — < 30–60 s","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><td><b>D</b></td><td>Demographics (age, sex)</td></tr> <tr><td><b>M</b></td><td>Mechanism / what happened</td></tr> <tr><td><b>I</b></td><td>Injuries / findings</td></tr> <tr><td><b>S</b></td><td>Signs: vitals, GCS, trend</td></tr> <tr><td><b>T</b></td><td>Treatments and response</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Same order every time, standard words; then written record</li> <li>Relay scene history (e.g. stroke signs before crash, drug quantities in overdose)</li> <li>Multiple casualties: use the local triage tool (START, Triage Sieve, CareFlight…)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"15","h":"Intrahospital transport (IHT)","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Minor events up to <b>68%</b>; critical <b>4.2–8.9%</b>; arrest 0.34–1.6%; <b>42.5%</b> of events on ED → ICU move</li> <li>Causes: equipment, human factors, organisation — patient severity predicts weakly</li> <li>Trained team of <b>2–3</b>; one on haemodynamics, one on ventilator</li> <li>Mobile bag: airway kit, drugs, scalpel, thoracostomy tray, POCUS, spare batteries / cylinders / adapters</li> <li>SOP with criteria to <b>abort or divert</b>; inform receiving unit</li> <li>Check equipment before + after every transfer</li> </ul>"}]},"20a-trauma-regions":{"slogan":"Haemodynamics, not injury grade, decides OR vs CT vs NOM · Expect the difficult airway in face and neck trauma","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Maxillofacial, Neck, Thoracic & Aortic Trauma; Abdominal, Pelvic & Extremity Trauma (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Face: airway first","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Le Fort <b>I</b> horizontal (palate separated) · <b>II</b> pyramidal (nose, orbits) · <b>III</b> craniofacial separation (orbits + zygoma)</li> <li>Malocclusion, trismus, periorbital haematoma, diplopia; <b>pulsatile exophthalmos → carotid-cavernous fistula</b></li> <li><b>Always expect a difficult airway</b>: mouth opening, Mallampati, nasal patency, teeth, oedema, C-spine / head / chest injuries</li> <li>Suction, nasopharyngeal airway; <b>video laryngoscopy or awake fibreoptic</b> first line (blood limits view); nasal route if mouth won't open; two-hand low-pressure BMV (emphysema); <b>front-of-neck</b> as backup (rarely primary)</li> <li>Bleeding: nasal packing / balloon → embolisation; careful gastric tube placement; watch airway at extubation</li> </ul>"},{"n":"2","h":"Neck: hard signs → theatre","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:50%\">Hard signs → immediate surgery</th><th>Soft signs → CTA / endoscopy</th></tr> <tr><td style=\"font-weight:400;color:inherit\">Active arterial bleeding, expanding / pulsatile haematoma, air bubbling, airway obstruction, haematemesis, shock unresponsive to fluids, cord deficit</td><td>Minor haemoptysis / haematemesis, stable haematoma, dysphonia, dysphagia, surgical emphysema, neuro symptoms without instability</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Zones: I clavicle–cricoid, II cricoid–mandible angle, III to skull base; <b>CTA</b> gold standard; stable without hard signs → selective non-operative</li> <li><b>Airway now</b> if stridor / hoarseness, massive emphysema, expanding haematoma, bleeding into airway: awake FOI (partial obstruction) or RSI; <b>cricothyroidotomy</b> = first emergency surgical airway</li> <li>Blunt: carotid / vertebral dissection (stroke 5–10%), retropharyngeal haematoma (late obstruction) → CTA, laryngoscopy</li> <li>Oesophagus repair &lt; 24 h + antibiotics + drain; dissection → anticoagulant / antiplatelet with neuro checks</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>RSI if laryngeal injury suspected (→ awake tracheostomy / cricothyroidotomy); blind intubation through neck wound</li></ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Chest","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Shocked: <b>eFAST + CXR</b> → CT when stable; not shocked: <b>CT first</b>. Shock: haemorrhagic (aortic isthmus, chest wall artery), obstructive (PTX, tamponade, diaphragm hernia), cardiogenic (contusion, valve), neurogenic (cord)</li> <li>High-risk for late respiratory failure: <b>&gt; 65 y</b>, cardiopulmonary disease, coagulopathy, penetrating / high-velocity. <b>TTSS ≥ 5</b> → ICU, regional analgesia, consider rib fixation</li> <li>NIV if stable, alert, mild distress; intubate if severe / NIV fails; cautious crystalloid (contusions)</li> <li>Drains: large-bore for haemothorax (drain if &gt; 500 mL), small-bore for PTX; no routine antibiotic</li> <li>Analgesia: epidural (not with coagulopathy, infection, raised ICP) or <b>serratus anterior block</b>; multimodal</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose\"><div class=\"mid\">Thoracotomy: initial drain <b>&gt; 1500 mL</b> · <b>&gt; 200 mL/h for 3 h</b> · instability despite resuscitation</div></div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Hidden chest injuries","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Myocardial contusion</b> (sternal fracture, direct impact): ECG + troponin, echo; <b>monitor 24–48 h</b>; inotropes / surgery if severe</li> <li><b>Tracheobronchial</b>: persistent air leak despite drain, emphysema; blunt within 2 cm of carina → early <b>bronchoscopy</b>; tube beyond tear, repair large tears</li> <li><b>Diaphragm</b>: usually left, often missed → CT coronal / sagittal; surgical repair</li> <li><b>Oesophagus</b>: emphysema, dysphagia, haematemesis → contrast study / CT / endoscopy; repair &lt; 24 h</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:34%\">Blunt aortic injury</th><th>Management</th></tr> <tr><td>I intimal tear · II intramural haematoma</td><td>Conservative: <b>BP + HR control</b> (beta-blockers)</td></tr> <tr><td>III pseudoaneurysm · IV rupture</td><td class=\"tgt\">Urgent repair — TEVAR preferred</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Isthmus (distal to left subclavian) after deceleration. CTA diagnoses (widened mediastinum on CXR insensitive). TEVAR can wait for other injuries if BP controlled; CTA surveillance.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Abdomen: decide by haemodynamics","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Shock reference <b>SBP &lt; 90</b>; warning: SBP &lt; 100, <b>HR &gt; 110, RR &gt; 25, shock index &gt; 0.9</b>; POC lactate, BE, Hb</li> <li>No pain does not exclude injury (10%); clinical exam insufficient → <b>whole-body CT</b> (arterial + portal phase)</li> <li>FAST: Morison, splenorenal, Douglas; misses &lt; 500 mL and retroperitoneum; <b>repeat if deteriorating</b>; positive FAST + shock → bleeding target</li> <li><b>Unstable + abdominal bleeding → OR</b> (mortality ~1% per 3 min delay); stable → CT ± embolisation or NOM</li> <li>Penetrating abdomen = <b>trunk</b> injury: shock → OR at once; search whole trunk and back</li> </ul> </div> <div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Damage control</b> for lethal diamond: <b>pH &lt; 7.2, T &lt; 34 °C, low Ca, coagulopathy</b>; surgery <b>≤ 60 min</b>: splenectomy, liver packing, ligation, bowel closed without anastomosis; do not open retroperitoneum</li> <li>DCR: TXA 1 g bolus + 1 g, treat low Ca, RBC : plasma : platelets 1:1:1 to 2:1:1, fibrinogen if &lt; 1.5 g/L; SBP 80–90 (&gt; 110 if TBI GCS &lt; 9)</li> <li><b>NOM</b>: stable, no major other injury; monitored unit able to intervene; <b>3–5 days</b> + repeat CT</li> </ul> </div> </div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:13%\">Organ</th><th>Key points</th></tr> <tr><td>Liver</td><td>Unstable → surgery (packing) before CT. Stable → NOM succeeds in 92%; embolisation; watch bile leak, abscess, necrosis after embolisation</td></tr> <tr><td>Spleen</td><td>NOM fails more with age &gt; 55, high ISS, transfusion, anticoagulants, cirrhosis; embolise if contrast blush or high grade; observe ≥ 3–5 days (&gt; grade 2); <b>rupture up to day 14</b>. Splenectomy → penicillin 2 y + vaccines from day 14</td></tr> <tr><td>Pancreas / bile</td><td><b>Missed on first CT in 40%</b> → amylase / lipase, repeat CT; grade III–V or duct injury → radiological / surgical intervention</td></tr> <tr><td>Hollow viscus</td><td>Seatbelt sign, peritonism, free fluid / air; CT unreliable → laparoscopy if doubt or unstable</td></tr> <tr><td>Kidney / bladder</td><td>Mostly NOM ± embolisation / stent; <b>intraperitoneal bladder → repair</b>; extraperitoneal → catheter (heals 97%)</td></tr> <tr><td>Vessels</td><td>Intimal flap → antiplatelet + surveillance; pseudoaneurysm / leak → stent / embolisation; shock → immediate surgery (mortality up to 60%)</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"6","h":"Face & neck after surgery","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Watch for airway obstruction, haematoma, re-bleeding; tracheostomy helps airway and secretions</li> <li>Check neurology, vocal cords and swallow; <b>early speech &amp; swallow review</b></li> <li>Antibiotics if pharyngeal / oesophageal injury or aerodigestive contamination</li> <li>Plan extubation with airway team ready</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Antibiotic prophylaxis","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Open / contaminated facial fracture: <b>≤ 24 h</b> cefazolin (or ceftriaxone, ampicillin-sulbactam; clindamycin if penicillin-allergic)</li> <li>Complex facial laceration / high-risk patient: amoxicillin-clavulanate ≤ 24 h</li> <li>Penetrating brain injury: short course</li> </ul> <ul class=\"xk\"><li>No prophylaxis: non-operative facial fracture, nasal packing, pneumocephalus, CSF leak</li></ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Pelvis: timing & recovery","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Definitive fixation if <b>T &gt; 34 °C, pH &gt; 7.2, fibrinogen &gt; 1 g/L</b>; else day 1 or days 4–10</li> <li>Sit at 45° (not 90°) for 45 days; no full bilateral weight-bearing for 3 months</li> <li>Nerve injury in half (L4–L5, S1): neuropathic pain → anti-epileptic drugs; check screw position on CT if new deficit</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Unstable pelvic ring","html":"<div class=\"split\"> <div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Binder / sheet at the greater trochanters</b> from the scene; bleeding is venous (presacral), bony and arterial</li> <li>Big bleed predictors: HR rises after 750 mL loss; Hct ≤ 30%, HR 130, displaced obturator ring, wide symphysis diastasis; symphysis gap &gt; 3 cm = unstable</li> <li>Tile <b>A</b> stable · <b>B</b> rotationally unstable · <b>C</b> translationally unstable (most embolised)</li> <li>Binder can make X-ray look normal → repeat AP film after removal</li> </ul> </div> <div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Haemostasis: <b>angio-embolisation</b> (internal iliac branches) or <b>preperitoneal packing</b> (remove ≥ 48 h); combine if needed; REBOA zone III only as bridge</li> <li>Binder &gt; 6 h → C-clamp + transcondylar traction; ex-fix if no definitive fixation by day 5</li> <li>Meatal blood / retention → contrast urethrogram; any resistance → <b>suprapubic</b> catheter</li> <li>Open pelvic fracture (risk doubled) → diverting colostomy; VTE risk ↑ (Doppler)</li> </ul> </div> </div>"},{"n":"10","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Let CT delay surgery in the unstable patient</li> <li>Trust a negative FAST or a normal binder X-ray</li> <li>Force a urinary catheter against resistance</li> <li>Use REBOA as more than a bridge, or delay OR / angiography for it</li> <li>Do definitive pelvic fixation in the acute unstable phase</li> </ul>"}]},"20b-thrombocytopenia-hit":{"slogan":"Repeat + smear first · Schistocytes = haematology now · HIT → stop ALL heparin + start an alternative","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Bleeding and Thrombosis: Coagulation Tests, Thrombocytopenia, HIT, ECLS (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Read the clotting screen","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:17%\">Pattern</th><th style=\"width:20%\">Problem</th><th>Common causes</th></tr> <tr><td>PT ↑, APTT normal</td><td>Extrinsic (FVII)</td><td>Warfarin (↓ II, VII, IX, X), vitamin K deficiency, liver failure</td></tr> <tr><td>PT normal, APTT ↑</td><td>Intrinsic</td><td>Heparin (or sample contamination), antiphospholipid / lupus anticoagulant, haemophilia A / B / C, severe von Willebrand; dabigatran</td></tr> <tr><td>PT ↑ + APTT ↑</td><td>Common / multiple</td><td>Low fibrinogen (DIC, dilution after massive transfusion), complex acquired disorder, inherited common-pathway defect</td></tr> <tr><td>Both normal + bleeding</td><td>Platelet / VWF</td><td>Thrombocytopenia, platelet dysfunction, von Willebrand disease</td></tr> <tr><td>Thrombin time ↑</td><td>Fibrin formation</td><td>Heparin, thrombin inhibitors (argatroban, hirudin), low fibrinogen, FDPs / D-dimers, myeloma / amyloid</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\"><b>ACT</b>: high-dose heparin (bypass target ~4 × baseline). <b>FDPs</b> = fibrinogen lysis (anticoagulant at high levels); <b>D-dimer</b> = cross-linked fibrin lysis. <b>TEG r-time</b> ↑ with heparin, warfarin, low fibrinogen; heparinase TEG shows clot during heparin. Persistent prolongation → haematology. Low Ca²⁺ also impairs clotting.</div>"},{"n":"2","h":"Thrombocytopenia: why?","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Common: 8–68% on admission, 13–44% acquired; risks: illness severity, organ failure, <b>sepsis (commonest)</b>, renal failure</li> <li>Mechanism: ↓ production, ↑ destruction, sequestration (splenomegaly, portal hypertension), haemodilution, lab artefact (clumping)</li> <li><b>Drugs ~20%</b>: heparin, cephalosporins, carbapenems, linezolid, co-trimoxazole; aspirin / NSAIDs worsen bleeding</li> <li>Devices: IABP, LVAD, PA catheter; recent (cardiac) surgery</li> <li><b>Abrupt fall 1–2 days → immune</b>; gradual over a week → consumption or marrow failure</li> <li>History: personal / family bleeding (epistaxis, bruising, haemarthrosis, dental), herbal remedies, alcohol, travel</li> <li>Pregnancy: gestational, HELLP, pre-eclampsia → joint ICU–obstetric–haematology pathway</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Work-up","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Unexpected low count → <b>repeat + blood smear</b></li> <li>Smear: sepsis (toxic granulation), giant platelets (hereditary), blasts (marrow), <b>schistocytes</b></li> <li>Renal + liver profile, infection screen, cultures</li> <li>DIC screen: <b>PT, APTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer</b></li> <li>Suspected TMA: <b>LDH, bilirubin, haptoglobin</b></li> <li>Examine spleen; bleeding pattern (generalised microvascular = coagulopathy)</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Schistocytes</b> → TTP / HUS / DIC: urgent senior haematology. <b>ADAMTS-13 &lt; 10%</b> = TTP → <b>urgent plasma exchange</b> (mortality &gt; 90% → ~10%)</div></div>"},{"n":"4","h":"Platelet thresholds & management","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:30%\">Situation</th><th style=\"width:20%\">Platelets (× 10⁹/L)</th><th>Action</th></tr> <tr><td>No bleeding, no procedure</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 10</td><td>Transfuse (spontaneous ICH most common here)</td></tr> <tr><td>Active bleeding or high-risk procedure</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 50</td><td>Transfuse; no rise at 30–60 min → destruction (immune / drug)</td></tr> <tr><td>Lumbar puncture / epidural</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 70–80</td><td>Strongly discouraged</td></tr> <tr><td>Pharmacological VTE prophylaxis</td><td class=\"tgt\">&lt; 30</td><td>Withhold (or if major bleeding risk); otherwise continue</td></tr> <tr><td>Large-bore lines in / out, IABP removal</td><td>—</td><td>Platelets usually given (weak evidence); CVC with ultrasound</td></tr> <tr><td>RRT, high bleeding risk, no liver failure</td><td>—</td><td>Consider <b>citrate</b> anticoagulation</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Treat the cause; stop suspect drugs. Immune thrombocytopenia: IVIG ± high-dose steroids. <b>Avoid prophylactic platelets in TTP, HUS, HIT, catastrophic antiphospholipid syndrome</b> — discuss with haematology.</div>"},{"n":"5","h":"Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia: suspect","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>IgG vs PF4/heparin → platelet activation → <b>thrombosis despite low platelets</b> (DVT/PE, limb ischaemia, MI, stroke, skin necrosis, adrenal haemorrhage)</li> <li>Risk: <b>UFH (10× LMWH)</b>, higher dose, longer use; cardiac / orthopaedic 1–5% vs medical 0.1–1%</li> <li>Platelets fall in 85–90%; thrombosis precedes the fall in up to 25%</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:26%\">Type</th><th>Timing</th></tr> <tr><td>Typical</td><td>Fall from <b>day 5–10</b> of heparin</td></tr> <tr><td>Rapid-onset</td><td>Within 24 h if heparin in last 30 (–100) days</td></tr> <tr><td>Acute</td><td>&lt; 30 min after bolus: fever, chills, ↑ BP, tachycardia, dyspnoea, chest pain</td></tr> <tr><td>Delayed</td><td>Median 10–14 days after heparin stopped; may cause DIC</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">After cardiac surgery: up to 50% antibody-positive but HIT only 1–5%. Suspect if platelets recover then fall again on day 5–14, or stay low &gt; 4 days.</div>"},{"n":"6","h":"HIT: confirm & treat","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Pre-test <b>4T score</b>: low score → HIT 0–3% (PPV only 9–17%)</li> <li><b>Antigen (PF4) assay</b>: sensitive, poor specificity. <b>Functional assay</b> (serotonin release, HIPA): sensitive + specific</li> <li>Early senior haematology advice</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>Stop ALL heparin</b> (flushes, coated lines) → start <b>non-heparin anticoagulant</b>: argatroban, bivalirudin, lepirudin, danaparoid (dose per CMH formulary)</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Choose by organ function: <b>argatroban</b> (hepatic; t½ 45 min) is the choice in renal failure; APTT-monitored</li> <li>Without alternative anticoagulation up to <b>50% develop DVT / PE</b></li> <li>Platelets only if bleeding or high-risk procedure (some keep &gt; 10)</li> <li>Warfarin overlap with DTI ≥ <b>5 days</b>; INR hard to read on argatroban → haematology-guided</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Anticoagulating ECLS circuits","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Circuit contact → inflammation + hypercoagulability → “partial” systemic anticoagulation</li> </ul> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\"><b>UFH bolus 50–100 IU/kg</b> at cannulation → infusion ~<b>20–70 IU/kg/h</b></div></div> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:26%\">Test</th><th>Target</th></tr> <tr><td>ACT</td><td class=\"tgt\">160–220 s (1.5 × upper limit); hourly until stable, then ≥ 4-hourly</td></tr> <tr><td>APTT</td><td class=\"tgt\">1.5 × baseline, ~50–70 s</td></tr> <tr><td>Anti-Xa · AT</td><td>More accurate (misleading with high bilirubin) · some replace AT to 80–100%</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Clots: observe 1–5 mm; <b>&gt; 5 mm on return side → change that part / circuit</b>; heparin 50 IU/kg bolus, new ACT target 220 s</li> <li>Clot risks: oxygenator failure, consumption, emboli, pump-head failure</li> </ul>"},{"n":"8","h":"Bleeding on ECLS","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Commonest reason to stop ECLS early; <b>cannula site</b> most common → delay heparin bolus until after dilation, sutures, compression</li> <li>↓ heparin (ACT &lt; 1.5 × upper limit), plasma, fibrinogen, antifibrinolytic if severe</li> <li><b>ICH 7–10%</b>: stop anticoagulation or come off ECLS; heparin-coated circuits allow runs without anticoagulation</li> <li>ELSO: keep platelets <b>≥ 80 × 10⁹/L</b>; fibrinogen daily, keep normal</li> <li>rFVIIa only with a <b>primed spare circuit</b> at bedside (thrombosis)</li> <li>HIT on ECLS: platelets stay &lt; 10 despite transfusion → DTI (argatroban / bivalirudin) + non-heparin-coated circuit</li> <li>From bypass to ECLS: wait for normal ACT / bleeding to stop before heparin</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Act on one low count without repeat sample and smear</li> <li>Give prophylactic platelets in TTP, HUS, HIT or CAPS</li> <li>Diagnose HIT on a positive PF4 antibody alone (esp. after cardiac surgery)</li> <li>Just stop heparin in HIT without an alternative anticoagulant</li> <li>Leave heparin flushes or heparin-coated lines in suspected HIT</li> <li>Start warfarin alone or with short overlap in acute HIT</li> <li>Delay plasma exchange in TTP</li> <li>Do LP / epidural with platelets &lt; 70–80</li> </ul>"}]},"20c-anticoag-periop-vte":{"slogan":"Every ICU patient gets VTE prophylaxis unless bleeding · Massive PE → UFH bolus, then alteplase 100 mg over 2 h","legend":"ACE content only · doses as stated in ACE","ref":"ESICM Academy ACE – Bleeding and Thrombosis: Anticoagulant Medications, Interruption of Therapy, Thromboprophylaxis (INDEX 2026)","secs":[{"n":"1","h":"Know the drug","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:15%\">Drug</th><th style=\"width:30%\">Action · half-life</th><th style=\"width:20%\">Monitor</th><th>ICU points</th></tr> <tr><td>Aspirin</td><td>Irreversible COX block; 7–10 days</td><td>—</td><td>Peak 30 min (enteric 3–4 h)</td></tr> <tr><td>Clopidogrel · prasugrel</td><td>Irreversible P2Y12 block</td><td>—</td><td>Prasugrel faster, more bleeding; stop 7 days before surgery (discuss with cardiology)</td></tr> <tr><td>GP IIb/IIIa</td><td>Abciximab t½ 30 min, effect 12–24 h; tirofiban / eptifibatide short</td><td>—</td><td>Major bleeding 10%; abciximab thrombocytopenia ≤ 5%</td></tr> <tr><td>Warfarin (VKA)</td><td>↓ II, VII, IX, X, protein C / S</td><td>INR</td><td>Reversal: 4-factor PCC 25–50 IU/kg + vitamin K 5 mg</td></tr> <tr><td>UFH</td><td>AT-mediated anti-IIa / Xa; t½ 45–90 min</td><td>APTT / ACT</td><td>APTT normal ~3–4 h after stopping; resistant → check <b>AT-III</b> (plasma / recombinant AT)</td></tr> <tr><td>LMWH</td><td>Mainly anti-Xa; offset 12 h (prophylactic) – 24 h (therapeutic)</td><td>Anti-Xa if needed</td><td><b>Renal dose adjustment</b>; anti-Xa in extremes of weight, renal failure, ECLS (bilirubin interferes)</td></tr> <tr><td>Fondaparinux</td><td>Indirect Xa; 17–20 h (72 h if CrCl &lt; 30)</td><td>—</td><td>Needs antithrombin; no antidote</td></tr> <tr><td>Argatroban</td><td>Direct thrombin inhibitor, hepatic; 45 min</td><td>APTT</td><td>HIT + renal failure; also prolongs PT, ACT, TT</td></tr> <tr><td>Bivalirudin</td><td>DTI; 25 min (3.5 h on dialysis)</td><td>ACT / APTT</td><td>PCI, bypass in HIT; 20% renal</td></tr> <tr><td>Dabigatran</td><td>Oral DTI; peak 2–3 h; 12–14 h (22–35 h if CrCl &lt; 30)</td><td>TT (APTT poor)</td><td>80% renal; dialysable; idarucizumab</td></tr> <tr><td>Rivaroxaban · apixaban</td><td>Oral Xa inhibitors</td><td>—</td><td>Rivaroxaban &gt; 90% protein-bound, not dialysable</td></tr> <tr><td>Alteplase (rtPA)</td><td>Plasminogen → plasmin</td><td>—</td><td>~6% cerebral haemorrhage in stroke</td></tr> </table>"},{"n":"2","h":"Stopping for a procedure","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Follow local guidelines; else ACCP:</li> <li><b>VKA</b>: stop ~<b>5 days</b> before; restart <b>12–24 h</b> after if haemostasis</li> <li><b>Bridge</b> (LMWH / UFH) only if high thrombotic risk: mitral prosthesis, AF with high CHADS₂, VTE &lt; 3 months; blood products ready</li> <li><b>IV UFH</b>: stop <b>4–6 h</b> before; confirm normal PT / APTT</li> <li><b>Therapeutic LMWH</b>: last dose <b>24 h</b> before; restart 24 h after (low–moderate bleeding risk) or <b>48–72 h</b> (high)</li> <li><b>Aspirin</b>: continue if moderate–high CV risk or CABG; stop 7–10 days only if low risk or critical site (brain, spine, eye)</li> <li><b>Thienopyridine</b> + aspirin before CABG: stop ≥ 5 days</li> </ul>"},{"n":"3","h":"Stents & NOACs","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Ask: stent for stable disease or ACS? Bare-metal or drug-eluting?</li> <li>DAPT minimum: stable + BMS <b>1 month</b>; stable + DES <b>6 months</b>; after ACS <b>12 months</b> (any stent)</li> <li>Urgent surgery inside these windows → <b>continue DAPT</b>; cardiology + haematology + surgery decide together</li> </ul> <table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:30%\">Stop before major surgery</th><th>Standard bleeding risk</th><th>High bleeding risk</th></tr> <tr><td>Dabigatran, CrCl &gt; 80</td><td class=\"tgt\">24 h</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 2 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Dabigatran, low CrCl</td><td>≥ 4 days → specialist</td><td>≥ 5 days → specialist</td></tr> <tr><td>Rivaroxaban, CrCl &gt; 30</td><td class=\"tgt\">24 h</td><td class=\"tgt\">≥ 2 days</td></tr> <tr><td>Rivaroxaban, CrCl ≤ 30</td><td>≥ 2 days → specialist</td><td>≥ 4 days → specialist</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Middle CrCl rows of the ACE tables are not legible in the source — check local guidance.</div>"},{"n":"4","h":"VTE prophylaxis in ICU","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Medical admission → 8× thrombosis risk. Risks: cancer, prior VTE, immobility, thrombophilia, trauma / surgery &lt; 1 month, age &gt; 70, MI / stroke, infection, obesity</li> <li>Bleeding risks: ulcer &lt; 3 months, <b>platelets &lt; 50</b>, age &gt; 85, liver / renal failure, ICU stay</li> <li>Give <b>LMWH, low-dose UFH or fondaparinux</b> (none superior) vs nothing; no routine screening</li> <li>Bleeding / high risk → <b>mechanical</b> until risk falls, then restart drug; continue until mobile</li> <li><b>IPC</b>: ↓ DVT (no effect on PE, mortality); fewer bleeds than heparin; IPC + drug ↓ DVT after surgery</li> <li>Stockings: no proven benefit; skin breakdown (5% in stroke)</li> </ul>"},{"n":"5","h":"DVT & IVC filters","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Proximal DVT embolises more than below-knee → UFH, LMWH or fondaparinux</li> <li>Provoked first DVT: <b>3 months</b>; recurrent / unprovoked: consider indefinite</li> <li>Cancer: weight-based <b>LMWH ≥ 3–6 months</b> or while cancer active</li> <li><b>IVC filter</b>: high PE risk + cannot anticoagulate (haemorrhagic stroke, recent surgery), recurrent PE despite anticoagulation; restart anticoagulation when safe</li> <li>PREPIC: ↓ PE but ↑ recurrent DVT, no mortality effect; late DVT 21%, IVC thrombosis 2–10%</li> </ul>"},{"n":"6","h":"Pulmonary embolism: classify","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th style=\"width:22%\">AHA class</th><th>Definition</th></tr> <tr><td>Massive</td><td class=\"tgt\">Sustained hypotension (SBP &lt; 90 for ≥ 15 min or inotropes), pulselessness, or HR &lt; 40 with shock</td></tr> <tr><td>Submassive</td><td>SBP ≥ 90 + RV dysfunction (echo / CT, BNP, ECG) or troponin rise</td></tr> <tr><td>Low-risk</td><td>Normotensive, normal biomarkers, normal RV</td></tr> </table> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Under-diagnosed in ICU (occult PE 9% on CT); 10% of hospital deaths; ~10% die within 1–3 months</li> <li>Dyspnoea, pleuritic pain, cough, haemoptysis, sweating; tachypnoea, tachycardia, loud P2, rub, fever</li> <li>Signs: ↑ JVP, RV heave, right S3; ECG: S1Q3T3, new RBBB, T inversion V1–4, AF</li> <li>Echo: <b>McConnell sign</b> (RV free-wall hypokinesis, apex spared); CTPA gold standard</li> </ul>"},{"n":"7","h":"Pulmonary embolism: treat","html":"<div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">Suspected PE → <b>weight-adjusted UFH bolus</b> now (dose per CMH formulary)</div></div> <div class=\"dose yellow\"><div class=\"mid\">Confirmed + hypotension → <b>alteplase 100 mg IV over 2 h</b> without delay</div></div> <ul class=\"ck\"> <li>Bleeding after lysis: major 36%, intracranial / fatal 4% → <b>not for submassive PE</b></li> <li>PE + PFO / paradoxical embolus → surgical embolectomy</li> </ul> <div class=\"note\">Absolute lysis contraindications: prior ICH, cerebral vascular lesion or malignancy, ischaemic stroke &lt; 3 months, aortic dissection, active bleeding, head / facial trauma &lt; 3 months.</div>"},{"n":"8","h":"When lysis is not an option","html":"<ul class=\"ck\"> <li><b>Catheter therapy</b> if lysis contraindicated / failed or surgery unavailable: aspiration, fragmentation, rheolytic thrombectomy</li> <li>Aim: ↓ PA pressure, RV strain, PVR → RV recovery; success 81%</li> <li>Only experienced operators with tamponade / PA rupture back-up</li> <li><b>Surgical embolectomy</b>: recent series 96% survival (older 70–80%); can be off bypass</li> </ul>"},{"n":"9","h":"IVC filter risks","html":"<table class=\"ft\"> <tr><th>Early</th><th>%</th><th>Late</th><th>%</th></tr> <tr><td>Malposition</td><td>1.3</td><td>Recurrent DVT</td><td>21</td></tr> <tr><td>Haematoma</td><td>0.6</td><td>IVC thrombosis</td><td>2–10</td></tr> <tr><td>Air embolism</td><td>0.2</td><td>IVC penetration</td><td>0.3</td></tr> <tr><td>Carotid puncture</td><td>0.04</td><td>Migration</td><td>0.3</td></tr> </table> <div class=\"note\">Insertion mortality ~0.1%; pneumothorax, AV fistula 0.02%.</div>"},{"n":"10","h":"Do not","html":"<ul class=\"xk\"> <li>Lyse submassive or low-risk PE</li> <li>Wait for CTPA before heparin when PE suspected</li> <li>Bridge low-thrombotic-risk patients</li> <li>Stop DAPT for urgent surgery inside the stent window without cardiology</li> <li>Forget renal dosing of LMWH or NOAC clearance in AKI</li> <li>Rely on stockings alone</li> </ul>"}]}};